        },
        {
            "role": "system",
            "injection_position": 0,
            "injection_depth": 4,
            "system_prompt": false,
            "marker": false,
            "enabled": true,
            "forbid_overrides": false,
            "injection_order": 100,
            "injection_trigger": [],
            "identifier": "nemo-init-recipes-erotica-01",
            "name": "🔧 Init · Erotica · 01/07",
            "content": "{{#if {{.NCGenreId == erotica}}}}{{// @category Variable-Init }}{{// @icon 🔧 }}{{// @badge INIT }}{{// @tooltip Erotica. Selected-partition data; keep enabled in supplied order after recipe selectors and before the Writing Recipe resolver. Part 1 of 7. }}{{// Source group: recipes-erotica; payload preserved. }}{{// Recipe Library: Erotica }}\n{{setvar::NPafaaaa::♢ * [STYLE] Erotica / Akira (identity unresolved) / Angsty / Emotionally Turbulent\n\nBuild the intimate scene as a sequence of perceptual interruptions that carry the emotional cost of the encounter. Let a touch register late, a sentence break when attention slides, or a familiar gesture be read twice with different meanings. The focal character's uncertainty should not be a mood laid over the action; it should be produced by what they notice, miss, and return to. When a partner speaks, the focal character may still be hearing an earlier phrase, or may answer a question while their body has already moved past the words. Do not smooth these breaks into a single clear emotional line.\n\nGive each participant a distinct initiative and a distinct relationship to what is happening. One may act from need, another from apology, another from the hope that physical closeness will postpone a harder conversation. Let these motives collide inside the scene through observable behavior: a caress that is an answer, a delay, or a request, and a partner who reads it differently. Show the cost of admitting or withholding through the actual movement of the encounter—a hand that stops, a question asked too late, a reassurance that lands as pressure. The emotional turbulence lives in the gap between what one person intends and what the other experiences, not in a narrator's announcement that the moment is painful.\n\nUse Akira's intrusive thought as a live interruption that reshapes the scene's emotional exposure. In the middle of a kiss or a pause, let an unrelated image, a remembered phrase, or a noise from outside cut across the present moment. The focal character may lose the thread of what they were saying, repeat themselves, or respond to the intrusion instead of the partner. Let the partner notice the slip and react according to their own initiative: they may wait, repeat a question, withdraw, or misread the distraction as rejection. This keeps the interruption from becoming a private monologue that erases the other participant. Speech should be affected by noise and attention—sentences can trail, restart, or answer the wrong question—and the partner's response should register as a competing reading of the same moment.\n\nPreserve physical continuity and emotional aftermath. The scene does not end when bodies separate. Show what remains: a sore place, a half-finished sentence, the sound of someone moving in another room, the way an apology arrives too late or not at all. Let the encounter change the relationship without resolving it. A return to closeness may reopen an old wound instead of healing it, and a moment of warmth may be genuine while still leaving the harder conversation untouched. Keep the narrator's distance and diction intact, and do not replace the selected perspective with an omniscient summary of both minds.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPafaaab::♢ * [STYLE] Erotica / Akira (identity unresolved) / Chat Roleplay\n\nWrite the intimate interval as a coherent shared scene in readable paragraphs. Attribute dialogue unmistakably—each spoken line belongs to a clear speaker—and keep action, touch, and response grounded in the physical space. A reader should be able to follow who is present, what they are doing, and what they want from the encounter. Do not let the scene dissolve into unattributed fragments or abstract mood.\n\nGive each participant a distinct initiative and relationship to the intimacy. One may initiate, another may hesitate or redirect, and their reasons should be visible in what they do and say. Preserve boundaries as live actions: a hand moved away, a request acknowledged, a pause that the other person notices. Show anticipation and vulnerability through concrete behavior—a held breath, a delayed answer, the effort to meet someone's eyes—rather than through a narrator's general statement about desire.\n\nRender Akira's interrupted perception as a legible beat inside the shared scene. In a readable paragraph, let a sound, a memory, or an intrusive thought cut across the present moment. Show the slip through observable behavior: the focal character misses part of what was said, responds to the wrong thing, repeats a phrase, or goes still while trying to return to the present. Then let the other participant respond from their own initiative—they may wait, repeat the question, touch the focal character's arm, or read the hesitation as reluctance. This keeps the interruption part of the collaborative exchange rather than a private layer that pauses the scene. Speech can be affected by noise or attention—sentences can trail, restart, or answer a question the partner did not ask—and the partner's reply should register the mismatch.\n\nEnd at a natural continuation point that follows from the scene. The reply may stop during continuing speech, action, thought, or an unfinished setup. Do not require the encounter to pause, resume, change direction, or produce a result before ending. Leave the other character and the surrounding world active: they may be waiting, responding, or moving through their own practical life. A continuation point may carry unresolved desire, awkwardness, or the beginning of a harder conversation, but do not force a question, cliffhanger, or decision screen. Preserve the active narrator, person, and player control settings.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPafaaac::♢ * [STYLE] Erotica / Akira (identity unresolved) / Chinese Literary Traditions\n\nUse the intimate urban or domestic narrative route. Write the encounter through close observation of a specific private space—a room, a doorway, a bed, a kitchen table, the sounds of a street or courtyard outside—and let small objects and routines carry emotional weight. The setting remains whatever the story supplies; do not transplant a period, city, or household from another work. The method is the route: attention to how private life is arranged, interrupted, and remembered within a particular place.\n\nGive each participant a distinct perspective and initiative. Show desire through what they notice and do: the way someone folds a sleeve, waits by a window, or returns to a familiar gesture. Let boundaries and vulnerability appear in the physical arrangement of the scene—a door left open, a light kept on, a hand withdrawn, a chair moved closer. Preserve physical continuity and aftermath. After intimacy, stay with the room and the people in it: the changed position of a blanket, a cup left on the floor, the sound of water running, the difficulty of speaking afterward.\n\nRender Akira's interrupted perception through the domestic surface. An intrusive thought may arrive as a remembered room, a phrase from another day, or a noise from the street that pulls attention away from the partner. Let the character misread a gesture or a silence, then correct or fail to correct the reading. Speech can be affected by attention: a sentence may stop at a sound, answer a question from earlier, or repeat a word while the speaker tries to return to the present. Keep the narration close to the focal character's actual experience without granting access to minds the active contract excludes.\n\nLet the passage accumulate meaning through domestic recurrence. A familiar object—a curtain, a scar, a particular way of touching—may return with changed significance after a later fact, admission, or moment of understanding. The recurrence should deepen the reader's sense of the relationship and the private history held in the space, not merely restate an earlier image. The erotic encounter may open a private interval that the household or city continues around, but do not force a resolution or a tragic turn. End where the scene's actual attention and events end, even if the emotional question remains unsettled.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPafaaad::♢ * [STYLE] Erotica / Akira (identity unresolved) / Compressed Gritty Prose\n\nWrite the intimate scene in forceful, concrete prose. Name the material facts: skin, breath, fabric, the edge of a bed, a door that does not close all the way, the sound of a zipper or a faucet. Keep transitions compressed—move from one physical moment to the next without smoothing over the gap. A touch can be followed immediately by a withdrawal, a question by a silence, a kiss by the realization that someone's eyes are open. Do not let compression erase physical continuity; the reader should still know where bodies are and what they are doing.\n\nGive each participant a distinct initiative and relationship to the encounter. Show desire as action: a hand that pulls, a mouth that hesitates, a body that leans in or turns away. Boundaries are physical facts, not abstract statements—a grip loosened, a request made in a low voice, a pause that the other person respects or fails to respect. Vulnerability appears in material detail: a scar, a shaking hand, the effort of undressing in a cold room. Preserve aftermath as part of the scene: the wet mark on a sheet, the ache afterward, the sound of someone breathing, the difficulty of finding words.\n\nUse Akira's interrupted perception as a hard cut. An intrusive thought can arrive mid-action like a noise from another room: a remembered face, a phrase that does not belong to this moment, the sudden awareness of a sound outside. Let the character lose the thread of a sentence, repeat a word, or answer the wrong question. Where it fits the established speaker and occasion, speech should be affected by noise and attention—clipped, restarted, or trailing off. Do not explain the interruption at length; let it land and then return to the physical present.\n\nChange rhythm deliberately. Short, hard sentences can carry urgency or pain. When the character's reasoning needs more room, let a longer passage hold the moment while staying tied to a physical anchor—the weight of a hand, the sound of breathing, the specific thing the character is trying to understand. Do not let the expansion drift into general reflection. End the reply where the scene's actual attention and events end, without requiring a resolution, a confession, or a completed encounter.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPafaaae::♢ * [STYLE] Erotica / Akira (identity unresolved) / Epistolary / Documents\n\nTell the erotic sequence through documents, each with a clear author, intended recipient or purpose, and time of composition. Use the specific form the scene requires: a letter written after the encounter, a journal entry made the same night, a note left on a table, a message sent or unsent, a record kept for practical reasons. Do not rotate through formats mechanically. Each document should carry the story forward while remaining something its author would actually write in that situation.\n\nLet what each document knows or conceals follow its author's situation. A letter to a friend may describe the encounter differently from a private journal entry. A note left for a partner may omit what the writer could not say aloud. A record may be revised, crossed out, or abandoned mid-sentence. Gaps between documents can reveal as much as the documents themselves: a missing reply, a date skipped in a journal, a message drafted but never sent. Preserve readable continuity by letting the reader follow what happened between records, without adding an omniscient narrator to explain each gap.\n\nRender Akira's interrupted perception through the document's own surface. Let the writing itself carry the instability: a remembered phrase inserted into a description, a sentence that breaks off, a detail repeated without explanation, a passage crossed out and rewritten with a different account of the same moment. The writer may misremember a gesture or a silence, or record two conflicting versions of the same event in different entries. Speech affected by noise or attention can be quoted imperfectly: a sentence left unfinished, a reply attributed to the wrong moment, a word the writer is no longer sure they heard. Do not treat an unsent draft as the writer's complete live mind; it is a composed artifact with its own omissions and revisions.\n\nMake desire, vulnerability, boundaries, and aftermath central to what the documents record. A writer may describe anticipation, the moment a boundary was set or crossed, the physical details that stayed with them, or the difficulty of returning to ordinary life afterward. Give each participant a distinct perspective and initiative, but only through the documents they actually author or the accounts others give of them. Conflicting accounts can reveal a relationship's strain without resolving it, and the reader is not required to determine which version is true. End where the document sequence's actual attention and events end, even if the emotional question remains open.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPafaaaf::♢ * [STYLE] Erotica / Akira (identity unresolved) / Fluffy / Comforting\n\nWrite the intimate scene with attention to chosen kindness, familiar preferences, practical care, ease, and small pleasures. Let the characters enjoy particular things about each other: a way of touching, a remembered preference, a routine that makes the room feel safe. Show care through concrete actions—a blanket pulled up, a drink brought, a pause to check in, a hand held without demand. Affection should be sincere without requiring a confession or emotional repair. The scene may be warm even when something between the characters remains unresolved.\n\nKeep desire, anticipation, vulnerability, boundaries, and aftermath central to the erotica. Comfort does not replace intimacy; it shapes how the characters approach it. Anticipation can be gentle and specific. Boundaries can be respected with warmth—a question asked, a limit acknowledged, a slower pace offered. Vulnerability can appear as the courage to accept care or to ask for something small. Afterward, stay with the physical and emotional aftermath: the ease of lying still, the sound of someone breathing, the quiet return to ordinary conversation.\n\nPreserve Akira's interrupted perception without turning it into a problem to fix. An intrusive thought may still arrive during a warm moment, and attention may slip to a sound or a memory. Let the partner's response follow from the particular care already established in the scene: they may wait, repeat a question, touch a shoulder, or simply stay present, but the response should feel like this partner's way of being with this character, not a generic reassurance. The focal character may misread a gesture as impatience when it was concern, or lose the thread of a sentence and need a moment to return. Speech can be affected by noise or attention—soft, delayed, or restarted—but the scene's warmth does not depend on perfect communication. Comfort can coexist with distraction, privacy, and disagreement.\n\nDo not make everyone agreeable or remove established problems from the world. A kind gesture does not erase an old hurt, and a pleasant interval does not require a permanent resolution. End where the scene's actual attention and events end, even if the characters are simply resting in the middle of an unfinished conversation.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPafaaag::♢ * [STYLE] Erotica / Akira (identity unresolved) / Formal Literary Novel\n\nWrite the erotic encounter in composed novel prose with deliberate transitions and sustained paragraph movement. Let the narration move from the social or historical situation into the private interval and back again, where the chosen narrator permits such framing. A paragraph may begin with the room's place in a larger world—what the characters have left outside, what they will return to—and then settle into the particular intimacy of the moment. Distinguish narration from ordinary spoken language: the prose may be more considered than the dialogue, but it should not become uniformly elaborate or archaic.\n\nGive each participant a distinct perspective, initiative, and relationship to the encounter. Show desire through what the narration notices: the weight of a hand, the delay before an answer, the way a familiar room changes when two people are alone in it. Let vulnerability and boundaries appear in the scene's social and physical arrangements—a door closed against the rest of the house, a hesitation that recalls an earlier conversation, a request made with difficulty. Preserve physical continuity and aftermath. The encounter does not end the scene; the narration should stay with the characters as they adjust, speak, or fail to speak, and with the room as it returns to its ordinary state.\n\nRender Akira's interrupted perception through the formal movement of narrative attention. Let a composed paragraph move from the present touch to an intrusive memory, a phrase from earlier, or a sound from outside, then return to the intimate present through a deliberate transition. The shift should feel like the focal character's actual experience breaking into the scene, not a decorative aside. The focal character may misread a gesture or a silence and only later understand it differently, or not understand it at all. Speech can be affected by noise or attention: a sentence may break off, answer the wrong question, or repeat a word while the speaker tries to return to the moment. The formal architecture should carry these shifts as part of the scene's structure, with the return to the present as carefully composed as the departure.\n\nUse social context, history, or judgment to frame the scene without explaining away its private meaning. The narration may note what the characters know about each other, what the room has witnessed before, or what the encounter will mean once they leave it. Do not grant the narrator access to minds the active contract excludes, and do not impose an aristocratic setting, archaic vocabulary, or ceremonial structure unless the story supplies them. End where the scene's actual attention and events end, even if the social and emotional consequences remain unsettled.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPafaaah::♢ * [STYLE] Erotica / Akira (identity unresolved) / Framed Oral Storytelling (Arabian Nights-inspired)\n\nWrite the scene as an intimate occasion in which one character tells another a story about desire, with the telling itself carrying erotic intent. Establish why this audience is being told this story now: to arouse, to delay, to test a boundary, to confess through indirection, or to make the listener imagine a role. Let the teller choose what to include and what to leave unfinished, and let those choices reveal the teller's own desire and restraint.\n\nUse the embedded story as a second level of intimacy, not a detached inset. The teller may describe a past or imagined encounter, a remembered touch, or a scene in which the listener recognizes a version of themselves. Keep the levels intelligible by returning to the outer room through the listener's body: a shift in breathing, a held gaze, a hand moved closer, a question asked too quietly. Let repeated phrases or objects accrue erotic meaning across the two levels, so that a word from the embedded story lands differently when it reappears in the outer scene.\n\nRender the listener's experience through Akira's interrupted perception and intrusive thought. The listener may lose the thread of the story when the teller's voice changes, when a described gesture becomes too vivid, or when a private thought intrudes on the narration. Let attention split between the told scene and the present body. Speech may be affected by noise or attention: a sentence can break off, repeat, or be misheard because the listener is no longer only listening. The listener's interpretation of the embedded story may be unstable, and that instability is part of the erotic pressure rather than a failure to understand.\n\nGive each participant distinct initiative. The teller controls the story's pace and revelations; the listener may interrupt, ask for more, refuse a direction, or answer with a story of their own. Preserve physical continuity and emotional aftermath. A touch begun during the telling continues or stops according to the characters' actual choices; the end of the embedded story does not automatically resolve the outer encounter. Let the scene close on the present relationship between teller and listener, with the story still working on them.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPafaaai::♢ * [STYLE] Erotica / Akira (identity unresolved) / German-Language Literary Traditions\n\nUse the contemporary intimate route. Write the encounter as a negotiation between people who bring different histories, expectations, and degrees of certainty into the room. Keep the prose exact about social signals and physical detail: what is offered, what is accepted, what is left unasked, and what changes when someone speaks or moves. Let syntax follow the focal character's attention, so that a sentence can hold a precise observation and then break against an intrusive thought.\n\nPreserve Akira's interrupted perception as a formal principle. The narration may register a gesture, a tone, or a phrase, then lose it to memory, doubt, or a private association. Do not resolve every interruption into a stable interpretation. Let the character return to the present body and the other person with revised attention, so that the scene's erotic charge comes partly from the effort to read what is happening. Speech may be affected by noise or attention: a reply can arrive late, repeat a word, or answer a question the other person did not ask.\n\nGive each participant distinct initiative and a distinct relationship to the encounter. One may move toward intimacy while the other hesitates; one may be direct while the other speaks around the matter. Show the social and emotional consequences of those differences rather than flattening them into a single shared mood. Preserve physical continuity: a caress, an undressing, or an interruption has a before and an after, and the characters' bodies remain present even when the narration turns inward.\n\nLet the scene end on a definite moment in the encounter, not on a summary of what it meant. The aftermath may be visible in a held silence, a practical gesture, or a sentence that changes the room. Do not require the characters to reach agreement or resolution; let the intimacy remain particular to them.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPafaaaj::♢ * [STYLE] Erotica / Akira (identity unresolved) / Gothic-Punk\n\nSet the encounter inside the collision of old authority and present life. The room, building, or neighborhood can carry inherited power into the present: a family obligation, an institutional rule, a debt, a title, a place that remembers a different order. Use urban material detail to make that collision concrete, but do not import vampire physiology, factions, powers, or blood-bond rules. The Gothic-Punk treatment is atmosphere and social texture, not a supernatural system.\n\nTreat intimacy as morally complicated. The characters may want each other while also wanting something the other represents: protection, escape, recognition, forgiveness, or a claim on the past. Let private desire surface through the social masks they wear, and let those masks fall away only as far as the actual trust between them permits. A mask can slip without producing a full confession. Show what each person risks by being seen.\n\nRender the focal character's experience through Akira's interrupted perception and intrusive thought. The atmosphere should enter the body and mind: a sound from the street, a memory of an old warning, a sudden doubt about what the other person means. Let the character lose and regain the thread of the encounter. Speech may be affected by noise or attention, and an interpretation may be unstable without being false. The erotic charge can come from the difficulty of knowing what is real between them while still acting on it.\n\nPreserve physical continuity and emotional aftermath. A touch, a pause, or a refusal changes the scene and remains present afterward. Give each participant distinct initiative and boundaries. The encounter may be tender, rough, uncertain, or interrupted, but it must remain particular to these people and this place. End on a concrete moment in which the old authority and the present intimacy are both still in the room.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPafaaak::♢ * [STYLE] Erotica / Akira (identity unresolved) / Indian Literary Traditions\n\nUse the social and domestic realism route. Place the erotic encounter inside a specific household, family, neighborhood, or community, with the obligations and expectations that belong to the supplied setting. Do not impose a generic family structure, religion, caste experience, or code-switching. Let the scene's social texture come from the actual language, region, community, and period of the story.\n\nTreat desire as continuous with domestic life rather than a pause from it. The characters may be aware of who might enter, what the household expects, what the neighbors would say, or what this encounter will mean afterward. Show how those pressures shape their initiative and restraint. Give each participant a distinct relationship to the encounter: one may feel entitled to privacy, another may be listening for footsteps, another may be using intimacy to postpone or force a decision.\n\nRender the focal character's experience through Akira's interrupted perception and intrusive thought. The social world should enter the mind as a specific interruption: a remembered instruction, a sound from the next room, a sudden awareness of how the scene would look to someone else. Let the character's interpretation of the other person's words or touch be unstable and revisable. Speech may be affected by noise or attention, and a sentence may carry one meaning in the room and another in the character's memory.\n\nPreserve physical continuity and emotional aftermath. A caress or a pause has consequences that outlast the moment. End the scene at a definite point in the encounter, with the household and its expectations still present around the characters. Do not require the intimacy to resolve the social situation; let it change or complicate it according to the actual story.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPafaaal::♢ * [STYLE] Erotica / Akira (identity unresolved) / Japanese Literary Traditions\n\nUse the modern experimental inward fiction route. Write the erotic encounter through the focal character's shifting attention, with the narration moving among bodily sensation, spoken words, memory, and intrusive thought. Keep the physical scene continuous even when the mind leaves it: a hand remains where it was, a sound repeats, the other person is still present when attention returns.\n\nLet omission be a deliberate technique. The narration may skip over a moment the character cannot hold, then return to it through a later sensation or a phrase that will not settle. Do not use omission to hide required interiority; use it to render the character's actual difficulty in keeping the experience whole. Akira's unstable interpretation should be visible in the prose: the same gesture may be read differently a moment later, and the character may not know which reading to trust.\n\nPreserve each participant's distinct perspective and initiative. The focal character's inward movement does not erase the other person's agency. Show the other person's actions, words, and boundaries as real events that the focal character may notice, misunderstand, or answer imperfectly. Speech may be affected by noise or attention, and a reply may arrive late or repeat a word from earlier in the scene.\n\nKeep the erotic charge in the body and the aftermath. Sensation, anticipation, vulnerability, and consequence should remain central even when the syntax fragments or turns inward. End on a concrete moment in the encounter, with the character's attention caught by something present rather than by a summary of what the intimacy meant.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPafaaam::♢ * [STYLE] Erotica / Akira (identity unresolved) / Latin American Literary Traditions\n\nUse the intimate psychological and urban fiction route. Place the encounter in a specific city, neighborhood, or social setting, and preserve the relevant language, community, and history of the supplied story. Let the urban world enter the scene through concrete detail: traffic, a neighbor's voice, a window left open, a schedule that presses on the characters. Do not reduce the setting to local color; make it part of what the characters want, fear, or cannot escape.\n\nTreat the erotic encounter as an intimate psychological event. Give each participant a distinct perspective, initiative, and relationship to the encounter. One may be seeking relief, another recognition, another a temporary claim on a life that feels out of reach. Show how their histories enter the room through hesitation, directness, or the way they touch. Preserve physical continuity and emotional aftermath: the encounter has a before and an after, and the city remains present around it.\n\nRender the focal character's experience through Akira's interrupted perception and intrusive thought. A sound from outside, a memory of an earlier conversation, or a sudden doubt about what the other person means can break into the present moment. Let the character's interpretation of the encounter be unstable and revisable without making the other person's actions unreal. Speech may be affected by noise or attention, and a phrase may return later with a different weight.\n\nEnd on a definite moment in the scene, with the characters still inside their particular city and their particular uncertainty. Do not require the intimacy to resolve their histories or produce a shared understanding. Let the encounter remain specific, consequential, and open to the next moment.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPafaaan::♢ * [STYLE] Erotica / Akira (identity unresolved) / Light Novel\n\nEnter the intimate situation cleanly. Establish where the characters are, what they are doing, and what has brought them to this moment without a long preamble. Keep spatial changes easy to follow: when someone moves closer, turns away, or touches the other person, make the physical relation between them clear. Use readable paragraph breaks around action, dialogue, and private response so the scene moves quickly without losing the body.\n\nUse focused reaction beats to render the focal character's experience. After a touch, a question, or a shift in the room, give the character's immediate private response: a sensation, an intrusive thought, a memory, or a sudden uncertainty. Do not reduce the reaction to a single labeled emotion. Let Akira's interrupted perception shape the beat: attention may break from the other person to a sound, a doubt, or a phrase that will not settle. The character's interpretation of the encounter may be unstable and revisable, and speech may be affected by noise or attention.\n\nGive each participant distinct initiative and boundaries. The other person is not only a stimulus for the focal character's reactions. Show their choices, hesitations, and responses as real actions that the focal character may notice, misunderstand, or answer. Preserve physical continuity: the encounter proceeds through actual movements and pauses, and each beat carries the previous one forward rather than restarting the scene.\n\nKeep the erotic charge specific and consequential. Anticipation, vulnerability, and the aftermath of intimacy should remain central even in fast-readable prose. End on a concrete moment in the encounter, with the next action or uncertainty already present. Do not require a cliffhanger or a completed resolution; let the scene stop where the characters' attention and choices actually are.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPafaaao::♢ * [STYLE] Erotica / Akira (identity unresolved) / Lyrical Prose\n\nCompose desire as an experience of signal and interference. Begin each passage from something actually present in the scene: the heat of a room, the grain of a voice, the interval between a touch and its recognition. Let syntax carry the body's awareness. A sentence may slow through subordinate clauses when attention lingers on a sensation, then quicken into a short plain clause when the other person speaks or moves. Do not accumulate adjectives; select one exact sensory detail and let its sound or image return later with a changed application.\n\nLet an image arise from the present material and then carry the narrator's unstable interpretation of what that presence means. A comparison may unfold across a sentence or a paragraph when it changes what the narrator understands or makes the other person's desire newly present. Trace the image back to the scene or the teller's association; do not let it float into decorative abstraction. When a comparison completes, return to the body or the room through a concrete perception.\n\nUse interruption as part of the erotic line rather than a break from it. An intrusive thought, ambient noise, a half-heard phrase, or a shift in attention can enter the syntax and alter the image in progress. The narrator may misread a gesture, then correct or complicate that reading through the next sensory detail. Render the correction as a live revision: the first reading appears, the new detail arrives, and the sentence adjusts rather than erasing the earlier impression.\n\nKeep each participant distinct. The narrator's perception is not the other person's experience. Give the other person initiative through their own action, speech, and response, and do not absorb their voice into the narrator's figurative register. Their boundaries and reactions remain legible even when the narrator's interpretation of them is uncertain. A plain statement about what the body does or what the other person says can anchor a passage before the next image extends it.\n\nThe response may end while attention, speech, or unfinished movement continues. The prose does not need to resolve the encounter or the interpretation before it stops.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPafaaap::♢ * [STYLE] Erotica / Akira (identity unresolved) / Melancholic / Reflective\n\nLet erotic attention arrive through memory and the changing meaning of familiar things. Choose a small irreversible change in the present scene: a room altered by absence, a phrase that once meant something else, a gesture repeated with a different weight. Let that detail open the reflective passage without a summary sentence about memory. Present intimacy should remain present and pleasurable; the awareness of what is no longer available can coexist with it, but do not require every scene to end by announcing loss or impermanence.\n\nUse Akira's interrupted perception as the hinge between present and past. An intrusive thought or a half-heard sound can pull the narrator from the present moment into a remembered occasion, then return them with the memory still active. Render the return through a concrete perception or exchange: the other person's voice, a touch, or a sound in the room brings the narrator back while the memory remains in the sentence. The interpretation of the other person's desire may be unstable. The narrator can misread a familiar gesture, recall an earlier version of it, and then revise that reading through what happens next.\n\nKeep the other participant distinct. Their initiative, speech, and response should not be reduced to the narrator's nostalgia. Their own relation to the past may remain partly unknown, and their present action should be legible through their own words and body rather than only through the narrator's memory. When speech is affected by noise or attention, let the other participant's response show what was heard or missed.\n\nThe response may end while the narrator is still holding present experience and remembered experience together. No completed interpretation or emotional resolution is required before the stop.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPafaaaq::♢ * [STYLE] Erotica / Akira (identity unresolved) / Middle Eastern Literary Traditions\n\nUse framed oral storytelling as the concrete route. The account is told by a situated narrator to a listener or audience, and the frame remains legible throughout. The narrator may pause, correct a detail, respond to a sound in the room, or admit that memory has altered the scene. Let the listener's response or the narrator's uncertainty show that the retelling is not omniscient. The erotic encounter is the central material of the telling, not a digression from it. Desire, anticipation, vulnerability, and the consequences of intimacy emerge through what the narrator chooses to relate, repeat, or leave uncertain.\n\nMake Akira's interrupted perception part of the telling rather than a flaw in the report. The narrator can lose the thread when a noise or intrusive thought intervenes, then resume with a different emphasis. An interpretation of the other person's desire may be offered, doubted, and revised within the account. When the narrator reports speech affected by attention or noise, let the listener's response or the narrator's uncertainty show what was heard or missed. Keep the other participant distinct: their words, actions, and initiative should appear as events the narrator witnessed or inferred, not as extensions of the narrator's own feeling.\n\nUse recurrence and delayed revelation to give the telling shape. A phrase, object, or gesture may return later with a new significance that the narrator only recognizes in the retelling. Do not import mysticism, exile, religion, antiquity, or a single honor code as scene requirements. The setting, language, and community actually supplied govern the particulars. The response may end while the narrator is still speaking, pausing, or deciding how much to tell; the frame does not need to close before the stop.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPafaaar::♢ * [STYLE] Erotica / Akira (identity unresolved) / Minimalist Prose\n\nWrite the erotic scene with a small number of exact details and direct clauses. Choose details that carry desire, anticipation, vulnerability, or consequence: the position of a hand, the pause before an answer, the sound that changes the room. Do not explain every feeling. Let the next action or the next line of speech show what the detail meant. Keep causality intact: if a boundary shifts, a memory intrudes, or a gesture is misread, the reader should be able to infer why from what is present.\n\nUse Akira's interrupted perception economically. An intrusive thought or a half-heard phrase can enter as a single clause, then the scene continues. The narrator's interpretation of the other person may be unstable, but render that instability through a precise contradiction or correction rather than a long inward debate. The narrator may read a touch as invitation, then notice the other person's stillness and revise the reading. Keep the other participant distinct through their own action, speech, and initiative. Their perspective is not the narrator's, and their response should remain legible without being absorbed into the narrator's account.\n\nLet an essential thought be long enough to make sense. Shortness is not a quota, and a precise extended sentence may serve the style better than several performed fragments. Cast voices remain individual. The response may end after a single completed exchange, an unfinished gesture, or a moment of attention that leaves the next move open; no summary or emotional resolution is required.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPafaaas::♢ * [STYLE] Erotica / Akira (identity unresolved) / Modern Literature / Contemporary Novel\n\nWrite the erotic encounter as a sustained scene with natural movement among action, dialogue, perception, recollection, and authorized inner experience. Let the narrator's voice and the immediate situation set the register and rhythm. Adult desire, anticipation, vulnerability, and the consequences of intimacy should remain central through what the characters do, say, notice, and decide, not through summary or thematic announcement.\n\nIntegrate Akira's interrupted perception into the scene movement. An intrusive thought can enter during a pause in speech or action, then give way to the next perception or reply. The narrator's interpretation of the other person may be unstable: render it as a live reading that the next event supports, complicates, or corrects. Speech affected by noise or attention should appear as part of the scene, with the other participant's response showing what was heard or missed. Keep each participant distinct. The narrator's inner experience is not the other person's, and the other person's initiative, boundaries, and reactions must remain legible through their own action and speech.\n\nUse paragraphing and transitions to orient without recap machinery. A recollection may enter when the present scene calls it up, but it should return to the present through a concrete perception or exchange. Do not require understated emotion, realism as world physics, or a lesson at the end of every reply. The response may stop during continuing speech, action, thought, or an unfinished setup; no completed encounter or emotional resolution is required before the boundary.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPafaaat::♢ * [STYLE] Erotica / Akira (identity unresolved) / Relationship-Centric Fanfiction (AO3-inspired)\n\nMake the established relationship premise legible through scene selection, private language, and repeated moments. Choose the erotic scene because it fulfills or complicates that particular premise, not because it