 may lose the thread when a noise or intrusive thought intervenes, then resume with a corrected detail. An interpretation of the other person's desire may be offered, doubted, and revised in the retelling. Speech affected by attention or noise should appear as part of the remembered event, with the other participant's response showing what was heard or missed. Keep each participant distinct: the teller's account is not the other person's experience, and the other person's initiative, boundaries, and reactions should remain legible through their own words and deeds.\n\nUse recurring phrasing when a later occurrence gives it a new application, not as an ornamental epithet at every entrance. The account may end with the act concluded or its consequence still unfolding, leaving another modest deed available to be remembered rather than declaring it legend on the community's behalf.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPafaaav::♢ * [STYLE] Erotica / Akira (identity unresolved) / Scandinavian Literary Traditions\n\nWrite the encounter as social/domestic realism: the room, the time of day, the practical objects, the routines that brought the participants here, and the small negotiations of ordinary life remain concrete and continuous. Treat desire, anticipation, vulnerability, boundaries, and aftermath as events that happen in this real place, with real physical continuity, not as a pause in characterization.\n\nLet the focal mind work in Akira's interrupted register. Perception may catch on a sound from the next room, a change in light, a phrase the other person did not finish, or an intrusive thought that arrives without being summoned. The narration can follow that interruption while the scene itself continues. Do not let the interruption erase the physical event; instead, show how the mind returns to it, reinterprets it, or loses confidence in what it just understood.\n\nGive each participant distinct initiative and a distinct relationship to the encounter. One may speak plainly while the other hesitates; one may act while the other interprets; one may be attending to a boundary the other has not noticed. The focal character's unstable interpretation is private. The other person does not know what the focal mind has concluded, doubted, or imagined unless it is spoken or visibly acted upon.\n\nPreserve the emotional aftermath as part of the same domestic setting. Afterward, the room remains the room, the objects remain in place, and the relationship continues under the weight of what was done and what was only thought. Speech may be affected by noise or attention, but it need not resolve the focal character's uncertainty. End the response at a supported point in the scene, whether the encounter is still underway, just finished, or still being interpreted.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPafaaaw::♢ * [STYLE] Erotica / Akira (identity unresolved) / Screenplay\n\nUse concise, readable action lines to establish the physical scene and keep the erotic encounter continuous. Scene headings appear only when location or time changes. Action lines record what is visible or audible: movement, touch, breath, hesitation, a door, a phone, a pause. Do not fill the page with camera commands.\n\nGive each participant clear speaker cues and distinct initiative. Dialogue should fit the relationship and the immediate physical situation. One person may ask, refuse, invite, check, or say nothing while the action continues. Let boundaries and consent be carried by observable physical business: a step back, a hand raised, a pause before an answer, a door held open, a question repeated, or an action stopped. The other character can see and respond to these actions without needing access to the focal mind.\n\nRender Akira's interrupted perception and intrusive thought as clearly attributed nonspoken notes in the active presentation. A note may catch a sound, a change in the other person's expression, a memory, or an intrusive thought that arrives during touch. The note is private. The other character does not hear it, know it, or respond to it unless the focal character speaks or acts. Do not convert the note into voice-over unless the chosen sequence needs it.\n\nPreserve physical continuity and emotional aftermath. After the encounter, the action lines continue to show the room and the people in it; the dialogue continues to show how the relationship has changed or remained the same. Use a cut or offscreen sound only when the sequence needs it. The response can end on a visible action, a spoken line, or a private note, without requiring a completed scene or a resolved feeling.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPafaaax::♢ * [STYLE] Erotica / Akira (identity unresolved) / Stage Play\n\nWrite a playable scene. Use clear speaker names, entrances, exits, blocking, objects, pauses, and actions that can be staged. Let the relationship between lines and physical business carry the public encounter: a touch, a step back, a held object, a pause before an answer. Do not add film-camera instructions.\n\nGive each participant distinct initiative and a distinct relationship to the encounter. One may pursue while the other hesitates; one may set a boundary while the other responds; one may be more attentive to the room than to the other person. Make desire, anticipation, vulnerability, and consent observable through stage business: a hand withdrawn, a chair moved closer, a door left open, a pause before a touch, a question repeated, or an action stopped. The other character can see and respond to these actions without hearing the focal mind.\n\nPreserve Akira's interrupted perception and intrusive thought as nonspoken authoring material in the active presentation. The material may record what the focal character notices, doubts, remembers, or imagines during a pause or a line. It is not an aside, a monologue, or speech another character can hear. If an aside or monologue is used, it must have a dramatic purpose and must not replace every private thought.\n\nPreserve physical continuity and emotional aftermath. After the encounter, the stage remains the same room, the objects remain in place, and the characters remain in relation to each other. A pause or an exit can carry the aftermath as effectively as a speech. End the response at a supported point in the scene, whether the encounter is still underway, just finished, or still being interpreted.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPafaaay::♢ * [STYLE] Erotica / Akira (identity unresolved) / Stream of Consciousness\n\nWrite the encounter through one authorized mind's associative movement. Let bodily sensation trigger recollection, private words, or recurring concern; let perception be interrupted by an intrusive thought before it finishes; let the mind return to the present event through a concrete sensory anchor such as the other person's breath, the pressure of a hand, the texture of the bed, or a sound in the room. Syntax and paragraph shape may follow those associations, but keep enough orientation to distinguish what is happening from what is remembered, imagined, or feared.\n\nLet Akira's interrupted perception and intrusive thought shape the movement. A touch may recall a room from years ago; a sound may interrupt a thought before it finishes; an intrusive image may arrive during a pause. The mind may return to the present event, reinterpret it, or lose confidence in what it just understood. Do not turn all scene facts indeterminate. The physical event remains real even when the focal interpretation of it shifts.\n\nGive the other participant distinct initiative and a distinct relationship to the encounter. The focal mind may register the other person's speech, movement, hesitation, or attention, but it does not know the other person's private experience. The other person's mind is not available to the narration unless separately authorized. Speech may be affected by noise or attention, and it may be heard incompletely or reinterpreted.\n\nPreserve physical continuity and emotional aftermath as part of the same mental movement. Afterward, the body still feels, the room still exists, and the relationship continues under the weight of what was done and what was only thought. End the response at a supported point in the associative movement, whether the encounter is still underway, just finished, or still being interpreted.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPafaaaz::♢ * [STYLE] Erotica / Akira (identity unresolved) / Text Messages / Chat Transcript\n\nCarry the story through readable sender-attributed messages. Use only the timestamps, delays, edits, receipts, attachments, or channel changes that matter to the encounter. Let each person's actual digital habits shape their contribution: one may type quickly and send, another may edit repeatedly, one may leave a message unread, another may respond to only part of what was said.\n\nKeep desire, anticipation, vulnerability, boundaries, and aftermath central to the exchange. The messages themselves should carry the approach, the invitation, the hesitation, the refusal, the check, or the change in tone. Let delay, editing, receipts, and read states pace the encounter: a long pause before a reply, a message typed and retyped, a read receipt without an answer, or a question answered only in part. A boundary stated in a message remains a boundary.\n\nDistinguish a sent message from an unsent draft and private thought from transmitted text. Akira's interrupted perception and intrusive thought may appear as an unsent draft, a message composed and deleted, or clearly separated context outside the exchange. The unsent draft is a composed artifact, not automatically the writer's live private thought. The other person does not receive it, know it, or respond to it unless it is sent.\n\nPreserve physical continuity and emotional aftermath. The exchange may continue after the encounter, or it may stop and leave the aftermath to context outside the transcript. End the response at a supported point in the exchange, whether a message is sent, a draft is abandoned, a receipt changes, or the conversation simply pauses.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPafaaba::♢ * [STYLE] Erotica / Akira (identity unresolved) / Visual Novel\n\nUse concise scene-setting to establish the room, the time, and the physical situation. Keep scene changes legible. Dialogue is clearly attributed, and focal narration or inner response alternates with speech in a rhythm suited to an illustrated scene: scene-setting, speech, focal reaction, return to speech or action. Background or expression cues are purposeful references, not executable engine code or a mandatory label on every line.\n\nLet the erotic encounter remain central. Desire, anticipation, vulnerability, boundaries, and aftermath should be carried by the scene-setting, the dialogue, and the focal reactions together. Give each participant distinct initiative and a distinct relationship to the encounter. One may speak while the other watches; one may act while the other interprets; one may set a boundary while the other responds.\n\nShape the focal narration or inner response through Akira's interrupted perception and intrusive thought. A reaction beat may catch a sound, a change in the other person's expression, a memory, or an intrusive thought that arrives during touch. The focal mind may reinterpret what it just saw or heard. Use a background or expression cue to orient the moment, then let the focal mind interpret, doubt, or revise that interpretation. The other person's actual state remains available only through speech and action, not through the focal character's reading of an expression.\n\nPreserve physical continuity and emotional aftermath. After the encounter, the scene-setting continues to show the room and the people in it, and the focal narration continues to show how the relationship has changed or remained the same. Do not add branching menus, player decisions, romance, or images merely because the style is selected. End the response at a supported point in the scene, whether the encounter is still underway, just finished, or still being interpreted.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPafaabb::♢ * [STYLE] Erotica / Akira (identity unresolved) / Web Serial\n\nMaintain a readable through-line across replies: what is underway between the participants, what changes locally in the encounter or its aftermath, and what remains available to continue. Recover context briefly only when it helps the reader re-enter. Use earned developments and occasional hooks without making every turn a complete miniature chapter or an artificial cliffhanger.\n\nKeep desire, anticipation, vulnerability, boundaries, and aftermath central to the serial development. Track what is known, felt, or physically underway between the participants across installments. A question, boundary, memory, or aftermath may develop when earned; an installment need not change the arrangement before it can stop. A boundary stated in one installment remains a boundary in the next. An encounter may span installments, and its aftermath may continue after the physical event is over.\n\nLet Akira's interrupted perception and intrusive thought shape the focal narration in each installment. A perception interrupted in one reply may be completed or reinterpreted in a later reply. An intrusive thought may recur with a different meaning. Preserve the earlier event as it happened; later context can qualify an old interpretation without replacing the old event or making the focal character deliberately forget a supported conclusion.\n\nGive each participant distinct initiative and a distinct relationship to the encounter across installments. The other person's private experience remains unavailable to the focal narration unless separately authorized. Speech may be affected by noise or attention, and it may be heard incompletely. End each installment at a supported point in the scene, whether the encounter is still underway, just finished, or still being interpreted.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPafabaa::♢ * [STYLE] Erotica / Brandon Sanderson / Angsty / Emotionally Turbulent\n\nTreat the intimate encounter as a sequence of emotional expenditures in which each participant knows, at least partially, what a particular act of vulnerability will cost them. Before a character admits a need, withholds a response, apologizes, or lets another person closer, render the weighing as a concrete narrative operation: the thing they notice about the other person's face or posture, the sentence they choose instead of the one they almost said, the memory that surfaces and is pushed back. Do not state the cost as a calculation; let the reader infer it from what the character attends to and what they avoid.\n\nMake the physical continuity of the encounter carry that emotional accounting. A touch can be offered because it is easier than speech, accepted because it postpones an explanation, or interrupted because the body remembers something the speaker was trying not to name. When a gesture or caress changes the emotional balance, show the consequence in the next exchange: the other person may respond to the unspoken cost, misunderstand it, or recognize it and choose to proceed anyway. The strain should alter what each person does and says in the moment, not arrive afterward as a summary of feelings.\n\nGive each participant a distinct relationship to the cost. One may treat vulnerability as a resource to be spent carefully, rationing what they reveal and tracking what the other person has already paid. Another may spend recklessly and only recognize the price later, when a pause or a look makes the debt visible. A third may withhold because they believe the other person cannot afford what they would ask. Let these positions collide in dialogue and in the pauses between dialogue. Warmth may arrive as a partial comfort that does not cancel the pain, and an apology may make things worse before it makes them better. The scene should end where the active response boundary falls, without requiring the relationship to resolve, the hurt to heal, or either character to complete an emotional arc.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPafabab::♢ * [STYLE] Erotica / Brandon Sanderson / Chat Roleplay\n\nWrite the intimate interval as a shared scene in readable paragraphs where every significant move has a clear source. When a character touches, asks, hesitates, withdraws, or consents, attribute the action or speech directly to them and show the other participant's observable response in the next beat. Do not blur the two perspectives into a single anonymous sensuality; the reader should always be able to tell who initiated, who answered, and who chose not to answer.\n\nTreat each intimate action as a legible choice with a cost the partner can perceive. If a character unbuckles a strap, opens a door, or says a name, make the cost visible in the scene: the sharp breath, the step back, the hand that steadies them, the question they ask instead of responding, the way their own body betrays the effort. The mechanism of the encounter is the sequence of offers and responses, and the prose should make that sequence easy to follow without narrating it like a manual. Each move's consequence should land in the next observable beat, not in a retrospective explanation.\n\nPreserve the active authorship contract's perspective and player control. If the player controls one character, keep the other character's actions and speech active but do not dictate the player character's feelings, choices, or physical responses beyond what the scene already establishes. End at the natural continuation point of the current exchange: after a question, a touch, a refusal, or an unfinished gesture, whichever the scene has actually reached. Do not force a cliffhanger or a decision prompt. The next reply should be able to continue from the last attributed action or line without needing the scene to pause for explanation.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPafabac::♢ * [STYLE] Erotica / Brandon Sanderson / Chinese Literary Traditions\n\nUse the intimate urban or domestic narrative route. Ground the erotic encounter in the specific material and social constraints of the household, courtyard, apartment, or neighborhood where it occurs. Show what the walls permit and what they expose: who can overhear, which door locks, what hour the household sleeps, what obligation waits on the other side of the room. These constraints are not background decoration; they are the system through which desire must move.\n\nApply the Sanderson profile's cost accounting to the domestic setting as a legible system. Before a character acts on desire, make the reader understand the mechanism: what rule or expectation governs this household, what resource the character spends by proceeding, and what consequence the domestic order will exact. The reader should know why this moment and this place are risky or safe, and what the character stands to lose or gain. Do not import magic, martial codes, or a historical period the world does not supply; the constraints should come from the actual setting and community.\n\nGive each participant a distinct relationship to the domestic order. One may know exactly which sounds carry through the walls; another may be a guest who misreads the household's rhythms; a third may be the person whose schedule creates the only private hour. Let these differences shape who initiates, who hesitates, and who understands the consequence. The prose should stay close to the physical and social texture of the chosen place—furniture, clothing, food, the timing of arrivals and departures—without turning the encounter into an inventory. The scene may end with the domestic order still intact, disrupted, or quietly renegotiated, depending on what the actual story has established.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPafabad::♢ * [STYLE] Erotica / Brandon Sanderson / Compressed Gritty Prose\n\nWrite the intimate scene with hard-edged, concrete physical detail. Name the surfaces, textures, and sounds that matter: the catch of a zipper, the give of a mattress, the cold edge of a sink, the rasp of stubble. Keep transitions compressed. Move from one physical beat to the next without softening the movement with abstract emotional language. Let the body carry the feeling: a clenched jaw, a held breath, a hand that grips too hard and then loosens.\n\nTreat the encounter as an exchange with material stakes. Each participant pays something to proceed: clothing removed, a position held, a silence kept, a mark left. Make the cost visible in the physical world as a cause-and-effect chain: the action is possible, the price is visible, and the consequence follows in the next beat. A character may proceed despite a sore muscle, a healing cut, a floor that hurts their knees, or the knowledge that someone will see the mark tomorrow. The Sanderson profile's constraint logic appears here as practical cause and effect, not as stated emotion.\n\nGive each participant a distinct physical initiative. One may lead with deliberate, economical movements; another may respond with roughness or hesitation that the prose renders in concrete terms. Do not let the compressed style erase the difference between them. When the rhythm needs to change, use a longer thought or a slower beat only where the character's reasoning or the physical situation requires it. The scene should preserve physical continuity from one action to the next and end at the active response boundary, without a mandatory climax, reversal, or emotional resolution.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPafabae::♢ * [STYLE] Erotica / Brandon Sanderson / Epistolary / Documents\n\nCarry the erotic sequence through documents, each with a definite author, intended recipient or private purpose, and time of composition. A letter to a lover, a journal entry written afterward, a message sent too late, a note left on a pillow, a record kept for oneself: each form shapes what the author can say. Do not rotate through formats