setvar::NPafadac::♢ * [STYLE] Erotica / Cormac McCarthy / Chinese Literary Traditions\n\nUse the intimate urban or domestic narrative route. Set the erotic interval within the physical particulars of a shared room, courtyard, stairwell, street, or workplace, and let those particulars carry the emotional and erotic weight. The prose should notice the same concrete details McCarthy's profile favors: the worn edge of a table, the sound of water in a basin, the way a curtain moves, the heat of a wall in the afternoon, the weight of a door. Do not turn the setting into a decorative backdrop. The characters' attention to these objects is part of how they approach or avoid each other.\n\nKeep each participant's perspective and initiative distinct. One may perform a small domestic labor while the other watches; one may speak a practical sentence about the room, the meal, the errand, or the hour while meaning something else. Where it fits the established speaker and occasion, let the dialogue remain spare and practical, carrying bargains, convictions, and withheld motives. Do not force the characters to announce their feelings. The intimacy emerges through the physical continuity of the scene: a hand that lingers on a cup, a body that shifts to make room, a silence that holds.\n\nPreserve the emotional aftermath. After the encounter, let the characters return to the ordinary tasks of the space, and let the reader see how the intimacy has changed their attention to each other and to the room. The route is intimate and domestic or urban, not a broad social panorama. Keep the language, period, and community distinct to the supplied setting, and do not import a generic cast or landscape. End at a natural boundary, with the consequence of the encounter still present in the physical scene.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPafadad::♢ * [STYLE] Erotica / Cormac McCarthy / Compressed Gritty Prose\n\nWrite the erotic scene in hard-edged, concrete prose. Use forceful verbs and specific nouns: a body does not simply move; it turns, presses, braces, or pulls back. A surface does not simply exist; it is rough, cold, worn, or slick. Keep transitions compressed. Move from one physical fact to the next without smoothing over the gaps. Let the reader feel the material wear of the scene: the edge of a table against a hip, the rasp of fabric, the heat of skin, the sound of breath in a close room.\n\nPreserve physical stakes without turning the genre into survival horror. The stakes are the vulnerability of the encounter: a boundary that holds or gives way, a touch that is accepted or refused, a body that tenses or relaxes. Let each participant have a distinct perspective and initiative. One may act while the other waits; one may speak a rough or practical sentence while the other answers with silence or a single word. Ground abrasive or rough speech in its speaker. Do not make every line of dialogue hard for its own sake. A character's roughness should come from who they are and what they are trying to do in the scene.\n\nUse useful changes of rhythm. Short declarative sentences can strike; a longer sentence can follow when the character's attention or reasoning needs to linger. Do not let the compression erase the emotional aftermath. After the encounter, let the reader see the physical and emotional consequence in the characters' bodies and behavior. End at a natural boundary, with the scene's physical facts still carrying its meaning.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPafadae::♢ * [STYLE] Erotica / Cormac McCarthy / Epistolary / Documents\n\nTell the erotic sequence through documents with a clear author, intended recipient or purpose, and time of composition. A letter may be written after the encounter and addressed to the other person; a journal entry may be written the same night and meant for no one; a report or record may be written later and meant for an official or practical purpose. Let the document's form shape what it says. A letter can address the other person directly and still withhold what the writer cannot bring themselves to write. A journal can record the physical details of the encounter with the writer's own attention and limits. A report can reduce the encounter to facts while its omissions reveal what the writer cannot or will not record.\n\nUse McCarthy's concrete nouns, forceful verbs, and paratactic movement within each document. The writer of a letter may describe the room, the weather, the state of their own hands, the exact moment a touch was given or refused. The prose should stay close to physical particulars rather than abstract emotional summary. Practical sentences can carry bargains, convictions, and withheld motives. A document may say something about the journey, the work, the hour, or the next meeting while the real subject remains between the lines.\n\nPreserve each document's limits. What a document knows or conceals follows its author's situation at the time of composition. Do not add an omniscient explanation after every record. Let gaps, revisions, conflicting accounts, and material traces reveal events across entries. A later document may correct or complicate an earlier one, but it cannot retroactively change what the earlier author knew. Keep the sequence readable by letting the documents themselves carry the story forward. End at a natural boundary, with the next document or the next silence left open.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPafadaf::♢ * [STYLE] Erotica / Cormac McCarthy / Fluffy / Comforting\n\nWrite the erotic scene around chosen kindness, familiar preferences, practical care, ease, and small pleasures. Let the characters attend to each other through concrete actions: a cup of tea set within reach, a blanket folded back, a hand placed where the other person likes it, a door closed against the cold, a pause that gives the other person room. Use McCarthy's concrete nouns and forceful verbs rather than abstract emotional summary. The comfort should be visible in what the characters do with their hands and bodies, not only in what they say.\n\nKeep each participant's personality, privacy, and disagreement intact. Comfort does not make everyone agreeable or remove established problems from the world. One character may be quiet while the other talks; one may accept care awkwardly; one may offer a small pleasure that the other receives without a confession or emotional repair. Let affection be sincere without requiring a declaration. The intimacy may be warm and still leave some things unsaid. Do not let the scene become a therapeutic resolution.\n\nPreserve the erotic genre's emphasis on desire, anticipation, vulnerability, boundaries, and aftermath. The small pleasures should carry erotic weight: the warmth of a body, the sound of breath, the give of a mattress, the smell of soap on skin. Let the physical continuity of the scene hold the reader's attention. Use paratactic movement and spare exchanges, with practical speech carrying bargains, convictions, and withheld motives. End at a natural boundary, with the warmth of the scene still present but the next move open.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPafadag::♢ * [STYLE] Erotica / Cormac McCarthy / Formal Literary Novel\n\nWrite the erotic scene as composed novel prose. Use deliberate transitions and sustained paragraph movement. Let a paragraph build from the physical particulars of the room or body to the social context, history, or judgment that frames the encounter, where the chosen narrator permits it. Distinguish the narration from ordinary spoken language. The narrator may observe that the room had known other such evenings, or that the two people had arrived at this moment through a history neither of them named. But keep the framing grounded in the concrete scene: the worn floor, the light at the window, the weight of a coat on a chair.\n\nUse McCarthy's concrete nouns, forceful verbs, and paratactic movement within the formal architecture. The prose may move in short declarative sentences and then extend into a longer accumulation when the narrator's attention or the character's reasoning requires it. Practical speech can carry bargains, convictions, and withheld motives. Where it fits the established speaker and occasion, let the dialogue remain spare even when the narration is composed. A character may say something about the hour, the weather, or the next obligation while the narration reveals what the sentence costs.\n\nPreserve each participant's distinct perspective, initiative, and relationship to the encounter. The narrator may frame the scene through social context or judgment, but the characters' private experience remains their own. Do not let the narrative distance erase the physical continuity and emotional aftermath. After the encounter, let the prose linger on the changed state of the room, the body, or the relationship. Ceremony, correspondence, or public/private contrast may organize a passage when relevant, but do not impose an aristocratic setting or uniformly elaborate speech. End at a natural boundary, with the scene's consequence still present in the composed prose.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPafadah::♢ * [STYLE] Erotica / Cormac McCarthy / Framed Oral Storytelling (Arabian Nights-inspired)\n\nWrite the scene around a teller recounting a past intimacy to a listener who has a present stake in the account. The telling is a negotiation: the teller wants something from the listener—a boundary acknowledged, a risk accepted, a question stopped, or an act made possible—and the embedded story is her instrument for getting it. The listener does not merely receive the story. He interrupts, asks about a detail, challenges an omission, or responds with his own body to what he hears, and each interruption forces the teller to decide what more to reveal.\n\nKeep the outer scene physically concrete. Name where they are, what they are doing with their hands, what the room or weather is doing, and let those physical facts carry the present pressure between them. Use McCarthy's paratactic movement for both levels. Prefer concrete nouns and forceful verbs: name the body's parts, surfaces, and actions plainly rather than through euphemism or abstraction. Let clauses accumulate with and, then, and but to carry the physical sequence of an encounter without smoothing it into a single flowing sentence. Spare exchange can carry bargains, boundaries, and withheld motives. A character may say less than she knows; the narration may show what the words cost her.\n\nGive the embedded story its own participants, initiative, and relationship. The teller describes what she wanted, what she feared, what she touched, what she refused, and what she understood only afterward. Do not make the past encounter a generic memory. The listener's questions should cut against the teller's selection: he may notice a gap in the sequence, a person whose motive is missing, or a moment where the teller's present account does not match what she says she felt then. The teller's answers may close the gap, widen it, or reveal that she herself has not settled what the past meant.\n\nLet a repeated object, phrase, or gesture accrue meaning across the levels. A word used in the past account can return in the present scene with altered weight. A physical detail—a scar, a doorway, a particular silence—can link the teller's memory to the listener's present attention without being explained immediately. Keep the narrative levels intelligible: the reader should always know whether the prose is in the outer scene or the embedded account.\n\nDistinguish what the teller knew then from what she knows now. She may describe an earlier self who misunderstood her own desire or another person's refusal. The present telling can reveal that difference through emphasis and omission, not through a clinical explanation. Preserve physical continuity and aftermath in both levels: after the past encounter, show its residue in the teller's body or choices; after the present telling, show what remains between the two people. End where the scene's actual attention ends, not with a required cliffhanger or completed act.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPafadai::♢ * [STYLE] Erotica / Cormac McCarthy / German-Language Literary Traditions\n\nUse the documentary and historical-memory route. Write the erotic relationship as a record the narrator is assembling from what she lived, what she remembers, what she wrote down at the time, and what she can no longer verify. The prose should move between present recollection and the concrete conditions of the past: the room, the work, the weather, the objects that outlasted the intimacy. Let McCarthy's concrete nouns and forceful verbs carry the physical facts of desire—the body's surfaces, the actions, the refusals—without ornament or abstraction.\n\nTreat the past encounter as an archive the narrator returns to. When memory fails or becomes uncertain, she may consult a surviving object, a recorded phrase, a scar, a stain, a letter, or the testimony of someone else who was there. Each piece of evidence can confirm, correct, or complicate what she remembers. Do not let the present voice overwrite the past self's uncertainty or misreading. The gap between record and memory is part of the erotic material, not a detour from it.\n\nGive each participant a distinct perspective and initiative. The narrator may reconstruct the other person's actions from observation, speech, and later evidence, but she cannot enter his mind. Show what he did, what he said, what he refused, and what his body revealed. Spare exchange can carry bargains and boundaries: a short sentence of consent, a practical instruction, a warning, or a silence that the narrator returns to. Let the other person's agency remain visible even when the narrator's memory is partial.\n\nUse paratactic accumulation for the aftermath. After an encounter, show its residue in the body, the room, the work that follows, the objects that remain. A piece of clothing, a stain