            "content": "{{#if {{.NCGenreId == erotica}}}}{{// @category Variable-Init }}{{// @icon 🔧 }}{{// @badge INIT }}{{// @tooltip Erotica. Selected-partition data; keep enabled in supplied order after recipe selectors and before the Writing Recipe resolver. Part 2 of 7. }}{{// Source group: recipes-erotica; payload preserved. }}{{setvar::NPafaeae::♢ * [STYLE] Erotica / Douglas Adams / Epistolary / Documents\n\nTell the intimate relationship through documents. Each entry must have a clear author, intended recipient or purpose, and time of composition. Use the document form the scene actually requires: a letter, a journal entry, a note left in a room, a message, a list, a record, or another authored text. Do not rotate through formats mechanically.\n\nGive each document a distinct writer. One may describe the affair as a manageable arrangement and fill the page with practical explanations; another may write around the thing they most want to say; a third may record an event in a way that carefully omits their own responsibility. The Adams contrast should operate inside the document: an apparently reasonable account of timing, privacy, or intention can expose its absurd consequences through the details the writer considers worth explaining. A long digression about an irrelevant practical problem should return, often without the writer noticing, to the desire or fear that prompted the document.\n\nLet gaps and conflicts carry information. What one writer records as a minor pause may appear in another document as a decisive rejection. A revised draft, a crossed-out line, a delayed reply, or a document never sent can reveal more than a completed account. Preserve readable continuity without adding an omniscient explanation after every record. The reader should assemble the affair from the documents, not from a narrator who resolves each discrepancy immediately.\n\nKeep the erotic material in the documents' actual knowledge. A letter may describe anticipation, a jour