sequence or a small logistical failure. Return the digression to the intimate scene through the current problem it was meant to clarify, and let that return change what the listener or the teller understands about the encounter rather than merely resuming the chronology.\n\nShow each participant's distinct perspective through the frame's interruptions and reported details. The teller may attribute motives, but a listener's challenge or a quoted detail can reveal that another participant understood the same moment differently. State physical continuity across the teller's digressions: the bodies remain where they were, the touch remains in progress, and the resumption of the account carries the accumulated complication. Do not let the frame moralize, sanctify, or turn the encounter into a lesson. End the telling with a practical uncertainty still open, a listener's response still forming, or the teller returning to a detail whose significance has just changed.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPafaear::♢ * [STYLE] Erotica / Douglas Adams / Minimalist Prose\n\nChoose a small number of exact details for each intimate scene and let them carry the emotional weight. Select details that are practical rather than conventionally romantic: the position of a chair, the state of a button, the distance between two bodies. Use direct clauses to state what happens and what a participant notices. Choose details whose significance the surrounding action makes inferable, so the anticipation, hesitation, or aftermath is present in what the prose includes rather than in commentary about it.\n\nCreate the Adams contrast through juxtaposition. Place a large claim or expectation next to a small physical fact that complicates it: a participant says something expansive about what the moment means, and the next clause records that the blanket is caught under a knee. Do not elaborate the absurdity. The gap between the two clauses does the work, and the scene continues without a narrator pausing to explain the joke.\n\nGive each participant a distinct relationship to the encounter through the details they notice and the actions they initiate. One may focus on a single recurring object; another may register only the practical obstacles. Keep physical continuity exact: if a hand moves, the next relevant clause records where it is. Omit the inward commentary that does not materially contribute, but include the essential thought even when it requires a longer sentence. End the passage at a point where the selected details have made the situation legible, without requiring the encounter to finish or the participants to resolve what the omission has left open.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPafaeas::♢ * [STYLE] Erotica / Douglas Adams / Modern Literature / Contemporary Novel\n\nWrite the intimate scene as continuous novel prose. Move among action, dialogue, perception, recollection, and the authorized inner experience of the viewpoint character as the scene's practical circumstances require. A conversation may pause for a physical adjustment, a recollection may surface because a current sensation resembles it, and a participant's explanation may begin as a reasonable account of what is happening before exposing its own absurd consequence.\n\nUse the Adams contrast as a structural movement within the scene. When a participant offers a large claim about the encounter or its meaning, let the next paragraph answer it with a small logistical fact: the arrangement of furniture, a misremembered instruction, a bodily inconvenience. Continue the scene through the complication, letting dialogue and inner experience absorb it, so the practical failure changes the emotional register without becoming the whole point of the encounter.\n\nPreserve each participant's distinct perspective and initiative. The narration may reveal the viewpoint character's reasoning while another participant's motives remain available only through what they say and do. Keep physical continuity across recollections and digressions: the scene does not reset after a memory, and the bodies remain in their last stated positions. Let the aftermath of intimacy remain present in the prose after the physical act, as a change in attention, speech, or silence. End the passage at a natural scene boundary, with the situation still capable of continuing, without requiring a completed emotional arc or a lesson.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPafaeat::♢ * [STYLE] Erotica / Douglas Adams / Relationship-Centric Fanfiction (AO3-inspired)\n\nChoose intimate scenes that make the established relationship premise legible through what the participants already share. Use private language, repeated moments, and callbacks that belong to this particular pair rather than to a generic set of romantic tropes. A phrase, an object, or a small ritual may return with new significance because the current scene has changed its application. Let the emotional payoff come from the fulfillment of the relationship's particular promise, not from an inserted crisis or a familiar trope checklist.\n\nWrite Adams-style practical absurdity as part of the couple's shared history. When a participant attempts a large romantic gesture or explains what the encounter is supposed to mean, let a small logistical problem intervene: a miscommunication about where to meet, an object that fails at the decisive moment, a plan that was reasonable on paper. Write the other participant's response as specific to their relationship. They may find it endearing, exasperating, or familiar, but they do not all become the same comic narrator. The absurdity becomes another thing these two people have lived through together, and the callback returns through the current scene rather than as a detached memory.\n\nPreserve each participant's distinct perspective and initiative. One may be the planner whose explanations keep failing; the other may be the one who notices the practical obstacle first. Keep physical continuity across the scene's emotional beats: the encounter does not reset after a callback, and the aftermath remains present in the way the participants speak or fall silent. Use only the actual supplied premise for the relationship. End the passage at a point where the scene's particular promise has been felt or complicated, without requiring the relationship to reach a fixed conclusion or the absurdity to be resolved into a lesson.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPafaeau::♢ * [STYLE] Erotica / Douglas Adams / Saga / Oral Epic\n\nOpen the intimate account with a deliberate declaration that establishes the participants and the deed to be remembered, then expand into the particulars that make the declaration incomplete. Use recurring phrasing only when a later occurrence gives it a new application. A phrase attached to an earlier encounter may return when the current scene changes what it means, so the recurrence deepens the account rather than decorating it.\n\nInterrupt the chronicle's authority with the Adams contrast. When the telling elevates an intimate act into something worthy of remembrance, let a small practical inconvenience answer it: a mislaid object, a bodily mishap, a plan that failed for a reason the teller must now explain. Keep the practical detail concrete, and let the telling absorb it as part of what will be remembered rather than as a flaw to be edited out. The participants remain particular people, not comic heroes, and the encounter remains an intimate act, not mock combat.\n\nShow each participant's distinct perspective and initiative within the chronicle. The teller may attribute motives, but a reported action or a quoted objection can show that another participant understood the same moment differently. Keep physical continuity across the telling's recurrences: the bodies remain in their last stated positions, and the aftermath remains part of the deed. End the account with the act concluded or still in progress, leaving another modest consequence available to be remembered, without declaring the encounter legendary on the participants' behalf.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPafaeav::♢ * [STYLE] Erotica / Douglas Adams / Scandinavian Literary Traditions\n\nWrite this as Scandinavian social and domestic realism. The erotic charge is produced through the particular obligations, rooms, routines, and social expectations that surround the encounter, not through scenery that merely decorates it. When a character reaches for the other person, the movement is entangled with what must be arranged, returned, waited for, or explained: a coat hung because the hallway is shared, a meal finished because the evening has a timetable, a door closed against a household that may return, a message answered because someone else's schedule depends on it. Let these domestic particulars create the rhythm of approach and delay.\n\nUse the Adams contrast between large claims and small logistical problems as the engine of anticipation. A character may announce what the evening should mean or how it should proceed, and the scene then works through the smaller operations that refuse to cooperate: the radiator that ticks through the first silence, the neighbor's expected call, the bed that must be remade, the key that will not turn quietly. Do not let the friction cancel desire. Instead, show how the large claim changes under practical pressure: the speaker revises the plan, becomes more determined, or keeps explaining because the explanation is itself a way of staying close. Give the two participants different relationships to the interruption. One may treat it as part of the evening's ordinary texture, another as an obstacle to be solved, another as a reason to slow down. They must not share one comic narrator's voice.\n\nKeep each participant's inward perspective and initiative distinct. The narration may stay close to one person's attention, but the other's desire, hesitation, and boundaries must remain legible through speech, touch, practical choices, and what they do not yet do. Preserve physical continuity as a sequence of embodied actions: a hand on a shoulder, a pause to remove a watch, a shift in position, the ordinary awkwardness of a limb finding room. Do not summarize the encounter into a smooth result. The physical sequence is where the characters' differing responses to the evening become visible.\n\nLet any digression about household procedure, social custom, or remembered embarrassment return through the current problem. If a character explains why the hallway light always takes three tries, that explanation must later change what a hand can do next, what can be said, or what the other person is asked to accept. The absurdity should expose something about the relationship or the moment: a need for control, a fear of being overheard, a wish to be known through the house's small failures. Do not replace intimacy with a joke.\n\nKeep the emotional aftermath concrete and social. Afterward, the two may talk about what the evening means for tomorrow, clean up, eat, or sit with a feeling neither names. Let the aftermath include practical speech and silence in whatever proportion the characters actually produce. The scene can end there without forcing a confession, a changed plan, or a completed emotional resolution.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPafaeaw::♢ * [STYLE] Erotica / Douglas Adams / Screenplay\n\nWrite in concise scene blocks. Use a scene heading when location or time changes, readable action lines for visible and audible events, and speaker-attributed dialogue. Build the erotic encounter through the practical steps the action lines can show: arriving, clearing a table, closing a door, reaching, stopping, adjusting, continuing. Keep each action line specific enough that the physical continuity of the encounter remains legible on the page.\n\nUse the Adams contrast between large claims and small logistical problems by placing the large claim in dialogue and the small friction in the action line that follows or interrupts it. A character may announce a perfectly reasonable plan for the evening, and the action line then records the coat that will not come off, the phone that must be moved, the lamp that makes the room too bright, or the chair that blocks the way. Let the dialogue explain, justify, or revise the plan while the action quietly complicates it. Do not make every line a punchline; the friction should change what the characters can do next, not merely decorate the exchange.\n\nGive each participant distinct initiative and response. One may talk through uncertainty, another may act first and explain later, another may become absorbed in a small task as a way of delaying or approaching. Their different relationships to the encounter appear in what they do and say, not in matching witty remarks. Let one character's practical competence be another character's hesitation, and let the dialogue register that difference without turning it into a debate.\n\nPlace required private experience in clearly attributed nonspoken notes, separate from dialogue. Use these notes to show anticipation, vulnerability, hesitation, or a private judgment that the other character cannot hear. Attribute each note to the character whose interiority is authorized, and keep it out of the spoken exchange. Do not turn the note into voice-over or let another character respond to it.\n\nUse cuts, offscreen sound, or a new scene heading only when the sequence needs them: to move past an interval, to signal a change of room, or to let an interruption arrive from outside. The scene can end during continuing action or speech, or in the aftermath, without requiring a completed emotional beat or a final line that resolves the encounter.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPafaeax::♢ * [STYLE] Erotica / Douglas Adams / Stage Play\n\nWrite a playable scene with clear speaker names, entrances, exits, and economical stage directions. Build the erotic encounter through what can be staged: a coat handed across a chair, a table cleared, a door closed or left open, a pause before a touch, a shift in position, an interruption from outside. Keep blocking and object use specific enough that the physical continuity of the encounter remains legible to a director and cast.\n\nUse the Adams contrast between large claims and small logistical problems by placing the large claim in the spoken line and the small friction in the stage direction that accompanies or follows it. A character may describe the evening they intend to have while the stage direction records the chair that must be moved, the cup that nearly falls, or the door that will not stay shut. Let the dialogue explain or revise the plan while the business complicates it. Do not make both characters deliver the same kind of witty aside; the friction should change what they can do next, not merely ornament the exchange.\n\nGive each participant distinct initiative and response. One may talk to fill the silence, another may act first, another may become absorbed in a practical task as a way of delaying or approaching. Their different relationships to the encounter appear in what they do, what they avoid doing, and how they speak. Let one character's practical competence be another character's hesitation, and let the dialogue register that difference without turning it into a debate.\n\nPlace required private experience as nonspoken authoring material in the active presentation, clearly attributed and separate from the performed scene. Use it for anticipation, vulnerability, hesitation, or a private judgment the other character cannot hear. Do not turn it into a monologue or aside unless the scene gives that performance a dramatic purpose. Keep the note out of the spoken exchange and do not let another character respond to it.\n\nLet the scene end during continuing action, speech, or aftermath. A pause, an unfinished sentence, or a simple stage action can close the response without requiring a final line that resolves the encounter or a theatrical revelation