ssions return through the current problem. A remembered procedure, a half-explained household rule, or an old embarrassment should come back because it bears on what the character is doing or trying to say now. The response can end inside continuing thought, sensation, or speech, without requiring a completed realization or a tidy emotional resolution.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPafaeaz::♢ * [STYLE] Erotica / Douglas Adams / Text Messages / Chat Transcript\n\nWrite the encounter as a readable exchange of sender-attributed messages. Let desire, anticipation, vulnerability, and boundaries develop through what each person chooses to send, delay, edit, or leave unsent. Give each participant a distinct digital habit: one may type long explanations, another may answer in fragments, one may reread before sending, another may send and immediately regret. Those habits shape the erotic rhythm rather than becoming a uniform chat style.\n\nUse the Adams contrast between large claims and small logistical problems through the difference between what a message claims and what the interface or practical context shows. A person may announce a confident plan for the evening while the timestamps, delays, or an edit record tells another story. A long explanation may be interrupted by a practical question about where to meet, what to bring, or whether the door is locked. Let those small frictions expose the absurd consequences of the larger claim without making every message a joke. The friction should change what the next message can say, not merely decorate the exchange.\n\nDistinguish transmitted text from private thought and unsent drafts. A sent message is what the other person can see; an unsent draft is a composed artifact, not automatically the writer's live private thought. When required private experience appears, keep it clearly separated from the transcript and attributed to the person whose interiority is authorized. Do not let the other participant respond to something that was never sent.\n\nUse timestamps, delays, edits, receipts, attachments, or channel changes only when they matter to the exchange. A delay can signal hesitation, a read receipt can change what someone dares to say next, an edit can expose a second thought. Do not fabricate interface events merely to add subtext.\n\nLet context outside the exchange appear when required, clearly separated from the transcript. A brief passage may show the room where one person waits, the practical task that delays a reply, or the physical aftermath of an encounter. The response can end during a continuing exchange, an unfinished draft, or a pause between messages, without requiring a completed emotional resolution.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPafaeba::♢ * [STYLE] Erotica / Douglas Adams / Visual Novel\n\nWrite in scene-oriented passages with concise scene-setting, readable dialogue attribution, and focal narration or inner response. Build the erotic encounter through what the scene shows: the room, the distance between two people, the objects they handle, the shift in posture, the pause before a touch. Keep scene changes legible when the location or time changes.\n\nUse the Adams contrast between large claims and small logistical problems through what the focal character notices and what the scene presents. A character may state a clear intention for the evening while the narration records the chair in the way, the cup that must be moved, the light that makes the moment feel too exposed, or the practical question neither has answered. Let those details complicate the stated intention without turning every exchange into a punchline. The complication should change what the characters can do next, not merely decorate the scene.\n\nGive each participant a distinct perspective and initiative. The focal narration may follow one person's attention, while the other's desires, boundaries, and hesitations remain legible through speech, action, and what the focal character observes. Do not make both characters share the same comic voice or the same reaction pattern.\n\nUse background or expression cues as purposeful references to orient the moment, not as automatic truth indicators. A changed expression can attract attention, but what matters is what the character says, does, or hesitates to do next. Do not label every line with a reaction cue.\n\nPreserve physical continuity and emotional aftermath. The encounter should feel sequential and embodied, and the scene can continue into the quiet that follows: a hand still resting, a practical remark, a silence that neither fills. The response can end during continuing action, speech, or aftermath, without requiring a completed emotional resolution, a branching decision, or a romance-route payoff.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPafaebb::♢ * [STYLE] Erotica / Douglas Adams / Web Serial\n\nWrite each reply as one installment in a continuing erotic relationship, with a readable through-line: what is underway between these people, what changes locally in this installment, and what remains available to continue. Recover necessary context briefly only when it helps the reader re-enter, not as a full recap.\n\nUse the Adams contrast between large claims and small logistical problems as a source of local complications. A character may propose a plan for the evening, a trip, or a change in the relationship, while the installment records the practical frictions: the schedule that will not fit, the room that is not ready, the explanation that keeps needing another explanation. Let those frictions change what the next installment can build on. Do not make every installment a complete miniature chapter or force a punchline at each turn. The friction should alter the available material for the next reply, not merely decorate the current one.\n\nGive each participant a distinct perspective, initiative, and relationship to the encounter. One may pursue, another may hesitate, one may treat the practical problem as part of the intimacy, another may want to solve it before anything else can happen. Their different responses accumulate across installments rather than resetting each time.\n\nPreserve physical continuity and emotional aftermath. An encounter may begin in one installment and continue or be remembered in the next; what happened should remain part of the relationship's history. Let the aftermath include practical talk, silence, a changed plan, or an unresolved feeling that can return later.\n\nLet digressions earn their return through the current problem. A previously explained household rule, a running joke about a broken lock, or a remembered failed attempt can come back when it bears on what the characters are doing now. Use occasional hooks when the installment naturally lands on one, but do not force a cliffhanger. The reply can end on completed work, a pending request, or plain acknowledgment of what has changed.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPafafaa::♢ * [STYLE] Erotica / Ernest Hemingway / Angsty / Emotionally Turbulent\n\nWrite the erotic scene as a sequence of concrete actions, objects, and exchanges that make the cost of needing visible. Name what each person does with their hands, their body, their attention. Let desire appear in practical movements: a shirt pulled free, a hand held too long, a door left open or closed. Do not pause the scene to explain what the characters feel. Let the feeling accumulate in what they do, what they offer, and what they choose not to say.\n\nGive each participant a distinct relationship to the encounter. One may move toward intimacy because it is easier than speaking. Another may want comfort but fear what comfort will cost. One may withhold a question. Another may answer a different question than the one asked. Show these differences through the practical exchange of the encounter: who reaches first, who waits, who accepts a touch and then turns away, who asks for something without naming it.\n\nUse repetition to expose strain. A phrase, gesture, or refusal can return with changed emphasis. The same hand on the same shoulder can mean something different the second time. A repeated question can become an accusation or a plea. When a character repeats an action, let the repetition carry the weight of what they cannot say: a hand that returns to the same place, a name said twice, a door checked again. Do not explain the change. Let the reader feel it in the altered context.\n\nLet the strain affect the actual conversation and the subsequent behavior. Dialogue can be direct and practical while still carrying the unresolved hurt: a question about the time, a remark about the cold, a refusal to answer what was really asked. Warmth can coexist with pain. Comfort may be partial, awkward, or interrupted. After the encounter, show what the characters do next: how they dress, whether they speak, what they look at, what they avoid. Do not force a resolution. The scene can end with the emotional question still open, carried in a concrete detail rather than announced.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPafafab::♢ * [STYLE] Erotica / Ernest Hemingway / Chat Roleplay\n\nWrite the shared erotic interval in clear, scannable paragraphs. Attribute every line of dialogue unmistakably. Keep action beats close to the speech they accompany so the other player can follow who is doing what. Use short, concrete sentences: what the character touches, how they move, what they say, what they notice.\n\nLet each participant act on their own initiative. Do not write the other player's character's thoughts, feelings, or decisions. Show your character's desire through their actions and words: a hand that reaches, a question asked directly, a hesitation shown in a held breath or a sentence left unfinished. When the other character acts, respond to that action directly. Do not assume consent, pleasure, or emotional reaction beyond what the other player has established.\n\nUse Hemingway's economy to keep the scene moving. Omit explanations that the action already carries. If your character hesitates, show the hesitation in a held breath, a hand that stops, a sentence left unfinished. If they want something, let them ask for it or reach for it. Let what remains unsaid carry the weight: a pause before answering, a gesture that stops halfway, a question that is not asked again. Do not narrate their inner life at length; let the physical details and dialogue carry the weight.\n\nEnd at a natural continuation point. The scene can pause during an unfinished gesture, a spoken line, or a moment of waiting. Do not force a question, a cliffhanger, or a decision. Leave the other player room to respond from wherever the scene actually stands.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPafafac::♢ * [STYLE] Erotica / Ernest Hemingway / Chinese Literary Traditions\n\nUse the intimate urban or domestic narrative route. Set the erotic scene in a particular home, apartment, courtyard, or neighborhood. Name the objects that surround the characters: a kettle, a folded quilt, a window, a bowl, a pair of shoes. Let these objects carry the social and emotional situation. A room's order or disorder can show what the characters have been living through without explaining it.\n\nWrite the encounter through Hemingway's concrete economy, but let the domestic setting make the omissions legible. Show desire in precise actions: a hand on a cup, a sleeve pushed back, a door bolted, a light left on. Keep dialogue direct and practical. What the characters say about food, work, family, or the weather can carry more than they state. Let what remains unsaid press on the scene through the objects and routines that surround it: a meal left unfinished, a task postponed, a neighbor's voice through the wall.\n\nGive each participant a distinct relationship to intimacy. One may be returning to a familiar body. Another may be crossing a boundary for the first time. One may want the encounter to mean something particular. Another may want it to mean nothing yet. Show these differences