ger thought run without losing the concrete particulars.\n\nGround any rough or abrasive speech in its speaker. If a character is blunt or harsh, let that come from who they are and what they want, not from a universal tone. Preserve the aftermath in concrete terms: how they clean up, what they put back on, what they say or do not say. The encounter leaves marks, and the prose should show them without moralizing.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPafafae::♢ * [STYLE] Erotica / Ernest Hemingway / Epistolary / Documents\n\nTell the erotic story through documents. Each document has a clear author, an intended recipient or purpose, and a time of composition. A letter may be sent or unsent. A journal entry may be written the same night or days later. A note may be practical, tender, evasive, or angry. Use the document form required by the scene rather than rotating through formats.\n\nWrite each document in Hemingway's economical, concrete language. Let the author record specific objects, actions, and exchanges: what happened in the room, what was said, what was left on the table, what time it was. Do not let the document become a full explanation of feeling. Let desire appear in what the writer chooses to record and what they leave out. The document's situation determines the omission: a letter to a lover may conceal what a journal entry admits, and a practical note may reveal more than it says.\n\nUse gaps and revisions to carry meaning. A letter may be crossed out and begun again. A journal may break off mid-sentence. A later entry may contradict an earlier one. These material traces show what the writer could not say, did not understand, or no longer believes. Let the documents speak next to each other. The reader should assemble the erotic encounter and its aftermath from the sequence of records, not from a summary between them.\n\nPreserve the difference between what the document's author knows and what the reader can infer. A letter may conceal something from its recipient while revealing it to the reader through its omissions. A journal may be honest about an action but blind to its own motive. The erotic encounter and its aftermath should emerge across the documents, in the gaps and contradictions between what different authors record.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPafafaf::♢ * [STYLE] Erotica / Ernest Hemingway / Fluffy / Comforting\n\nWrite the erotic scene as an interval of chosen warmth and care. Use Hemingway's concrete attention to make the comfort specific: a familiar preference remembered, a cup of tea made the right way, a blanket pulled up, a hand placed where it is wanted. Do not generalize the affection. Show it in small, precise actions and objects. Let the comfort be the means by which desire is expressed: the same practical care that makes the tea also undresses the body, touches with attention, speaks quietly.\n\nLet desire and intimacy remain central. The comfort is not a pause before the real scene. The characters can move toward each other through the same practical care: undressing slowly, speaking quietly, touching with attention. The erotic encounter and the comfort are the same event, not separate modes. Show how the warmth of the room, the familiarity of the objects, and the care in the actions all belong to the same intimate interval.\n\nKeep personality and privacy intact. A character can be comforted without becoming agreeable. A disagreement can remain unresolved while the characters still choose to be close. Do not require a confession, an apology, or an emotional breakthrough. Affection can be sincere and partial at the same time. Let the comfort be chosen, not automatic: a character may accept a kindness, refuse it, or offer one in return.\n\nPreserve the aftermath as a continuation of warmth. Show what the characters do afterward: how they rest, what they eat, what they say in the quiet. Let the scene end in ease without forcing a resolution. The comfort is enough.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPafafag::♢ * [STYL