 or completed act.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPafafao::♢ * [STYLE] Erotica / Ernest Hemingway / Lyrical Prose\n\nBuild the erotic scene from a small set of exact objects and actions, named plainly at first: the glass, the belt, the hand on the doorframe, the sound of water in the next room. Let syntax then extend around one of those details so the sentence itself performs the movement of attention. A long clause can follow the hand as it travels, hesitate, and return; a repeated sound can carry the rhythm of waiting. Keep every extension traceable to the scene's actual objects and the focal character's associations. The prose should make desire present as an unfolding physical event, not replace it with an emotional label.\n\nUse comparison as a way of making a private state available through something already in the room. A held breath may resemble the stillness before rain only if the character has reason to notice that resemblance; a repeated touch may become a phrase the character cannot finish because the rhythm keeps arriving before the words do. Let a comparison change what the narrator understands or makes present, then return to the plain object. A short declarative sentence after an extended one can be the moment a boundary is spoken, a permission is given, or a decision is made. The contrast in rhythm is itself part of the erotic information.\n\nGive each participant distinct initiative and physical continuity. One may move, one may wait, one may answer a question with a practical action. Show the aftermath in the body and the room: the sheet's position, the water still running, the glass now empty, the particular silence after speech. Repetition should expose what a character will not say or cannot leave behind: a name said twice, a motion begun and stopped, a sentence the narrator returns to because it still matters. End when the physical moment and its immediate consequence have been rendered, leaving the next action available rather than announcing a completed feeling.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPafafap::♢ * [STYLE] Erotica / Ernest Hemingway / Melancholic / Reflective\n\nLet a present intimate act carry the weight of a remembered one by making the resemblance or difference physical before it becomes reflective. Name the exact detail: the same doorway, the same scar, the way a hand rests now where it once rested differently. Then let the focal character notice one small irreversible change in that detail. The change may be in the body, the room, the timing, or the absence of an old hesitation. Keep the reflection attached to that specific observation. The pleasure can remain pleasure; the present scene does not need to be diminished to prove that time has passed.\n\nUse memory as a second presence in the room, introduced through a current gesture or object rather than as a pause from the encounter. A character may recall an earlier occasion because a present touch resembles it, or because this is the first time something has happened without the old fear. Keep the memory concrete: what was said, what was worn, what the weather did, what was left unfinished. Do not let the past erase the present partner's distinct initiative. The person in the room now acts, answers, and wants in their own way, and their difference from the remembered person is part of the scene's meaning.\n\nShow aftermath through ordinary details whose meaning has changed for the focal character. A made bed, a closed door, a glass left on the table can hold unfinished attachment without a concluding statement about loss. Repetition can expose a refusal to move on: the narrator returns to the same phrase or motion because it still holds something unresolved. End when the present scene has been lived alongside its memory and its immediate consequence is clear, leaving the next action available rather than requiring a final statement of regret.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPafafaq::♢ * [STYLE] Erotica / Ernest Hemingway / Middle Eastern Literary Traditions\n\nUse a framed oral account in which a situated teller speaks to a particular listener who needs to hear the story, or retells an incident already shaped by being told before. Make the frame concrete: where the teller sits, what work is being done, what the listener does not yet know, and why the telling is happening now. The frame should not be a neutral container. The teller's reason for speaking, and the listener's reason for staying, should affect what is said, what is delayed, and what is left unsaid.\n\nRender the intimate encounter itself in direct, physical language. The teller names the room, the objects, the exact motions, and lets what is unsaid carry the private meaning. Keep the social and domestic setting present without turning the encounter into a lesson about honor or tradition. The participants have distinct desires and initiatives. One may initiate, one may hesitate, one may set a boundary that the other respects or negotiates. Show the practical exchanges around intimacy: the tea left to cool, the door closed against the household, the errand used as a reason to be alone. The teller's direct language should preserve the physical continuity of the encounter rather than dissolve it into commentary.\n\nUse the frame to manage knowledge and consequence. The teller may know the aftermath and withhold it until the listener asks, or may tell the story because the aftermath has already changed the household. The listener's interruptions can be practical, skeptical, or curious, but they should not force the teller to moralize. Repetition in the teller's voice can mark what the community already knows and what the teller is still unwilling to explain. End the account when the teller has delivered what the occasion requires, leaving the private experience partly unspoken rather than converting it into a public verdict.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPafafar::♢ * [STYLE] Erotica / Ernest Hemingway / Minimalist Prose\n\nChoose a small number of exact details and let them carry the desire, anticipation, and boundary of the encounter. The bed, the lamp, the sound of a zipper, the way a hand stops. Use direct clauses. When a detail is kept, make it work: the hesitation in a motion can carry fear, the stopped hand or the spoken word can carry a boundary, the glass handed over can carry an unspoken question. Do not add a paragraph of explanation when the selected detail already supplies the inference.\n\nKeep physical continuity exact so the reader knows where each person is, what they are doing, and what changes in the room. Give each participant a distinct action and initiative: one speaks, one moves, one waits. A practical exchange can carry more than a declaration: a shirt folded, a question answered with a touch. When a thought or inward connection is essential, let it be long enough to make sense, but keep it tied to the immediate physical facts. Omission should leave something inferable rather than delete the reason for what happens next.\n\nUse repetition sparingly and for emphasis. A phrase returned to can expose a refusal to move on or a change in meaning. Show aftermath in one or two concrete details: the sheet on the floor, the water still running, the door not closed all the way. End when the physical moment and its immediate consequence are clear, leaving the next action available rather than adding a summary of feelings or a lesson.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPafafas::♢ * [STYLE] Erotica / Ernest Hemingway / Modern Literature / Contemporary Novel\n\nWrite the intimate encounter as a sustained scene with natural movement among what the characters do, say, perceive, remember, and privately think. Begin in the physical present: the room, the time, the practical reason they are there. Let dialogue and action carry the approach. A question about the window, a hand on a sleeve, a silence that one of them decides to break. Use direct, concrete language for the body and the objects in the room.\n\nMove into recollection or inner experience when the scene calls for it, not as a pause from characterization. A character may remember an earlier touch because the present one differs, or may think through a hesitation before acting. Keep the thought tied to the immediate physical facts, and return to the partner's continuing action, speech, or response. The partner in the room remains distinct and consequential; their initiative is not suspended while the focal character thinks. Interiority should make the encounter's desire, vulnerability, or boundary more precise rather than replace the other participant's presence.\n\nUse paragraphing to orient without recap machinery. A new paragraph can mark a shift from speech to touch, from present action to memory, or from one participant's attention to the other's response. Let repetition work as emphasis: a phrase said twice, a motion begun again, a name returned to. Show aftermath in the scene's continuing physical details rather than a concluding emotional summary. End when the present moment has been fully lived and its immediate consequence is clear, leaving the next action available without forcing a resolution.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPafafat::♢ * [STYLE] Erotica / Ernest Hemingway / Relationship-Centric Fanfiction (AO3-inspired)\n\nChoose scenes that make the established relationship premise legible through what the characters do and do not say. The reader should understand what these two people have been to each other from the scene's selection and its concrete details: which room they are in, what they are doing there, what has been left unfinished between them. Use private language that belongs to them: a particular phrase, a nickname, a way of asking for something without naming it. Let a repeated moment return with new weight. The same doorway, the same joke, the same small ritual can show what has changed and what has not.\n\nGive the emotional payoff enough space, but let it arrive through concrete action and dialogue. Affection can be a