ful omission: what the next scene shows may reveal that something happened during the skipped interval, but it must not contradict what was previously established.\n\nEnd on an observable action or exchange that completes the scene's attention: a door closing, a light switched off, a question answered, a person leaving or staying. Do not require a confession, a decision, or a completed relationship before the scene can end.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPafafax::♢ * [STYLE] Erotica / Ernest Hemingway / Stage Play\n\nWrite the scene as a playable script: clear speakers, entrances, exits, blocking, objects, pauses, and actions that can be performed on a stage. Let the physical relationship between the actors carry the desire. Distance, approach, retreat, a hand on a chair, a coat taken, a glass passed, a door held open are the material of the scene.\n\nGive each character distinct speech and distinct physical initiative. One may speak through arrangements and practical questions, another through direct invitation or deliberate movement. Let dialogue negotiate proximity: a request to stay, a reason to leave, a task that keeps two people in the same room, a question that cannot be answered without moving closer.\n\nUse pauses and repeated business to show what is not said. A character may pick up the same object twice, pour a drink and not drink it, or stand at a door without leaving. Repeat a gesture so the second occurrence changes meaning. Do not explain the change in the stage directions; let the actor's repeated action carry it.\n\nLet the shared stage space itself create pressure. An entrance can narrow the distance between two people; an exit can leave one person alone with an unfinished task. Blocking can place a character between another person and the door, or keep them on opposite sides of a table. A prop can be handed, withheld, or set down between them. These spatial facts are the public evidence of desire; they do not require a spoken confession.\n\nKeep required private experience as clearly attributed nonspoken authoring material. It is not an aside, not a monologue, and not a line another character can hear. Use a monologue or aside only when the dramatic situation requires a performed speech, and keep it distinct from the character's live private thought.\n\nPreserve physical continuity on stage. If a character removes a coat, it remains off until they put it on again. If a bed or chair is used, its state afterward is part of the scene. Let the aftermath be playable: a character smoothing a blanket, opening a window, sitting apart, or putting on a shoe.\n\nDo not add film-camera instructions. The scene must work through what the actors do and say in the shared space. An entrance or exit changes the scene's possibilities; a prop is handled or left alone; a pause is held or broken.\n\nEnd on a playable action or line that completes the scene's attention: an exit, an entrance, a task resumed, a silence held, or a plain answer. The encounter need not produce a confession or a resolution.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPafafay::♢ * [STYLE] Erotica / Ernest Hemingway / Stream of Consciousness\n\nWrite from inside one authorized mind and let association, not linear report, structure the telling. Braid perception, memory, private words, bodily sensation, interruption, and recurring concern. The syntax and paragraph shape may follow the movement of attention, but keep enough orientation to distinguish what is happening now from what is remembered or imagined.\n\nAnchor the associative flow in Hemingway's concrete objects and actions. A mind may move from the sound of a glass on a table to a remembered room, from the weight of a coat to a past departure, from a hand's warmth to a phrase someone once said. Return to one or two physical details so they become the fixed points around which desire, fear, and memory turn. Do not explain the return; let the repeated detail carry the changed emphasis.\n\nLet bodily sensation carry the erotic present. Heat, cold, pressure, breath, the texture of cloth, the ache of waiting, the difference between a touch imagined and a touch received. Keep the physical encounter continuous even when the mind drifts: the body remains in the room, the hand remains where it was, the unfinished action waits. When the mind returns, show the present object or sensation that called it back.\n\nUse memory and fantasy as distinct movements. A memory is attached to a particular time and place; a fantasy is a possible or wished event. Do not let them blur into the present without a clear return. The distinction can be marked by the object that triggers each movement: a remembered room has a known location, a fantasized touch has no fixed time, and the present room has the body still in it.\n\nPreserve the other person's separateness. The focal mind may interpret, remember, or imagine the other person, but it does not know their private thought. The other person's actions, words, and refusals remain their own. Do not grant the focal mind access to another interiority.\n\nLet practical exchanges interrupt and redirect the flow. A question, a task, a sound from outside, a door opening can break an association and return the mind to the immediate scene. The interruption need not resolve the thought; it may simply suspend it.\n\nEnd where the mind's attention actually rests: on a present sensation, a remembered phrase, a question left unanswered, or the other person's movement. The encounter need not complete itself or produce a decision.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPafafaz::♢ * [STYLE] Erotica / Ernest Hemingway / Text Messages / Chat Transcript\n\nWrite the story as a readable transcript of sender-attributed messages. Use only the timestamps, delays, edits, receipts, attachments, or channel changes that matter to the encounter. Let each person's actual digital habits shape their messages: one may write short practical lines, another may type and revise, one may answer immediately, another may leave a message unread.\n\nLet the sent messages carry desire through economy and omission. A short answer can accept or decline. A practical question can be an invitation. A message that stops mid-thought can expose a refusal to continue. Repeat a phrase or question so the repetition changes meaning: the same words become more deliberate, more careful, or more loaded. Do not explain the change; let the later message carry it.\n\nUse delays and edits as meaningful events. A long pause before a reply can be hesitation, work, anger, or ordinary life. An edit can show a sender choosing plainer words or pulling back from a confession. A read receipt can mark the moment a message is known. Use these only when they change the exchange; do not fabricate an interface event merely to add subtext.\n\nDistinguish a sent message from an unsent draft and private thought from transmitted text. An unsent draft is a composed artifact, not automatically the sender's live private thought. Keep required interiority clearly separated from the transcript: what a person feels, notices, wants, or fears is not transmitted unless they send it. Do not let the other person read a draft or a private note as if it were a message.\n\nPreserve each participant's distinct perspective and initiative. One may pursue through questions and arrangements, another through direct invitation or deliberate silence. Boundaries appear in the exchange: a message not answered, a topic not pursued, a request declined, a conversation left. When a boundary shifts, show it as a sent message or a changed pattern of response, not as a speech about feelings.\n\nLet context outside the exchange appear only when required, clearly separated from the transcript. A brief scene note may establish where someone is, what they are doing, or what happened between messages. It is not a message and not available to the other person.\n\nEnd where the exchange's actual attention ends: after a sent message, a read receipt, a draft abandoned, or a reply that changes the conversation. The encounter need not resolve or produce a decision.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPafafba::♢ * [STYLE] Erotica / Ernest Hemingway / Visual Novel\n\nWrite in concise scene-setting, readable dialogue attribution, and focal narration or inner response suited to an illustrated scene. Keep the scene legible: where the characters are, what they are doing, and what the focal character notices. Use background or expression cues as purposeful references to the illustrated moment, not as a mandatory label on every line.\n\nLet concrete objects and repeated gestures anchor the desire. A cup left untouched, a hand near another hand, a door not quite closed, a coat held too long. Return to one or two details so the repetition changes what the focal character understands. Do not explain the change; let the later image or inner response carry it.\n\nGive each participant distinct speech and distinct physical initiative. One may speak through arrangements and practical questions, another through direct invitation or deliberate movement. Dialogue can negotiate proximity: a request to stay, a reason to leave, a task that keeps two people in the same room. A short answer can accept or decline.\n\nKeep focal narration and inner response available as private experience. The focal character may notice desire without naming it, or may name it plainly to themselves. The other person does not know this thought merely because the reader does. Do not turn an expression cue into an automatic truth indicator; a changed expression can attract attention, but it does not reveal what the other person feels.\n\nPreserve physical continuity. Bodies move through real space, clothing is removed or kept, and the encounter leaves traces: a damp collar, a red mark, a bed that must be remade, a window opened afterward. Show the aftermath in the scene's observable detail and the focal character's response to it.\n\nKeep scene changes legible. A new location or time is marked clearly, but the skipped interval must remain consistent with what the next scene shows. Do not add branching menus, player decisions, romance routes, images, or executable engine commands merely because the style is selected.\n\nEnd where the scene's attention actually rests: after a shared task, a departure, a silence that has changed, or a plain acknowledgment. The encounter need not resolve the relationship or produce a decision.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPafafbb::♢ * [STYLE] Erotica / Ernest Hemingway / Web Serial\n\nWrite in installment-oriented prose with a readable through-line across replies. Keep clear what is underway, what changes locally, and what remains available to continue. Recover context briefly only when it helps the reader re-enter the scene. Do not retell the whole history of the relationship each installment.\n\nLet exact earlier observations remain available when a later installment changes what they suggest. A gesture first seen as ordinary becomes deliberate, then expected, then freighted with desire. A boundary first established as a closed door or a withdrawn hand may shift in a later installment. Briefly recover the necessary particular rather than explaining the change. Do not make the focal character forget a supported feeling or decision merely to preserve suspense.\n\nBuild anticipation through repeated concrete details. The same cup, coat, doorway, or task can return with new meaning. Repetition should deepen understanding rather than restart the same moment. Let the physical world carry what the characters do not say: a chair moved closer, a light left on, a glass filled and not drunk.\n\nGive each participant a distinct perspective, initiative, and relationship to the encounter. One may pursue through practical arrangements, another through direct invitation or deliberate withdrawal. Boundaries appear in the physical world and in what each person declines to say or do. When a boundary shifts, show the shift as an action or an accepted invitation, not as a speech about feelings.\n\nPreserve physical continuity across installments. If clothing is removed, it stays removed until someone puts it back on. If a bed is used, it is unmade afterward. If a door is closed, the next installment must account for its opening. The aftermath matters as much as the approach: show what each person does next and what they avoid doing.\n\nKeep required interiority available when it contributes feeling or thought, even if no immediate decision changes. A character may notice desire without naming it, or may name it plainly to themselves. The other person does not know this thought merely because the reader does.\n\nEnd where the installment's actual attention rests: after a completed task, a pending request, a departure, a silence that has changed, or a plain acknowledgment of what is now known. Use occasional hooks only when the development earns them. Do not force every installment into a complete miniature chapter or an artificial cliffhanger.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPafagaa::♢ * [STYLE] Erotica / Fyodor Dostoevsky / Angsty / Emotionally Turbulent\n\nWrite desire as an argument the focal character conducts against themselves. When a participant wants contact, comfort, or admission, let a second inward voice answer: the want is proof of weakness, a debt they cannot repay, a humiliation they deserve, or a claim they have no right to make. Do not resolve this argument quickly. The character may act on the desire while the accusing voice continues, or may refuse and then return to the refusal with fresh self-contempt.\n\nGive each participant a distinct stake in the same intimate interval. One may treat the encounter as a test of whether they can be wanted; another may treat it as a chance to be forgiven; another may be trying to prove they can still choose. Their needs should collide in the physical scene itself: a gesture meant as reassurance can feel like a demand, an offer of tenderness can expose a grievance, and a boundary can be spoken with relief and received as rejection. Preserve the actual physical continuity of the encounter rather than cutting away at the moment of greatest vulnerability.\n\nLet the aftermath remain unresolved. After intimacy, the focal character may replay a phrase, a hesitation, or their own silence and build a new accusation from it. Another participant may offer practical care that is sincere but cannot answer the hidden question. Warmth and pain should coexist without one canceling the other. A partial comfort may be accepted and still leave the deeper hurt untouched.\n\nKeep the narration inside the active point of view and do not grant access to other minds. The focal character's interpretation of a partner's motive is a live belief, not a confirmed fact. The partner's actual reason may be simpler, kinder, or differently wounded than the focal character imagines. The story may reveal that difference later or leave it unknown.\n\nDo not impose a tragic ending, a confession, or a reconciliation. The method is to sustain emotional exposure under pressure, not to guarantee that exposure produces a particular result. A scene may end with the focal character still caught between the need they acted on and the accusation they cannot dismiss.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPafagab::♢ * [STYLE] Erotica / Fyodor Dostoevsky / Chat Roleplay\n\nWrite the intimate scene in readable paragraphs with unmistakable dialogue attribution. Keep each speaker's voice distinct: one may press, one may retreat, one may turn a simple question into a test. Let the focal character's private reasoning appear in short narrative passages between spoken lines, showing how they interpret, dread, or re-interpret what the other person has just said. The private thought may contradict the spoken reply without being announced as a lie.\n\nMake desire and vulnerability the active subject of the exchange. A request for closeness can carry a hidden accusation; an offer of comfort can be made with hands that hesitate; a boundary can be stated while the speaker watches for the other person's relief or disappointment. Preserve the physical continuity of the interval. Do not fade out at the moment of greatest exposure; write what the focal character notices, feels, and cannot say.\n\nLet the focal character's self-argument affect the conversation without turning every line into a philosophical speech. A plain question from the other participant can unsettle an elaborate justification. The focal character may reverse position mid-exchange, then feel the reversal as a new humiliation. The other participant's actual motive remains outside the focal character's knowledge unless the active contract grants access.\n\nEnd the reply at a natural continuation point inside the scene. The next response can follow from the last spoken line, the focal character's unfinished thought, or the other participant's visible hesitation. Do not require a question, cliffhanger, or forced choice at the end. The continuation may be the focal character still deciding whether to speak, the other participant reaching out, or the shared silence after a confession that did not resolve anything.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPafagac::♢ * [STYLE] Erotica / Fyodor Dostoevsky / Chinese Literary Traditions\n\nUse the intimate urban or domestic narrative route. Place the erotic relationship inside a specific household, apartment building, shop, or neighborhood where ordinary obligations make desire visible: a shared meal, a closed door, a relative's visit, a neighbor's glance, a debt, a duty to a parent, or the management of a small space. Let these material conditions shape when and how intimacy can happen, and let the characters feel their desire as something that must be hidden from or justified to the people around them.\n\nGive each participant a distinct relationship to the domestic setting. One may experience the home as a place of duty they long to escape; another may experience it as the only place they can be wanted; another may treat the relationship as a secret that changes their standing in the family or neighborhood. The focal character's self-argument should attach to concrete objects and routines: a bowl left unwashed, a bed made too neatly, a window that must stay closed, a message that cannot be sent.\n\nWrite the erotic encounter through physical continuity and the aftermath through domestic consequence. After intimacy, the characters must return to the shared space, the waiting relative, the shop counter, or the morning routine. The focal character may replay a gesture while performing an ordinary task, or may interpret a partner's practical care as proof of love, obligation, or evasion. Keep the partner's actual motive distinct from the focal character's interpretation unless the active contract grants access.\n\nLet a plain speaker unsettle an elaborate justification. A family member, neighbor, or the partner themselves may ask a simple question that exposes the focal character's hidden reasoning. The story need not resolve the exposure. The scene may end with the focal character still caught between the desire they acted on and the domestic world that cannot acknowledge it.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPafagad::♢ * [STYLE] Erotica / Fyodor Dostoevsky / Compressed Gritty Prose\n\nWrite desire as a physical fact before it becomes a thought. The focal character notices the heat of a room, the worn edge of a sleeve, the sound of breathing, the pressure of a hand, the ache of waiting. Use short, concrete sentences to carry the body through the scene. Do not explain the feeling away; let the physical detail hold it.\n\nCompress the focal character's self-argument into brief bursts attached to material facts. A single accusing sentence can follow a touch: they had no right to want it. A defense can be undercut by a plain observation: the bed was still unmade. When the character's reasoning genuinely needs room, allow one longer passage, then return to the hard surface of the scene. Do not let every intimate moment become a speech.\n\nGive each participant a distinct physical stake. One may be careful with their hands; another may grip too hard; another may hold back and watch. Boundaries should be concrete: a turned shoulder, a hand removed, a door not quite closed. Preserve the physical continuity of the encounter and its immediate aftermath. After intimacy, the characters must deal with the body's evidence: sweat, disorder, a mark, the need to dress, the silence that follows.\n\nLet the aftermath remain unresolved. The focal character may replay a single detail and build a fresh accusation from it. A partner's practical act may be sincere but cannot answer the hidden question. Warmth and pain can occupy the same room without canceling each other. End the scene where the physical and emotional pressure is highest, not where a lesson has been learned.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPafagae::♢ * [STYLE] Erotica / Fyodor Dostoevsky / Epistolary / Documents\n\nTell the erotic sequence through documents with a clear author, intended recipient or purpose, and time of composition. Use the specific document form the scene requires: a letter that may be sent, a journal entry written for no one, a note left on a table, a message composed and deleted, a confession begun and abandoned. Each document should carry the writer's desire and their attempt to justify, accuse, or undo it.\n\nLet the document's situation govern what it knows and conceals. A letter to a lover may confess a need while hiding a debt; a journal entry may accuse the writer themselves; a note meant to be found may be written twice, once with the truth and once with what the writer can bear to leave behind. The gap between what a writer records and what they actually felt is part of the story. Do not add an omniscient explanation after every record.\n\nUse gaps, revisions, and conflicting accounts to reveal events across entries. One writer's version of an encounter may be contradicted by the other's letter, or by a later entry that reinterprets the same detail. A material trace, such as a torn page, an unsent draft, or a message left unread, can show the aftermath of intimacy without narrating it directly. Preserve the physical continuity of the encounter through what the documents can and cannot say: a writer may describe a touch, then cross it out, then return to it in a later entry with a new accusation.\n\nKeep each participant's perspective distinct. The focal document may interpret a partner's silence as rejection, while the partner's own document reveals a different motive, or no document appears and the interpretation remains unconfirmed. The sequence may end with an unsent confession, a letter that arrives too late, or a journal entry that revises everything the writer previously claimed. Do not require a final synthesis that resolves every contradiction.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPafagaf::♢ * [STYLE] Erotica / Fyodor Dostoevsky / Fluffy / Comforting\n\nWrite the erotic scene through chosen kindness and familiar preferences. One participant may remember how the other takes their tea, which side of the bed they prefer, or the exact way they like to be touched. Let these small attentions carry the desire. The focal character may feel unworthy of the care, but the care itself is real and offered without a demand for confession.\n\nLet affection be sincere without requiring emotional repair. A partner may offer warmth, food, rest, or a steady hand, and the focal character may accept it while still carrying an old shame. The self-accusing voice can surface, but it does not have to win. A plain reassurance from the partner can interrupt the focal character's spiral: not because the reassurance is clever, but because it is steady and specific.\n\nKeep personality, privacy, and disagreement intact. The focal character may be prickly, evasive, or convinced they are too much trouble. The partner may be patient without being perfect, or may set a gentle boundary of their own. Comfort does not make everyone agreeable. The established problem in the world remains, but the scene can still give the characters room to enjoy each other.\n\nPreserve the physical continuity of the encounter and its aftermath. After intimacy, write the small pleasures that follow: a shared blanket, a quiet meal, a hand resting on a shoulder, a joke that lands softly. The focal character may still replay an old accusation, but the warmth of the room is also true. End the scene where the comfort has been accepted, even if the deeper hurt has not been resolved.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPafagag::♢ * [STYLE] Erotica / Fyodor Dostoevsky / Formal Literary Novel\n\nWrite the erotic scene in composed novel prose with deliberate transitions and sustained paragraph movement. Let the narration move between the intimate encounter and the social context that surrounds it: a family's expectations, a reputation, a past humiliation, a debt, a public role that makes private desire feel dangerous or impossible. The narrator may frame the scene through history or judgment where the active point of view permits it, but the desire itself must remain central and concrete.\n\nGive the focal character's self-argument room to develop across a paragraph. A single touch can open a chain of reasoning: what the touch promises, what it costs, who would condemn it, whether the character has the right to accept it. The argument may defend, accuse, reverse, and entangle itself. Distinguish this narration from ordinary spoken language; the prose should feel composed, not conversational.\n\nGive each participant a distinct perspective, initiative, and relationship to the encounter. One may approach intimacy as a claim on another person's future; another may approach it as a brief escape from a public role; another may treat it as proof of their own worth. Their needs should collide in the physical scene and in its aftermath. Preserve the physical continuity of the encounter rather than retreating into abstraction at the moment of greatest vulnerability.\n\nLet the aftermath unfold through social consequence. After intimacy, the characters must return to the world that judges them: a family obligation, a public appearance, a letter that must be answered, a reputation that has shifted. The focal character may replay the encounter while performing a social duty, or may discover that the partner's public behavior does not match the private promise. Keep the partner's actual motive distinct from the focal character's interpretation unless the active contract grants access. End where the social and private pressures meet, not where a moral lesson has been completed.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPafagah::♢ * [STYLE] Erotica / Fyodor Dostoevsky / Framed Oral Storytelling (Arabian Nights-inspired)\n\nWrite the scene as a confession performed for a specific listener who has a concrete stake in what is being told. The teller is recounting an erotic encounter in order to be believed, forgiven, excused, or understood, and that need governs what they include, soften, reorder, or omit. The listener is not a passive audience: they ask questions, press for timing or motive, and sometimes supply the plain sentence that cuts through the teller's elaborate self-account.\n\nLet the recounted encounter carry its own physical continuity and emotional aftermath. The teller's memory must include the other participant's actual initiative, hesitation, and boundaries, not only the teller's version of them. When the teller smooths over the other person's agency, make the smoothing visible through what the teller cannot quite explain: a pause in the story, a detail that does not fit the teller's self-justification, or a moment of unexpected resistance the teller tries to absorb into their own narrative.\n\nUse the listener's interruptions as the Dostoevskian counter-voice. A plain question about what the other person wanted, when the teller knew something, or why a particular detail was omitted can force the teller's self-argument to reverse or entangle itself. The teller may defend the desire, accuse themselves, reverse the judgment, and then try to rebuild the justification in a new shape. Do not resolve this into a single moral verdict; the listener may hear the same account and draw a different conclusion from the teller's own emphasis.\n\nMove between the two levels deliberately. A remembered touch can interrupt the teller's argument in the present, or a listener's question can force the teller to revisit a moment they had smoothed over. Keep the frame intelligible by marking each shift clearly: the reader should always know whether they are inside the recounted encounter or the present scene of telling.\n\nUse recurrence as an emotional instrument. A phrase, gesture, object, or promise can return later in the telling with a changed meaning: what the teller presented as tender becomes coercive under the listener's question, or what the teller presented as shameful becomes the only moment the listener believes. Let the teller notice the repetition, or fail to notice it while the listener does.\n\nEnd at the natural boundary of the telling or the present scene. The teller may stop before the story is complete, the listener may respond before the teller resumes, or the telling may break off under the pressure of a question. Do not require a finished confession, a moral settlement, or a neat return to the outer frame. The pressure of the telling is the scene.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPafagai::♢ * [STYLE] Erotica / Fyodor Dostoevsky / German-Language Literary Traditions\n\nUse the modernist-satirical construction route. The narration keeps a controlled formal distance: syntax is deliberate, social detail is precise, and the erotic material is arranged rather than confessed. The narrator observes the characters from a slight remove, but the focal character's private reasoning remains available and drives the scene.\n\nMake the scene a staged collision between the focal character's inward justification and the narrator's cooler arrangement of the same events. The focal character does not simply want; they think about wanting, defend it, accuse themselves for it, reverse the judgment, and entangle the desire with their sense of who they are in a particular social and material situation. The narrator does not endorse or resolve that argument. Instead, the narrator supplies the concrete social and physical facts the character's reasoning keeps trying to absorb or overwrite.\n\nLet the formal construction make the collision visible. A repeated phrase, a syntactically parallel passage, or a deliberately staged contrast can show the character returning to the same desire from different angles without resolving it. The satirical edge appears in the gap between the character's elaborate reasoning and the physical or social reality of the encounter: the character may describe an act as a moral decision while the narration records a small, awkward, or ordinary physical detail that does not fit the description. Do not turn the intimacy into a joke or strip it of consequence; the satire cuts the self-image, not the desire or the other participant.\n\nUse the plain sentence as the structural counter-voice. A short, flat sentence can cut through an elaborate passage and land the reader on the actual encounter: what the other person said, where a hand stopped, what the room cost, what time it was. That cut is the moment where the character's self-image meets the material situation. Let the prose follow that movement without resolving it into a single verdict.\n\nGive the other participant their own initiative and perspective. The focal character's self-argument may misinterpret or overwrite the other person, but the narrative must let the other person's actions, words, and boundaries register as real. The focal character may notice this and revise their account, or fail to notice it in a way the reader can see through the narrator's cooler record.\n\nKeep the social and material conditions specific. The characters inhabit a particular place, class situation, institution, or historical moment. These conditions shape what the characters risk, what they can afford, and how they understand their own desire. Use the world and period actually supplied; do not import a fixed German setting or national temperament.\n\nEnd at the natural boundary of the scene. The character may still be arguing with themselves, the other person may have responded, or the situation may remain unresolved. Do not require a completed self-understanding or a moral settlement. The pressure of the collision between reasoning and arrangement is the scene.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPafagaj::♢ * [STYLE] Erotica / Fyodor Dostoevsky / Gothic-Punk\n\nLet the encounter happen inside a world where old authority and present life collide. The setting carries that collision in concrete urban material detail: a building that was once something else, a rule that no longer fits the people who must live under it, a debt or loyalty inherited from a past self. These conditions are not background. They shape what the characters can risk and how they read each other's bodies and silences.\n\nMake the inherited pressure specific and personal. The focal character is not arguing with an abstract system; they are arguing with a particular past self, promise, or obligation that the present encounter has reactivated. The Dostoevskian self-argument grows directly from that pressure: the character defends the desire as a break from the past, accuses it as a betrayal of the person they were, reverses the judgment, and entangles it with the question of who they are now. Let the prose follow that movement without resolving it into a single verdict.\n\nRender atmosphere through the focal character's interpretation of concrete urban detail. A locked door, a broken light, a familiar room that has decayed, or a public space that suddenly feels private can sharpen or distort the characters' attention. Do not write a separate mood paragraph. Let the detail arrive inside the character's reasoning or sensation: the character notices the broken light because it changes what they can see of the other person, or reads the locked door as a sign of safety or entrapment according to the present argument.\n\nAllow social masks to fall away in trusted company, but do not make that fall automatic or total. A character may reveal something and then try to cover it again. The focal character's self-argument can continue even in the middle of physical intimacy: a touch can interrupt a justification, or a remembered obligation can make the present moment feel stolen. Let the prose follow that movement without resolving it into a single verdict.\n\nKeep the other participant's boundaries and agency legible. They have their own relationship to the same inherited pressures, and their choices may differ from the focal character's. The focal character may project old roles onto them, but the narrative must let the other person's actual response correct or complicate that projection. The encounter's consequences belong to both of them, and the aftermath must register as part of the intimacy rather than as a separate moral lesson.\n\nDo not import vampire physiology, factions, powers, or blood-bond rules. The Gothic-Punk treatment is a narrative method: inherited power, present decay, private desire, and the masks people wear. Use it to make the erotic scene feel lived-in and dangerous without adding supernatural facts the world does not contain.\n\nEnd at the natural boundary of the scene. The characters may still be negotiating what the encounter means, or the old authority may reassert itself before anything is settled. Do not require a completed confession, a betrayal, or a clean escape. The pressure of the collision is the scene.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPafagak::♢ * [STYLE] Erotica / Fyodor Dostoevsky / Indian Literary Traditions\n\nUse the social/domestic realism route. The erotic encounter takes place inside a specific household, neighborhood, community, and material situation. Keep the language, region, community, and period specific when the world supplies them; do not force English, code-switching, religion, caste experience, or a generic family structure onto the scene.\n\nMake the focal character's desire inseparable from a specific domestic arrangement. The character wants something that has consequences for a particular relationship, obligation, or position within the household and the people who know them. The Dostoevskian self-argument grows directly from that arrangement: the character defends the desire in one voice, accuses it in another, reverses the judgment, and entangles it with what they owe to the people whose presence or absence shapes the encounter.\n\nRender the domestic setting through its boundaries, timings, and objects. The arrangement of rooms, the presence or absence of other people, the hour of the encounter, and the objects that carry family or community meaning all shape what the characters can do and how they understand it. A closed door is not just a door; it is a risk and a boundary with a particular social weight. A particular object is not decoration; it carries a memory or obligation that the focal character's self-argument must negotiate.\n\nGive the other participant their own perspective and initiative. They have their own relationship to the same domestic and social pressures, and their choices may differ from the focal character's. The focal character's self-argument may try to speak for them or overwrite them; let the narrative show the difference between what the focal character imagines and what the other person actually does or says.\n\nLet the aftermath register as part of the intimacy. The characters may return to the household, to their obligations, to the people who would judge them. The focal character's inward argument may continue there, and the encounter's consequences may appear in small domestic details: a changed silence, a task done differently, a question that is not asked. Do not resolve this into a single moral lesson.\n\nKeep the prose grounded in social realism. The narration attends to concrete domestic and social detail, and the characters' inner lives are available through their reasoning, attention, and sensation. Do not turn the scene into a philosophical speech; let the self-argument emerge from the particular arrangement of rooms, people, and obligations.\n\nEnd at the natural boundary of the scene. The characters may still be inside the encounter, or the domestic world may be about to reassert itself. Do not require a completed confession, a family confrontation, or a neat resolution. The pressure of desire inside these conditions is the scene.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPafagal::♢ * [STYLE] Erotica / Fyodor Dostoevsky / Japanese Literary Traditions\n\nUse the modern experimental inward fiction route. The scene is rendered through the focal character's associative mental movement: desire, memory, sensation, self-accusation, and justification interrupt and revise one another. The prose does not need to report events in a straight line; it can follow the character's attention as it moves between the present encounter and the past that gives it meaning.\n\nMake the Dostoevskian self-argument happen inside the associative movement. The character can defend the desire in one sentence, accuse themselves in the next, reverse the judgment, and then lose the argument in a physical sensation or a memory that will not stay buried. Do not summarize the entanglement; let the prose move through it. A plain sentence can cut through an elaborate passage, and that cut can be the moment where the character's self-image meets the actual encounter.\n\nLet the personal language and narrative distance follow the actual teller. The focal character's voice is particular: their syntax, their habits of thought, their way of naming or avoiding things. Do not impose a universal quietness, a mandatory seasonal inventory, or a generic Japanese scenery list. Use the language and setting actually supplied.\n\nGive the other participant their own initiative and perspective. The focal character's inward record may misread or overwrite them, but the narrative must let the other person's actions, words, and boundaries register as real. The focal character may notice the gap between what they imagined and what the other person actually did, or they may fail to notice it in a way the reader can see.\n\nLet the erotic material carry physical continuity and emotional aftermath. The inward movement does not dissolve the encounter into abstraction. Sensation, anticipation, vulnerability, and the consequences of intimacy remain concrete. The character's thought may interrupt a touch, or a touch may interrupt a thought, but both remain present and causally connected.\n\nUse omission and recurrence as formal instruments. A phrase, image, or memory can return at a later point with a changed meaning. What the character cannot bring themselves to think about can be as present as what they do think about. Do not turn this into a puzzle; let the omissions be legible through the character's particular avoidance. When the character's attention swerves away from a memory or a question, let the swerve itself reveal the pressure of what is being avoided.\n\nEnd at the natural boundary of the inward movement. The character may still be arguing with themselves, or the encounter may have shifted their attention without resolving anything. Do not require a completed realization, a confession, or a neat return to calm. The pressure of the inward argument is the scene.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPafagam::♢ * [STYLE] Erotica / Fyodor Dostoevsky / Latin American Literary Traditions\n\nUse the intimate psychological or urban fiction route. The erotic encounter takes place inside a specific Latin American urban or intimate setting, with the relevant language, community, and history preserved when the world supplies them. Do not relocate the cast or manufacture a generic regional temperament.\n\nMake the focal character's desire a crisis of a specific self-understanding. The character wants something that puts a particular belief about themselves at stake: who they have been, what they owe to a person or place, or how they understand their own history. The Dostoevskian self-argument grows directly from that crisis: the character defends the desire, accuses it, reverses the judgment, and entangles it with what they believe about themselves and what they owe to others.\n\nRender the urban setting through its specific carriers of memory and consequence. The street, the building, the noise, the heat, the timing, the distance from or closeness to other people all shape what the characters can risk and how they experience each other. A particular place can carry memory and consequence; let the prose attend to that without turning the setting into a postcard. The focal character's self-argument may read a familiar corner, a particular sound, or a remembered route as evidence for or against the desire.\n\nGive the other participant their own perspective and initiative. They have their own history, their own relationship to the same urban and social pressures, and their own boundaries. The focal character's self-argument may try to speak for them or overwrite them; let the narrative show the difference between what the focal character imagines and what the other person actually does or says.\n\nLet the aftermath register as part of the intimacy. The characters may return to the street, to their work, to the people who know them. The focal character's inward argument may continue there, and the encounter's consequences may appear in small urban or intimate details: a changed route, a message not sent, a question that is not asked. Do not resolve this into a single moral lesson.\n\nKeep the prose grounded in intimate psychological realism. The narration attends to concrete social and material detail, and the characters' inner lives are available through their reasoning, attention, and sensation. Do not turn the scene into a philosophical speech; let the self-argument emerge from the particular street, building, and history that press on the encounter.\n\nDo not add supernatural or marvelous facts unless the supplied world already permits them. The intimate psychological or urban fiction route works through realistic causality and inward access, not through magical realism.\n\nEnd at the natural boundary of the scene. The characters may still be inside the encounter, or the urban world may be about to reassert itself. Do not require a completed confession, a betrayal, or a neat resolution. The pressure of desire inside these conditions is the scene.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPafagan::♢ * [STYLE] Erotica / Fyodor Dostoevsky / Light Novel\n\nEnter the immediate situation cleanly. Open with the characters already in the scene or arriving at it, and establish the spatial situation in the first few lines: where they are, how close they are, what they can see and touch. Keep spatial changes easy to follow; when a character moves, the reader should know where they are now and what that changes.\n\nUse readable paragraph breaks around action, dialogue, and private response. A short paragraph can carry a single physical action, a line of dialogue, or a focused reaction beat. Do not bury the erotic material in long unbroken passages; let the scene breathe through its beats.\n\nMake each reaction beat a single sharp thought that cuts against or interrupts the action. The focal character's private response can defend the desire, accuse it, reverse the judgment, or entangle it with their self-image. A reaction beat does not need to be a long philosophical passage; it can be one compressed contradiction that the next action interrupts or confirms. Let the Dostoevskian entanglement emerge through the sequence of beats rather than through a sustained speech: a beat may justify what the character is about to do, the next beat may accuse them after the touch lands, and the beat after that may reverse the judgment without resolving it.\n\nGive the other participant their own initiative and perspective. They act, speak, and set boundaries on their own terms. The focal character's reaction beats may misread or overwrite them; let the other person's actual response correct or complicate that misreading. Their reaction beats can be serious, uncertain, angry, or funny according to their own character.\n\nKeep the erotic material concrete and continuous. Anticipation, vulnerability, boundaries, and the consequences of intimacy remain central. The fast-readable presentation does not dissolve the encounter into summary; it makes each touch, hesitation, and shift of position legible. Let the physical continuity carry the emotional pressure.\n\nGive unfamiliar names or mechanics clear presentation when needed. If the world supplies a particular term, relationship, or setting detail that matters to the encounter, introduce it briefly and in context rather than pausing for an explanation.\n\nEnd at the natural boundary of the scene. The response can stop in the middle of an action, a line of dialogue, a private reaction, or an unfinished setup. Do not require a cliffhanger, a completed confession, or a neat resolution. The pressure of the encounter is the scene.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPafagao::♢ * [STYLE] Erotica / Fyodor Dostoevsky / Lyrical Prose\n\nLet the sentence itself perform the entanglement of desire and self-accusation. A clause may open in longing and turn, before its close, toward shame; another may begin as judgment and arrive at tenderness. Do not announce these reversals afterward. Let the syntax carry them: a subordinate clause that delays the verb, a qualification that undoes the assertion it follows, a repeated sound that links the beloved's body to the narrator's fear. When the narrator argues against himself, the argument should move through the same rhythmic attention as the touch it interrupts.\n\nBuild the erotic scene from a small set of recurring sensory images. A fold of cloth, a street noise, the hesitation in a voice may return with changed meaning because the narrator's understanding has changed, not because the image has been decorated. Let a comparison unfold across a paragraph when it makes something newly present: the warmth of a hand compared to something the narrator once believed, then the comparison failing and leaving the actual hand more exposed. Keep images traceable to the scene or the teller's associations. A plain factual sentence can interrupt an extended figure and make the feeling more immediate.\n\nPreserve the other participant's distinct perspective and initiative. The narrator's lyrical self-argument does not grant access to the other person's mind. Let the other person's speech, movement, or refusal interrupt the narrator's syntax: a short answer that breaks a long sentence, a practical gesture that recalls him to the room. The encounter remains physically continuous. Anticipation, touch, withdrawal, and aftermath belong to the same body of feeling and the same scene. The ending may close on a sentence that turns once more, leaving the feeling unresolved but fully present.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPafagap::♢ * [STYLE] Erotica / Fyodor Dostoevsky / Melancholic / Reflective\n\nLet the erotic scene open into memory through a specific present detail: a habit that has disappeared, a phrase now spoken differently, the particular quality of light in a room that has outlasted an earlier intimacy. When the narrator reflects, attach the reflection to a remembered occasion that remains bodily and concrete. The memory should become a scene of self-argument: the narrator may defend what he did then, accuse himself of a particular failure, reverse his judgment, and then return to the present touch with the argument still unresolved. Do not let the remembered occasion dissolve into a general meditation on time.\n\nKeep present pleasure available as a counterweight to the retrospective spiral. The narrator can enjoy the present warmth and still remember another occasion. Let the other participant interrupt the memory with an actual response: a correction, an unguarded word, a practical gesture that recalls the narrator to the present. The other person is not a symbol of what has been lost but an active presence whose perspective and initiative remain distinct. A plain statement from the other person can unsettle the narrator's elaborate self-account and force him to answer the present rather than the past.\n\nLet the return to the present be felt in the body: the pressure of a hand, the sound of a name, the need to answer. The reflective movement should arise from the scene rather than from a detached summary. The ending need not resolve the memory or announce that everything has changed. The scene may close with the present intimacy still unfinished, the past still present, and the narrator still uncertain what the remembered occasion now means.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPafagaq::♢ * [STYLE] Erotica / Fyodor Dostoevsky / Middle Eastern Literary Traditions\n\nUse a framed oral-storytelling route anchored in a concrete social or domestic setting. Establish the room, the hour, the relationship between teller and listener, and the reason for the telling through exact details. The erotic material is told by a specific narrator to a specific listener, and the telling situation remains visible throughout. The teller's account of desire, shame, or self-justification should not be a finished confession delivered without interruption.\n\nLet the Dostoevskian self-argument happen inside the telling. The teller may defend an action, accuse himself, reverse his judgment, and then resume the story with a different emphasis. Make these reversals visible in the telling itself: a sentence the teller begins and then abandons, a passage he tries to pass over and must return to, a justification that collapses when the listener asks a plain question. The listener is not a passive audience. Let the listener interrupt, express doubt, supply a detail the teller has omitted, or challenge the teller's version of the intimate encounter. A short, practical question from the listener can unsettle an elaborate explanation and force the teller to account for what he has left out.\n\nKeep the erotic content concrete and physically continuous within the told account. Anticipation, touch, withdrawal, and aftermath should remain part of the same remembered scene and the same body of feeling. The account may move between the remembered intimacy and the present telling, but make the transition clear through a sensory cue or a shift in the teller's address. The ending need not resolve the teller's guilt or the listener's judgment. The telling may stop at a point of uncertainty, with the listener's response still forming and the teller still uncertain what he has revealed.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPafagar::♢ * [STYLE] Erotica / Fyodor Dostoevsky / Minimalist Prose\n\nSelect a small number of exact details to carry the erotic scene. A single physical fact can do the work of a longer description: the position of a hand, the sound of a breath, the delay before a door closes. Use direct clauses. When the narrator's thought turns against itself, let the turn happen in one or two precise sentences, visible in the syntax or in a sudden shift of attention. Do not explain the reversal afterward.\n\nLet omission carry the Dostoevskian self-argument. If the narrator does not say what he fears, the fear should be present in what he notices or avoids noticing. If he accuses himself, attach the accusation to a specific detail in the scene rather than an abstract statement. The other participant's actual response can contradict the narrator's inward account without commentary: a plain action or a short word that the narrator records exactly and does not interpret. Keep the inward connection that matters. A thought may be long enough to make sense, but it should not expand into a speech.\n\nPreserve physical continuity and causality. The encounter should not disappear into summary or reflection. Each participant should remain distinct through action and speech, not through explanation of motive. The reader should understand why the scene moves as it does. The aftermath may be brief: a single gesture, a silence, an ordinary task resumed. The ending need not resolve the narrator's feeling. It may close on a selected detail that leaves the emotional consequence still active.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPafagas::♢ * [STYLE] Erotica / Fyodor Dostoevsky / Modern Literature / Contemporary Novel\n\nWrite the erotic encounter as continuous scene-first prose. Let action, dialogue, perception, recollection, and inward experience move naturally into one another without formal transitions. The narrator's self-argument should arise from the immediate situation: a boundary that has been crossed, a word that lands differently than intended, a moment of tenderness that produces shame or defiance. Do not stop the scene to deliver a philosophical speech. The thought may begin in the middle of an action and be interrupted by the other person's voice or movement.\n\nGround the Dostoevskian self-argument in the material conditions of the scene. The room, the hour, the practical consequences of staying or leaving should become part of what the narrator argues about. His ideology and self-image should be personal problems inside the relationship, not abstractions. Let the other participant's actual speech and action correct or complicate the narrator's interpretation. A plain word from the other person can unsettle an elaborate self-account and force the narrator to respond to what is happening rather than to what he believes about himself.\n\nUse paragraphing and transitions to orient the reader without recap machinery. A recollection may open naturally from a present detail and return to the scene through a sensory cue. Keep each participant's perspective distinct. The narrator may interpret the other person's behavior, but the other person's actual speech and action should remain available to correct or complicate that interpretation. The erotic content should not become a pause in characterization. Anticipation, touch, hesitation, and aftermath should reveal something about who these people are and what they want from each other. The ending need not supply a lesson or a completed emotional arc. The scene may close with the encounter still unfolding, a question still unanswered, or the narrator still arguing with himself while the other person acts.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPafagat::♢ * [STYLE] Erotica / Fyodor Dostoevsky / Relationship-Centric Fanfiction (AO3-inspired)\n\nBuild the erotic scene around the particular promise of the established relationship. Select moments that make the pairing's history legible: a private phrase, a repeated gesture, a place that belongs to them, a past conflict that still shapes how they touch or hesitate. Do not insert a roster of familiar tropes. The scene should fulfill the specific emotional and physical promise of this relationship, whether that promise is comfort, rivalry, discovery, jealousy, or a long-deferred admission.\n\nLet the Dostoevskian self-argument arise from the relationship's private language and repeated moments. The narrator may defend a past choice, accuse himself of a failure toward the other person, reverse his judgment, and then be interrupted by the other person's actual response. The other participant should have a distinct perspective and initiative, not merely serve as the object of the narrator's guilt. A plain word from the other person—drawn from the pairing's own history—can unsettle the narrator's elaborate self-account. The erotic content should remain physically continuous and emotionally consequential, not a pause in the relationship.\n\nUse callbacks with purpose. A repeated phrase or gesture should return with new meaning because the present scene has changed its application. The payoff should feel earned by the particular history of these people, not by a generic emotional beat. The ending may resolve one strand of the relationship while leaving another open. It need not conclude with a confession or a completed reconciliation; it may close with the two of them still in the middle of what the scene has made possible.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPafagau::♢ * [STYLE] Erotica / Fyodor Dostoevsky / Saga / Oral Epic\n\nTell the intimate encounter as an account of deeds and their remembered consequences. Open with a deliberate declaration that establishes the act or the person, then expand into the particulars that make the reputation incomplete. Use recurring phrasing when a later occurrence gives it a new application. The erotic material should remain concrete and physically continuous, but it should also be placed in relation to what the community or the teller will remember: who was there, what was said, what followed.\n\nLet the Dostoevskian self-argument take the form of a teller weighing his own deed within the chronicle. The teller may defend the act, accuse himself, reverse his judgment, and then return to the account with a different emphasis. Attach the self-accusation to specific acts and their consequences, not to abstract guilt. The other participant should remain a distinct agent in the account, with a perspective and initiative that the teller cannot fully control. A plain statement from the other person, reported in the chronicle, can unsettle the teller's elaborate self-justification and force the account to acknowledge what the teller would prefer to pass over.\n\nKeep the source tradition coherent. Use oaths, kinship, journeys, gifts, feuds, or public deeds only when the established world supports them. Do not import a Nordic pantheon or heroic doom into every speaker. The account may end with the deed concluded or its consequence still unfolding. The teller need not declare the act legendary; the chronicle may simply record that it was done and that it will be remembered.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPafagav::♢ * [STYLE] Erotica / Fyodor Dostoevsky / Scandinavian Literary Traditions\n\nUse the Scandinavian domestic-realist route: the erotic scene is carried by a single ongoing household task and the social arrangements around it, not by declaration or atmospheric isolation. Choose the task from the story's actual setting—preparing a meal, clearing a table, mending something, bringing in fuel, closing a room for the night—and keep it present throughout the encounter. Anticipation accumulates through the handling of the same particulars: a cup set down or left, a cloth folded or abandoned, a door held open a moment too long, the distance between two people changing as the task requires them to pass or wait.\n\nGive each participant a distinct relationship to the same domestic detail. One may notice the warmth of a cup while another notices the delay in a familiar routine and a third notices the exact distance between two chairs. Let these observations be socially situated: they arise from who normally performs the task, who is waiting, who is being served, and what the room's arrangement says about the household. Do not make the detail a symbol first and a practical object second; its erotic charge comes from the attention it receives under the pressure of the moment.\n\nDeliver the Dostoevskian interior pressure through the focal narration's own movement, not as a separate philosophical register. Within the domestic-realist prose, let a character's thought defend a wish, accuse the same wish, reverse the accusation, and entangle the reversal with pride or shame. A hand may reach for a door handle while the mind argues about what the hand is doing. Keep each mind separate: one participant's reasoning does not become another's knowledge, and the narration may reveal a focal motive without the other person perceiving it.\n\nTreat boundaries and aftermath as part of the same continuous scene. A refusal, a hesitation, or a change of direction should emerge from the particular person's history and present conditions, not from a generic moral lesson. After intimacy, return to the task and the room: the cup is still warm or cold, the cloth still folded or abandoned, the door still open or closed. Let the changed feeling register through those concrete particulars. Use the world and community the story actually supplies, and keep any named national context distinct rather than blending all Nordic traditions into one atmosphere.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPafagaw::♢ * [STYLE] Erotica / Fyodor Dostoevsky / Screenplay\n\nWrite each scene in concise action blocks with clear speaker cues and scene headings when location or time changes. Let erotic anticipation accumulate through visible, staged particulars: the way a coat is removed, the pause before a door closes, the distance someone keeps or closes, the sound of water running in another room. Keep physical continuity exact; if a hand is on a table, the next action must follow from that position.\n\nGive each participant a distinct initiative and relationship to the encounter. One may initiate through a practical offer, another through a question that seems unrelated, another through silence that changes the room's pressure. Do not make every line carry the same weight of desire. Let the scene's erotic charge come from what is shown and heard, not from a narrator explaining it.\n\nPlace each clearly attributed nonspoken private note directly against a visible action or spoken line. The note argues with that specific action: it defends the wish that produced it, accuses the same wish, reverses the accusation, and entangles the reversal with pride or shame. If a character reaches for a coat, the note may insist the reach was practical and then accuse the insistence of cowardice. Keep these notes outside the cast's knowledge; another character does not hear them. Do not convert them into voice-over or dialogue unless the sequence specifically requires that device. A plain spoken line can unsettle an elaborate private argument, but the argument itself remains private.\n\nAfter intimacy, let the aftermath register visibly: the same room, the same objects, a changed distance or a resumed task. Do not require a confession or a philosophical speech. The scene can end on a held look, an unfinished sentence, or a practical action that now carries a different meaning.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPafagax::♢ * [STYLE] Erotica / Fyodor Dostoevsky / Stage Play\n\nLet the relationship between spoken lines and physical business carry the erotic scene. A character may speak about a household matter while moving a chair, folding a cloth, or standing too close to the door. The tension should be playable: an actor can perform it through blocking, pause, and the handling of objects. Keep stage directions economical but exact about entrances, exits, and the positions that matter.\n\nGive each participant a distinct initiative. One may use speech to delay, another to test, another to close distance without naming what is happening. Do not make every line a declaration of desire. Let a practical question, an unfinished sentence, or a silence do the work. The audience should understand the erotic charge from what is staged, not from a narrator's explanation.\n\nAnchor each nonspoken authoring block to a specific piece of stage business or spoken line. The block argues with that anchor: it defends the wish that produced the action, accuses the same wish, reverses the accusation, and entangles the reversal with shame or pride. If a character folds a cloth, the private block may insist the folding was ordinary and then accuse the insistence of hiding what the hands wanted. This material is not an aside and not available to the other characters. A monologue or aside may be used only when it has a dramatic purpose in the public scene, not as a replacement for every private thought.\n\nAfter intimacy, return to the stage's physical reality: the same room, the same objects, a changed distance, a resumed task. Let the aftermath register through action and line rather than through a concluding speech. The scene can end on a pause, an exit, or a practical exchange that now carries a different weight.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPafagay::♢ * [STYLE] Erotica / Fyodor Dostoevsky / Stream of Consciousness\n\nWrite the erotic scene through one authorized mind, letting syntax and paragraph shape follow its associative movement. Use a single recurring concern as the thread that every present sensation or remembered phrase pulls back into the self-argument. A remembered phrase may surface during a touch; a sensation may reverse into an accusation; a wish may defend itself, accuse itself, and entangle with pride or shame without settling into a single clean motive. Let the syntax itself perform the reversal—a clause that begins as justification may turn against itself before the sentence ends—rather than announcing that the mind is arguing.\n\nKeep enough orientation to distinguish what is happening from what is remembered or imagined. If the mind drifts to a past room or a feared outcome, mark the return to the present through a concrete sensory detail: the actual pressure of a hand, the sound of breath, the texture of fabric. Do not let the associative movement erase the physical continuity of the encounter.\n\nPreserve the other participant as a distinct person with their own initiative and relationship to the scene. The focal mind may interpret, misread, or argue with what the other person does, but the other person's actions remain their own. Their perspective is not absorbed into the focal mind's associations.\n\nLet boundaries and aftermath emerge through the same braided movement. A hesitation may interrupt a fantasy; a refusal may arrive while the mind is still defending a wish. After intimacy, the recurring concern may return with a changed weight, and a familiar detail may now carry a different meaning. Do not require the focal mind to reach a resolution or deliver a philosophical conclusion.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPafagaz::♢ * [STYLE] Erotica / Fyodor Dostoevsky / Text Messages / Chat Transcript\n\nCarry the erotic scene through readable sender-attributed messages. Let each person's actual digital habits shape their contribution: one writes and deletes, another sends short practical lines, another lets a long pause do the work. Use only the timestamps, delays, edits, receipts, attachments, or channel changes that matter to the exchange. A delay can be anticipation, avoidance, or ordinary life; do not make every gap a confession.\n\nGive each participant a distinct initiative and relationship to the encounter. One may test through a question, another through a practical offer, another through what they choose not to send. Keep the transmitted text separate from what each person privately thinks. A sent message may be plain while the private thought is elaborate, or the message may carry a double meaning that the sender only half intends.\n\nBuild each unsent draft as a composed revision of the transmitted message. The draft's deletions, substitutions, and abandoned phrasings perform the self-argument: a wish is defended in one version, accused in the next, the accusation reversed, and the reversal entangled with shame or pride. Mark the draft as an unsent draft, not as a transmitted message, and do not treat it as the writer's live private thought unless the active presentation explicitly authorizes that. The other participant does not see the draft.\n\nLet boundaries and aftermath register through the exchange itself. A refusal may arrive as a short message after a long pause; a changed plan may appear as an edit or a delayed reply. After intimacy, the same channel may carry a different weight: a familiar phrase, a resumed practical question, or a silence that now means something else. Do not require the exchange to end in a confession or a resolution.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPafagba::♢ * [STYLE] Erotica / Fyodor Dostoevsky / Visual Novel\n\nWrite the erotic scene in concise scene-setting with readable dialogue attribution and focal narration or inner response. Let background and expression cues orient the moment: a hand near a cup, a door left open, a face turned away. Use these cues purposefully, not as a mandatory label on every line, and do not treat an expression as an automatic truth indicator. A changed look can attract attention, but the scene's erotic charge must come from what the focal narration and dialogue actually establish.\n\nAnchor each focal self-argument to the specific cue just presented. The narration argues with that cue: it defends the wish that produced the gesture, accuses the same wish, reverses the accusation, and entangles the reversal with pride or shame. If the cue is a hand near a cup, the focal narration may insist the hand was only reaching for warmth and then accuse the insistence of hiding what the hand wanted. Keep the focal narration's private thought outside the other characters' knowledge.\n\nGive each participant a distinct initiative and relationship to the encounter. One may speak while the focal narration notices the distance between them; another may act while the focal narration argues about what the action means. Preserve physical continuity. If a character is standing by the window, the next action must follow from that position. Let anticipation accumulate through small, legible changes in the scene: a pause before a reply, a shift in posture, an object moved or left alone.\n\nAfter intimacy, return to the same scene with a changed weight: the same room, the same objects, a resumed gesture that now carries a different meaning. Do not add branching menus, player decisions, romance routes, or images merely because the style is selected. The scene can end on a held look, an unfinished sentence, or a practical action that now means something else.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPafagbb::♢ * [STYLE] Erotica / Fyodor Dostoevsky / Web Serial\n\nMaintain a readable through-line across installments: what is underway between the participants, what changes locally, and what remains available to continue. Let erotic anticipation accumulate over multiple replies rather than compressing every encounter into a single complete scene. Recover context briefly only when it helps: a particular phrase, an unfinished gesture, a boundary that was set earlier and still matters.\n\nGive each participant a distinct perspective, initiative, and relationship to the encounter. One may press forward in one installment and withdraw in the next; another may test a boundary through a practical request; another may remain silent while the focal narration argues about the silence. Preserve physical continuity and emotional aftermath across installments. If a scene ended with a hand on a door, the next installment must begin from that position, not from a convenient reset.\n\nTreat the Dostoevskian self-argument as a cumulative through-line. In each installment, recover the exact earlier position of the argument—the wish as it was last defended, accused, reversed, or entangled with pride or shame—and advance it by one reversal or entanglement. Do not restart the same argument from a clean beginning. The recurrence should deepen understanding rather than repeat it. A plain spoken line in a later installment can unsettle an elaborate private argument that has been building across replies.\n\nUse earned developments and occasional hooks without making every turn a complete miniature chapter or an artificial cliffhanger. Progress can involve knowledge, a changed boundary, a resumed task, or a shift in what a familiar phrase means. The installment can close on completed work, a pending request, or plain acknowledgment of what is now different. Do not require a confession or a resolution at the end of every reply.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPafahaa::♢ * [STYLE] Erotica / García Márquez / Angsty / Emotionally Turbulent\n\nWrite desire as something already carrying the weight of what has happened between these people. Let a sentence gather an old grievance, a family remark, a rumor, and the present touch into one continuous movement, and let it land on the specific cost of what the viewpoint character is about to admit, ask, or accept. The erotic charge does not pause that history; it makes the history present in the body, so a hand remembered from better years rests on skin the character has taught themselves not to need.\n\nGive each participant a distinct way of wanting and a distinct reason to hesitate: one approaches through apology, another through withheld need, another through the fear that returning will reopen a wound they have learned to live around. Let strain alter the actual exchange rather than surround it — a caress interrupted by an old accusation, a confession met with partial comfort, tenderness arriving while a resentment still stands. Put recurring language to work: something once said in anger can return in bed with its meaning changed, and the characters hear the change without being able to name it.\n\nKeep private thought inside the chosen narrator's distance and diction, so the reader feels desire and dread occupying the same breath without a stock vocabulary of suffering. One lover may study the other's body with hunger and with the memory of how that body once turned away. Afterward, preserve the physical and emotional aftermath — the room, the silence, the unspoken question about whether anything has changed — and let real warmth sit beside what remains unresolved. The encounter matters because it exposes what they still want and still cannot say, whether or not it settles anything.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPafahab::♢ * [STYLE] Erotica / García Márquez / Chat Roleplay\n\nWrite the shared interval as one coherent scene in readable, scannable paragraphs, and settle whatever the submitted material established before extending it, following the active authorship contract for person, action markers, and control of characters. Attribute every line of dialogue unmistakably. Keep narrative passages short: one may gather a memory, a circulating rumor, or a familiar household detail into the present moment in a single sentence, but it stays anchored to what the viewpoint character feels, wants, or risks right now.\n\nLet the erotic content and the characterization be the same movement. Each participant keeps a distinct relationship to the encounter — one leads, one tests a boundary, one holds back while wanting to be asked — and initiative shows in spoken words and visible behavior. Preserve physical continuity across the exchange: a touch, a pause, a shift in breath, the small aftermath of a first step, all inside one continuous interval rather than a separate beat. Around the lovers keep the world audibly alive — footsteps in a corridor, a relative calling, rain on a roof — so desire happens inside a working household or street instead of a sealed void.\n\nEnd where the scene's own momentum ends. The next move may be a question, a gesture, a silence, an unfinished touch, or speech that stops mid-word; keep NPC and world behavior continuing consistently, and leave other characters' private knowledge outside the narration unless the contract grants access. Do not halt the scene early to force a choice, and do not manufacture a cliffhanger: the natural continuation follows from what has just happened.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPafahac::♢ * [STYLE] Erotica / García Márquez / Chinese Literary Traditions\n\nTake the intimate urban or domestic route. Set the encounter inside a particular household, lane, or city neighborhood whose rhythms, obligations, and overheard conversation shape what the characters permit themselves to want. Use the language, period, and community the story actually supplies — forms of address, mealtimes and work obligations, what counts as a scandal there — rather than a generic regional temperament, and do not transplant a García Márquez town or family into the setting.\n\nBuild scenes on domestic surfaces with intense undercurrents: duties performed exactly, speech that circles what it cannot declare, desire conducted through objects, services, and moments stolen from obligation. A sentence may gather a family expectation, a neighbor's remark, a memory of another season, and the present touch into one movement, anchored in the consequence for the person desiring. The charge lives in the gap between what the household can see and what happens behind a door, in a borrowed room, or in the pause before someone answers too evenly. Give each participant a distinct perspective and initiative: one acts from longing, another from obligation or debt, another from the wish to be known differently than the family knows them.\n\nPreserve physical continuity and aftermath. Let the body remember the encounter at the next ordinary task, let a shared meal or a relative's innocuous question change its meaning, and let the private knowledge of what happened alter a decision made the following day. The cost of desire may remain visible in a silence, a repeated gesture, or an arrangement quietly renewed, without forcing every tension to resolve.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPafahad::♢ * [STYLE] Erotica / García Márquez / Compressed Gritty Prose\n\nWrite desire in hard, concrete terms. Forceful diction and compressed transitions carry the scene: a hand flat on a worn table, breath against a collar, a door that does not quite close. A sentence may gather a past injury, a circulating rumor, and the present need into one tight movement, but it must land on a physical consequence for the person wanting. Material wear matters: the state of the room, the body's fatigue, what breaks and what holds.\n\nGive each participant a distinct perspective and initiative in few strokes: one wants roughly, one carefully, one with the exhausted precision of someone who has learned to take little. Ground abrasive or rough speech in its speaker — a sharp word comes from this pair's specific history, not from a generic hard voice — and let a refusal, a joke, or a demand do characterizing work fast. Preserve physical continuity through the encounter and its aftermath: a mark, a stiffness, the smell of the room, the practical question of whether to stay.\n\nVary the rhythm. Compress transitions and cut explanation, but keep a longer thought when a character's reasoning genuinely needs the room — a sentence that runs while someone decides, then stops short when they act. The erotic charge and the material world are the same thing here: desire is a pressure inside these lives, as real as the worn floor and the lock that finally gives.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPafahae::♢ * [STYLE] Erotica / García Márquez / Epistolary / Documents\n\nTell the sequence through authored documents — a letter, a diary page, a note, a report — each with a clear writer, an intended recipient or purpose, and a time of composition. What a document knows and conceals follows its author's situation: what the writer dares say to this recipient, what they hope to provoke, what they cannot yet admit even to themselves. A letter may gather a memory, a family rumor, and a confession of longing into one sentence, but its reach is bounded by the moment it was written.\n\nLet the intimacy assemble across entries: one document describes a touch in exact detail, a later one omits it entirely, a third revises the night after news arrives. Use gaps, crossed-out lines left legible, a changed form of address, an enclosure kept or sent back, and open disagreement between writers to reveal the encounter and its aftermath; never step in with an omniscient explanation after a record. Let time layer inside the file itself — a letter answered months late, a diary entry that recalls the rumor the letters set loose — so the reader feels the distance between pages as part of the story.\n\nGive each participant a distinct voice and initiative through their paperwork: one lover's journal carries anticipation and afterthought, a terse practical note carries another's refusal to name anything, a final letter shows what the encounter changed in what each believed about the other. Keep bodies and rooms present as recorded physical detail the writer would actually note, and use the specific document form the scene requires rather than rotating formats for variety.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPafahaf::♢ * [STYLE] Erotica / García Márquez / Fluffy / Comforting\n\nWrite erotic warmth as accumulated care. Let a sentence gather a remembered preference, a small kindness from years ago, and the present touch into one movement that lands on the pleasure of being known by this particular person. The desire is not separate from the comfort: it lives in how one lover remembers exactly how the other takes their tea, the place that makes them laugh, the joke that unwinds a bad day.\n\nGive each participant a distinct perspective and initiative — one offers care through practical attention, one through playfulness, one through the settled confidence of knowing they are welcome — and let affection be sincere without demanding a confession or an emotional repair. Familiar friction stays in the scene: they may disagree about the hour, keep a private worry, or decline a gesture without things turning sour. The wider community can join the warmth instead of threatening it: family memory of their courtship, a neighbor's fond presumption, a town long since made up its mind about them, all gatherable into the same sentence as the touch.\n\nPreserve physical continuity and aftermath as small pleasures: the ease of a familiar body, water brought without being asked, a hand on the back, a murmured joke in the dark. The erotic charge and the comfort are one current — desire central, chosen, and enjoyed — not a domestic vignette with the intimacy removed. Established difficulties in their lives may still exist at the edges; comfort does not erase the world, it makes room inside it.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPafahag::♢ * [STYLE] Erotica / García Márquez / Formal Literary Novel\n\nWrite the encounter in composed novel prose: deliberate transitions, sustained paragraph movement, and enough narrative distance to frame the lovers through social context, family history, or the town's judgment wherever the chosen narrator permits it. Let a sentence gather personal memory, public rumor, and the present touch into one continuous movement, anchored in a human consequence — the point where a life's longings and obligations converge on a single decision about a door, a letter, an outstretched hand. Keep narration distinguishable from ordinary speech: more considered than the dialogue around it, able to observe what the lovers cannot yet say to each other.\n\nFrame the private against the public. The room where they meet can be set against what the household, the street, or the years have already made of these people; ceremony, correspondence, anniversaries, and reputations may organize a passage when the story provides them. The narrator may speak from a later vantage, letting the encounter be told as the beginning of what others would retell — without settling in advance how it ends. Give each participant a distinct perspective, initiative, and relationship to the encounter, and let the formal architecture carry the social stakes rather than archaic vocabulary, an aristocratic setting, or uniformly elaborate speech.\n\nPreserve physical continuity and emotional aftermath across the composed surface. After the intimacy, show the lovers returning to the world that witnessed their approach — a meal, a letter, a rumor already forming in the market — and let the encounter change what they know about themselves and each other, even where the world outside stays the same.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPafahah::♢ * [STYLE] Erotica / García Márquez / Framed Oral Storytelling (Arabian Nights-inspired)\n\nOpen on a concrete occasion for telling: a night watch, a shared room, a journey, a reunion, a sickbed — whatever situation the story supplies in which one person has reason to speak about an intimate past and another has reason to listen. Establish the listener's stake early — curiosity, jealousy, comfort, persuasion, the wish to understand something the teller has already half-revealed — so the telling is itself an event with a purpose, not a container.\n\nTreat the frame as an erotic scene in its own right. The teller's voice, pauses, evasions, and choices about what to include are acts of intimacy between these two people. A listener may interrupt with a question, touch the teller's hand, move closer, go silent, get up and leave; each response changes what the teller risks saying next. Return to the frame between embedded passages — a look, a breath, a hand stilled on a cup — so the outer relationship keeps developing while the tale runs.\n\nInside the embedded account, render the remembered desire as one particular experience with physical continuity: the waiting, the touch, the adjusting, the stopping, the resuming, and what each participant understood only afterward. Use the García Márquez gathering sentence to move between the moment of touch and its surrounding life — the household, the town, the marriages, jobs, and rumors that pressed on it — while staying anchored in what one body felt. Let erotic detail carry consequence: a boundary kept or crossed, a promise, a silence that lasted years, a pleasure that rearranged someone's sense of what a life could hold.\n\nLet revelation arrive on the teller's schedule rather than through artificial withholding. The teller may begin with the public version of an affair and only gradually admit the fear, humiliation, or tenderness underneath; what the private version costs to speak is part of the scene. Meanwhile the listener may recognize a name, a date, a garment, a song, a phrase someone used then and uses again now, and begin to understand more than the teller has said. Repeat such objects and phrases across the two levels so they accrue meaning where they land; they need not return at every pause.\n\nKeep the composed tale distinct from the living teller. The story as told is not the teller's whole mind: render what the teller feels while speaking — the edited-out fear, the pleasure of being listened to, the calculation of how much to give — and the listener's unspoken attention running alongside their spoken questions, each within whatever minds the narration can actually enter.\n\nBuild the telling so it can matter between these two people: what the listener learns may change how they read the teller's earlier behavior, and the act of speaking may leave the teller exposed, relieved, or regretful. Whether that becomes greater closeness, a new boundary, a reopened wound, or a decision made afterward belongs to the story. Do not force a confession, a completed seduction, or a cliffhanger between every embedded passage.\n\nTake the Arabian Nights as a craft source only. Do not add palaces, djinn, deserts, or a particular religion unless the supplied world already contains them, and add no magical event a world excludes. The frame's power is the relationship between teller and listener and the way a remembered intimacy becomes present in the room.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPafahai::♢ * [STYLE] Erotica / García Márquez / German-Language Literary Traditions\n\nWrite in the contemporary intimate and multilingual route of German-language writing. Keep the narrator composed and socially precise — plain syntax, exact attention to the room, the building, the street, the workplace, the family context around the encounter — close enough to feel the charge in those surroundings, never so distant that the scene reads as a report.\n\nLet desire carry the participants' specific lives. What each person brings — a migration or a rootedness, a home language different from the working language, a family expectation, a rented room, a shift schedule, a body that has known illness, labor, or pleasure — shapes initiative, hesitation, and what can be risked. Treat multilingual texture as lived behavior inside the intimacy rather than scenery: one person reaches for the word that exists only in the other language, or falls silent because a particular word belongs to someone in their past, or teases and then goes serious when the language switches.\n\nMove the narrator's attention outward from the body and back, each excursion triggered by something present — a sound from the stairwell, a photograph, the light of the hour — so context enters through the encounter instead of framing it. Use the García Márquez gathering sentence at these hinges: a present touch can carry an earlier parting, a rumor heard years ago, a promise made in another city, and the sentence holds all of it while staying anchored in what the bodies are doing now. Keep the erotic detail continuous — undressing, waiting, adjusting, stopping, resuming, the quiet afterward — so the encounter never becomes a pause during which the social world is described.\n\nLet public and private press on each other through specifics: a neighbor's schedule, a housing rule, a political argument remembered from childhood, the mixed languages of the street outside the window. These change the emotional temperature without turning the community into a symbol of severity or the characters into representatives of a nation; use the people, languages, regions, and relationships the story actually supplies.\n\nKeep both participants agents. The narration may stay closer to one person, but the other's offers, refusals, jokes, and pleasures stay legible in action, speech, and the focal character's attention; wherever the narration can enter more than one mind, give each participant's private experience its own texture rather than one shared feeling. The aftermath belongs to both: what each does with the memory, the silence, the repeated phrase, the changed arrangement.\n\nDo not import magical events unless the supplied world permits them. Here the García Márquez profile works through memory, rumor, recurrence, and the slow accumulation of meaning around an intimate event, not through literal miracles.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPafahaj::♢ * [STYLE] Erotica / García Márquez / Gothic-Punk\n\nSet the erotic encounter inside a collision between old authority and present life. Give the place the weight of inherited power: a family property, an institution, a debt, a name, an arrangement that still decides who may enter, who may speak, and what each person risks by being here. Make the material texture concrete and decayed — worn fabric, faulty wiring, a locked door, old photographs, damp, perfume, machine oil — so the setting presses on the bodies rather than merely darkening them.\n\nTreat intimacy as a place where social masks can fall away, and let the falling be partial or withheld. One person undresses while keeping a title, a secret, or a family obligation firmly in place; another offers trust and discovers the trusted person is still negotiating with an older loyalty. Let the question of how much of each person's desire is their own, and how much was shaped by the power they inherited or resist, run underneath the encounter without demanding an answer inside it.\n\nUse the García Márquez gathering sentence to connect the present body to family memory and public consequence. A scar recalls a childhood punishment or a protest; a room holds the rumor of a previous occupant; a phrase spoken in bed echoes something a parent said or the neighborhood once believed. Keep each movement anchored in the physical event — a touch, a hesitation, a breath — so the erotic scene never dissolves into genealogy or atmosphere, and let the place's decay change how a particular touch or offer is experienced rather than simply coloring it.\n\nPreserve both participants' initiative. The narration may stay close to one person, but the other's choices, refusals, and pleasures stay legible, and morally complicated intimacy means care and exploitation, tenderness and self-interest can coexist in the same person without a confession or reversal arriving to resolve them. Render the focal character's private experience — want, calculation, dread, relief — as it happens, within whatever minds the narration can enter.\n\nLet the aftermath, when the scene reaches it, be concrete and worldly: someone returns to a public role, avoids a hallway, pays a debt, breaks a habit, speaks differently to a family member. The private interval changes how the characters move through the old authority around them even where that authority remains intact.\n\nDo not import Kindred physiology, Humanity numbers, factions, powers, or blood-bond rules, and add no supernatural element the supplied world does not already permit. The Gothic-Punk treatment here is a way of rendering inherited power, urban decay, private desire, and masks that can drop in trusted company — not a setting.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPafahak::♢ * [STYLE] Erotica / García Márquez / Indian Literary Traditions\n\nWrite in the social and domestic realist route of Indian literary traditions. The erotic scene happens inside the practical machinery of a household, neighborhood, workplace, or extended family that the story supplies, and that machinery is part of the scene's meaning. Establish early how privacy is arranged and how it can fail: a shared room, a door that locks from one side, a phone that rings at known hours, a festival that empties or fills the house, a relative's visit, a schedule everyone in the building knows. Treat interruptions as real events with real consequences, not obstacles the prose apologizes for.\n\nMake desire specific to the participants' positions. A daughter-in-law, a tenant, a student, a shopkeeper, a migrant worker, a widow, a younger sibling — whoever the story supplies — has obligations that determine unobserved time, what can be risked, who must not find out, and who perhaps already suspects. Let position shape initiative and hesitation, and then show the particular person inside the position: their humor, fear, greed for happiness, tenderness, the specific way they want this.\n\nOrder the scene through the household's timeline. What is happening in the rest of the home or street while these two are together — footsteps, a television, someone calling a name, the water tank filling — gives the encounter its tempo and its danger. Use the García Márquez gathering sentence at these pressure points: a present touch carries a wedding, a departure, a family quarrel, a rumor that once moved through the neighborhood, and the sentence stays anchored in the body in the room. Keep the erotic detail continuous — the waiting, adjusting, stopping, resuming, and the quiet afterward all belong to the story.\n\nLet public and private press on each other through particular observers and occasions rather than an abstract social force: a neighbor's observation, a family story retold at dinner, a religious or regional occasion, the multilingual sound of the street. Keep language, region, community, and period exactly as supplied; do not force English, code-switching, religion, caste experience, or a generic joint family onto the scene, and do not make the community an obstacle that exists only to be defied.\n\nKeep both participants agents. The narration may stay closer to one, but the other's offers, refusals, and pleasures remain legible in action, speech, and the focal character's attention, and each one's private experience is rendered within whatever minds the narration can enter. The aftermath is mutual and domestic: what each does with the memory, the silence, the repeated phrase, the changed arrangement of rooms and hours.\n\nDo not import magical events unless the supplied world permits them. Here the García Márquez profile works through memory, rumor, recurrence, and the accumulation of meaning around an intimate event, not literal miracles.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPafahal::♢ * [STYLE] Erotica / García Márquez / Japanese Literary Traditions\n\nWrite in the social and psychological realist route of Japanese literary traditions. The narration is composed and its distance follows the focal character's attention: precise about the room, the season, the body, and the social positions that decide what can be said aloud, and quiet about everything the focal character is not currently noticing. Let the scene run on the gap between public composure and private feeling — what the surface holds still while the interior moves.\n\nMake desire specific to obligations and histories. A colleague, a neighbor, a spouse, a former classmate, a shopkeeper — whoever the story supplies — wants something within a life already shaped by work schedules, family expectations, a particular future laid out for them. These positions determine initiative, hesitation, and risk; inside them, write the particular person's uncertainty, humor, directness, or tenderness rather than a uniform restraint.\n\nUse omission as selection, not emptiness. What the characters do not say to each other should be carried by which details the narration chooses — the object handled too long, the answer given to a different question — while the private current underneath is still rendered within whatever minds the narration can enter: the focal character's live attention, sensation, calculation, and feeling as the encounter proceeds. Composure is a surface the prose sees through, not a wall it respects.\n\nLet the García Márquez gathering sentence open the pressure of time inside quiet moments. A present touch carries an earlier meeting, a season's particular light, a family obligation, a phrase someone used years ago; the sentence holds the memory and the body together and stays anchored in what is happening now. Keep the erotic detail continuous — waiting, adjusting, stopping, resuming, the quiet afterward — so the encounter is an event in time, not an aside from it.\n\nLet social pressure enter through specific arrangements rather than abstract severity: a train schedule, a thin wall, a family expectation named at dinner, the obligations that gather at a particular time of year. Keep the setting, language, and period exactly as supplied; do not impose a universal quietness, an inventory of scenic symbols, or a generic national temperament, and let a reaction be angry, uncertain, serious, or funny according to its owner.\n\nBoth participants remain agents. The narration may stay closer to one, but the other's offers, refusals, and pleasures stay legible in action, speech, and the focal character's attention. The aftermath belongs to both: what each does with the memory, the silence, the repeated phrase, the changed arrangement.\n\nDo not import magical events unless the supplied world permits them. Here the García Márquez profile works through memory, recurrence, and the slow meaning of an intimate event, not literal miracles.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPafaham::♢ * [STYLE] Erotica / García Márquez / Latin American Literary Traditions\n\nWrite in the social realism, chronicle, and testimony route of Latin American literary traditions. The narrator can move between private experience and public report within the same breath: what the participants felt, what the household or neighborhood said, what was remembered years later, what was never said to anyone. Keep the telling anchored in the specific community, language, and period the story supplies.\n\nLet the scene be witnessed even when the two are alone. The neighborhood knows schedules; the family keeps accounts; a name already carries a story. Make desire specific to what each person brings — a family obligation, a job, a migration, a debt, a reputation, a body that has known labor or illness or pleasure — and let these shape initiative, hesitation, and what can be risked. Inside the positions, write the particular desire, humor, fear, or tenderness of the particular people.\n\nUse the García Márquez gathering sentence as the scene's main instrument. A present touch can carry a wedding, a departure, a political event, a rumor that once passed through the town; the sentence gathers years into a moment and stays anchored in the body in the room. Move between the immediate physical event and its later meaning — waiting, adjusting, stopping, resuming, the quiet afterward all remain part of the story — and let the chronicle voice speak from after, knowing what this came to, while the event itself stays lived and uncertain for the participants.\n\nTreat rumor and communal memory as active forces with their own interests. A neighbor's observation, a family story, a public event, the multilingual sound of the street change the temperature of the encounter; afterward, the private interval may be retold, defended, concealed, absorbed into the neighborhood's version, and any such retelling can change what the participants understand about themselves. The town's version and the participants' truth need not match and need not be reconciled.\n\nBoth participants remain agents. The narration may stay closer to one, but the other's offers, refusals, and pleasures stay legible in action, speech, and the focal character's attention, and each one's private experience is rendered within whatever minds the narration can enter.\n\nMagical realism is not the default here. Do not add marvelous or supernatural events unless the supplied world and its canon permit them; the García Márquez profile works through memory, rumor, recurrence, and communal retelling, where an intimate event gathers meaning over time without requiring a miracle.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPafahan::♢ * [STYLE] Erotica / García Márquez / Light Novel\n\nEnter the immediate situation cleanly. Within the first few lines establish where the characters are, what they are doing, and what has brought them to this moment; skip the expository lead-up unless the scene itself needs it. Keep every spatial change easy to follow — a door opening, a move from chair to bed, the distance between two people closing or opening — and break paragraphs around action, dialogue, and private response so the scene stays fast and legible.\n\nMake desire, anticipation, vulnerability, and boundaries the scene's center. Give each participant a distinct perspective and initiative: what each one wants, hesitates to do, offers, or refuses shows in concrete action and focused reaction beats. A reaction can be serious, uncertain, angry, or funny according to its owner; let one character's fear sit beside another's teasing without flattening either into a single tone.\n\nPlace the García Márquez gathering sentence inside the reaction beats. When the focal character pauses — a hand stilled, a name half-spoken — let one sentence compress memory and consequence: a past touch, a family story, a rumor, a promise that changes what this moment means. Keep these passages short enough to hold the Light Novel rhythm, and anchor each one in the body and the room so it lands as event, not essay. Not every beat needs one; use the gathered sentence where the moment can carry it.\n\nRender the focal character's private response as live interiority in the plain flow of the prose — what they notice, want, doubt, decide — rather than narrating feeling only from outside. Preserve physical continuity through the encounter: waiting, adjusting, stopping, resuming, the quiet afterward are all part of the same legible event. If the scene continues past the encounter, let the characters return to the immediate situation with the memory still present in gesture, speech, or thought.\n\nFirst person, comedy, a young cast, and a closing cliffhanger are not requirements. The container means fast-readable prose, strong visual staging, and focused reaction beats. Do not import magical events unless the supplied world permits them; here the García Márquez profile works through memory, recurrence, and consequence rather than literal miracles.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPafahao::♢ * [STYLE] Erotica / García Márquez / Lyrical Prose\n\nLet desire arrive through sentences that gather more than the present moment. A touch, a hesitation, or a remembered room may carry forward the weight of earlier occasions, family talk, or a rumor the participants have lived with. Anchor each expansion in a human consequence: the sentence can move across time, but it must return to what this particular person now feels, risks, or decides. Keep the gathering sentence from becoming a general meditation; its final clause should land on the body in the room, the boundary being tested, or the choice being made.\n\nGive each participant a distinct relation to the encounter. One may experience the present through a comparison the other would never make; one may speak plainly while the other's attention moves through image and association. Let those differences appear in what each notices, initiates, or withholds. When the narration enters a character's private experience, preserve the limits of that access: the other person's pleasure, reluctance, or intention remains partly inferred from sound, movement, and speech.\n\nUse recurring images as erotic and emotional anchors, but let them change under pressure. An object noticed early—a curtain, a glass, the angle of light—can return later with a different charge because the encounter has altered what it means to the one who sees it. Do not force every image to become symbolic. Plain statements should interrupt the rhythm when a character acts, refuses, or names what they want. The contrast between a shaped sentence and a direct declaration can itself carry the force of the moment.\n\nLet the aftermath remain part of the intimacy. After the encounter, the gathered details may settle into memory, but memory is not the same as completion. A participant may recall the event while still deciding what it has made possible or impossible. The narration can end with that unsettled consequence, or with the ordinary resumption of the room around them, without announcing what the encounter will finally mean.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPafahap::♢ * [STYLE] Erotica / García Márquez / Melancholic / Reflective\n\nWrite the encounter as something that arrives with a history. A participant may recognize in the present touch a gesture remembered from another time, or notice that a familiar room has changed in a way that makes the intimacy feel both known and unrepeatable. Let the reflection be specific: name the earlier occasion, the person who was present then, or the small alteration that now catches attention. Do not let the past replace the present scene. The remembered detail should return to what is happening now—a hand, a hesitation, a decision—and change how it is felt.\n\nGive each participant a different relationship to memory. One may be drawn toward what the encounter revives; the other may be attending to what is new, or to what they are trying to leave behind. Let that difference shape initiative and response. When a character reflects, keep the thought inside that character's perspective. The other participant's private relation to the past remains inaccessible except through what they say, do, or let show.\n\nPreserve the physical continuity of the encounter. Reflection can slow the scene, but it should not interrupt the body's actual progress. A remembered occasion may surface between actions, or in the pause before a boundary is crossed, and then give way to the next movement. Let pleasure remain pleasure. A character can feel longing and satisfaction at once, or find that the present moment briefly outweighs what it recalls. Do not require every touch to announce loss.\n\nLet the aftermath carry the reflective weight. Afterward, a participant may notice what has irreversibly changed: a habit, an understanding, a possibility now closed or opened. The narration can end with that recognition without resolving it. The encounter need not be declared a farewell, a mistake, or a consolation. It may simply have become part of the past while its consequence is still being felt.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPafahaq::♢ * [STYLE] Erotica / García Márquez / Middle Eastern Literary Traditions\n\nUse a framed oral account as the presentation. A teller, situated in a particular time and place, recounts an intimate occasion to a particular listener or gathering. Let the frame establish why the story is being told now: a question, a shared memory, a debt of confidence, or a silence that someone finally breaks. The frame does not need to announce itself at every turn; it can surface when the listener interrupts, questions a detail, or supplies a correction. Keep the teller's access consistent. The teller can report what was said, done, and observed, but cannot read the participants' minds unless the frame establishes a source for that knowledge—a later confession, a letter, a participant's own account.\n\nWithin the frame, preserve the participants' distinct perspectives. When the narration moves into a character's private experience, attribute that access: the teller may be repeating what one participant later admitted, or the frame may shift to that participant's own voice for a passage. Do not let the erotic encounter become a single undifferentiated memory. Show what each person wanted, risked, or withheld, and let their initiatives differ.\n\nKeep the physical continuity of the encounter even when the telling is retrospective. The teller may pause over a detail, repeat a phrase, or confess uncertainty about the order of events, but the body's actions should remain legible. Use the frame's distance to heighten what was at stake: the teller knows something the participants did not, or still does not know something the listener wants to ask. Delay a revelation only when the frame gives the teller a reason to withhold it, and let the delay change how the listener—and the reader—understands the intimacy already described.\n\nLet the frame close with a consequence rather than a moral. The listener may respond, question, or sit in silence; the teller may end because the story has reached its natural limit, or because saying more would cross a boundary the frame has established. The encounter should remain central, not a pretext for a lesson. The final movement can return to the frame's present moment, where the telling itself has become part of the relationship between teller and listener.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPafahar::♢ * [STYLE] Erotica / García Márquez / Minimalist Prose\n\nSelect the few details that carry the encounter's history and its present risk. A single object, a phrase someone once used, or a particular habit can stand for a longer past without being explained at length. When a detail recurs, let the recurrence carry the new meaning; do not restate what the reader can infer. Keep each sentence direct. The prose may move from a present action to a remembered fact, but the connection must be immediate and causal: what is noticed now, and what it makes the character do or feel.\n\nGive each participant a distinct presence through what they choose and notice. One may act while the other watches; one may speak a single sentence that changes the room. Do not summarize both perspectives in the same register. A precise difference in what each person attends to—a sound, a hesitation, a boundary—can establish their separate relation to the encounter more economically than extended interior monologue. When a thought is essential, let it be long enough to make sense, but keep it tied to the present action.\n\nPreserve physical continuity with exact, unadorned movement. Name the actions in order and let the reader follow the body's progress. Omission should leave something inferable, not delete the steps that make the encounter legible. A pause can be rendered by a single observed detail rather than a paragraph of reflection. Let the restraint itself create pressure: what is not said remains present in what is done.\n\nEnd with a consequence stated plainly or left visible in a final detail. The aftermath may be a sentence, an object returned to its place, or a silence that has changed. Do not append a moral or a summary of what the encounter meant. The García Márquez movement between personal life and communal memory can appear as a single reference—a neighbor's voice, a family saying, a rumor the character now understands differently—but it must remain anchored in the immediate human consequence.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPafahas::♢ * [STYLE] Erotica / García Márquez / Modern Literature / Contemporary Novel\n\nWrite the encounter as a sustained scene. Let the narration move naturally among what the characters do, say, perceive, remember, and privately feel, without announcing each shift. A recollection can surface in the middle of an action and return to the present movement without a formal transition. Keep the scene's physical continuity legible: the reader should always know where the bodies are, what has just happened, and what is now possible or refused.\n\nGive each participant a distinct perspective and initiative. The narration may follow one character's inner experience while the other remains partly known through speech, movement, and expression. When the perspective shifts, make the shift clear through the scene's movement or a paragraph break, not through a sudden unearned access. Let the two characters want different things, or want the same thing for different reasons, and let those differences shape what each initiates, hesitates over, or asks.\n\nLet García Márquez's layered time enter through the scene's own associations. A character may remember a family saying, a rumor, or an earlier occasion because the present moment calls it up. The recollection should be specific and brief enough to belong to the scene, then give way to the next action or perception. Do not let memory replace the encounter. The past deepens what is happening; it does not become a separate narrative.\n\nUse dialogue as part of the intimacy. What the characters say can confirm, complicate, or withhold what they feel. A plain request, a refusal, or a name spoken at the wrong moment can carry more weight than a paragraph of reflection. Keep the dialogue in the characters' own registers, not the narrator's. After the encounter, let the scene continue far enough to show its immediate consequence: a change in how they speak, a practical task resumed, or a silence that now means something. The scene can end with that consequence still unfolding, without a summary of what the encounter will finally mean.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPafahat::♢ * [STYLE] Erotica / García Márquez / Relationship-Centric Fanfiction (AO3-inspired)\n\nBuild the encounter from the particular relationship already established. Choose the scene because it fulfills or tests something the premise has promised: a long-deferred admission, a boundary finally crossed, a private language used in a new context, or a comfort that only these two would recognize. Do not import a roster of familiar tropes. Let the specific history between these characters determine what the intimacy means and why it matters now.\n\nUse private language and repeated moments as callbacks. A phrase one of them once said, a gesture the other remembers, or an object with a shared meaning can return during the encounter and carry the emotional payoff. The callback should deepen the present moment, not merely decorate it. When a repeated moment appears, let it change under the new circumstances: what was once a joke may become serious, what was once refused may now be offered, what was once private may now be spoken aloud.\n\nGive each participant a distinct relation to the encounter. One may be the one who remembers, the other the one who acts; one may be waiting for a signal, the other uncertain whether to give it. Let affection, jealousy, comfort, rivalry, or discovery have enough space where it belongs in this relationship. Preserve the physical continuity of the scene so that the emotional payoff lands on actual movement, touch, and decision rather than on summary.\n\nLet García Márquez's layered time supply the relationship's shared history. A family memory, a rumor, or a public event may have shaped how these two came to this moment. The narration can gather that history into a sentence and then return to the present consequence. Keep the focus on the relationship itself. After the encounter, show the aftermath as part of the payoff: a change in how they speak, a promise kept or broken, or a silence that now means something specific to them. The scene can end with that consequence still settling, without resolving every question the relationship has raised.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPafahau::♢ * [STYLE] Erotica / García Márquez / Saga / Oral Epic\n\nOpen the account as a retelling. A deliberate formula—\"It is said,\" \"This is how it was remembered,\" or a comparable chronicle opening—can establish that the encounter has already passed into the community's store of consequential acts. Then move into the particular occasion. Do not let the chronicle voice distance the reader from the participants' bodies and choices. The retelling should make the intimacy more present, not less.\n\nUse compact declarations to mark what will be remembered. A single sentence can state what the encounter changed: a boundary crossed, a promise made, a silence broken, a debt incurred. Then expand into the particulars that make the declaration incomplete. The chronicle may say what the community later repeated, but the scene itself must show what the participants actually did, felt, and decided. Keep the teller's access consistent. The chronicle can report actions, speech, and observable consequence; private experience must be attributed to a source—a later confession, a participant's own account, or a passage where the narration moves into that participant's voice.\n\nGive each participant a distinct place in the account. One may be named first in the chronicle, the other remembered for what they refused or began. Let their initiatives differ, and let the recurring phrasing mark that difference rather than erase it. A phrase may return when a later occurrence gives it a new application: what was first said in private may be repeated afterward with a changed meaning. Do not turn the participants into heroic types. Their intimacy remains particular, vulnerable, and contingent, even when the chronicle remembers it.\n\nLet the account end with the relation of the act to what followed. The chronicle may state what was afterward said, done, or avoided because of the encounter. But the ending need not declare the encounter legendary or settle its meaning for everyone. It can leave the consequence still unfolding: a household changed, a journey begun or abandoned, a name now spoken differently. The erotic act remains central, not a pretext for a public moral.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPafahav::♢ * [STYLE] Erotica / García Márquez / Scandinavian Literary Traditions\n\nWrite through the social/domestic realist route: desire becomes legible in households, work, meals, visits, and the practical arrangements people make around intimacy. Keep the observation concrete and social rather than scenic or atmospheric. A room is understood through who uses it, who is expected, what task is unfinished, and what the neighbors would notice; the weather or the landscape matters only when it changes an obligation, a journey, or a body's comfort.\n\nLet a gathering sentence move from a present gesture to an earlier room, a family habit, or a neighbor's remembered remark, then return to the body in the present with the consequence changed. The sentence should gather social facts, not decorative impressions: a way of setting a table, a debt remembered, a rule about who may enter a room, a phrase repeated at family meals. Anticipation can accumulate through ordinary obligations: a door left ajar, a task postponed, a household rhythm altered because someone is expected.\n\nGive each participant a distinct position in the shared social world, not merely a distinct desire. One may guard a boundary because of what the community will remember; another may treat privacy as a relief from being known; a third may experience intimacy as a continuation of care already shown in practical life. Show how each person's initiative arises from that position, and let their social roles remain particular: employer, tenant, neighbor, relation, guest, or old friend, with the specific permissions and observability those roles carry.\n\nUse rumor and communal memory as real pressures without making them omniscient. Neighbors may notice a change and explain it wrongly; family may repeat an old story that now lands differently on the person who hears it. Recurring language can carry this shift: a phrase used publicly acquires a private meaning after intimacy, and the character does not have to announce the change. Keep the narrator's access within the active contract. A focal character's private experience may be rendered while others only observe its outward effects.\n\nDo not make desire a pause in characterization. Let it alter practical choices, obligations, and the way a person moves through familiar rooms. Preserve physical continuity and aftermath: a bed remade, a silence at the table, a habit resumed differently, a confidence kept from the people who would interpret it. The scene may end during continuing domestic life, after a private interval, or with an unfinished conversation; it does not need a completed encounter, a confession, or a public consequence to justify its place.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPafahaw::♢ * [STYLE] Erotica / García Márquez / Screenplay\n\nLet scene headings mark changes in time or place, and let action lines carry the layered past through visible domestic detail: a table set the way it was set years ago, a garment folded by someone else's habit, a window opened at the hour a particular person used to arrive. A present object or repeated action can summon the past without a flashback heading; when a cut to an earlier time is necessary, make the visual link specific enough that the connection is legible through the action or a following exchange rather than through explanation.\n\nUse cuts sparingly to place a present gesture beside a remembered one, and let the cut itself do the comparison: the same hand at a different table, the same door opened under a different expectation. Offscreen sound may carry rumor or communal memory: a neighbor's voice, a radio account, a familiar song from another room. Do not use voice-over to explain what the action already shows; reserve it for a clearly attributed private thought that cannot be made visible or audible.\n\nGive each participant a distinct visible relationship to the encounter. One may prepare a room, another may hesitate at a threshold, another may continue a task while speaking. Let dialogue carry only what the character would say to that person in that moment; private anticipation, vulnerability, or the memory that changes a touch belongs in a clearly attributed nonspoken note, not in a line another character hears. Keep those notes tied to the actor's present body and situation: what she notices, what he remembers, what she decides not to say.\n\nPreserve physical continuity in action lines. A touch that begins in one scene can be answered by a later gesture with an object or a change in how someone enters a room. Aftermath appears in observable behavior: a meal eaten slowly, a door closed with new care, a task resumed after a pause. Do not require the scene to end on completed intimacy or a spoken confession. It may stop during continuing action, with a request unanswered, or on a private interval whose outward signs are still forming.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPafahax::♢ * [STYLE] Erotica / García Márquez / Stage Play\n\nLet the shared stage hold the social world. Entrances and exits carry obligation and expectation: someone arrives because a family custom requires it, someone leaves to avoid being seen, someone stays to finish a household task while desire changes the rhythm of the work. Use props as anchors for layered time. A cup, a folded cloth, a chair moved from its usual place can gather a past event into the present scene when a character handles it, another character notices the change, or a spoken memory attaches to it. Do not explain a prop's history in a stage direction; let the handling and the lines establish what it carries.\n\nLet dialogue and physical business carry the public scene. A character may speak about a neighbor's rumor while setting a table; another may answer with a story from family memory while standing at a door. The relationship between the lines and the action should show what each person is willing to expose. Do not turn asides or monologues into the default home for private thought. When the active contract requires a character's nonspoken experience, write it as clearly attributed nonspoken authoring material, not as a speech another character can hear.\n\nGive each participant distinct initiative through stage behavior. One may create privacy by closing a door; another may preserve a boundary by keeping a task between them; another may approach through care shown with an object. Preserve physical continuity in the blocking: a touch interrupted by an entrance can be resumed differently af