who is now in the room.\n\nThe scene may end while the tale is unfinished, with the teller still choosing what to reveal, so long as the interruption or delay is an intimate event rather than a mere cutoff. Do not require a cliffhanger, a completed seduction, or a symmetrical exchange of stories; the telling's consequence may be a changed understanding that continues beyond the response.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPafalai::♢ * [STYLE] Erotica / Jin Yong / German-Language Literary Traditions\n\nWrite in the route of contemporary intimate realism grounded in social observation. Treat the encounter as part of an ongoing life shaped by work, housing, family expectations, money, and the small negotiations of daily routine. Let the physical setting carry precise material weight: the condition of a room, the timing of a shift, the presence of a shared or borrowed space, the practical arrangements that make privacy possible or difficult. Do not aestheticize these details into atmosphere alone; show how they create the conditions under which the characters can speak, touch, or withdraw.\n\nUse the narrator's distance and syntax to render the erotic scene as a particular body in a particular room. If the narration is closely focalized, move between external detail and private thought without announcing the shift: a hand on a sleeve, the awareness of time, the memory of the person who might come home. If the narration is more detached, keep the characters' inner lives available through what they do and say, their hesitations, and the narrator's precise account of what they choose not to say. In either case, preserve physical continuity so that a gesture, a pause, or a moment of discomfort remains part of the same embodied scene rather than being skipped for a smooth emotional beat.\n\nMake the practical arrangements function as erotic constraints, not merely as background. The borrowed room, the door that does not lock, the shift that ends too soon, the obligation waiting afterwa