e it.\n\nGive each participant a distinct relationship to the past. One may remember more, one may resist remembering, one may notice a change the other has already accepted. Let that difference shape initiative and response: the one who remembers may pause, the one who resists may move faster, the one who has accepted may offer a correction or a reassurance. The public and private tension from the author profile can appear as a remembered obligation, a name that cannot be spoken here, or a duty that made an earlier parting necessary.\n\nKeep the erotic event itself present and continuous. Anticipation, vulnerability, and physical aftermath should be written as current experience, not dissolved into nostalgia. After the encounter, allow the reflection to settle without forcing a conclusion. The characters may notice what has changed and what has not, and the scene may end with that knowledge still unfinished.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPafalaq::♢ * [STYLE] Erotica / Jin Yong / Middle Eastern Literary Traditions\n\nUse a situated oral frame as the governing presentation. A storyteller addresses a particular audience and chooses what to tell, what to delay, and what to explain. The frame may open the scene, return briefly at a moment of heightened tension, or close it, but the intimate encounter remains the center of the telling. Let the storyteller's selection create meaning: a detail withheld until after the event, a phrase repeated when the audience least expects it, an explanation offered only when a listener asks. The frame is not a moral lecture or a rumor. The teller may know the participants, may have been one of them, or may be reconstructing an account from what was later said.\n\nMake the teller's access control the rendering of private experience. If the storyteller was one of the participants, write that participant's anticipation, hesitation, and choice as direct interiority while the other's inner state remains available only through what the teller observ