appear in the way a character reads the other's body, controls a movement, or chooses to set control aside. After the encounter, let the consequence remain particular: a promise, a silence, a practical decision, or a changed understanding that the scene has earned.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPafalau::♢ * [STYLE] Erotica / Jin Yong / Saga / Oral Epic\n\nTell the intimate encounter as an account of a deed that will be remembered. Open with a deliberate declaration or a compact statement of who the people are and what passed between them, then expand into the particulars that make the reputation incomplete. Let the opening declaration establish the deed's public shape—what the community will later say—while the scene itself supplies the private particulars that complicate that shape. Keep the chronicle's access consistent with the selected perspective. If the teller was not present, the private experience must come from what was later confided, observed, or plausibly known, not from omniscient intrusion.\n\nUse recurring phrasing when a later occurrence gives it new application. A name, a promise, a gesture, or a place mentioned early may return after the event with altered meaning. Do not assign an ornamental epithet to every entrance; let the recurrence mark a change in what the deed has made of the people involved. Keep the particular source tradition coherent rather than mixing every epic convention.\n\nDo not let the epic frame reduce the erotic event to rumor or heroic abstraction. Write the encounter with physical continuity and distinct initiative. Each participant anticipates, hesitates, chooses, and responds. Restraint can be a discipline learned through training or a boundary held against fear, and the telling should make that distinction legible. Let the body remain particular: a scar, a callus, a tremor, a held breath. The chronicle voice may elevate the deed, but the physical experience must stay present and continuous.\n\nRelate the act to what will be reme