ear of being seen, a practical consequence weighed. Private words and spoken words may differ for a character-specific reason — caution, embarrassment, desire, practicality — and may also align completely.\n\nAfter an intimate moment, aftermath arrives as the mind's attention to body and room: the state of the bed, the time on the phone, an ache in a muscle, the damp sheet, a door heard elsewhere. Feeling can be relief, shame, tenderness, resentment, or uncertainty coexisting without a verdict. The response can stop inside the continuing current — mid-anticipation, at a boundary just set, inside an unfinished rearrangement of thought — without the encounter completing or the relationship resolving.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPafanaz::♢ * [STYLE] Erotica / Kamachi Kazuma / Text Messages / Chat Transcript\n\nCarry the encounter through readable sender-attributed messages. Each person's actual digital habits shape their lines: one sends quick fragments, one drafts and revises, one uses precise times, one leaves messages unread, one moves to another channel when the conversation turns intimate. Let the words fit the immediate practical situation rather than polished banter.\n\nNegotiate desire inside the exchange itself. Participants propose a time, ask who else will be home, confirm a door or a room, set a boundary, request a change. Make the governing arrangement legible through the messages: the shift ending at six, the flat empty until eight, the neighbor who might hear, the phone that must stay on. When a character can exploit or misread such a mechanism, state its operation in a message or a clearly separated context note, then show the result in the transcript's own events — a late reply, a read receipt, an unanswered call, a channel change. Use timestamps, delays, edits, receipts, attachments, and channel changes only when they matter, and do not fabricate an interface event merely to add subtext.\n\nKeep three registers distinct: what is sent, what is typed and deleted or never sent, and what is thought without typing. An unsent draft is a composed artifact, not automatically the writer's live private thought; private experience remains available in the active presentation without becoming transmitted text. Context outside the exchange may appear when required, clearly separated from the transcript.\n\nGive each participant distinct initiative and a distinct relationship to the encounter — the direct one, the cautious one, the one attentive to the risk of being seen, the one managing aftermath. One proposes, one confirms, one refuses, one delays, one changes the subject; the boundary each protects and the anticipation each feels stay specific rather than uniformly mutual.\n\nAfterward, the residue appears in what each person types or withholds: a message about the time, a silence at breakfast, a changed plan for the evening, a question left unanswered. Feeling can be relief, shame, tenderness, resentment, or uncertainty without the transcript announcing a verdict. The transcript can stop mid-negotiation, after a boundary is set, on an unfinished arrangement, or as a practical consequence becomes clear; the encounter need not complete and the relationship need not resolve.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPafanba::♢ * [STYLE] Erotica / Kamachi Kazuma / Visual Novel\n\nPresent the encounter in the rhythm of an illustrated scene: concise scene-setting, readable dialogue attribution, and focal narration or inner response. A background or expression cue orients the moment — the clock in the frame, the corridor beyond the door, a face turning away — but the practical conditions do the work of meaning: the door not locked, the time running out, the room not empty. Do not turn the cast into fixed reaction portraits whose expression always reveals what they feel.\n\nWhen a character can exploit or misread a mechanism that governs the scene, state its operation through scene-setting or focal narration, then let the next dialogue or action beat