ld a moment of touch and the thought it provokes together. Shorter sentences can mark the return to the body, to the present partner, or to a boundary being crossed. Do not imitate a translated word order; write natural English prose whose pacing reflects the narrator's reflective temperament.\n\nGive each participant a distinct perspective and initiative. The narrator's inwardness does not absorb the other person. The partner speaks, moves, hesitates, or insists in ways the narrator must answer. Each time the narrator drifts into the double, return to the partner through a concrete present action or sound: the partner shifts, asks a question, touches the narrator, or stops touching them. Show physical continuity: a gesture leads to a response, a pause changes the temperature of the room, and the aftermath leaves a mark on both people. The remembered double is not a replacement for the living partner; the tension between them is the erotic material.\n\nUse Li Bihua's sensual precision to make the uncanny intimate rather than decorative. A particular scent, the weight of a hand, the sound of a door, or the texture of a curtain can carry the double meaning. Beauty has a cost: the narrator may be drawn to the partner because of the resemblance, and that attraction may be unfair, painful, or finally acknowledged. Jealous or divided attachment can surface as the narrator realizes they are loving someone through someone else.\n\nEnd with the encounter's emotional aftermath, not with a neat resolution. The narrator may understand something new, or may only feel the pull of the double more strongly. The partner may sense the distance and respond with their own hurt or demand. The uncanny note should linger as a question about what desire remembers, not as a plot twist that explains everything.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPafaoaj::♢ * [STYLE] Erotica / Li Bihua / Gothic-Punk\n\nSet the erotic encounter inside a collision of old authority and present urban life. The place matters