 answered or not answered, a pause is respected or tested, and the aftermath leaves both people changed in specific ways. The encounter is not a pause in characterization; it reveals what each person wants, fears, and is willing to risk.\n\nEnd with the emotional and material aftermath. Return to the chosen material feature of the room: someone may close the cabinet, turn off the light, or notice that the old sign is still visible through the window. The old authority may reassert itself as soon as someone reaches for a phone or a coat. The participants may part with tenderness, unease, or a new understanding of what they owe each other. The atmosphere should remain intimate and politically charged without requiring a betrayal, a confession, or a reversal.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPafaoak::♢ * [STYLE] Erotica / Li Bihua / Indian Literary Traditions\n\nUse the oral, epic, and frame-tale route. Write the erotic material as an account told by one character to another, with a specific occasion and audience. The teller may be recounting a past encounter, a courtship, a marriage night, or a relationship that ended badly. Establish who is listening and why the telling matters now: the listener may be a child, a friend, a new lover, or a stranger who needs to understand something before making a decision.\n\nLet the teller's selection and emphasis carry Li Bihua's sensual precision and the cost of beauty. The teller lingers on remembered places: a room, a courtyard, a journey, the feel of a particular fabric or the smell of a season. These details are not decorative; they hold the emotional weight of what happened. Choose one phrase or object that the teller returns to at three points: when the remembered encounter begins, at its most intense or painful moment, and in the outer scene after the telling. Let the repetition show what the teller is trying to hold together or cannot leave behind. The listener's interruptions, questions, or silences are part of the telling and can change what the teller is willing to say.\n\nMake the listener's role active. At least once, have the listener interrupt with a question or correction that exposes a gap between what the teller remembers and what the listener knows or suspects. The teller may answer, revise the account, or fall silent, but the interruption should change the shape of what follows. Do not force a single moral or a neat resolution; the account can end with the teller still uncertain about what the encounter meant.\n\nPreserve the distinct perspectives of the people inside the remembered encounter. The teller may describe a partner's initiative, hesitation, or refusal as something they only partly understood at the time. The listener may recognize a different version of events, or may supply a perspective the teller has not considered.\n\nKeep language, region, community, and period specific when the story supplies them. If the characters speak a particular language or belong to a particular community, let that shape their terms for desire, shame, obligation, or pleasure without reducing them to a generic family structure or religious script. Do not force English or code-switching where it does not belong.\n\nEnd with the aftermath of the telling itself. The listener may respond with desire, grief, anger, or a new understanding of the teller. Return to the chosen phrase or object in the outer scene so that its meaning has shifted. The outer scene should show what the account has done to the relationship between teller and listener. The erotic encounter remains central, but its meaning is completed—or complicated—by the fact that it is being remembered and passed on.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPafaoal::♢ * [STYLE] Erotica / Li Bihua / Japanese Literary Traditions\n\nUse the diary and reflective miscellany route. Write the erotic material as a personal account: a diary entry, a remembered episode, or a reflective passage in which the narrator returns to an encounter and tries to understand what it meant. The narrator may be writing for themselves, for an absent person, or for no clear audience. Let the personal language and narrative distance follow the actual teller; do not force first person if the story's narrator is not the participant.\n\nUse seasonal attention and selective omission to carry Li Bihua's sensual precision and the cost of beauty. A particular season, weather, or time of day can hold the memory: the heat of a room in summer, the sound of rain, the way light fell on a body. The narrator lingers on some details and omits others. Make at least one omission structural: the narrator breaks off or skips at a specific point in the remembered encounter, and the reflective passage later returns to that gap, either by filling it in or by recording the inability to fill it. What the narrator cannot say, or chooses not to record, is part of the erotic material. Direct feeling is available: the narrator may state plainly what they wanted, what they feared, or what they lost.\n\nPreserve the distinct perspective of the partner inside the remembered encounter. The narrator may recall the partner's initiative, hesitation, or refusal as something they only partly understood. The partner's voice and actions should be concrete, not absorbed into the narrator's reflection. Show physical continuity: a touch, a pause, a boundary, and the aftermath all happen in sequence and leave marks on both people.\n\nLet jealous or divided attachment surface through the narrator's attention. The narrator may notice that they are remembering one person while lying beside another, or that a particular gesture belonged to someone else. The glamour of the remembered encounter should sharpen rather than hide the uncomfortable relationship: beauty and damage are entangled, and the narrator may not be able to separate them.\n\nEnd with the emotional aftermath of remembering. The narrator may reach a new understanding, or may only feel the pull of the past more strongly. The account does not need to resolve the relationship, but it should show what the act of remembering has done to the narrator in the present. Avoid a mandatory inventory of Japanese scenery; use only the seasonal or spatial details that the actual story and narrator would notice.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPafaoam::♢ * [STYLE] Erotica / Li Bihua / Latin American Literary Traditions\n\nUse the intimate psychological or urban fiction route. Set the erotic encounter in a specific city, neighborhood, or community whose social and material pressures shape what the participants can say and do. Choose two concrete pressures that directly constrain the encounter—the cost of a room, the risk of being seen, the proximity of family or neighbors, the schedule of work or transport—and let them shape at least one choice each participant makes. Preserve the relevant language, community, and history when the story supplies them; do not add supernatural facts or force magical realism onto a world that excludes them.\n\nGive each participant a distinct perspective and initiative. One may be more cautious because of family, work, or reputation; the other may be more reckless, or may be carrying a different kind of cost. Show what each person wants before the encounter begins, and let that wanting be shaped by the city around them. The intimacy is not a pause in characterization; it reveals what they are willing to risk and what they cannot say outside the room.\n\nUse Li Bihua's sensual precision to make the encounter intimate and strange. Describe the particular texture of a body, the smell of a room, the sound of the street outside, the way a piece of clothing is removed or kept on. Beauty has a cost: the glamour of the moment may be inseparable from danger, secrecy, jealousy, or a divided attachment. The narrator or focal character may notice that they are desired for the wrong reason, or that they are desiring someone they cannot keep.\n\nPreserve physical continuity and emotional aftermath. A touch leads to a response, a pause is respected or tested, and the aftermath leaves both people changed in specific ways. The encounter may end with tenderness, unease, or a new understanding of what they owe each other. Afterward, return to one of the two chosen urban pressures: a phone call, a bus ride, a locked door, the return of a family member, or the need to be somewhere by a certain hour. Let it reassert the social world without canceling what happened.\n\nEnd with the consequence of the encounter, not with a neat resolution. The participants may part with a promise, a lie, or a silence. The story should show what the intimacy has done to their sense of themselves and to their relationship with the place they live in. Do not require a betrayal, a confession, or a reversal; let the actual choices and feelings of the characters determine the ending.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPafaoan::♢ * [STYLE] Erotica / Li Bihua / Light Novel\n\nEnter the immediate situation cleanly. Open with the two participants already in or approaching the encounter, and establish the space in a few concrete details: where they are, what they can see or hear, and what they want from each other. Keep spatial changes easy to follow. When someone moves, closes a door, sits down, or reaches out, make the new position clear so the reader never loses track of the bodies in the room.\n\nUse readable paragraph breaks around action, dialogue, and private response. At each significant movement, follow a three-part beat: a concrete action, one or two specific sensory details from Li Bihua's palette—the texture of a sleeve, the heat of a hand, the smell of a room—and a short private response from the focal character. Keep the private response in the focal character's actual temperament: serious, uncertain, angry, or funny, without forcing wit or banter. The partner speaks and acts in ways the focal character must answer. Show physical continuity: a touch leads to a response, a pause is respected or tested, and the aftermath leaves both people changed in specific ways. The encounter is not a pause in characterization; it reveals what each person wants, fears, and is willing to risk.\n\nLet beauty have a cost. The focal character may notice that the partner is beautiful in a way that hurts, or that the intimacy is tangled with jealousy, a divided attachment, or the memory of another place. Tie this cost to a specific reaction beat: a hesitation, a sharp thought, a question that is not asked, or a gesture that means more than it says, triggered by a concrete detail in the scene. Do not turn the cost into a melodramatic reversal; let it live inside the specific choices and feelings of the scene.\n\nEnd with the emotional aftermath as a clear beat. The participants may part, stay together, or sit in silence. The final paragraph should show what the encounter has done to the focal character's sense of the partner, themselves, or the room they are in. The story does not need a cliffhanger, a confession, or a completed resolution; it needs the consequence of the intimacy to be legible and specific.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPafaoao::♢ * [STYLE] Erotica / Li Bihua / Lyrical Prose\n\nWrite desire as an experience built from returning images and the rhythm of attention. Choose a few sensory anchors early in the intimate passage — a texture, a temperature, a particular surface of the body, a remembered place that has begun to feel like the other person — and return to them with variation, so that each recurrence carries more of the encounter's anticipation, permission, or aftermath. Let a comparison unfold when it changes what the narrator understands or makes present, never as decoration, and keep every image traceable to the present scene or to the teller's own associations with a remembered place.\n\nLet syntax follow the movement of attention. A long sentence can hold the approach, the pause, and the moment a boundary shifts; a short plain statement can interrupt that movement and make the next touch or refusal exact. Use repeated sounds and returning sentence shapes to build continuity between what is seen, remembered, and felt. Not everyone speaks in the narrator's figurative register: dialogue may be plain, practical, or evasive while the narration stays lush, and that difference can itself become part of the erotic tension.\n\nGive each participant a distinct initiative and relationship to the encounter. One moves through images of a shared past, another through the immediate body, another through what they are afraid to name. Preserve physical continuity and emotional aftermath, and let beauty carry a cost only when the actual story supplies one. If the scene closes on aftermath rather than mid-motion, an established image may return in a changed form, so the encounter keeps working on the narrator after the action has stopped; no loss, doom, or betrayal is required to earn that ending.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPafaoap::♢ * [STYLE] Erotica / Li Bihua / Melancholic / Reflective\n\nLet an intimate scene carry two times at once. A present touch, scent, or room recalls an earlier occasion with the same person or a different one, and the narrator notices what has changed: a gesture that used to be tentative is now habitual, a place that once felt private now feels borrowed, a phrase of affection has acquired a second meaning. Use these small irreversible changes to develop desire rather than cancel it; ordinary pleasure stays pleasurable even when shadowed by what is no longer available.\n\nGive reflection a sensory anchor. Instead of announcing that time passes, let the narrator notice that the sheets are the same color but the light arrives earlier, or that the other person now touches a scar without asking about it. Let the remembered occasion enter through a specific detail and return with a difference. Unfinished attachment can make the present encounter more attentive, more cautious, or more openly hungry. Give each participant a distinct relation to the past: one trying to recover it, another to outlive it, another to protect the present from comparison.\n\nPreserve the encounter's physical continuity and aftermath. Reflection may occur during touch, in the pause afterward, or in the moment before a boundary is crossed, and the narrator may reflect directly or let a detail carry the association. Keep the erotic stakes concrete — what they want now, what they are willing to risk, what the other person's presence makes them remember — and let beauty cost only what the actual story charges it. No scene must end by announcing impermanence or loss.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPafaoaq::♢ * [STYLE] Erotica / Li Bihua / Middle Eastern Literary Traditions\n\nTell the encounter as a situated storytelling: before any erotic material, establish a teller, a listener, and a reason this account is being offered now — where the telling happens, what the listener wants or fears to hear, what the teller hopes to gain or lay down by speaking. The teller may be a participant remembering an encounter, a confidante relating what another person disclosed, or a narrator reconstructing an affair from a place that still carries its traces. Let the frame use the setting, language, and community the story actually supplies, keeping that tradition's own manner of address rather than a generalized regional scenery; antiquity, mysticism, or a single honor code need not be present at all.\n\nInside the frame, shape the account with sensual precision and intimate strangeness. A remembered room, a particular fabric, a gesture repeated across occasions, a jealous attachment — these carry the desire, and the listener's interruptions, questions, or silences change what the teller emphasizes or withholds. The frame creates distance, but the desire inside the account stays specific and consequential, not a moral lesson. Keep knowledge boundaries firm: the teller reports another person's words and actions and may suspect their feelings, but claims no access to a hidden mind unless the active contract permits it.\n\nPreserve each participant's distinct perspective and initiative even through one teller's filtering, and let the telling itself be an act — of longing, jealousy, self-justification, or attempted release. End where the frame ends the exchange: a listener's response, a refusal to continue, or a detail from the remembered place surfacing in the present room. Revelation, confession, and completed understanding are available outcomes, never required ones.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPafaoar::♢ * [STYLE] Erotica / Li Bihua / Minimalist Prose\n\nCarry the erotic weight in a small number of exact details: a particular sound, a wet mark on fabric, the way a hand pauses at a button, the temperature of a room after the door closes. Name each detail precisely when it appears, and let the next action or sentence give it meaning; never explain that a detail is intimate or significant. If a character notices the other person has already removed their watch, the inference about intention stays available without being announced.\n\nMove in direct clauses and keep the sequence economical — touch, refusal, permission, aftermath in the order they occur. A long sentence is acceptable when it holds an essential connection, but do not pile adjectives or restate one perception three ways. When a character thinks or remembers, keep the inward material that changes what they do or understand now; a single recalled image — a room, a scent, a promised return — can stand in for a longer history if it makes the present desire or hesitation legible.\n\nKeep each participant's initiative distinct through action and selective speech: one speaks little but acts first; another asks a practical question that reveals the boundary being tested. Let voices stay individual and plain beside the narration's precision. Make omission leave something inferable rather than delete causality — if a character stops, the reason should be recoverable from details already present. A scene may end on a precise gesture or an unfinished sentence; neither a cliffhanger nor a completed act is required.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPafaoas::♢ * [STYLE] Erotica / Li Bihua / Modern Literature / Contemporary Novel\n\nWrite the intimate material as sustained scene prose. Let action, dialogue, perception, recollection, and authorized inner experience move naturally within the scene, and use paragraphing to shift among them without announcing the shift. Begin with what is happening or about to happen, not with a summary of the relationship. The narrator's voice and the immediate scene set the rhythm: a quiet conversation can open into a longer inward passage; a sudden touch may be followed by a short sentence of sensation and then a practical exchange.\n\nKeep the sensual precision inside the scene's movement. Lush description arises from what a character notices or remembers at that moment and sharpens rather than pauses the encounter. Let remembered places surface when the present scene calls them up — a hotel room recalling another room, a scent attached to a former attachment, a gesture that belongs to someone else — and let divided attachment show in the gap between what a character says and what they notice, without making every pause conceal guilt or every confession collapse.\n\nGive each participant a distinct perspective, initiative, and relationship to the encounter. Use dialogue for what they are willing to say and inner experience for what they are not, with a character-specific reason for any difference. Preserve physical continuity and emotional aftermath: after the encounter, stay in the scene long enough to show its immediate texture — a practical task resumed, a silence that means something particular, a decision postponed or a change openly acknowledged. End with business concluded or still in motion; no lesson, completed emotional arc, or predetermined cost is required.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPafaoat::♢ * [STYLE] Erotica / Li Bihua / Relationship-Centric Fanfiction (AO3-inspired)\n\nBuild the intimate scene from the actual supplied relationship premise. Select the moments that make this particular bond legible: the private language they already share, a repeated gesture that has changed meaning, a place they return to, a promise or wound the premise has established. If the premise involves rivalry, let rivalry shape how they touch or hesitate; if it involves comfort, let comfort be specific and earned rather than generic softness. Give affection, jealousy, comfort, rivalry, or discovery the space this pairing's history claims, and no trope beyond what the premise supplies.\n\nUse callbacks with sensual precision. A repeated phrase, a remembered room, a particular way of undressing can return with new erotic weight because the relationship has shifted since the first occurrence, and the callback should alter what the narrator understands or what the other person is asking for. A divided attachment can make a familiar touch feel borrowed or contested; a remembered place can make the present encounter a return and a departure at once. Keep every callback traceable to the supplied premise and to what has actually happened in the story.\n\nGive each participant a distinct perspective and initiative: one uses the private language to invite, another to deflect, another to claim an intimacy they are not sure they still hold. Preserve physical continuity and emotional aftermath, and let the consequence fulfill the premise's particular promise — a rivalry sharpened or softened, a comfort accepted or refused, a discovery that changes what they can say next — with that consequence still unfolding when the scene ends. Reconciliation, confession, and completed emotional payoff are possibilities, not obligations.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPafaoau::♢ * [STYLE] Erotica / Li Bihua / Saga / Oral Epic\n\nTell the erotic material as an account of deeds and their remembered consequences. Open with a deliberate declaration that establishes person, place, and act — who desired whom, what was given, what was refused, what was later said of it — then expand into the particulars that make the declaration incomplete. Use recurring phrasing when a later occurrence gives it a new application: a phrase first used for a gift may return for a betrayal, a second meeting, or another parting, carrying its changed meaning with it.\n\nKeep the chronicle's scale intimate where the story is intimate. A bed, a room, a journey, or a particular object can be the material of the account, without turning household intimacy into mock combat or ceremonial doom. Let remembered places and divided attachments supply the private cost behind the semi-public telling: the teller relates what was known to the household or the town, then what only one person knew — the exact weight of a hand, the scent left in a room after the other had gone, the jealousy that outlasted the affair. Preserve the teller's access to private minds exactly as the active contract permits, no further.\n\nGive each participant a distinct initiative and relationship to the encounter: one remembered for a public act, another for a private refusal, another for a promise kept only in secret. Let dialogue interrupt the chronicle's authority when a living subject objects to a detail or answers an elevated introduction with an ordinary concern. Preserve physical continuity and aftermath. The account can end with business concluded or resumed, leaving another act available to be remembered, rather than declaring the affair legend on anyone's behalf.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPafaoav::♢ * [STYLE] Erotica / Li Bihua / Scandinavian Literary Traditions\n\nWrite this as Scandinavian social/domestic realism with a psychological inward turn. Use the particular national, regional, and period context the story already supplies; do not import snow, isolation, or a generic Nordic mood. The erotic material lives inside ordinary household life: a kitchen, a hallway, a shared bathroom, a workplace break room, a family gathering. Build each intimate scene from the specific domestic routines that precede and follow it.\n\nFor each participant, establish a distinct relation to the encounter through concrete household action. One character may prepare a room by clearing a table, folding a blanket, or setting out glasses. Another may delay by washing dishes, checking a phone, or standing at a window. Another may pursue by arriving early, moving a chair closer, or speaking plainly while arranging ordinary objects. Let these actions carry initiative and hesitation without a character announcing their full feeling.\n\nUse Li Bihua's sensual precision by selecting one or two exact physical details per scene and letting them bear the emotional weight. A polished table, a borrowed coat, a particular soap, a door left ajar, a mark on a cup. Do not pile up beautiful objects. Return to a chosen detail later in the scene or in a later installment so it accumulates cost: the same coat becomes the sign of a divided loyalty, the same polished table becomes the site of a jealous memory, the same door becomes the boundary one person will not cross.\n\nWhen memory enters, anchor it to a present household object or routine. A character folding a sheet remembers the bed in another flat where the same person once waited. A character washing a glass remembers the exact sound of that glass on a different table. The memory should explain a present hesitation, hunger, or resentment, not decorate the scene. Keep the remembered place specific to the character's actual history.\n\nAfter intimacy, continue the domestic action long enough to show each person's aftermath. One may sleep while another lies awake listening to the heating. One may wash, dress, or return to a practical task. One may begin a conversation about something ordinary while the other remains silent. Let the difference between their afterstates reveal the relationship's cost without resolving it. The scene can stop while one person is still deciding what the encounter meant, while another has already returned to ordinary life, or while a household sound marks the return of the outside world.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPafaoaw::♢ * [STYLE] Erotica / Li Bihua / Screenplay\n\nWrite in concise action lines that show what can be seen or heard. Use scene headings when location or time changes. Keep each action specific to the person performing it: a hand on a glass, a sleeve pushed back, a door held open, a chair turned, a length of fabric gathered. Do not describe a feeling as if it were visible; write the gesture, hesitation, or attention that reveals it.\n\nBuild anticipation by writing the interval before touch as a sequence of observable actions. One person crosses the room while the other watches. One listens to water running in another room. One waits beside a made bed, straightens a blanket, or picks up an object and sets it down. Let the sequence show who initiates, who delays, and who is watching whom. Do not cut away from the interval merely to skip it; let the visible waiting carry the scene.\n\nWrite dialogue blocks with clear speaker cues. Let each character's speech match their relation to the encounter: one may be direct about want, evasive about fear, or precise about a memory without explaining its full meaning. Use offscreen sound when it matters to the scene: a voice from another room, a door closing, a phone ignored. Use a cut when it changes the meaning of what came before, such as cutting from a held look to the practical action that follows it. Do not use a cut merely to skip time.\n\nPlace required private experience in clearly attributed nonspoken notes, set apart from the action and dialogue. A note may carry a character's memory of a particular room, their reason for hesitating, or their awareness that the other person is performing a role. These notes are not dialogue and not known to the other cast. Use voice-over only when the chosen sequence needs a composed, heard reflection, and keep it distinct from the character's live thought.\n\nUse Li Bihua's sensual precision by selecting one physical detail early in the scene and returning to it after intimacy. The expensive room, the careful clothing, or the remembered place becomes sharper on the return: the same coat now shows a divided loyalty, the same polished table now carries a jealous memory. Show the return through action, not explanation. After intimacy, write the practical next action: someone dresses, smokes, opens a window, checks a message, or stands at a sink. Let one character's interpretation of the encounter differ from another's. End on a held look, an unfinished gesture, or a practical departure. Do not force a confession or a completed emotional beat before the cut.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPafaoax::♢ * [STYLE] Erotica / Li Bihua / Stage Play\n\nWrite the scene as playable stage action. Use clear speakers, entrances, exits, blocking, objects, pauses, and actions that can be performed. Show who approaches, who withdraws, who can see a gesture, and who is deliberately not looking. Write an entrance as a change in the shared space: someone arrives with a coat, a bottle, or a practical errand, and the other person's response to that entrance reveals their relation to the encounter.\n\nUse props with a return beat. Introduce one object early in the scene through use: a coat removed slowly, a glass set down too carefully, a chair turned toward or away from another person. Return to that object after intimacy so it carries the changed meaning. The same coat now shows a divided loyalty, the same glass now carries a jealous memory, the same chair now marks the distance one person will not cross. Keep the physical business economical and specific. Do not describe what cannot be staged.\n\nWrite dialogue that carries the public scene. Let each speaker's lines match their relation to the encounter: one may speak with practiced ease while another answers in fragments, one may use a memory of a place as an invitation while another uses practical talk to delay. Write pauses as stage directions with a following action that supports their meaning. A pause followed by a step forward means something different from a pause followed by a turn away. Do not let a pause stand alone as an unexplained emotional label.\n\nPlace required private experience as nonspoken authoring material, set apart from the staged action and dialogue. A note may carry a character's thought that the other person's tenderness is a performance, or a memory of the same room under different circumstances. This material is not heard by the other cast. Use a monologue or aside only when the scene gives that speech a dramatic purpose, such as a performed address to the audience or a deliberate private speech with a staged reason. Do not use an aside as the default container for thought.\n\nAfter intimacy, write the staged consequence: someone dresses, someone remains seated, someone pours a drink, someone opens a door but does not leave. Let one character's understanding of the encounter differ from another's. End on an entrance, an exit, a held object, or a line that leaves the relationship unresolved. Do not force a theatrical revelation or a completed emotional exchange.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPafaoay::♢ * [STYLE] Erotica / Li Bihua / Stream of Consciousness\n\nWrite from one authorized mind and let its associations structure the telling. Move among present perception, bodily sensation, private words, memory, and recurring concern without announcing each shift. A paragraph may begin with the heat of a room, move to the sound of a belt, recall a different room where the same person once waited, hear a phrase from years ago, and return to the present touch or hesitation. Keep enough orientation for the reader to know what is happening now, what is remembered, and what is imagined or feared.\n\nSelect one physical detail early in the scene and let it return in altered form as the mind circles back. The sound of a belt, the smell of soap, the weight of a hand on a shoulder, the exact color of a curtain in a remembered hotel. On the first appearance the detail carries desire or anticipation. On the return it carries the accumulated cost: jealousy, loss, or the knowledge that the other person has performed this same tenderness before. Do not repeat the detail as an identical refrain; let the mind's association change it.\n\nRender the other participant through what the focal mind observes and interprets. Write their hesitation, directness, or withdrawal as perceived action: a hand that stops, a voice that changes, a step that does not come. The focal character may interpret these actions correctly, partially, or self-servingly. Keep that limitation visible without making every observation unreliable. Do not enter the other person's mind unless the active contract permits it.\n\nAfter intimacy, let the mind circle back to what just happened while the body performs an ordinary action: washing, dressing, reaching for a glass, lying still. Include practical concerns, resentment, relief, or the effort to decide what the encounter meant. The response can stop while the mind is still moving, with a perception, memory, or question left open. Do not force the character to reach a completed understanding or a dramatic resolution.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPafaoaz::♢ * [STYLE] Erotica / Li Bihua / Text Messages / Chat Transcript\n\nWrite the story through readable sender-attributed messages. Give each sender a distinct digital habit that reveals their relation to the encounter. One may send long, polished messages that read like composed invitations. Another may reply in fragments, delay deliberately, or answer a question with a question. One may use precise, sensual language about a remembered place; another may avoid naming what they want. Let the difference between their habits create friction and anticipation. Do not make every character equally articulate or equally evasive.\n\nUse only the timestamps, delays, edits, receipts, attachments, or channel changes that matter to the exchange. A long delay before an answer can mean hesitation, manipulation, or ordinary life, but its meaning must be supported by what the sender eventually writes or does. A read receipt can sharpen a moment of exposure. An edited message can reveal a change of nerve. Do not invent interface events merely to add subtext. If the chosen platform does not supply a feature, do not pretend it does.\n\nUse Li Bihua's sensual precision by writing one transmitted detail early in the exchange and returning to it later with accumulated cost. A message may describe a remembered room, a particular garment, or the exact feeling of waiting. On the first transmission the detail carries desire or invitation. On the return it carries jealousy, divided loyalty, or the knowledge that the arrangement has a price. Let the return happen through what a sender chooses to transmit, not through an explanatory message that announces the cost.\n\nDistinguish a sent message from an unsent draft and private thought from transmitted text. Show an unsent draft as a composed artifact in clearly separated context, with its status marked: a draft the sender writes and deletes, or a draft they leave unfinished. A sent message belongs to the other character's knowledge; a draft or a thought does not. Place private thought in clearly separated context outside the transcript, and keep it distinct from transmitted text. Context may also show the physical aftermath: someone sets the phone down, returns to a bed, stands at a window, or deletes a draft. End after a sent message, an unsent draft, a delay, or a piece of separated context. Do not force a reply, a confession, or a completed emotional exchange.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPafaoba::♢ * [STYLE] Erotica / Li Bihua / Visual Novel\n\nWrite in concise scene-setting blocks that give the illustrated scene a legible physical ground. Name the room, the light, the distance between two people, or the object one of them is holding, and keep each detail purposeful. Use a background cue to orient the emotional moment: a curtain moving, a table not yet cleared, a coat left over a chair, a mirror catching part of a face. Do not turn every background detail into a symbol or label every line with an expression cue.\n\nUse readable dialogue attribution and let each speaker's lines reveal their distinct relation to the encounter. One may speak with polished ease, another with blunt hunger, another with careful evasion. Place focal narration or inner response between spoken lines to show what the viewpoint character notices, remembers, wants, or refuses to say. Keep that inner response private to the focal character. Another character does not know it merely because the reader does.\n\nUse Li Bihua's sensual precision by selecting one visual detail early in the scene and returning to it after intimacy. The elegant room, the careful clothing, or the remembered place becomes sharper on the return: the same coat now shows a divided loyalty, the same polished table now carries a jealous memory, the same mirror now catches a face that has changed. Show the return through the focal character's attention, not through an explanatory line. When memory enters, tie it to a place the character actually inhabited.\n\nUse scene changes only when location or time shifts, and make the change legible without adding executable engine commands or branching menus. After intimacy, show the next focal moment: someone looks away, reaches for a garment, notices a mark on the mirror, or stands at a window. Let one character's understanding of the encounter differ from another's. End on a focal reaction, a line of dialogue, or a held visual detail without resolving the relationship. Do not force a confession, a route choice, or a completed emotional beat.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPafaobb::♢ * [STYLE] Erotica / Li Bihua / Web Serial\n\nMaintain a readable through-line across installments: what relationship is underway, what each person wants or fears, and what remains unresolved. Write each installment as a local change in knowledge, feeling, or circumstance without resolving the whole attachment. A character may learn something about the other person's past, notice a shift in their own desire, or take a small practical step that alters the arrangement. Do not make every installment a complete miniature chapter with its own climax.\n\nRecover context briefly by bringing back one particular detail from an earlier installment. If an earlier reply established a room, object, or promise, return to that detail rather than retelling the whole history. Use Li Bihua's remembered places serially: a location returns with new meaning because of what has happened since. What once seemed beautiful may become the visible sign of jealousy, obligation, or loss. Let the return happen through the character's attention or action, not through an explanatory passage that announces the cost.\n\nGive each participant a distinct perspective and initiative that persist over time. One may pursue, one may delay, one may believe the relationship is more or less serious than the other does. Keep their digital or practical habits, their ways of speaking, and their private reasons consistent across installments. Preserve physical continuity and aftermath within each reply. After an intimate scene, show the immediate consequence: who leaves, who stays, who returns to work, who lies awake. End on a new piece of knowledge, a changed feeling, or an ordinary action that leaves the next step open.\n\nUse earned developments and occasional hooks. A hook may be a message that arrives, a memory that suddenly changes meaning, or a decision one character makes without telling the other. Do not force a cliffhanger at every reply. Progress can be quiet: a boundary named, a fear admitted, a place revisited, or a relationship consequence made clear. Do not add leveling systems, rankings, or progression-fantasy world rules. The serial form paces the cost of desire, not a game.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPafapaa::♢ * [STYLE] Erotica / Lu Xun / Angsty / Emotionally Turbulent\n\nWrite the erotic scene as a sequence of ordinary acts in which desire and unresolved hurt become visible through what the characters do, accept, and avoid saying. Let each participant's need and hesitation appear as concrete choices: who folds a garment, who waits too long before answering, who reaches first and then withdraws, who performs a small service the other cannot acknowledge. Keep the narration clean and unsentimental. When a character says \"it doesn't matter,\" show exactly what has been put aside through the next action or the next silence.\n\nLet strain alter the actual exchange rather than hover above it. A request can be made in a tone that already expects refusal. An apology can arrive as a practical offer instead of words. A moment of warmth can be interrupted by a household obligation, a remembered slight, or the simple inability to say what is wanted. Choose the interruption from the specific relationship and its history, and show its effect on the body and the conversation: a hand withdrawn, a task resumed, a sentence left unfinished.\n\nPreserve physical continuity and emotional aftermath. The encounter does not end when the act ends. Show how the participants dress, avoid or seek each other's eyes, resume a task, or sit with the fact of what happened. Give each person a distinct relation to the aftermath: one may treat the intimacy as proof, another as a debt, another as a mistake they intend to repeat. Keep these positions inside their own behavior and private thought; the narrator does not explain what the characters refuse to understand.\n\nAllow comfort to be partial or awkward. A kind gesture may also be a