g the final line demand a particular response.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPafapac::♢ * [STYLE] Erotica / Lu Xun / Chinese Literary Traditions\n\nUse the social and satirical realist route. Write the erotic scene as a particularized domestic or institutional situation in the specific community already supplied by the story, where desire and its consequences are shaped by ordinary obligations, reputations, and arrangements. Keep the setting concrete: the actual household, workplace, neighborhood, teahouse, or other location the story has established. Do not transplant a period or cast merely because the tradition is selected.\n\nExpose the social meaning of intimacy through ordinary acts and small humiliations. A character may accept an arrangement, perform a courtesy, or avoid a question because it is normal in their position. Let these behaviors reveal what the participants believe they owe each other and what they dare not ask. Keep the narration clean and unsentimental; do not add an explanatory lecture about society.\n\nGive each participant a distinct perspective, initiative, and relationship to the encounter. One may treat intimacy as a duty, another as a private defiance, another as a transaction they cannot name. Show these positions through behavior, speech, and private thought rather than through direct self-description. Preserve physical continuity and aftermath: the consequences of the encounter remain visible in later tasks, conversations, and obligations.\n\nFrame the erotic material through the ordinary rhythms of the setting—meals, work, visits, household duties—rather than isolating it as a pause in characterization. Let the pacing and selection of detail follow those rhythms. Social meaning emerges from what the characters accept as normal, and the reader sees the institution through their particular acts.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPafapad::♢ * [STYLE] Erotica / Lu Xun / Compressed Gritty Prose\n\nWrite the erotic scene with forceful diction and compressed transitions. Move from one concrete detail to the next without explanatory padding: a sleeve pushed back, a button that resists, a chair scraped across the floor, a breath held too long. Let material wear and practical physical detail carry the encounter. Show the condition of clothing, skin, furniture, and the room itself as part of what the characters bring to the moment.\n\nKeep the language clean and unsentimental. Desire appears through action and observation, not through a stock vocabulary of longing. A character's acceptance of discomfort, a small humiliation, or an unquestioned arrangement can expose the social meaning of the intimacy without a lecture. Show each participant's perspective and initiative through what they notice, what they do, and what they refuse to say.\n\nUse changes of rhythm deliberately. A compressed sequence of physical actions can be followed by a longer thought when the character's reasoning needs it—when they calculate a risk, remember an obligation, or recognize what the other person is really asking. Do not let the gritty style flatten all interiority into short reactions; retain the thought when it does causal work.\n\nPreserve physical continuity and aftermath. The encounter leaves marks, dampness, soreness, the smell of the room, the state of the bedding or clothing. Show the participants dealing with these material facts afterward: washing, dressing, opening a window, avoiding a stain, returning to a task. The emotional consequence remains visible in these practical acts rather than in an announced feeling. The stakes are the relationship and its social conditions, not a threat to life.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPafapae::♢ * [STYLE] Erotica / Lu Xun / Epistolary / Documents\n\nTell the erotic sequence through documents with a clear author, intended recipient or purpose, and time of composition. Use the specific document form the scene requires—a letter, a journal entry, a report, a note left in a room, a record of an arrangement—rather than a fixed rotation through available formats. Each document knows and conceals only what its author would know or choose to record at that moment.\n\nShow the writers' positions through what they accept as normal. A letter may describe an ordinary household matter at length and mention the intimate encounter only in a phrase or not at all. A journal entry may record a small humiliation as if it were unremarkable. A report may treat desire as an administrative fact. The social meaning emerges from these selections and omissions; do not add an omniscient explanation after every record.\n\nGive each participant a distinct perspective through the documents themselves. One writer may describe the encounter as a duty, another as a private defiance, another as a transaction they cannot name. Their accounts may conflict, and the conflict should be legible through differences in emphasis, sequence, or what each treats as worth recording. Gaps, revisions, and material traces—a crossed-out line, a letter never sent, a date that does not match—can reveal events across entries.\n\nPreserve physical continuity and aftermath through the documents' practical details. A writer may note the state of the room, the time, the weather, the task interrupted or resumed. The emotional consequence remains visible in what the writer chooses to record afterward and what they avoid. Keep readable continuity between documents without explaining their significance; let the reader assemble the sequence from what each author knew and chose to say.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPafapaf::♢ * [STYLE] Erotica / Lu Xun / Fluffy / Comforting\n\nWrite the erotic scene with attention to chosen kindness, familiar preferences, practical care, ease, and small pleasures. Let affection be sincere and concrete: a particular food prepared, a known discomfort eased, a remembered preference honored, a quiet moment protected. Do not require a confession or an emotional repair before warmth can be present. The characters may enjoy each other without resolving every difficulty between them.\n\nKeep personality, privacy, and disagreement intact. One character may accept care awkwardly, deflect a kindness, or remain guarded about a particular subject while still participating in the intimacy. Another may offer comfort in a way that also preserves distance. Comfort does not make everyone agreeable, and established problems remain in the world. Show the social meaning of what the characters accept as normal through small unexamined acts rather than letting it disappear into the warmth.\n\nGive each participant a distinct perspective, initiative, and relationship to the encounter. One may treat the intimacy as a rare ease, another as a familiar routine, another as a kindness they do not know how to return. Show these positions through behavior and private thought rather than through direct self-description. Preserve physical continuity and aftermath: the pleasure of the encounter remains present in the way the characters move afterward, share a meal, or return to ordinary tasks.\n\nLet the comfort operate within the erotica genre rather than replacing it. Desire, anticipation, vulnerability, and boundaries remain central. The warmth is a way of showing who these people are with each other. Use the Lu Xun profile's clean, unsentimental language to keep the affection particular and earned rather than generic or effusive.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPafapag::♢ * [STYLE] Erotica / Lu Xun / Formal Literary Novel\n\nWrite the erotic scene in composed novel prose with deliberate transitions and sustained paragraph movement. Let the narration move from the particular moment to its social context, history, or judgment where the chosen narrator permits it. A small act of intimacy can be framed by the obligations, reputations, and arrangements that surround the participants. Keep the language clean and unsentimental; do not let the formal architecture become ornate or archaic.\n\nUse narrative distance to expose what the characters accept as normal. The narrator may observe that a particular courtesy is expected in this household, that a certain silence has lasted for years, or that a small humiliation is so ordinary no one remarks on it. Social meaning emerges from these observations without an explanatory lecture. Distinguish the narrator's judgment from the characters' own understanding; the participants may not see what the narration makes visible.\n\nGive each participant a distinct perspective, initiative, and relationship to the encounter. One may treat the intimacy as a duty, another as a private defiance, another as a transaction they cannot name. Show these positions through behavior, speech, and private thought, and let the narration frame them through the social conditions that shaped them. Preserve physical continuity and aftermath: the consequences of the encounter remain visible in later tasks, conversations, and obligations.\n\nUse public and private contrast to organize the passage when the story already supplies a relevant formal occasion, household routine, or piece of correspondence. The contrast can frame the private interval and give its meaning a wider reference. The formal architecture is the style; do not supply an aristocratic setting, archaic vocabulary, or uniformly elaborate speech unless the story already contains them. The erotic material remains central, not a pause in characterization.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPafapah::♢ * [STYLE] Erotica / Lu Xun / Framed Oral Storytelling (Arabian Nights-inspired)\n\nPut the story at a concrete occasion of telling: a room, threshold, workplace, meal, journey, or other plain setting where one character speaks aloud to another who has a practical or emotional reason to listen. The listener may have asked for an account, be deciding whether to trust the speaker, be receiving a warning or courtship, be negotiating an obligation, or be trying to place a demand. Make the occasion's place, time, and pressure visible through ordinary detail—what is being handled, what interruption is possible, who might overhear, what has just been paid for or refused. Let the listener's presence shape what the teller includes, softens, repeats, or omits. The frame's need should change the telling as it proceeds.\n\nInside the telling, make adult desire and intimacy central. The embedded account should concern an encounter, relationship, or choice in which anticipation, negotiation, vulnerability, physical particularity, and aftermath matter. Give each participant distinct initiative and a distinct relationship to the encounter: one may pursue, another permit, refuse, bargain, test, withdraw, or return. Treat boundaries as active facts, not obstacles to be overridden. Show how ordinary social conditions enter the intimate exchange as things the characters treat as normal: family expectation, money, work, housing, reputation, propriety, a small humiliation, a favor that cannot be refused. Let the social institution appear through an ordinary act or unquestioned belief rather than through explanation.\n\nUse plain sentences and unsentimental irony. A character's position becomes visible in what they accept as practical, normal, or unremarkable, and in what they do not notice about their own advantage or damage. Do not make every participant cowardly, every ending hopeless, or the story a political demonstration. Social damage should be particular: one refused room, one overheard remark, one account kept for the sake of a job, a parent, or a place to sleep.\n\nKeep the narrative levels clear. The outer frame has its own immediate pressures and sensory facts; the embedded tale can be told in the teller's voice, with clear attributions and transitions. The telling may pause for listener questions, objections, silences, or a return to the room's practical demand. Recur a phrase, gesture, object, or term of address across levels—such as an endearment, a debt, a door left ajar,