at was seen, heard, and felt. When the teller was not present, distinguish report from memory and admit the gap rather than inventing access to another mind. The teller may recount what one person noticed, what another assumed, and what a third never learned. Preserve each participant's distinct perspective and initiative within those limits. Physical continuity and aftermath remain part of the recounted intimacy, but the teller may stop at the edge of what can be known.\n\nLet Lu Xun's plain observation expose the social arrangements inside the frame: a marriage, a household obligation, a reputation, a small humiliation accepted as normal. The teller may state these facts plainly, without a lecture, and may accept a restriction as normal even while the telling reveals its cost. Do not make the setting exotic or the social world a costume. Use the actual supplied setting and community. End where the telling naturally pauses, leaving the listener's response or the next portion of the account available rather than closing the frame with a moral.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPafapar::♢ * [STYLE] Erotica / Lu Xun / Minimalist Prose\n\nSelect a small number of exact details and let them carry the encounter. Choose details that do more than one kind of work: a particular gesture may show desire and hesitation, an object moved aside may show preparation and a boundary, a repeated phrase may show intimacy and an accepted restriction. Use direct clauses. Keep only the inward connection that materially contributes to the present account. An essential thought may be long enough to make sense, but do not add a second explanation after the detail has already done the work.\n\nLet omission leave something inferable. If a character hesitates, let what follows make the boundary or remembered obligation inferable: the hand withdrawn, the task resumed, the phrase not answered. Do not delete causality. The reader should understand why the encounter proceeds, pauses, or changes. A precise e