counter. A shared task, an errand, a meal, or the rearrangement of a room can begin the scene and then give way to desire. Afterward, return to the same practical object or movement so that the erotic event takes its place in the characters' durable life. When reflection occurs, keep it specific: not the general feeling that time is passing, but the particular change the character notices—a scar, a loosened ring, a phrase no longer used, a slant of evening light.\n\nAllow remembered tales and names to function as private vocabulary. A place they once planned to visit, a bargain made lightly, a path taken in another season may increase the weight of the present encounter without becoming a symbol. Let grief, resignation, or an announcement of impermanence appear only where the actual scene supports it. End while the aftertaste is still forming, with one person speaking about a practical next hour or another noticing what will be different when they stand up. Preserve physical continuity and emotional aftermath as aspects of the same scene. Give the focus character actual private response—longing mingled with satisfaction, a regret, a small surprise at one's own feeling—rather than a manufactured lesson about time.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPafaraq::♢ * [STYLE] Erotica / Neil Gaiman / Middle Eastern Literary Traditions\n\nUse a framed oral narration in which the erotic encounter is told as a remembered tale, but tell it from inside the participants' live experience rather than as a decorative performance. Establish the frame concretely: one character may speak to the other while they are together, or the scene may proceed through a voice that is retelling what happened. Make the telling itself intimate. Let the listener answer, correct a detail, or add what the teller has not said, so that the account becomes a shared construction.\n\nGive the tale a deliberate opening that does not interrupt the genre's physical continuity. The focus character may begin with a phrase suited to the story's frame, such as 'There was a night when,' and then fall into the present action; later, a repeated phrase from that opening returns at the moment of climax or aftermath. Make recurrence meaningful, not mechanical: the sound of a key, the name of a town, a line from a childhood story may surface again because the encounter has changed what it means.\n\nKeep the participants' perspectives distinct. The teller's account may be interrupted by the other character's touch or by a remembered correction. If a register shifts between languages or dialects, let it serve the intimacy rather than an exotic atmosphere. Do not import antiquity, mysticism, exile, or an honor code as automatic scene furniture. The setting, language, and community remain those supplied by the story.\n\nLet the erotic event carry its own second story, as in the author profile: a bargain, a path, a meal, or a family anecdote may acquire weight because it is now part of how they touch each other. Leave the frame open as the response ends, with the teller turning to a practical remark or the listener beginning a new phrase, so that the story feels continued rather than sealed. Preserve emotional aftermath and physical particulars. Give the focus character actual private response to the telling—what they leave out, what becomes possible to say—while the other character's private response remains separate unless access is authorized.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPafarar::♢ * [STYLE] Erotica / Neil Gaiman / Minimalist Prose\n\nWrite the erotic scene through a small number of exact details that the participants themselves can sense. A hand on a cupboard door, the sound of a phone vibrating, the smell of rain on a coat—each detail earns its place by affecting the present action or the relationship. Use direct clauses and let the reader infer the emotion from what the characters do and notice. Avoid summary of desire; show its edge through restricted narration: 'She took off his glasses' or 'He