e to the focus character's sensations and judgments while allowing the other participant to act with their own initiative. Do not summarize the intimacy as 'they made love'; render the body's particulars and the sequence of touch, speech, and attention as part of the same continuous prose.\n\nUse paragraphing and transitions to move among action, dialogue, recollection, and private thought. Let a recollection arise from a present detail: the texture of a shirt, the corner of a room, a phrase someone used earlier. Give the focus character actual interiority—anticipation, boundary negotiation, uncertainty, desire, or a small recognition that changes the meaning of something past. Keep the other character's private experience distinct; the reader may observe their action and speech, but render only authorized inner access. Where both perspectives are available, keep their styles and concerns separate.\n\nGive names, bargains, paths, and routines a quiet significance. The path they took to the kitchen, the bargain about who would lock the door, the name of a film they once discussed—these ordinary elements may surface during or after intimacy and carry another story. Keep this resonance in the author's direct telling rather than a supernatural intrusion. Use concrete local detail rather than mythic decoration: the weight of a full cup, the sound of a distant bus, the scratch of a pen on a shopping list.\n\nDo not stop the novel at climax. Preserve physical continuity and emotional aftermath in the same scene: washing, dressing, opening a window, answering a delayed question, falling into post-coital talk. Let the encounter change something between the characters—perhaps a fear is reduced, a debt is named, a plan becomes possible—without resolving every tension. End within the ongoing business of the day or night, leaving the next scene natural. If the aftermath is warm, let it be warm; if it is uncertain, let the uncertainty remain particular rather than announced.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPafarat::♢ * [STYLE] Erotica / Neil Gaiman / Relationship-Centric Fanfiction (AO3-inspired)\n\nBuild the erotic scene around the specific relationship premise supplied by the current story. Do not import a roster of familiar tropes—only one bed, fake dating, enemies to lovers—unless the premise itself contains them. Instead, identify the pair's particular promise: the habit they repeat, the thing one has apologized for, the private joke, the unequal want, the way their bodies know each other before words catch up. Let the intimacy fulfill that promise while remaining unpredictable in the moment.\n\nEstablish the scene through a choice action that exposes the relationship's pressure point: one character cooking, the other waiting; a shared errand; a bed made in a way only one of them does. Use private language and callbacks sparingly but with clear payoff. A phrase from an earlier argument may return during sex and change its meaning; an object the pair has handled together may become part of the present touch. Make recurrence signal emotional development, not nostalgia for its own sake.\n\nGive each participant a different initiative and interiority. The focus character's feelings may include desire, relief, jealousy, affection, or the particular anxiety of being known; the other character should act from their own motives and may say the unexpected thing. Let them negotiate boundaries where the story requires it—not as a regulatory pause, but as the kind of speech that already belongs to them. Preserve physical continuity: move from anticipation through touch to aftercare or post-coital conversation without cutting to declaration.\n\nLet the author profile's resonance appear as an unspoken second story. A bargain they made, a path they used to take, a place one of them still mentions—these can color the encounter and its aftermath. Keep the telling direct and familiar; carry any weight through ordinary details. End where the relationship remains