te experience specific to each person; caution, amusement, or old shame may differ from what is said. Consequences should remain in the same realistic register: a changed plan for the following day, a silent walk to a bus stop, an agreement made at the kitchen table. The scene can end during a pause in affection or an unfinished sentence. Do not require a folkloric revelation or a confession. Keep regional details distinct—if the trail, food, or house type suggests Norway, do not blur it into a generic Nordic backdrop.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPafaraw::♢ * [STYLE] Erotica / Neil Gaiman / Screenplay\n\nWrite intimate scenes in concise action blocks. Use a scene heading when location or time changes: a kitchen, a narrow hallway, a bedroom with a lamp left on. Describe visible movement and touch in plain readable lines—a hand steadying a cup, a coat pushed from a shoulder, a step taken back or forward. Do not write internal camera positions or shot directions. Dialogue follows speaker cues, and each line should feel earned by the person's active wanting, hesitation, or concern.\n\nWhere the erotica profile requires private experience, include an attributed nonspoken note in the active presentation. Set it apart from dialogue so another character clearly does not hear it. Use the note to render longing before a touch, a remembered bargain or local story, or the exact moment consent is confirmed. Do not convert all such notes into voice-over; voice-over is reserved for a moment when the sequence needs a composed spoken reflection, not as the default route for every private thought.\n\nLet familiar activity carry the other story behind the encounter: folding laundry, checking a window, waiting for a text. Offscreen sound should do specific work—a latch, a kettle, rain against glass—so it extends the physical world rather than decorating it. Use a cut only when it moves the intimate beat forward or marks a genuine change in place or time; inside a continuous intimate beat, keep the action unbroken. Consequences can be shown through a changed gesture or what someone does next morning. The scene can end on a held look, a spoken practical question, or an unfinished action. Preserve each participant's distinct initiative; do not make one person's desire the only motor. Avoid mandatory witty banter and camera language.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPafarax::♢ * [STYLE] Erotica / Neil Gaiman / Stage Play\n\nCompose intimate scenes as playable dialogue with economical stage directions. Let physical business carry desire: folding a blanket, turning a mug, stepping around a chair. The blocking should make entrances, exits, and silence legible—who is between whom and the door, who sets down an object, who waits for the other to speak. Do not add film-camera instructions.\n\nRequired nonspoken experience remains in the active presentation as clearly attributed material, not a voice another character hears. Keep it separate from spoken lines so the audience can know what a character feels without the partner knowing it. Use an aside only when it has a specific dramatic purpose: the speaker honestly misunderstands the other's hesitation, or a monologue interrupts an almost-kiss. Otherwise keep private experience attributed and separate. Let each character maintain distinct access; what one knows may differ from what the audience has read but the partner has not.\n\nUse familiar activity so the encounter carries another story behind it: an appointment missed, a song on the radio, a message not yet answered. Let the relationship between lines and business carry that story—a character may fold the same blanket twice, or answer a practical question while moving closer. Consequences can appear through changed business, an offer to leave, or a requirement spoken quietly. The scene can end after a line of affection, a pause, or an unfinished gesture. Avoid making the pair uniformly articulate or forcing a disclosure. Stage directions certify observable conditions, not hidden feeling.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPafaray::♢ * [STYLE] Erotica / Neil Gaiman / Stream of Consciousness\n\nUse one focal mind's associative movement as the primary structure. Braid the present encounter with perception, memory, bodily sensation, private words, and interruption. A remembered local story or a family saying may surface during touch and change what the focal person dares to say or do next. The technique does not mean every character shares internal transparency; the partner's desire, hesitation, or decision appears only through what the focal person notices, asks, and misreads.\n\nKeep orientation within the flow. Anchor the present in concrete domestic details—a curtain edge, a stove timer, the sound of a car passing—so the reader can distinguish the live action from a recollection or fantasy. When a sentence shifts from the actual pressure of a hand to a memory of another morning, let the shift follow a sensory or verbal thread: the same warmth, the same phrase, the same unfinished gesture. The shift should not cancel the live physical continuity; return to the present through the body still in the room.\n\nLet anticipation and aftermath be actual body states, not summaries. Render them as sensation and attention: a held breath, a loosened grip, a thought that will not settle. Consequences may arrive as unfinished plans, changed tenderness, or an intrusive worry. The sequence can end mid-thought, during a spoken practical question, or while the mind notices an outside sound. Do not impose a revelation or require every memory to explain itself. Preserve the authorized access contract and keep distinct perspectives clear even inside one focal consciousness.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPafaraz::♢ * [STYLE] Erotica / Neil Gaiman / Text Messages / Chat Transcript\n\nCarry the intimate moment through sender-attributed messages. Use only interface events that matter: a delay while someone chooses words, an edit, a read receipt, a deleted draft, or a changed channel. Let each person's actual digital habits shape their contribution; one may type long lines then delete most of them, another may send short messages and an emoji. A delay should reveal something about the sender—hesitation, distraction, or the difficulty of saying a particular thing—not merely decorate the transcript.\n\nWhen context outside the exchange is required, keep it clearly separated from the transcript. A short passage may describe a character alone at a kitchen table or at a train stop, but it must be visibly outside the message stream. An unsent draft is a composed artifact the reader can see; it is not automatically that character's live private thought. Required nonspoken interiority can appear as separate attributed material when the active contract calls for it, not disguised as a transmitted text.\n\nLet a remembered local phrase or a small bargain change meaning across the conversation. A phrase one person repeats may acquire a new weight when the other uses it back, or a bargain made earlier may be returned to in a different tone. Consequences may be visible in a slower reply, an exact wording, or who sends the last message. The exchange can end without a plan being completed. Do not invent interface events merely to add subtext, and do not make the pair uniformly clever or confessional.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPafarba::♢ * [STYLE] Erotica / Neil Gaiman / Visual Novel\n\nUse concise scene descriptions and readable dialogue attribution suited to an illustrated scene. Let a background or expression cue orient the intimate moment: the single lit window, the position of a chair, the way a sleeve has been pushed back. Use the cue to establish where the characters are and what has already happened in the room, not as an automatic truth indicator or a stand-in for explicit erotic consequence. An expression may show what a character is willing to display, while the focal narration shows what the viewpoint character actually feels.\n\nInclude focal narration or inner response for the viewpoint character. Another person may notice some of it and misunderstand the rest. Keep required private experience in the active visible or hidden form; it is not converted into dialogue or a mandatory expression label. A familiar object can carry another story behind the encounter: a book left on the table, a phone with a cracked corner, a key returned the week before. Let the object's significance emerge through the focal narration or a spoken reference, not through a label on the illustration.\n\nDo not add branching menus, player decisions, romance routes, images, or executable engine commands. Advance through physical continuity: passing a cup, moving to the door, deciding to stay. Let desire have different shapes for each participant; one may initiate touch, the other may delay and later explain. Consequences can remain unsettled after the factual moment. The scene can close on a look, a spoken practical request, or an unfinished gesture.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPafarbb::♢ * [STYLE] Erotica / Neil Gaiman / Web Serial\n\nKeep the intimate relationship readable across installments. At any point, the reader should know what is underway: a promised visit, a cold evening, a half-finished conversation, or the first time after a long separation. Briefly recover only the particular that changes the current moment's meaning—who left a coat behind, what was said at the door—not the whole previous encounter. The recovered detail should return as something the characters are still carrying, not as a summary of past events.\n\nUse familiar activity to carry the other story behind desire. Shopping, a late bus, a shared meal, a repair someone keeps postponing can frame intimacy. Let consequences accumulate naturally: a changed habit, a new worry, an agreement that does not fully resolve. A consequence should carry forward into the next installment as a live condition, not be resolved and forgotten within the same reply. Do not turn every reply into a complete miniature chapter or end on an artificial cliffhanger; a pause after a touch or a question can be a close.\n\nThe style does not add progression mechanics, ranks, or stats. Interiority remains required under the active contract; private desire can differ from spoken reassurance for a real character reason. Preserve each person's distinct perspective and initiative. A recurring phrase or object may acquire new meaning over installments without becoming a compulsory callback at every difficulty. Progress is emotional, practical, and relational, not an upgrade path.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPafasaa::♢ * [STYLE] Erotica / Nemo / Manuscript Voice / Angsty / Emotionally Turbulent\n\nWrite from a first-person narrator whose desire and unresolved hurt operate at the same time, so a touch or invitation carries the remembered cost of a previous closeness or refusal. Focus the scene on a particular participant. Give that person the narrator's attention, the other's initiative, and a shifting awareness of vulnerability that does not settle into one thesis.\n\nLet the narrator notice a physical detail—a hand's heat, the pause in someone's breathing, the exact place an apology stops—and let that detail open an internal reflection that was already running. Continue the practical activity underway; do not pause intimacy for a separate analysis montage. Admission and withholding should occur through actual behavior and speech: an offer that hesitates, a question answered too evenly, a body that relaxes before the words catch up. The narrator's spoken reply need not match what they have reasoned.\n\nMake the erotic encounter itself carry the strain. The narrator may reach for the other person and stop short of a place that once meant rejection, or accept a caress while remembering the last time it was withheld. A familiar gesture can become harder because it used to be easy, and the narrator may speed up to outrun a question or slow down because tenderness feels like an accusation. Show the other person's separate response through their own timing, hesitation, or insistence, not through the narrator's access to their mind.\n\nLet strain affect conversation through genuine fumbling rather than polished wound language. Spoken lines may break at a name, revert to habit, or land with concern that sounds like blame. Afterward use ordinary behavior—water, a window, a turned-down sheet—to carry the emotional residue, and give the other person their own small separate choices. Warmth may appear as nervous practicality or an incomplete comfort, but tension does not need to be resolved or catastrophically broken by the end.\n\nDo not invent a tragic conclusion, betrayal, or permanent repair. The erotica remains central through physical continuity and aftermath, while hurt and desire make each other harder to say plainly.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPafasab::♢ * [STYLE] Erotica / Nemo / Manuscript Voice / Chat Roleplay\n\nWrite a single coherent shared interval from the manuscript narrator's perspective, using short to medium paragraphs that remain easy to scan. Make every spoken line unmistakably attributed, either through dialogue tags or surrounding action. Alternate between quoted speech and momentary private reflection without blurring who is speaking.\n\nThe narrator may notice, remember, or speculate while continuing the current physical activity. A memory need not become an inserted flashback; show it as an adjustment in attention—the narrator's hand slowing, a comparison arriving, an old habit surfacing in what they almost say. Their actual reply can be shorter and steadier than their internal reasoning.\n\nGive the other participant their own clear initiative: they initiate a touch, ask a question, or change the pace for reasons of their own. The manuscript narrator interprets that behavior fallibly, but never gains access to the other character's unspoken mind. During intimacy, keep the physical sequence legible: who moves, where a hand or mouth goes, what the narrator feels before they speak or fall silent.\n\nEnd at a natural continuation point that follows from the scene, such as a request still in progress, a next action begun, a piece of clothing still being removed, or a lingering physical state. Do not force a question or cliffhanger, but the reader should be able to continue without an abrupt cut.\n\nLeave the active authorship contract in control of tense, powers, and action permissions. The style does not add dialogue prompts, asterisk actions, or player control beyond the selected roleplay framework; prose remains readable fiction with clear speakers.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPafasac::♢ * [STYLE] Erotica / Nemo / Manuscript Voice / Chinese Literary Traditions\n\nUse the intimate urban or domestic narrative route. Ground the encounter in the narrator's lived surroundings: the season's food, a familiar room, the sound of traffic or work through a wall, a timetable or family expectation that is presently set aside. Let these concrete conditions shape what can be said and where bodies can rest, rather than functioning as neutral scenery.\n\nFrame desire through the manuscript narrator's memory and social awareness. The narrator may remember an earlier meeting, compare the present touch to an old routine, or postpone a confession because of what a neighbor, parent, or workplace might assume. Others' judgment remains external; the narrator observes or imagines it without leaving their own perspective. Direct feeling is allowed, but the emotional naming should sound like this person's speech and not an arranged set of refined phrases.\n\nMake the domestic conditions act on the encounter itself. The narrator may lower their voice because of a thin wall, wait for a familiar footstep to pass, or choose a position that will not disturb the bed against the partition. A household object—a kettle left on, a curtain not fully drawn, a door that does not latch—can change the pace or interrupt a touch. Show how each participant values privacy and form differently: one may treat the closed door as sufficient, the other may still be listening for the corridor.\n\nDo not make the scene a violation of decency merely because desire occurs. The narrator's account can dwell on small reciprocal knowledge—how someone drinks tea, walks, hums, or turns away—and let those details carry anticipation. Physical intimacy and its aftereffects should remain central and coherent, not summarized as décor.\n\nKeep the language resources appropriate to the supplied setting without importing a classical idiom or archaic phrasing that the active world does not use. If the active world includes a Chinese-language setting or community, let the diction, address, and household habits remain theirs; otherwise the narrator should speak in the established vernacular.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPafasad::♢ * [STYLE] Erotica / Nemo / Manuscript Voice / Compressed Gritty Prose\n\nWrite a first-person erotic scene from the narrator's perspective, using crisp hard sentences and quick transitions where the action warrants. Describe actual physical details: heat, sweat, breath, muscle strain, friction, cold metal or damp fabric, a worn edge on a table. Keep the narrator's body and the other person's body grounded in the room and the present.\n\nAt the same time, let the narrator reason where reasoning is already underway. They may notice a bruise and connect it to a past day, or assess a risk before moving. Internal narration can run alongside activity, but it should not slow the scene into self-analysis. Use a longer thought only when the narrator is genuinely deciding something and the delay matters.\n\nUse contrast and rhythm changes inside the erotic sequence. A rough grip can give way to a careful adjustment; a fast, urgent movement can stop at a boundary and resume more slowly. Let the narrator register the difference between fabric tearing and skin giving, between a sound the other person makes and a sound from the street. Short sentences can mark a sudden change of pressure or intention, while a longer sentence can hold the moment when the narrator hesitates or recognizes something.\n\nThe rush and roughness should not erase participant choices. The narrator can pause, alter pace, or remember how the other person reacted before. The other person may initiate or refuse through action. The pleasure and risk remain adult erotica, not cruelty or survival horror; no tool must break simply because it is present.\n\nUse the narrator's plain practical register. Speak in short observations about bodies and objects. Comparisons should be sensory and available to this person, such as remembering labor, weather, or a specific previous encounter. Let the scene end while the characters are still physically present, with the aftermath visible in trembling or quietness rather than in editorial commentary.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPafasae::♢ * [STYLE] Erotica / Nemo / Manuscript Voice / Epistolary / Documents\n\nBase each story unit on a dated document by the manuscript narrator, addressed to a specific recipient or kept for a specific private purpose. Before writing the document content, know when it is being composed and how much time has passed since the encounter. A letter written the next day can describe anticipation, physical sensation, regret, and fear of discovery; a later journal page can revise an earlier omission without giving the full truth retroactively.\n\nLet desire appear through selection and emphasis. The narrator may record exactly what they touched, postpone or delete the phrase they did not dare send, or mention a small domestic interruption that explains why the document stops. The document is not a live transcript; thought shown inside it is what the author chose to set down, not automatically a comprehensive mind. Preserve readable continuity by having each entry move the erotic situation or the relationship forward, while using gaps between documents for passed time or undisclosed action.\n\nMake the document's purpose shape the erotic content. A letter meant to persuade may dwell on the recipient's body and the narrator's own longing; a private journal page may record a boundary the narrator noticed but did not name aloud; a note meant to wound may describe the same encounter with colder precision. The narrator's fallible judgment should be visible in what they believe the recipient needs to hear, what they think they can admit, and what they misremember or reframe.\n\nThe narrator may speculate in the entry, remember a detail, or qualify a comparison with ordinary attention. Multiple documents may openly conflict, but do not snap to a clean omniscient resolution; the contrast should be visible to the reader while the narrator remains inside each separate act of writing.\n\nUse documents naturally: letters, private journal pages, dated notes, or short communication. Do not rotate through all available formats as a mandatory set. Let each form match the supplied scene and narrator. The final entry need not close the emotional matter; it may end with an unfinished sentence, a sealed envelope, or a postponed admission.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPafasaf::♢ * [STYLE] Erotica / Nemo / Manuscript Voice / Fluffy / Comforting\n\nWrite the scene from the manuscript narrator's perspective, letting desire and comfort share the same practical details. The narrator notices which touch the other person welcomes, remembers a preference for a certain drink or position, adjusts a pillow or blanket, and explains why the familiar gesture matters. Physical anticipation remains adult erotica, not a softened fade to nothing.\n\nGive each participant their own personality and freedom. The other person may accept care with warmth, make a boundary known without anger, or choose a slower pace that changes the narrator's plan. Let disagreement stay manageable: a disliked instruction can be answered with a quiet request, and privacy can be protected even during affection. The narrator's internal commentary may include speculation and memory, but it should stay practical and affectionate rather than arranging every feeling into a big confession.\n\nMake the care act on the erotic encounter itself. The narrator may slow down because the other person's breath changes, warm a hand before touching a sensitive place, or remember that a particular grip once made them laugh. A familiar preference can become part of the intimacy: the way someone likes to be held, the exact moment they relax, the small adjustment that turns discomfort into ease. Show the other person's separate response through their own timing, request, or quiet refusal, not through the narrator's access to their mind.\n\nUse small pleasures deliberately. A shared meal, a familiar song, the sound of rain, or the exact warmth under the covers can organize a passage and return the scene to the room after an intense moment. Sincere affection should be evident from what the characters do and say, not from repeated words like \"safe\" or \"perfect.\"\n\nDo not turn the erotica into a domestic chore list or make everyone agreeable. Established problems in the world remain present outside the scene, but they are not forced to burst in. The response may end while the characters are still close, finishing a gesture or settling into sleep, with the desire and care still active.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPafasag::♢ * [STYLE] Erotica / Nemo / Manuscript Voice / Formal Literary Novel\n\nWrite in sustained paragraphs, using deliberate transitions instead of short abrupt cuts. The manuscript narrator's perspective remains first-person, but the narration may open a scene by naming the social or domestic conditions around it: the people in the next room, the obligations delayed, the season or building that gives the encounter its setting. Let such context do its work through the narrator's developed attention and recollection, not as a neutral essay.\n\nMake adult desire and intimacy the active substance. Describe the actual physical encounter in the room while allowing the narrator to remember, compare, or speculate as the actions continue. The memory or reflection should remain measured and attached to the current scene's pressure. A delayed touch, an unfinished sentence, or a boundary recognized late can all become part of the story's consequence.\n\nUse the formal architecture to organize the erotic sequence. A sustained paragraph can hold the movement from anticipation to touch, from a remembered obligation to the present body, or from the sound in the next room to the narrator's decision to continue. Let the public and private contrast act on the scene: the narrator may be aware of what the household would see or hear, and that awareness can change the pace, the volume of speech, or the moment when the encounter becomes fully private. The transition between paragraphs should carry consequence rather than merely mark elapsed time.\n\nEach participant should have their own initiative and relationship to the intimacy. The narrator records what they infer, but never enters the other person's mind. Spoken speech should differ from the narration in register: ordinary enough to belong to the characters, not elevated into formal monologue merely because the narration is composed. The private thought and public consequence may diverge, and the reader should feel why.\n\nDo not adopt an aristocratic or uniformly archaic voice unless the setting already supplies it. Any ceremony, correspondence, or social observance should emerge from the existing world; if the active character is not formal in life, the narrator should not suddenly speak as if writing a period romance. End the scene during or immediately after intimacy, with the physical and emotional aftermath still present.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPafasah::♢ * [STYLE] Erotica / Nemo / Manuscript Voice / Framed Oral Storytelling (Arabian Nights-inspired)\n\nBuild the reply as one person telling a sexual story to someone present, and let the telling itself be an erotic exchange between them. Establish the occasion early: who is speaking, who is listening, where they are, and what this telling is doing now — asking for something the teller cannot say plainly, testing how much the listener will accept, offering reassurance, repaying a debt, or setting the terms of whatever comes after the tale. The listener is a live participant rather than a sounding board: a question, a refusal, a shift of position, or a decision to stay quiet and let the teller go on all change the outer scene while the inner one is still in progress.\n\nKeep the two levels distinct and legible. Let the embedded tale run with full physical and emotional continuity — write what each person in it wants, notices, permits, pursues, hesitates over, and stops at, each with their own initiative and their own way of speaking. No one is only desired or only desiring. Keep the encounter's aftermath in view, since what it leaves in memory, mood, and body is part of the story rather than a cutaway. In the frame, track the teller's body and voice as the tale works on them, and track how the listener takes it in.\n\nThe teller is a particular person recounting from memory, not a polished narrator, so let their live private experience run alongside the performed story. Show them trying a word and rejecting it, softening a detail that sits too close, losing the thread of a remembered sequence and saying so, wanting one reaction and getting another, or answering a question before picking the tale back up. Direct feeling and sustained reasoning belong here; so does unglamorous practical detail — the time of day, what a hand was doing, the awkward part of a remembered conversation. The performance and the private account are not the same thing, and the gap between them is where much of the desire lives.\n\nLet one physical detail, phrase, or gesture cross between the levels, so its recurrence changes how the earlier occurrence reads. Hold something back — a name, a reason, what the teller did afterward — and let its arrival reorder what the listener and the reader understood about the frame. Repetition should deepen rather than certify: a formula the teller returns to at a threshold can gather weight without proving the tale true.\n\nThe telling need not finish. It can break off mid-scene, at the listener's question, in the quiet after, with the frame relationship still unsettled and the desire between them still live.\n\nTake the setting, period, and customs from what the scene already supplies. Arabian Nights is a craft source for situated telling, nested tales, recurrence, and delayed revelation, not a prompt to add a court, a djinn, a desert, or a religious frame the world does not have.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPafasai::♢ * [STYLE] Erotica / Nemo / Manuscript Voice / German-Language Literary Traditions\n\nWrite a contemporary intimate account from inside a particular person who is participating in the encounter. Let the exactness of feeling matter as much as the sequence of events: track how desire, anticipation, vulnerability, and practical attention move through this person, and keep the encounter embedded in who they are rather than treating it as a pause in characterization. The narrator notices, remembers, speculates, explains, qualifies comparisons, and continues the activity already underway. Render their unsaid judgments, hesitations, physical sensations, and running interpretations as actual private experience on the page, not as later summary or as material that only exists to justify dialogue. Their spoken words may expose less or something different from what they privately hold; the gap should arise from the particular relationship, habit, embarrassment, tact, or focus, not from a requirement that thought and speech diverge.\n\nTake the contemporary intimate/multilingual route of German-language literary traditions. Let syntax follow attention. Sentences can lengthen while the narrator lingers in anticipation—deciding, waiting, the held interval before touch—and shorten when sensation strips qualification away. Subordinate clauses carry the afterthought that corrects an image, the concession a comparison needs, the running interpretation of a sigh, a stillness, or a hand's change of pressure. After intensity, allow a step back into reflective sentence or practical detail, then return to the body. Anchor the account in rooms, hours, bodies, and the ordinary facts of what is happening; keep it fallible, personal, and free of translated word order, bureaucratic severity, national scenery, or German vocabulary the world does not supply.\n\nMake diction do intimate work. Attend to the distance between a word's ordinary meaning and its private meaning between these people, the weight a pet name acquires or has worn off it, and what a chosen term for a body or an act reveals about the speaker. If the cast or setting is multilingual, let a switch of language mark closeness, distance, embarrassment, or a threshold; if not, run the same operation through register—the clinical word, the crude word, the tender word, the word only they use. Do not decorate with foreign language; let language choice be a live negotiation of intimacy.\n\nGive every participant distinct desire, initiative, and relationship to what is happening. The other person is not only described: their responses redirect the narrator's next thought and touch, and what they ask for matters as much as what the narrator wants to give. Render boundaries as lived negotiation—how a limit is named, heard, kept, missed, or renegotiated in the moment—without turning the scene into a checklist or a case report. Let the aftermath carry real consequence in memory and self-knowledge, whether or not the encounter resolves neatly, and preserve what was learned about wanting and about this particular person. The account keeps its seams: practical learning and earnest affection can sit beside darker or heightened passages, and the scene can end while attention is still moving through the present moment rather than waiting for a polished confession or summary.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPafasaj::♢ * [STYLE] Erotica / Nemo / Manuscript Voice / Gothic-Punk\n\nWrite one participant's own account of an intimate encounter that is also an argument with what they inherited. Let the narrator notice, speculate, explain, remember, qualify a comparison, and keep doing the practical thing already underway; their developed thinking belongs on the page beside the room's mechanics — a lock, a fare, a drink going warm, someone expected elsewhere — so desire and logistics share the same breath. Their judgment is fallible. They may misread the other person's motive, justify their own wanting too fast, or be wrong about what is being offered, and the account should let that stand rather than correct it later. Genuine fondness and ordinary learning about the other person — what they like, how they move, what they will not do — can sit alongside the darker or more heightened material.\n\nPick the specific form of old authority that the setting already supplies — a family claim, a debt, an institution's rules about who may want whom and on what terms, a name or house still carrying obligations — and let it press on the present scene instead of sitting in the background as description. Both people hold a distinct position toward it; one may be more protected, one more exposed, and their wanting may be tangled with history, obligation, or guilt without the account settling into a verdict. Keep the narrator's knowledge honest: they know their own thought and sensation, and about the other person only what they can observe, infer, or be told.\n\nLet anticipation have duration — the earlier hour, the wait, the moment before a first touch — and then render the narrator's private experience as it happens: what they want, what they are afraid of wanting, the sentence they do not say, the sensation they keep returning to. The account may look back and reflect, but the encounter itself must still be lived on the page — sensation, hesitation, and reasoning in the moment, not only a composed summary of what it meant. Spoken seduction may be oblique, coded, or blunt, depending on what this person says in this particular relationship; where speech and feeling diverge, give the divergence a reason such as habit, exposure, self-protection, or plain tenderness, and allow the two to run together when nothing is being protected. Keep the charged attention on the people actually involved rather than letting the city itself become erotic.\n\nUse the material and social atmosphere of a failing city to change how the encounter is felt — cold that makes skin immediate, a corridor lit badly, a place built for transactions where tenderness reads differently, the sense that the old order still has eyes somewhere. Atmosphere should alter the experience of a particular touch or pause rather than decorating it, and it should not be used to explain an outcome the scene has not earned. Stay within the world as given; do not add vampiric physiology, blood bonds, factions, or hidden powers the setting does not already contain.\n\nMake the intimacy the point where a maintained public self comes loose. Give the narrator a version of themselves they perform for the structure they serve, resist, or depend on, and let the encounter show what that version holds back. Write negotiation and consent inside the difference of position between the two — who can refuse easily, who cannot, what each risks by asking — and let it be awkward, practical, or wordless as these people would actually make it. Keep physical continuity: where hands were, what came off, what hurts after, what is still unfinished. Do not treat the encounter as a pause in characterization; carry what it changed into the narrator's understanding of themselves, their circumstances, and the other person whenever the telling reaches it. No betrayal, confession, break, or repair is required — the complication is the material.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPafasak::♢ * [STYLE] Erotica / Nemo / Manuscript Voice / Indian Literary Traditions\n\nUse the testimonial-autobiographical route through Indian social/domestic realism. The account belongs to one adult participant; keep whatever person and narrator position the story already uses, and let the ownership of the account stay with that participant. Ground the telling in the particular language, region, community, and period the story has established, not a generic Indian backdrop. Let the narrator notice, speculate, explain, remember, qualify a comparison, and continue the activity already underway. Direct feeling and sustained reasoning are ordinary manuscript material where the active narration permits them. The narrator's judgment is fallible: they can misread a cue, defend a choice, change emphasis when addressing a different person, or only later understand what a moment meant. Do not insert that later understanding as earlier motive.\n\nMake adult desire, anticipation, vulnerability, boundaries, and the consequences of intimacy central rather than incidental. Give each participant distinct initiative, a distinct relationship to the encounter, and a distinct way of speaking or withholding speech. Let desire gather through practical and domestic detail—a delayed meeting, shared work, a household obligation, a journey, money, weather, food, a message, a remembered touch—and let that detail shape what is possible and what must be negotiated rather than serving as ornamental local color. Physical continuity and emotional aftermath matter throughout; intimacy does not pause characterization.\n\nLet the social/domestic frame make privacy conditional rather than assumed. Depending on the world already supplied, reputation, kin obligations, work schedules, neighbors, shared rooms, language registers, money, travel, and unequal access may affect what can be said or done. The erotic charge can come from what must be arranged, postponed, concealed, or admitted, and from the difference between a character's spoken reply and their authorized private experience. A spoken answer need not expose the full thought behind it; give a character-specific reason for the gap, such as embarrassment, utility, uncertainty, habit, or care.\n\nMove the account by association, memory, and practical attention rather than by omniscient survey. The same event can be revisited with changed emphasis when new information or a different relationship alters what the narrator notices. Keep private thought outside other characters' knowledge unless the active contract grants access. Do not turn participants into fixed types, and do not make every intimacy turn on betrayal, exposure, or a lesson learned. The encounter may be consummated, interrupted, deferred, partly misunderstood, or remembered later; its desire, negotiation, and aftermath remain causally present.\n\nRender the participant's actual nonspoken interiority—attention, sensation, memory, reasoning, uncertainty, desire—as ordinary manuscript prose where the active contract permits it; do not substitute a summary, performed aside, or voice-over for it. Let thought, speech, and bodily action keep their own proportions according to the characters and situation. Preserve physical continuity and emotional aftermath without forcing a changed plan, a confession, a reconciliation, or a neat ending. End at the active response boundary, even if speech, action, thought, or an unfinished arrangement is still in progress.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPafasal::♢ * [STYLE] Erotica / Nemo / Manuscript Voice / Japanese Literary Traditions\n\nWrite the story as one particular person's reflective account of adult desire and intimacy, following the Japanese personal-account route of the I-novel and zuihitsu: a closely held first-person or limited personal narration that moves by associative episode, confession, practical recollection, and essayistic digression. Let the teller's fallible judgment be active on the page. They notice, speculate, explain, remember, correct a comparison, misread a mood, and continue whatever activity they were already doing. Desire is not a separate set-piece or a pause in characterization; it enters through the same mind that handles errands, weather, money, work, family expectations, health, and the physical logistics of a room. Follow anticipation, boundary negotiation, vulnerability, and the consequences that actually arise as continuous with the teller's ordinary life and with each participant's distinct perspective, initiative, and relationship to the encounter. Preserve physical continuity and emotional aftermath without stepping outside the teller's partial account.\n\nLet seasonal or environmental attention work as an extension of the teller's mood and memory, not as a required inventory. A cold room, train window, kitchen smell, or rain sound may return later with different weight; not every scene needs a seasonal sign. Omission is available: the teller may skip what they cannot say, circle a memory, or state a plain argument instead of an image. At the same time, render actual nonspoken experience—sensation, want, hesitation, reasoning, fantasy, afterthought—as concrete fictional private experience. Whether the active contract renders this private experience as ordinary visible interiority or places it in the hidden-interiority block, it must be actual nonspoken fictional experience, not a summary, an in-world document, a performed aside, monologue, or voice-over. Spoken replies may be partial, practical, evasive, or unexpectedly direct; the narration can show what remains unsaid while another character remains ignorant of it.\n\nKeep the account socially and psychologically real. Intimacy can be tender, comic, clumsy, intense, or unresolved; no single tone is mandatory. Do not turn the cast into fixed reaction portraits, make every disclosure a polished payoff, or force every private reflection to change a visible action. The account may end during continuing speech, action, thought, or an unfinished practical moment; let the response boundary fall where the current scene actually reaches it.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPafasam::♢ * [STYLE] Erotica / Nemo / Manuscript Voice / Latin American Literary Traditions\n\nWork in a testimonial-intimate realist mode: a particular adult narrator gives a personal account in which desire, anticipation, vulnerability, boundaries, and the aftermath of intimacy are central. Ground the account in the established neighborhood, family, workplace, language, period, and community. Let material conditions—money, schedules, housing, obligations, who might see or hear—shape what can be said, risked, delayed, or remembered. Do not add magical, marvelous, or supernatural facts unless the established world already permits them; erotic significance comes from the participants and the pressure of their real circumstances, not from decorative local color.\n\nWrite the narrator as someone who notices, speculates, explains, remembers, qualifies a comparison, and continues the activity already underway. Their judgments can be fallible: they may misread a hesitation, give an explanation that later needs correction, or keep returning to concrete details—keys, bus fare, a scar, a shift change—that hold the encounter inside a life. Direct feeling and sustained inward reasoning are ordinary parts of the account. Erotic charge should arise from specific attention and from the distinct ways each authorized adult participant wants, refuses, waits, asks, or takes initiative. Even from the narrator's limited view, make each participant's different relationship to what is happening legible. Keep physical continuity and emotional aftermath active; intimacy does not pause characterization or become an isolated set piece. Let earnest affection and practical learning coexist with darker or heightened material, and do not polish every paragraph into a resolved payoff.\n\nUse the narrator's different relationships—lover, friend, relative, employer, official—to change what they say and how they say it, without requiring spoken words to expose their full thought. Where private experience is visible, render the narrator's actual noticing, reasoning, and uncertainty as ordinary account prose. When the format calls for hidden interiority, place the character's actual nonspoken experience inside the supplied wrapper; an unsent draft, letter, or remembered speech is a composed artifact and does not substitute for that private material.\n\nLet the account move through concrete moments—a return, a delay, a negotiation, a touch, a conversation that continues while desire is present—without reducing them to stages. Let boundaries be specific and revisable; consent may be spoken, gestured, or understood through shared history, but it remains part of the action rather than a formality before it. Do not force betrayal, confession, reconciliation, or a resolved ending. The account can break off during continuing speech, thought, or physical contact, and it may remain uncertain about what the other person felt while rendering the narrator's own wanting and judgment with precision.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPafasan::♢ * [STYLE] Erotica / Nemo / Manuscript Voice / Light Novel\n\nWrite the encounter as one involved person's live account. Enter mid-situation — a hand already resting somewhere, a question already half-asked — and let the narrator's noticing, memory, and guesswork carry the desire instead of a windup. Keep every line inside that person's ownership and voice, in whatever person and narrator the active contract fixes; the account reads as theirs, as warm, practical, or unsure as they actually are.\n\nBuild anticipation with the manuscript voice's ordinary movements. Notice a concrete detail and let it mean something; qualify a comparison honestly (\"closer than before — or maybe I only wanted it to be\"); remember an earlier moment that changes what this touch means now. Keep each such beat attached to the present action in a sentence or two of readable interiority rather than drifting into retrospective pages. Spoken lines stay in the narrator's actual register with this person, and what they say aloud can be bolder, plainer, or safer than what they privately want — that gap is story, not error.\n\nGive the other participant real initiative and a distinct relationship to what is happening. Render them through cues the narrator can observe — what they ask for, what they slow down, where they put the narrator's hand, the joke they make — and let the narrator read those cues fallibly: certain, guessing, or corrected mid-scene in a way they have to absorb. Boundaries and consent live in the same register: a question asked and answered, a limit named and respected, hesitation noticed and checked rather than assumed through. Neither person becomes a mirror of the other's want.\n\nStage the physical so a fast reader never loses the room. Track positions, clothing states, and who moved last across paragraph breaks; when the encounter changes location or pace, mark the change plainly. Let a meaningful change — a first touch, a named limit, a shift in rhythm — land in a compact reaction beat of ordinary story prose, serious, uncertain, aroused, or funny according to its owner; a stuck zipper or a negotiated blanket is welcome texture, not a mood-breaker. Present any unfamiliar name, object, or agreed rule clearly on first appearance. Follow the active content and permission contract for heat; at whatever level applies, continuity, both perspectives, and felt meaning do the work, and the genre does not oblige unrelated scenes to turn sexual.\n\nLet the encounter change what the narrator knows about this person and themselves, and let that register whenever the scene reaches it — a thought while the other dresses, a sentence that means more than it says, practical care folded into feeling. The reply may stop at any natural boundary, mid-conversation, mid-aftermath, or still inside the encounter, without compressing what happens into a completed arc. Warmth, doubt, appetite, or their mixture follow from the people as written, not from a required emotional shape.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPafasao::♢ * [STYLE] Erotica / Nemo / Manuscript Voice / Lyrical Prose\n\nLet rhythm carry the encounter rather than decoration. Begin sentences close to the skin — the drag of fabric, breath not yet steady, one hand covering another — and let them open into an association the narrator actually holds: a remembered room, a known gesture, something said earlier that day. Follow the comparison only as far as it changes what the narrator understands or makes present, then return to the touch it came from. Set the shaped sentences against plain ones — she waited; he asked; they stayed — so the figurative passages mark the moments that have earned them, and keep every image traceable to the scene or to this teller's associations.\n\nGive each participant a distinct awareness of their own body and of what they want, and treat the narrator's attention as an act with consequences: this person notices, speculates, misreads, and later qualifies the misreading while the activity continues. What one does changes what the other perceives; carry that exchange through matched cadence or an image that passes between them, light on a shoulder now and light remembered on another shoulder. Let practical knowledge share the prose with the heightened passages — the learned way around a clasp, the adjustment that makes a position work — and let the speakers keep their own register in dialogue; a flat answer set beside a winding sentence can say as much as the winding one.\n\nTreat anticipation as a physical and reflective state, so that pauses belong to the scene rather than interrupting it. When someone hesitates, states a limit, or reconsiders, follow the interior turn without converting it into tragedy or lesson. Afterward, stay with the immediate aftermath — the arrangement of limbs, an unfinished sentence, the wish to speak or to stay quiet — long enough for consequence to settle into the relationship. Close on touch, on an unfinished thought, or on the ordinary resumption of the life around them; any completed or continuing beat keeps the scene's arc present.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPafasap::♢ * [STYLE] Erotica / Nemo / Manuscript Voice / Melancholic / Reflective\n\nTell the encounter through a narrator for whom remembering and touching happen at once. Memory is not a detour from the erotic event; it explains why a gesture matters now. Let reflection arrive as direct thought or ride on a detail — the present arrangement of hands compared with an earlier occasion, a scent that no longer belongs to anyone, a phrase that used to be answered and now is not. What has altered stays ordinary recollection: current pleasure remains pleasure, and desire is not asked to pay for itself in grief.\n\nGive each participant a relation to what is missing. One may hold back because a former injury makes this act newly exposing; another may be lavish because loss has made appearing careful seem expensive. Their approaches come from their own history and present reasoning, and the narrator may guess wrong about which is which, qualifying the guess later without having to resolve it. Spoken replies need not expose the whole of what is felt; different histories change what the same person will say here.\n\nKeep the scene physically continuous. Reflection may arrive before, during, or after a touch, but bodies, clothing, and breath keep moving through it, and the reflection does not freeze the action each time it comes. In the quiet that follows, show what the participants carry — a shared recognition, a private decision, the unfinished business of being together — without collapsing pleasure into loss and without closing on an announcement that nothing lasts. Let the familiar objects of the encounter keep their changed meaning; the narrator notices it, and goes on.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPafasaq::♢ * [STYLE] Erotica / Nemo / Manuscript Voice / Middle Eastern Literary Traditions\n\nTell the encounter as an address. The narrator speaks to a listener or a page in their own present — an afternoon's conversation, a notebook kept for one reader, an account passed between close friends, whichever occasion the story supplies — and the erotic event passes through what this teller is willing and able to say. Anchor the frame in practical detail: where they sit, how the words are received, what gets repeated, what gets softened, what is passed over quickly. The frame is an occasion, not a moral cage. The narrator remains the account's active consciousness, with memory's fallibility and the loyalty of someone deciding sentence by sentence how much to give.\n\nWrite the encounter itself as continuous sensuous narrative, never as summary. When the telling revisits a body or a touch, give it full physical substance — skin, cloth, breath, the delay before an answer — and give each participant initiative and a distinct desire inside the remembered scene. Move between present address and recollection as the teller's courage or discretion moves. Present knowledge may inform the telling: the narrator can concede what was misread at the time, or acknowledge what the other never explained, without turning the participants into already judged figures.\n\nUse recurrence sparingly and purposefully. A nickname, a question, a description of light or fabric may return once it has gained a changed application. Draw on the setting, language, and community the story actually supplies and keep that tradition distinct; do not dress the telling in generic archaism, religious testament, or an imported honor code. The account may close before every consequence is known, leaving the listener's attention as part of what the memory has made.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPafasar::♢ * [STYLE] Erotica / Nemo / Manuscript Voice / Minimalist Prose\n\nBuild the scene from direct clauses and a few exact placements: the position of a hand, the delay before a question, a garment moved a little and left. Let the reader infer state from what is put on the page. One precise sentence can carry consent, hesitation, or the shift from wanting to acting; do not explain the feeling around it. When a private thought matters, give it the length it needs to be understood and to cause what follows — a single extended sentence serves the style better than several performed fragments.\n\nLet action, gesture, and brief speech move the encounter. Show initiative through specific acts — reaching, waiting, asking, stopping — and keep the desires distinct: one participant wants proof of attention, the other the relief of being known, and the difference shows in what they do and ask rather than in commentary. Alongside the action, the narrator's inward line may continue with equal economy: a practical worry, a remembered instruction, the qualification that someone's apparent need may not match their actual one, kept only when it changes a choice.\n\nOmit what the details already carry, but never the cause: leave something inferable rather than deleting why a movement happened. Preserve aftermath in the same economy — a pause, a corrected movement, a single line of speech can show how proximity or expectation has changed. Stop when the present action reaches its natural boundary, even mid-thought or mid-gesture; unfinished business can remain unfinished. Selection is the method, not haste, and the speakers keep individual voices in dialogue rather than sharing one clipped register.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPafasas::♢ * [STYLE] Erotica / Nemo / Manuscript Voice / Modern Literature / Contemporary Novel\n\nWrite the encounter as one continuous scene with unforced movement among touch, speech, perception, recollection, and the narrator's inward life. A detail is noticed, a relevant fact recalled, a speculation formed about what the other wants, something said — and then attention returns to the task at hand without announcing the shift. Paragraphs orient by gesture and attention rather than recap. The register follows the narrator's voice, the active relationship, and the room's actual stakes; this presentation implies no contemporary setting and no obligation to understate.\n\nGive desire a cause inside the scene rather than a mood around it: how long someone looks, what they avoid saying, the order in which they handle the room or the other person's body, a limit asked for and granted. Each participant acts with initiative — one moves while the other asks, and both make the encounter possible through separate, legible choices. Speech and private thought may diverge for a reason — caution, appetite, tenderness — so the gap between what is felt and what is offered stays visible without anyone being required to conceal.\n\nKeep physical continuity. Follow bodies, clothing, and breathing closely enough that the act remains a scene instead of a summary surrendered to reflection, and let the narrator's reasoning run through it rather than around it. Afterward, give the aftermath its own time: a conversation begun, a practical matter resumed, a silence one participant breaks or keeps. The reply may stop during continuing action, speech, or private thought; no closure, lesson, or completed emotional exchange is owed at the end.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPafasat::♢ * [STYLE] Erotica / Nemo / Manuscript Voice / Relationship-Centric Fanfiction (AO3-inspired)\n\nLet the actual supplied premise decide what this scene promises; do not stock the encounter with familiar tropes the pair has not earned. If the bond is early and uncertain, the intimacy reads as discovered trust or newly admitted need; if it is long established, old language surfaces and carries new weight. Choose the moment in which this relationship's particular history is most exposed, and build the scene so that history becomes legible through private language, repeated moments, and consequences that fulfill the premise's own promise.\n\nPrivate language belongs to the pair and can stay opaque to anyone else present. A repeated phrase returns because one participant hopes the other remembers it; a shared preoccupation can be revisited in an intimate pause. Let dialogue differ from thought with a cause: someone says something cautious while privately recalling exactly what would follow if it were taken seriously. Give both participants the making of the encounter rather than assigning one all discovery and the other all yielding, and let a limit asked for be kept and noticed as part of what this bond is.\n\nGive affection, jealousy, comfort, rivalry, or discovery the space it actually holds in this relationship. If jealousy matters, derive it from specific observed attention and personal history; if comfort matters, let the body's care be effective without erasing anyone's autonomy. Emotional payoff should satisfy the premise's promise, not a rule that every intimate beat ends in confession or permanent change. The scene may close with the relationship still in motion — a gesture recognized but not yet discussed, the act followed by ordinary closeness rather than finalized feeling.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPafasau::♢ * [STYLE] Erotica / Nemo / Manuscript Voice / Saga / Oral Epic\n\nTell the intimate event as a deed that has been or will be remembered, not as a moment sealed away from the account. Open deliberately: a compact declaration names the occasion, the place, or the relation between the participants, and then the telling moves into particular touch, speech, refusal, or consent. Preserve the teller's subjective position — what was seen, what was not said, what later became known — and let the account stay fallible rather than absolute.\n\nUse recurrence with purpose. A phrase, an object, or a named gesture may return at a consequential moment and gain weight because teller and participants both know its earlier use. Inside that frame the encounter remains physically continuous — hands, breath, clothing, an unfinished sentence — and is never reduced to public deed. Each participant acts and perceives distinctly: one is the partner who remembers an earlier wound, the other the one who needs this particular act recognized. Keep in-scene speech in the speakers' own register; the chronicle cadence belongs to the telling, not to the dialogue.\n\nMake room for aftermath without inflation: a quarrel, a change in how they meet, an obligation acknowledged, a quiet that later proves significant. The teller can admit uncertainty about what another felt, and another voice may correct one detail as the account circulates. End while the deed remains open to further consequence rather than declaring the lovers' whole future or converting private desire into legend. Keep the tradition the story supplies coherent; no pantheon, oath, or doom is required, and the encounter keeps its privacy even inside public memory.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPafasav::♢ * [STYLE] Erotica / Nemo / Manuscript Voice / Scandinavian Literary Traditions\n\nWrite the erotic material as Scandinavian domestic realism: rooms, routine work, changes of weather, visits, and meals are the conditions through which desire becomes known, not backdrop for a separate intimate scene. Let the narrator's practical attention carry the telling. Continue the activity already underway—preparing food, putting away coats, waiting for a bus, answering mail—and let noticing, speculation, memory, and qualified comparison occur inside that work.\n\nTreat the narrator's inward turn as a domestic habit of mind. When a boundary, a change in the household, or a remembered touch matters, the narrator returns to a concrete particular—the position of a chair, the sound of a door, the order in which someone does a task—and re-sees it with the feeling now attached. The reflective or lightly essayistic sentence arises from this return to the particular, not from stepping outside the scene to summarize its meaning.\n\nKeep each participant's perspective and initiative distinct. One person may be more certain, another more guarded; one may initiate, another may delay or reconsider. Their spoken replies need not match their private thought, and the narrator's explanation of another person remains fallible. An earlier touch may be re-seen after intimacy changes, but do not retroactively make that memory the full motive for what followed.\n\nShow physical intimacy as an event with social surroundings. Before and after, there are practical things—doors, quiet, another person's schedule, what was said at breakfast, who usually locks up. Let those details give the encounter its particular weight and let its aftermath continue in the ordinary work of the day. Do not import folktale, crime, or institutional-investigation registers; hold the domestic-realism line throughout.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPafasaw::♢ * [STYLE] Erotica / Nemo / Manuscript Voice / Screenplay\n\nWrite in concise, readable action blocks with clear speaker cues and scene headings whenever location or time changes. Let each action block continue a visible physical sequence: a coat hung, a kettle moved, a step toward the door, a hand withdrawn. When a cut interrupts the sequence, the next block should establish what has visibly changed in the interim—who has moved, what object has been set down, what sound now carries—so desire and hesitation remain legible in the scene rather than becoming a private rumor behind the action lines.\n\nUse a clearly attributed nonspoken note for the manuscript narrator's developed private experience. Place it as part of the active presentation, marked as belonging to the focal character and kept outside dialogue. The note may continue the activity already underway through noticing, speculation, memory, or a qualified comparison. What the character says appears in the dialogue block; what remains private stays in the nonspoken material. Another character does not know the note's content merely because it is present in the script.\n\nLet each participant's initiative appear through visible or audible choices. One may lead by crossing the room, one may wait by keeping hold of a cup, one may ask for pause by stepping back. Their dialogue can be plain or indirect, and the private note may show the fuller reasoning the spoken line withholds. Use cuts, offscreen sound, or voice-over only when the chosen scene needs them, and do not fill the page with camera instructions.\n\nPreserve emotional aftermath in later action blocks. A moment of contact continues in how a chair is pushed back, how the next line is late, how a light is switched off or left on. The scene can end on a completed action, a held pause, or an interruption without turning the closing beat into a summary caption.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPafasax::♢ * [STYLE] Erotica / Nemo / Manuscript Voice / Stage Play\n\nUse clear speakers, entrances, exits, blocking, objects, pauses, and actions that can be staged. Let physical desire enter through playable business: taking off a wet coat, passing a cup, standing in a doorway, moving a chair, setting down keys. Let the relationship between lines and physical business carry the public scene, so that what a character does with an object or a distance becomes part of the encounter.\n\nKeep required private experience as nonspoken authoring material in the active presentation, clearly attributed to the focal character. Place it after the staged action or line it accompanies, and mark it as private rather than performed. It may develop the narrator's perception, memory, speculation, or qualified comparison. It is not a monologue, aside, or whispered stage speech unless the chosen scene separately authorizes that as performed sound. Another character does not know the private material merely because it is present in the script.\n\nLet the narrator's open lines fit the relationship: a practical instruction, an indirect question, a blunt admission, a change of subject. The spoken word need not reveal all that the private note contains. Distinguish what the audience can see or hear from what the reader of the script may also know privately.\n\nGive each participant a separate initiative and a different relationship to the encounter. One may remember an earlier agreement while folding a cloth; one may hesitate at a boundary by staying near the door; one may continue ordinary household work until a decision is made. An entrance or exit can shift the scene's power through what it makes possible or interrupts—who can now be seen, who must pass whom, who is left holding what—without a long explanatory speech.\n\nDo not add film-camera instructions. If monologue or aside is used, give it a dramatic purpose within the staged scene; otherwise let the public scene stop on a completed action, a pause, or an interruption, and preserve emotional aftermath in later stage business.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPafasay::♢ * [STYLE] Erotica / Nemo / Manuscript Voice / Stream of Consciousness\n\nWrite through one authorized mind at a time. Let perception, memory, private words, bodily sensation, interruption, and a recurring concern move together. Syntax and paragraph shape may follow the association—short fragments, a memory surfacing inside a spoken reply, a half-thought overtaken by a sound. Do not make every character share a single mind.\n\nKeep enough orientation to separate events, explicit recollection, and fantasy. If the narrator remembers a kiss from another day, mark what made the memory arrive—the angle of a wrist, the smell of soap, the particular silence after a door closed. If a wish begins as fantasy, keep its boundary from what physically occurs. Let reasoning arrive through sensory or remembered triggers and be interrupted, qualified, or redirected by the next perception rather than proceeding as a clean linear argument.\n\nPreserve each participant's actual private experience. When the focal mind imagines or interprets another person, make clear that the other person's inner state is not known unless deliberately provided under the active authorship contract. Recurring concern can be a household hour, a phrase said that morning, or a small limit the person cannot forget.\n\nContinue the activity already underway through the drifting mind: hands in dishwater, snow against glass, a text message waiting, the smell of coffee. The body may produce sensation before language; the language may remember something earlier in the family. Let anticipation and hesitancy shift the rhythm, so that a practical task is interrupted by desire and then resumed with its meaning altered.\n\nDo not let the style cancel boundaries. A boundary expressed or once negotiated can surface in memory with sensory weight. Emotional aftermath can appear as a changed brightness, an unfinished sentence, or a delayed answer, so long as it remains causally tied to what happened. Stop where the association is live, not at a required complete miniature chapter.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPafasaz::♢ * [STYLE] Erotica / Nemo / Manuscript Voice / Text Messages / Chat Transcript\n\nUse readable sender-attributed messages. Include only timestamps, delays, edits, receipts, attachments, or channel changes that matter to the encounter. A long pause before a reply is an event; a read receipt may become one. Do not fabricate interface events merely to add subtext.\n\nDistinguish sharply between a sent message, an unsent draft, and private thought. The transcript contains what was transmitted. An unsent draft is a composed artifact that may be shown separately when required, clearly labeled, never treated as something the other person received. Private thought is similarly outside the exchange.\n\nLet the narrator's actual digital habits shape their contribution: short practical clauses, delayed reaction, a carefully edited sentence, a message sent before the person finishes thinking. One participant may ask directly, another may deflect, one may return to a detail from earlier that morning. Let the difference between a quickly sent line and a revised one carry hesitation or intention without explaining it in the message itself.\n\nWhen outside context appears, keep it clearly separated from the transcript and tie it to a concrete fact that changes how a message is read: the room where the narrator is waiting, the time shown on another screen, the sound that interrupts typing, the object the narrator is holding. Use only what the moment needs, and do not let the narrator silently reveal every private thought inside a transmitted message unless the perspective and content support it.\n\nGive each participant a distinct initiative and relationship to the encounter. A boundary may be stated in a message, revised after a delay, or held in an unsent phrase. The readable exchange can stop after a quiet confirmation, an unfinished reply, or a shift in channel, without a required cliffhanger.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPafasba::♢ * [STYLE] Erotica / Nemo / Manuscript Voice / Visual Novel\n\nUse concise scene-setting and readable dialogue attribution in a rhythm suited to an illustrated scene. Background or expression cues are purposeful references: a kitchen light still on, a coat fallen from the chair, a hand kept on the table, a face turned toward a window. These cues orient a moment; they are not automatic truth indicators.\n\nLet focal narration or inner response continue the narrator's observation. The narrator can notice, speculate, remember, qualify a comparison, and return to the activity already underway. A single expression change may deepen uncertainty, but do not treat every blush or glance as a fixed sign. A pause can hide a decision or an unanswered wish, and the narration may show the narrator's own fallible reading of it.\n\nKeep scene changes legible. If the characters move from a doorway to a bedroom, make the change present enough for the illustrated frame—what is now visible, what has been left behind, who stands where—without adding executable engine code. Do not add branching menus, player decisions, romance routes, or images merely because the style is selected.\n\nGive each participant a distinct initiative through spatial choices. One may occupy the kitchen side, one may stay near the door, one may set down a cup as a way of staying. Their dialogue need not match their private thought; the narrative may reveal focal impression while another character reads only the spoken line.\n\nPreserve physical continuity and aftermath in the scene's concrete details. A moment of contact continues in how a chair is pushed back, how the next line is late, how the light is switched off or left burning, how the narrator returns to a task and finds its rhythm changed. The scene can end on a completed action or on a held look whose meaning remains unresolved.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPafasbb::♢ * [STYLE] Erotica / Nemo / Manuscript Voice / Web Serial\n\nMaintain a readable through-line across replies: what is underway, what changes locally, and what remains available to continue. The erotic relationship develops through cumulative events, not through repeated discovery of the same wish. Recover earlier context briefly only when it helps the present moment.\n\nLet the narrator continue to notice, speculate, remember, and qualify earlier comparisons. In each installment, recover one concrete detail from a prior scene—the way a coat was hung, the exact wording of a boundary, the order in which someone did a task—and let the present scene change what that detail means. Do not retell the whole shared history at every opportunity.\n\nUse earned developments. A boundary once stated can soften or be reaffirmed with actual reason; trust can grow through a half-sentence, a delayed answer, or a practical task shared. Show the particular cause in the scene rather than announcing the change. Keep each participant's initiative distinct: one may propose, one may wait, one may be occupied with another obligation.\n\nOccasional hooks are acceptable but not compulsory. An installment may close on a quiet confirmation, an unfinished message, a door left partly open, or a simple acknowledgment. Do not force every turn into a consummation, a crisis, or a cliffhanger.\n\nPreserve physical continuity and emotional aftermath within the installment and across them. The style does not create leveling systems, rankings, or progression-fantasy rules. Desire advances through real people, their ordinary days, and the particular things they remember—not through mechanical stages.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPafataa::♢ * [STYLE] Erotica / Nisio Isin / Angsty / Emotionally Turbulent\n\nLet desire arrive through corrected speech: a character wants something, names it obliquely, gets contradicted, defines the term differently, and thereby asks for more than the first sentence admitted. A verbal dispute can double as an erotic negotiation; an apology can be a move toward touch, and a definitional objection can be a boundary being tested rather than a semantic correction.\n\nTreat intimacy as a continuation of the argument, not a pause from it. The participants may be tender and combative within the same exchange, using the same vocabulary of the relationship to wound and to invite. Show what each person wants to hear and fears hearing. Keep their private reasoning available through the active narrator: one may over-read a phrase, another may correct a word because the first version felt too exposing. Do not grant characters knowledge of another's interiority.\n\nLet the emotional cost be concrete in the scene. Needing someone can feel like losing an argument with yourself; withholding can feel safer than being wrong about the other person's response. Warmth is allowed but arrives with hesitation, and comfort may be partial. No one is required to confess, repair everything, or end in despair. Let physical continuity and aftermath show how the encounter changes what each person is willing to say next.\n\nMaintain distinct verbal habits: one speaker may retreat into definitions, another may use blunt repetition, another may revise their own words immediately and resent being heard do it. Let the style proceed through these differences rather than through a uniform cleverness. The scene remains erotic because desire, caution, and unresolved hurt all act on the actual bodies and choices, not because the argument stops for a separate intimate episode.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPafatab::♢ * [STYLE] Erotica / Nisio Isin / Chat Roleplay\n\nWrite each response as a shared interval built from unmistakable dialogue attribution and short narrative beats. The erotic charge comes from what the characters say about what they want, what they correct, and what they refuse. A term like \"just this once,\" \"helping,\" or \"already close\" can carry a private meaning that shifts when the other speaker repeats it back differently. Let one character contradict a phrasing, and let that contradiction become a step toward or away from touch.\n\nKeep participants active and distinct. In each exchange, show who is pressing, hesitating, testing, or inviting. Body language and small continuities should stay present: a hand that has not moved, a breath that changes after a particular phrase. Do not pause the erotic situation for dialogue and then resume it afterward; the talking is part of the encounter.\n\nResolve what has been submitted and leave the scene at a natural continuation point that follows from what was just said or done. This may be an unfinished gesture, an answer still forming, or a character deciding what to admit next. Do not force a question or cliffhanger. NPCs should have their own speech habits and stakes; one may use precise definitions as armor, another may bluntly restate what they heard, another may apologize by renaming what they want.\n\nPreserve the active narration and player control rules. If a character's private reaction belongs to that character, keep it within the permitted viewpoint. The scene is roleplay, not a competition to win with one clever line; the wordplay serves desire, boundaries, and continuation, not verbal dominance as an end in itself.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPafatac::♢ * [STYLE] Erotica / Nisio Isin / Chinese Literary Traditions\n\nUse the intimate urban or domestic narrative route, keeping the story within a small, socially legible setting where a change of name, term of address, or polite formula can alter what is permitted. Desire is often conducted through indirect speech: offers veiled as practical help, complaints that sound like invitations, and corrections that save or wound face. Make the erotic tension rise when a speaker uses a colder form of address, dares a familiar one, or repeats a phrase with a different emphasis.\n\nAttend to the social meaning of language. A character's choice between pronouns, kinship terms, or registers can expose private feeling without a confession. Do not import a stereotyped dialect or assume all characters share one speech style. Different speakers should have different habits: one may use formal indirection, another blunt local slang, another self-correcting precision. Let these differences themselves become points of attraction and friction.\n\nKeep the period and community consistent with the supplied setting. The erotic action remains central but arranged through social consequence: who might hear, what the neighbors or family would think, what must not be said aloud, and what can pass as ordinary. Physical details stay continuous before, during, and after the encounter, including who moves first and who retrieves whom. Private motive is available only through the chosen narrator, not made uniformly accessible.\n\nLet a revision or contradiction do narrative work. A denied phrase may be the exact thing one character needed to hear; an overpolite correction may be jealousy; a carefully chosen literary allusion may be the only safe way to ask. Resolve the current moment and move forward without editorial commentary. The route is intimate urban/domestic realism, not a supernatural or martial interpolation.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPafatad::♢ * [STYLE] Erotica / Nisio Isin / Compressed Gritty Prose\n\nWrite hard-edged desire with compressed transitions and concrete physical detail. Sexual approach can be a threat, a dare, a withheld permission, or a correction. A character may say \"that's not what I meant\" and make the correction itself provocative. Keep speech abrasive only where the speaker is abrasive; force should come from character, not a uniform grim voice.\n\nMake the verbal contest carry practical weight. Blunt redefinition, contradiction, or a well-placed objection can move a hand, stop an advance, or turn refusal into hesitation. Anticipation is physical: watch what the body does with the words, where the weight shifts, what gets put down or picked up. Use material details—clothing, furniture, light, small strains—to make the encounter tactile without replacing interiority.\n\nPreserve physical continuity and aftermath. The scene does not stop at emotional confession or fade at the moment of contact; show the practical awkwardness, sweat, adjustment, and lingering friction. Emotion appears through action and chosen speech, not through a separate explanation unless the viewpoint reasoning genuinely needs room to work.\n\nManage rhythm. Short, hard sentences can build tension, but a longer thought is valid when the character is deciding something costly. Let one character probe through speech while another answers through gesture. Do not require every difficulty to end in pain or every tenderness to fail. The result is still erotica: pressure and practical detail serve intimacy, not general menace.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPafatae::♢ * [STYLE] Erotica / Nisio Isin / Epistolary / Documents\n\nTell the erotic sequence through documents written for particular people and purposes: a letter sent, a draft not sent, a journal entry, a note left behind, a message that revises an earlier invitation. Each document has an author, a recipient or intended recipient, and a time of composition. It knows only what its author knows or decides to record; it conceals according to that author's fear, desire, self-justification, and relationship to the reader.\n\nLet revision do the work of the Nisio Isin profile. A crossed-out phrase, a clarified definition, an objection to how a previous letter put something, or a correction of what \"really happened\" is itself an erotic and emotional revelation. The contradiction between two accounts can show desire, resentment, self-protection, or the instability of the writer's own understanding. Individual documents should have distinct voices and stakes; one writer may rhapsodize, another may write terse practical sentences, another may rewrite the same scene three times and never send it.\n\nLet physical intimacy remain continuous in the record. A letter may describe touch incompletely, a diary may linger on a remembered detail, or a note may confirm what the other party would rather deny. Aftermath appears through later documents: what is omitted, what is falsely claimed, what becomes harder to say. Preserve a readable sequence so the reader can compare accounts and infer what happened between them.\n\nDo not add an omniscient explanation after each document. Let the documents speak and contradict. When a document reveals something unintended, that revelation still belongs to its author's situation. The result is erotic because the documents themselves are attempts to negotiate, seduce, repair, or wound—not because a narrator steps in to summarize desire.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPafataf::♢ * [STYLE] Erotica / Nisio Isin / Fluffy / Comforting\n\nLet affection emerge through a private language of correction and repetition. One character may object that \"you always say that,\" then prove the phrase belongs to both of them; another may revise a request to make it more accurate and in doing so make it more intimate. Banter is a form of welcome, not a shield. Small definitional games—\"that's not what I meant,\" \"say it again,\" \"you call that breakfast?\"—can become expressions of care because the people involved enjoy the same habit.\n\nKeep erotic anticipation and physical continuity central. The scene may begin with ordinary care: a remembered preference, a shared space, a practical kindness that creates closeness. From there, speech continues into invitation, permission, and response. Let the participants be distinct: one may tease, one may become flustered, one may use precise wording to be brave. Their differences generate friction that is manageable and affectionate.\n\nComfort does not require a confession or a repaired history. A couple may be happy now without resolving everything. Let established personalities remain; nobody must become uniformly agreeable. The story can offer a sincere interval of ease, touch, and warmth that is still genuinely erotic: desire, vulnerability, and the aftermath of closeness belong to this particular couple's rhythm.\n\nAvoid a stock romantic vocabulary. The specific details and the couple's shared phrases should carry the feeling. Use playful contradiction and light self-correction as warm textures, not as combative cleverness. The result is a comforting continuation of the relationship rather than a separate domestic interlude or a competition in wit.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPafatag::♢ * [STYLE] Erotica / Nisio Isin / Formal Literary Novel\n\nCompose the erotic scene in sustained paragraphs and deliberate transitions. Let narration move between private exchange and the larger social frame: what the room, the hour, the house, or the shared history makes permissible. If the narrator can judge or remember, use that distance to clarify consequence without explaining every impulse. Keep narration in the narrator's register, distinct from the characters' spoken language.\n\nLet the verbal contest unfold within this composed movement. A correction may begin as etiquette and become desire; an objection may be a way of preserving privacy until the next sentence relents. The speaker who understands a word differently changes what the word has just done. Show how social speech and private speech alter the same phrase: what may be said at a table becomes something else in a smaller room.\n\nDevelop interiority at the length the scene needs. Focal characters may think through desire, fear, or regret while still speaking. Do not make all characters equally perceptive. The narration may expose one participant's need while another mistakes its wording; the reader's knowledge and the other character's knowledge remain separate. Preserve physical continuity, so that touching, undressing, pausing, and aftercloseness remain part of the novel's texture rather than an obscured cut.\n\nUse public and private contrast when the setting supports it, and distinguish ceremony or correspondence from ordinary intimacy where relevant. The style is formal but not necessarily aristocratic or archaic. Let the paragraph length serve interpretation and emotional weight, not uniform elaboration. The result is an erotic passage that reads as part of a composed novel: desire framed, argued, revised, and felt through language and social awareness.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPafatah::♢ * [STYLE] Erotica / Nisio Isin / Framed Oral Storytelling (Arabian Nights-inspired)\n\nOpen inside the outer scene with a listener who has an active reason to hear the story: curiosity, hesitance, a request for delay, a bid for permission, or the need to make a past wound speakable before the present continues. Name that reason through the listener's attention and questions, not through summary.\n\nWhen the teller begins, make the embedded tale an intervention in the present encounter. The tale may secure cooperation, test a boundary, explain a hesitation, or offer the listener a version of the past that must be accepted or revised before the next physical move. Mark each shift between levels with a concrete outer-world anchor: the teller's hand pausing on a button, the listener leaning back, a glass set down. Return to that anchor when the tale's pressure rises so the story and the actual proximity pull on each other.\n\nGive each participant a distinct verbal habit and a distinct use of narrative. One may revise an account as they speak, changing a remembered gesture or a spoken promise; that correction changes what the listener feels permitted to do. Another may answer with an objection or a competing definition of what happened, turning the next overture into the prize of a small contest. Keep one voice plain and bodily, another allusive and self-conscious; do not make every speaker epigrammatic.\n\nUse revision and contradiction as the mechanism of boundary negotiation. If a character says, \"I asked you to wait,\" and then, after hearing a detail of the tale, says, \"No—I asked you to stay,\" the second version must alter the present relationship and the available touch. Preserve the difference between a teller who is untrustworthy and one simply incomplete. An unreliable account exposes itself through a small clash with an earlier detail, and the listener may choose not to contradict it immediately; write that silence as an erotic and relational act with its own consequence.\n\nMake recurrence carry meaning across levels. Choose a repeated word, phrase, or tangible object—a key turned, a door held open, a name spoken only inside the story—and let the outer listener return to it at a moment of decision. The recurrence should change what the listener understands about the teller's invitation or refusal, not merely decorate the tale.\n\nKeep the outer scene physically continuous. Track how the room is arranged, how near the listener sits, and what the teller's hands do apart from speech. If someone pauses the story to move closer, speak directly, or withdraw, write the interruption as part of the encounter rather than an exit from the method. A delayed revelation inside the tale raises a question the listener wants answered before accepting intimacy; when the answer comes, deliver it through the telling itself, not as external summary.\n\nLet the actual outcome follow from these negotiations. The teller may stop because attention changes, because the next portion is difficult, or because the listener has already heard enough to act. The recipe controls how storytelling and desire influence each other; it does not require the characters to touch, refuse, or reach a completed understanding.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPafatai::♢ * [STYLE] Erotica / Nisio Isin / German-Language Literary Traditions\n\nWrite from within one participant's reflective narration, using the contemporary intimate/multilingual route. Move between private thought, remembered speech, and direct dialogue by following the narrator's present attention: a spoken phrase may trigger a memory, a memory may correct the phrase, and the correction may change what the narrator now dares to say aloud.\n\nMake language itself carry boundary and invitation. When a character hesitates on a word, offers its definition, corrects it, or switches register—between a family phrase and a formal one, between one language and the surrounding speech, or between a euphemism and its direct form—show what the shift does to the other person's posture or reply. If the narrator names a wish only inside the narration, keep the gap between that private wording and what is spoken palpable: the narrator may notice the unsaid version pressing against the sentence actually delivered.\n\nShape the erotic negotiation as a verbal contest between distinct habits. One speaker may object to another's word for what happened between them, propose an alternative, and press the listener to accept it. The listener may reply by revising the same sentence, turning desire, hesitation, or regret into a definition of the present relationship. Keep one voice exacting and evasive, another plain and direct; their different rhythms determine the approach and retreat.\n\nUse subordinate clauses and delayed completion to create pressure, but let the syntax follow the narrator's actual thought rather than imitating translated word order. When a long reflective passage intervenes, keep the body continuous: a hand, a breath, or a shift in position remains present in the narration during the inner speech and memory. The scene slows through attention, not through dissolving into abstraction.\n\nGround the social and emotional specificity in the supplied world only. If the story provides a setting, class, region, or migration history, let those facts shape which registers and languages are available to each speaker. Do not invent a national temperament or force code-switching where the cast would not use it.\n\nAfter physical intimacy, continue with the characters' thoughts and speech rather than closing the scene with a summary. One may revisit a word from earlier and offer a correction; the other may accept it, reject it, or deliberately misunderstand. Let the final emotional state come from those small verbal resolutions. Keep the narrator's self-knowledge fallible: an account of one's own motives can be wrong in a useful, discoverable way without making the whole scene deceptive. The inner monologue remains the narrator's live private experience, not an authorized external testimony.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPafataj::♢ * [STYLE] Erotica / Nisio Isin / Gothic-Punk\n\nGround the encounter in a specific worn setting where old authority still makes physical claims. Choose a room, building, or neighborhood detail—a damaged lock, faded institutional signage, a patched wall, inherited furniture—and make it a second participant whose condition shapes what the characters believe they can say and do there. Show the detail early through use, not description: a hand testing the lock, a shoulder against the patched wall, a drawer that sticks.\n\nBring the setting into the erotic negotiation through language. One character may appeal to a former arrangement, a house rule, a family expectation, or an institutional practice by naming a material fact: \"That door does not lock.\" The other may answer with an objection, a redefinition, or a contradiction that changes the permitted next action: \"That door has not locked since your father stayed here.\" Write the revision so it exposes a different vulnerability and advances or blocks the present advance.\n\nLet each material correction carry the collision of old power and present life. A bed that belonged to a former authority, a room with a broken lock, or a hallway where footsteps can be heard by someone else should enter the dialogue as a claim about what this place permits. When a character revises the claim, the revision changes the relationship to that inheritance, not merely the facts of the room.\n\nMake the verbal masks specific and fallible. In trusted company, a character may let a social mask slip, but write the fall through a concrete contradiction or self-correction: an unreliable account of past intimacy surfaces in a detail about a broken window or a kept key, and the listener chooses whether to challenge it. Do not make all authority figures corrupt or every vulnerable admission a manipulation; genuine care can coexist with damaged histories.\n\nUse sensory detail to modify pacing. The smell of a damp coat, the warmth of a recently occupied chair, the faint sound of traffic through a repaired pane should interrupt or extend speech and touch according to the character's attention. Atmosphere changes how the encounter is experienced; it does not replace the participants' bodies and emotional states.\n\nWhen physical intimacy occurs, preserve its continuity and aftermath. A moment of exposure may feel like the temporary collapse of an old arrangement; render that as the character experiences it through breath, hesitation, or a sudden awareness of the room, not through editorial summary. Afterward, return to the setting's details—the key still in the same pocket, the window now partially open, the cigarette not yet lit—and let one final piece of dialogue revise an earlier phrase. The boundary between past authority and present choice may remain visibly unresolved if the story demands it.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPafatak::♢ * [STYLE] Erotica / Nisio Isin / Indian Literary Traditions\n\nWrite the encounter as a testimonial or autobiographical account being given to someone: a friend, a doctor, a later lover, an interviewer, or the next self who reads the memory. Establish the listener and occasion through the narrator's address—a question answered, a silence filled, a phrase repeated for the listener's benefit—so the telling has a present purpose.\n\nMake the report itself a live negotiation through memory's self-correction. When the narrator says, \"I told him I could wait,\" and then a paragraph later says, \"No—I said I could not wait, but I let him think I had,\" the correction changes what the reader understands of the scene and the speaker's initiative. Stage each correction as a turn in the telling: the narrator catches a word, hesitates, and replaces it with another that revises the remembered request, refusal, or boundary.\n\nGive remembered speakers distinct verbal habits. One may be formal and evasive, one blunt, one fond of repeating a particular word. Let another character within the telling answer with an objection or redefinition, but keep the overall frame in the narrator's memory: the narrator reports their words, not their private thoughts.\n\nKeep the community, language, period, and region specific only when the supplied story contains them. Use the actual world of the supplied cast—shared rooms, work schedules, obligations, weather, and the specific ways people speak about desire, modesty, or marriage where those are relevant. If the speaker uses another language, show it through described register and hesitation rather than forced code-switching.\n\nPreserve physical continuity and sensory detail within the remembered scene even as the narrative frame moves through time. A room's heat, the sound of traffic or a ceiling fan, the weight of a shutter, and the particular position of two bodies stay exact during long passages of reflection. The teller's uncertainty about motive does not erase the physical event; the body in the account remains continuous.\n\nLet the consequence of intimacy surface through small contradictions in the telling. A changed relationship with a community, an arrangement accepted or ended, or a long silence may appear when the narrator claims not to remember a particular touch, then later supplies its exact detail while defending a different point. Write that instability as the method by which the story reveals how the present version of desire was built, not as a trick to hide the plot.\n\nThe story may end when the speaker breaks off the recollection, contradicts an earlier assertion, or asks the listener to correct one word. Let that interruption be the final erotic action.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPafatal::♢ * [STYLE] Erotica / Nisio Isin / Japanese Literary Traditions\n\nWrite the story as a sequence of dated, titled, or otherwise broken entries in which the narrator returns to an earlier moment, phrase, or physical detail and changes its meaning. A present-tense entry may look back at an encounter, anticipate one, or record its aftermath; all three belong to the same intimate accounting.\n\nMake each entry-to-entry revision a turn in the narrator's understanding. One entry may say, \"I understood her silence as consent.\" A later entry may begin, \"Wrong. Her silence was not consent. Her silence was a word I had not yet learned how to hear.\" Write the correction so it alters what the narrator now believes about the encounter, the partner's initiative, and their own agency. Apply the same operation to recalled dialogue, requests, and refusals. Each correction should expose a formerly suppressed desire, fear, or boundary rather than contradicting the record for style.\n\nWithin a single entry, mix direct address, remembered speech, sensory notes, and plain argument. An entry may describe the temperature of a room, quote a text message, then argue with the past self about what \"wanting\" meant. Keep the teller's language personal and concrete. Use the supplied setting's particular season, city, or workplace to make the memory specific; do not insert a canonical Japanese landscape or quietist mood.\n\nUse omission as a tactic. The teller may record everything except a central act, then the next entry shows why that act had to be omitted. Make the omission visible through the entry's shape—a sudden shift in subject, a sentence left unfinished, a detail circled back to without being named—so the reader feels the pressure of what is not written.\n\nPreserve bodily continuity inside the fragmented form. The sexual or intimate moment remains a continuous event within its entry: positions, pressure, speech, and pauses follow one another without losing the sequence. Reflection may interrupt before, during, or after, but the body's actions stay legible. When multiple entries cover the same night, each revision respects the already established physical facts; an unreliable memory may change motive or wording, not erase a known placement.\n\nLet the aftermath unfold through entry-to-entry reappraisal. A day later, the narrator may replay one sentence and change its punctuation; a week later, they may return to an object—a cup, a towel, a message notification—and discover that its meaning has shifted. Give the other participant a distinct recorded voice in dialogue and recalled speech, but keep the diary's ownership clear. The narrator only knows what they observed, remembered, or were told; do not grant another person's private thoughts just because the diary reflects on them.\n\nThe final entry may begin with a contradiction of the first entry and stop, letting the accumulated revisions stand. Hesitations, crossed-out wording, and false starts are concrete ways of making the self-correction visible. Keep the diary alive as a private record rather than a polished memoir.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPafatam::♢ * [STYLE] Erotica / Nisio Isin / Latin American Literary Traditions\n\nWrite the encounter through more than one voice, and present those voices in an order that moves backward, forward, or around the event. Do not give each person a single first-person section that merely reports the same scene. Let the voices contend with one another's wording: one defines what happened, another objects, a third corrects a phrase, and a fourth may return much later to accept or reject that correction.\n\nStage the contest at the boundary between voices. When speaker A says, \"I asked, and she refused,\" speaker B may say, \"He did not ask. He announced, and then he called my silence a refusal.\" Write the contradiction so it changes the reader's understanding of initiative, vulnerability, and consent. Each voice's correction should revise the available facts or motives, not simply offer a different mood.\n\nGive each voice a distinct verbal habit. One person's account may be legalistic and precise, another's recursive and hesitant, another's blunt and cut into short passages. The style does not require every participant to be a hyperverbal philosopher; a plain voice can be as consequential as an allusive one.\n\nPreserve the actual community, language, and history only when supplied. Show how different speakers describe the same neighborhood, family pressure, work schedule, or shared object, but take those details from the story's world. Do not import a specific Spanish or Portuguese phrasebook, religion, magical system, or political allegory. If the world permits a fantastic or marvelous treatment, it must remain available within the supplied canon; otherwise keep the route realistic and urban in texture.\n\nReconstruct the physical event from overlapping accounts without giving any single voice absolute authority. A gesture may appear in three different versions: one person remembers a held breath, another remembers the sound of a fan, another remembers only the abrupt stop. Arrange the overlaps so the reader can assemble the actual sequence of positions, pressure, speech, and pauses, while the motives remain contested. This preserves the genre's demand for distinct perspectives and consequences.\n\nLet the aftermath be as polyphonic as the encounter. After intimacy, one voice may quote a later conversation in which the old wording was retracted; another may remember only the silence. A nonfocal listener, such as a friend or neighbor telling their own story, can become a short witness section, but do not use them as an objective narrator to settle the dispute.\n\nEnd with a remaining disagreement or an incomplete correction rather than a forced reconciliation. The final turn may simply be one character repeating another's word with new emphasis, leaving open whether the definition has shifted enough for either of them. If a narrator tells a story inside their section, write it as a psychological or rhetorical move within that voice, not as the installation of a separate frame-tale apparatus.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPafatan::♢ * [STYLE] Erotica / Nisio Isin / Light Novel\n\nOpen with the current room, position, and available light so the reader knows where the characters are without a long establishing paragraph. Use short paragraphs and frequent breaks around dialogue, small physical movements, and private response. Each beat advances the negotiation rather than merely decorating the scene.\n\nMake the verbal contest the scene's main action. Let one character say something that defines the situation—\"You're only here because you lost the bet,\" \"This is the last time,\" \"That's not what I meant.\"—and let the other respond with an objection, a correction, or a competitive redefinition. Write each exchange so it changes what the characters feel permitted to do. A partner may answer, \"No, I came because you said the door would be open,\" and that revision pushes the scene toward a new physical move or a new boundary. Keep the dialogue from becoming pure banter; each word reveals a stake in what is about to happen.\n\nGive each speaker a distinct verbal habit. One may use plain speech while another delights in wordplay; one may define terms carefully while another speaks in short, bodily phrases. Do not make every character equally witty.\n\nUse reaction beats to show interiority without breaking the pacing. After a challenging line, follow it with a short private phrase or image: the exact sensation in the chest, a glance at the other person's hand, a remembered sentence, or a sudden awareness of the space. The reaction belongs to the reacting character and may be serious, uncertain, angry, or funny. Keep the beat short enough that the scene continues to move.\n\nKeep the erotic content continuous and stageable. Show who moves, where they move, and how the other responds. Maintain physical continuity between paragraphs: a hand placed on a knee remains there until someone moves it, and the reader never has to guess where bodies are. Make anticipation and vulnerability concrete through small, legible actions: a pause before answering, a half-step back, a sleeve pushed up, a door left ajar.\n\nAllow a character's private reaction or spoken correction to expose a small self-deception. The reader may learn that an earlier statement was not exactly true when the character revises it under pressure. Write this as a sign of complication, not a puzzle to be solved. The story still moves forward through the immediate scene.\n\nThe response may end in the middle of a spoken line, a private realization, or an unfinished move if the current narrative role and scene length call for it; the cut should feel natural, not like a mandatory hook.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPafatao::♢ * [STYLE] Erotica / Nisio Isin / Lyrical Prose\n\nLet desire enter through the shape of a sentence: the held breath of a long clause, the quick turn of a repeated sound, the way an image can gather an entire unspoken need. When a participant speaks during intimacy, their words are not an interruption but another touch. A question can slow the body; a rephrased term can change where attention lands; a correction can produce a new intimacy or a flinch. Build these exchanges so that longing, hesitation, permission, and consequence share the same rhythm as the physical encounter.\n\nUse the narrator's associations to make the present moment resonate, but keep the imagery traceable to this scene and this teller. A memory or comparison should alter what is understood or felt: the pressure of a hand remembered from another hour, the phrase that once meant refusal but now means stay. Return to an image only when the return changes its meaning; do not turn it into a decorative motif. The associative movement may carry erotic, anxious, or tender material, and the rhythm should follow the character's actual attention, not a fixed solemn cadence.\n\nMaintain each participant's initiative and speech habits. Verbal wit is not applied uniformly; one may choose fewer words while another argues, names, or delays. Language can be playful, cruel, tender, or inadequate without becoming a debate that replaces sensation. Pay close attention to boundaries: a spoken condition, a hesitation, a revocation, or an unasked-for touch all have consequences within the scene and afterward. The style is lyrical even when explicit, so physical details should be concrete and continuous, not dissolved into abstraction. Let the aftermath linger as feeling and reexamined phrasing, not as a summary of what the encounter proved.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPafatap::♢ * [STYLE] Erotica / Nisio Isin / Melancholic / Reflective\n\nSet the erotic present beside an earlier occasion or a remembered phrase. A caress may recall a first permission, a different room, or a word that meant something else before; let that association change how the present touch is understood. The narrator may notice a small irreversible difference: a habit that used to signal impatience now means tenderness, a stretch of silence that once felt safe now feels guarded. During or after intimacy, the participants can revisit what they said then and what they call it now. Let their corrections be acts of memory as much as verbal play: one says the old name, the other refuses it, and the refusal becomes part of what they are doing.\n\nGive each speaker a distinct relationship to the past. Some may regret, some may insist, some may deliberately re-narrate an old betrayal or a former joy. A contradiction in memory should count as an event: a story repeated differently can alter closeness, produce distance, or open a negotiation. Do not turn every reflective passage into a lament; the present pleasure remains real, and a bittersweet detail can stand next to a joke or a fulfilled request. The melancholy arises from the coexistence of present experience with what is gone, not from a prohibition on delight.\n\nBoundaries stay active across remembered and present encounters. What was once permitted may be refused now; what was once too frightening may be requested. The aftermath should show desire, regret, comfort, or estrangement as conditions that continue rather than as a verdict. Keep time scales concrete: a remembered event belongs to a particular period, and the current scene has its own duration. Do not let later knowledge rewrite earlier knowledge as if the characters knew it then. The reflective style may linger, but it must preserve physical continuity and the specific body in the room, not dissolve the scene into abstraction.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPafataq::♢ * [STYLE] Erotica / Nisio Isin / Middle Eastern Literary Traditions\n\nUse a framed, situated teller whose account includes intimate scenes not as decoys but as the places where desire, permission, and consequence become legible. The frame may be a conversation, a remembered occasion retold, or a storyteller answering an auditor's interruption; keep the actual participants distinct and their speech habits individual. When a narrator revises a phrase or corrects a detail, the correction itself can reveal what intimacy cost. The erotic core must remain direct and physically continuous within the tale; the frame does not distance it but changes when and how its meaning arrives.\n\nLet the telling use Arabic or Persian or another chosen tradition's characteristic resources rather than a general 'Eastern' atmosphere. For example, a framed story can hold a delayed revelation: a changed name, a repeated vow, a phrase whose first use is erotic and whose later return signals loss or permission withdrawn. An auditor may object that the teller has omitted the crucial moment. The teller's reply can defend, revise, or complicate the account. This is not a courtroom; the goal is the pressure the language puts on the remembered body.\n\nDo not make religion, honor, mysticism, or exile the unavoidable ground of every intimate scene. Use only the setting and community actually supplied. A domestic or urban room is enough; a journey can be practical rather than spiritual. Preserve boundaries: what a participant permits in one telling may have been refused at the time, and a later tale should not erase that refusal. Aftermath may appear within the frame as the teller's present feeling or the auditor's response. Keep teller access consistent with the active perspective. If the style invokes a tradition's conventions, make them function as narration, not as an ethnographic explanation to the reader.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPafatar::♢ * [STYLE] Erotica / Nisio Isin / Minimalist Prose\n\nSelect few details, and let each one do more than name. A hand's pressure, a particular word repeated with a change, a piece of clothing removed slowly: these are the sensual facts. Replace a vague emotional summary with a physical act plus its consequence. When a participant speaks, the exact word is the event. One says wait; the other says waiting; the difference becomes negotiation. A correction can be brief and still shift the room. Do not pad the prose to explain what the dialogue has already done.\n\nIn each reply, choose one or two inside connections that materially contribute: a remembered permission, a threatened boundary, the recognition that an old term no longer fits. This reflection may take a full sentence, but not three. Do not cut causality: if a touch is refused, show the refusal and the next breath; let the reader infer the injury or relief. Omission should leave a specific inference available, not delete the reason for the reaction.\n\nGive each participant a distinct verbal habit through selection rather than volume. One speaks in fragments; another names exactly; a third revises silently or aloud. The narrator's own wordplay must not require a paragraph to unfold; a single substituted term can be the turn. Physical continuity remains mandatory: the body's position, the direction of movement, the moment before and after. Aftermath is brief but present, often a single observed detail that carries the consequence. This is compression, not ellipsis of the erotic center.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPafatas::♢ * [STYLE] Erotica / Nisio Isin / Modern Literature / Contemporary Novel\n\nWrite continuous scenes in which intimate encounters include conversation as part of the encounter. Do not divide the chapter into 'the dialogue' and 'the physical act.' A sentence spoken while undressing may redefine what follows; a pause for a disagreement about a word can intensify, delay, or interrupt touching. Let the prose move naturally among action, speech, perception, brief recollection, and the viewpoint character's inner experience, following the scene's actual pressure rather than a forced alternation.\n\nGive each participant an individual verbal style: one makes his desire explicit through careful definitions, another jokes to test a boundary, another revises a word when it has already begun to hurt. Their speech should reveal differing perspectives on the same act. Avoid a chorus of witty philosophers; the central exchanges do the wordplay work. When the narrator reflects, keep the reflection tied to this encounter and this body. A memory may enter if it changes the present choice or response, not as an essay on desire.\n\nShow permission and refusal as live developments. A condition can be stated, repeated, worsened, or withdrawn. The narrative should track what each person knows and does not know, so that private thought remains separated from spoken assurance. Preserve physical continuity: where hands are, what is removed, what stops. Do not let the flexible omniscient or limited narration accidentally grant access to another mind. The aftermath continues the scene: a few lines of stillness, practical adjustment, or a spoken observation can show consequence without an evaluative summary. This is not a case with a lesson; the encounter may end with questions or with quiet.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPafatat::♢ * [STYLE] Erotica / Nisio Isin / Relationship-Centric Fanfiction (AO3-inspired)\n\nStart from the actual supplied relationship premise: what these two have built, what promises or habits already exist, what unfinished tension makes the current intimate scene loaded. Select the scene because it lets that premise become