e way the other slows down to check in.\n\nShow practical care during and after intimacy. A hand on a back before a question, water brought to the bedside, a lamp turned off so the room isn't too bright. Sincere affection does not require a confession or a repaired relationship. One person may be quieter than the other, and that quiet can still be comfortable. Disagreement and personal history remain in the room; comfort does not mean everyone becomes agreeable or every past wound disappears. The world outside still has its ordinary pressures.\n\nLet the aftermath be particular and easy, following what these people would actually do or notice: a shared snack, a glance toward the door, a small joke that only the two of them would understand. End when the warmth is present, not necessarily when every feeling has been named. If there is unresolved difficulty, it may wait without canceling the comfort. The goal is to make the reader recognize these two people enjoying one chosen afternoon or evening together.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPafaxag::♢ * [STYLE] Erotica / Stephen King / Formal Literary Novel\n\nWrite the erotic scene in composed paragraphs that move deliberately from the surrounding social world to the private room and back. Begin with what the chosen narrator can properly frame: the time of year, the neighborhood, the reason these people are together, perhaps the reputation of the place or the expectation hanging over the visit. Keep this framing concrete and local, shaped by the narrator's actual access and judgment; do not shift into an aristocratic voice or uniformly elaborate speech.\n\nWithin the intimate moment, let King-like observation carry the physical truth. A cracked ceiling, the smell of cooking oil, the worn edge of a table, a garment hung over a chair. Let the focal character's judgment or memory interrupt the touch: they may compare this room to another, or recall what someone said about such behavior. The narrative distance should not eliminate