ts, receipts, attachments, or channel changes that matter. Do not invent interface events merely to add subtext.\n\nLet each person's digital habits characterize them through concrete typing patterns. One may type in fragments, send rapid messages, edit before sending, leave a message on read, or attach a picture; another may write in complete sentences, delay replies, or ignore a pending question. These habits become evidence of desire, hesitation, or power. A pause in typing can carry more weight than a paragraph of explanation.\n\nGive each participant a distinct perspective and initiative through message content, timing, and response patterns. Show who initiates by sending the first message after a silence, who avoids by answering a practical question instead of the implied one, who tests a boundary with a question or an image, and who reaches back after a delay. Preserve physical continuity across the conversation: if a character describes an act or sends an image, later messages should reflect the changing situation—what happened after, what was felt, what was left unsaid.\n\nDo not assume that a sent message equals the writer's complete private thought. A character may type a declaration, delete it, and send something safer. If the transcript includes an unsent draft, label it clearly as a composed but unsent message, not as live private thought. For required private experience, add a clearly separated context note outside the transcript, such as:\n\n    <nemo-interiority character=\"NAME\">\n    Actual private perception, memory, or hesitation for this character.\n    </nemo-interiority>\n\nThis note is authoring material, not something the recipient can read.\n\nAllow context outside the exchange when required, clearly separated from the transcript. It may describe a character's physical surroundings, a memory, or an action taken between messages. Do not fabricate an interface event to force a beat. Emotional aftermath appears in the next messages or in the