            "name": "🔧 Init · Erotica · 07/07",
            "content": "{{#if {{.NCGenreId == erotica}}}}{{// @category Variable-Init }}{{// @icon 🔧 }}{{// @badge INIT }}{{// @tooltip Erotica. Selected-partition data; keep enabled in supplied order after recipe selectors and before the Writing Recipe resolver. Part 7 of 7. }}{{// Source group: recipes-erotica; payload preserved. }}{{setvar::NPafayah::♢ * [STYLE] Erotica / Terry Pratchett / Framed Oral Storytelling (Arabian Nights-inspired)\n\nWrite the framing scene with a particular storyteller and a particular listener whose present relationship carries desire, curiosity, or reluctance. Make the occasion practical: one person wants something, is delaying something, or is testing something. The teller's purpose shapes the told story as a proposal, a memory, a warning, or a test, and that purpose becomes visible in concrete narrative choices. Expand the episodes that flatter or unsettle this listener; give the tale's central lover the teller's own hesitation or the listener's own habit of speech; linger over the detail that answers a question the listener has not yet asked aloud.\n\nSet the outer frame and the nested story in the same world, and let that world's actual institutions and conventions shape what can be said. Apply the Pratchett social intelligence to the difference between what people claim about desire and what they do. Inside the told story, give the lovers jobs, families, timetables, and belongings that their desire must work around: a shared room with a thin door, a promise made to a third person, a borrowed garment, an obligation that ends by morning. Let the tale's official moral—patience, loyalty, discretion—be quietly contradicted by the practical detail the teller cannot resist including, so that the listener hears both the lesson and the teller's own preference underneath it. Keep that comedy concrete to the participants. Do not make every speaker witty; one may be careful, one may be teasing, one may be direct.\n\nWhen the frame returns, let a repeated object, phrase, or gesture acquire a new charge. The listener remembers a word from the story and applies it to the teller; the teller picks up a cup the story's lover was imagined to hold; someone interrupts with a question that reveals which part of the tale they were actually attending to. Let the interruption change what the teller includes next or remain a private clue in the listener's face. Show the consequence of an intimate choice within the nested story, and let the frame's listener notice what the teller has chosen to leave out—not as a puzzle to solve, but as a pressure the listener may accept, challenge, or ignore.\n\nKeep the narrative levels clearly marked through the teller's voice and the listener's responses, not through explanatory signposts. The erotic content belongs to the framed relationship: the story is for this listener, on this occasion, and its heat comes from the teller's awareness that the listener is comparing the tale's lovers to the two people in the room. Do not require the listener to be won over, enlightened, or seduced before the response ends. The embedded tale may work; it may also be challenged, partially accepted, ignored, or answered with another story. End the response wherever the present scene naturally pauses—during the telling, in the silence after a question, or in a shift of attention toward the listener—rather than forcing a completed seduction or a tidy lesson.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPafayai::♢ * [STYLE] Erotica / Terry Pratchett / German-Language Literary Traditions\n\nUse social and developmental realism as the governing route. Narrate the erotic scene through continuous, observant prose that remains close to a particular protagonist's perception rather than alternating freely among all minds. Let the focal character notice the material and institutional texture of the moment: the room, the time of day, the duties that have just ended or will begin, and the social rules that make one touch significant in a way another room could not make it.\n\nApply the Pratchett social intelligence to the institutional facts without letting them become background decoration. An office schedule governs how long two people can remain alone; a household's custom about a closed door changes what a kiss can mean; a club's membership rule may be mentioned by one lover and understood differently by the other. Let the difference appear between an official explanation for why the pair is here and the particular desire that becomes visible now. When a comic correction occurs, make it specific and shared: one participant names the actual practical reason behind a careful arrangement, and the other recognizes the naming as a relief rather than an insult. The pretext falls away, and the intimacy continues with less ceremony but more exposure.\n\nKeep each participant's desire distinct. One may be forward but anxious about the institutional consequences tomorrow; the other may be unwilling to break a spoken rule but willing to find a space inside it. Show their physical choices in sequence—who closes the distance, when a pause lasts too long, whose hands are steady—so that the sensual development remains causally continuous rather than cut into disconnected images. Make room for emotional aftermath within the scene and at its rim: a joke shared after a moment of exposure, a practical remark that lands more tenderly than intended, a brief return to the official language each person uses outside this room.\n\nShape the syntax to the focal character's attention. Use longer, accumulative sentences when the character is holding a social meaning and a physical sensation in mind at once—the rule about the door and the warmth of the other person's hand, the schedule and the smell of the room. Cut to shorter sentences at decision or surprise: a hand moves, a name is said without its title, a lock turns. Do not use stream of consciousness as a default; report the character's private thought and reaction only where the chosen perspective permits.\n\nDo not import a particular German-speaking country or city unless the story supplies one. If the setting is unspecified, keep concrete details regional but non-national: local habits of address, work rhythms, and domestic arrangements can carry the social realism without a manufactured landscape inventory. The response may end during the encounter, after it, or at a threshold where one returning obligation becomes audible. Do not force the intimacy to resolve all institutional tensions or to conclude with a clear victory over the rule. The scene's erotic charge lies in the particular way these two people negotiate their present constraints and each other, with consequences that will remain visible in their habits, speech, or silences after the response.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPafayaj::♢ * [STYLE] Erotica / Terry Pratchett / Gothic-Punk\n\nBuild the encounter inside a present world where inherited authority and modern decay meet: an old institution repurposed for new work, a grand house divided into awkward flats, a family precedent that still controls who may enter or remain. Let the urban or architectural gothic atmosphere be literal—damp, wiring trouble, old locks, long stairways, a painted-over crest—rather than a supernatural state. Then attach erotic possibility to this uneven environment: a private room beyond the reach of the household's ears; a cold corridor that makes one person reach for another's sleeve; a social rule left over from an earlier era that both participants quietly agree to ignore.\n\nApply the Pratchett social intelligence to the old authority. Show the difference between the official history of a place or family and the practical arrangements people make within it. One participant may perform the old deference exactly as expected in public and then speak plainly in private. Make the mask fall through concrete narrative moves: first a direct sentence that breaks the public tone, then a slower unbuttoning, then a phrase that the other person did not expect to be understood. Do not let the comedy flatten the desire. The same detail that exposes an obsolete rule—a room thermostat set for an era when someone else paid for heat, a plaque commemorating a predecessor nobody remembers—can also free the participants to treat each other as present, particular people, because the old authority no longer covers this moment.\n\nKeep the erotic progression physically continuous and emotionally loaded. Let touch, waiting, and undressing occur amid difficult material details: a narrow bed, a jacket placed on a radiator, a door that must be wedged shut. Let the participants' masks not be sinister controls but acquired public faces: the careful secretary's tone, the committee member's polite boredom, the junior partner's practiced acceptance. Their gradual removal should cost something—a confession about what the public role hides, a hesitation that the other person recognizes, a consequence already owed to another social obligation. Show aftermath in the same atmospheric register: the room's smell, the state of the clothes, one person's changed understanding of what tomorrow's meeting now means.\n\nDo not import vampire physiology, factions, blood rules, or any supernatural truth into the style. If the story itself grants supernatural facts, the gothic-punk method may describe the resulting private experience in the same social and material way, but this recipe does not create such facts. The response may stop while the atmospheric detail is still charged—one person turns out a faulty light, or throws a coat over a chair, or remembers the public promise waiting outside—without requiring the old authority to be defeated. The erotic consequence remains in how these two people now read the room, each other, and the mask each will put back on.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPafayak::♢ * [STYLE] Erotica / Terry Pratchett / Indian Literary Traditions\n\nUse the oral, epic, and frame-tale route. Let the erotic scene sit within a social frame: a particular household, neighborhood, workplace, or extended-community setting whose members talk, recite, warn, and joke about love through older stories. The central intimate encounter can be narrated directly, but its meaning is built from the surrounding talk and tales. When a character recalls a family saying, a wedding anecdote, a local legend, or a fragment of remembered verse, let it interpret the present desire rather than decorate it. A repeated phrase—\"first comes the season, then the council\"—accrues meaning through its repetition by different speakers in different contexts: one says it as teasing, another as warning, another as a joke the lovers now share.\n\nTreat the frame-tale form practically. There may be a brief outer moment before the lovers are alone, then an embedded recollection inside that private time, then a return to the room where someone quotes or corrects the remembered story. Keep the levels easy to follow by giving the embedded material its own concrete sense: a neighbor told this in the kitchen; a grandmother said this while folding cloth; a friend posted this as a warning. The teller of each fragment matters. One quote may be offered as compassion; another as a joke; another as a family expectation that one lover privately rejects. Let the erotic progression be interrupted, shaped, or advanced by the tale: a remembered line makes one lover pause mid-touch; a corrected ending makes the other laugh and pull closer; a warning quoted at the wrong moment becomes a shared dare.\n\nApply Pratchett social comedy to the practical gap between public stories and private behavior. A respected saying about patience may be used to excuse a room left too long; an epic comparison may be deployed to flatter while both people know it is overblown and enjoy the exaggeration; an older relative's warning about scandal may be quoted to make the risk feel more real and more chosen. Let each participant's relation to these stories differ: one may find them comforting, one may find them suffocating, one may enjoy retelling them with a wrong ending. When a comic correction occurs, make it specific to the social institution at hand—the neighbor, the family, the landlord, the temple or workplace calendar—not a comment on the entire region.\n\nKeep physical continuity and emotional aftermath in the foreground. Narrate how a hand moves from a sleeve to a wrist, when a story's recitation makes one lover stop listening, and what is said after the first silence. Let the characters' private thoughts or reactions be available according to the active narrator, not forced into a generic frame-tale narrator's voice. The response may include the moments immediately after the intimacy: someone straightens a sheet, opens a shutter, revises the moral of the tale, or thinks about the people whose words entered the room. End wherever the present scene pauses. Do not require the couple to reconcile with their whole community or to learn the proper version of a tradition. The desire and its consequences—tenderness, regret, laughter, awkward rehearsal of what to say next—should remain specific to this pair and this occasion.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPafayal::♢ * [STYLE] Erotica / Terry Pratchett / Japanese Literary Traditions\n\nUse social and psychological realism. Narrate the intimate scene through one focal participant's attention, and let that attention move between the public role that brought the two characters together and the private desire now taking over. Make the institutional setting matter: a workplace after-hours obligation, a neighborhood association's meeting, a school or office relationship with unspoken rules, a rental situation with fixed times and smells. The narrator notices the practical details of the transition—the change as one person switches from formal address to a shorter name, the moment when a shared task is set aside, the particular sound of the room after the outside obligations stop.\n\nLet language and authority register in physical behavior. One participant may remain careful in speech while their hands are less careful; the other may answer in the formal style and then repeat the sentence without its polite ending because the formal ending no longer covers what they are about to do. Show the difference between what the institution's rules say and what these two are actually using the rules to create: a meeting may be a pretext for forty minutes alone, a form may create a reason for one person to lean across another, a seniority arrangement may let someone close a door without being contradicted. Apply Pratchett comedy here, but keep it particular. A comic correction might be a junior partner realizing that the supervisor has saved the safer side of the desk for them, or a clerk noticing that the official instruction about ventilation is being cited only so that the window can be opened for a different reason.\n\nKeep the erotic progression continuous. Do not cut the scene into separate images or let physical details become mere mood-setting. Follow the chain of touch, hesitation, undressing, and restraint with a clear eye on who initiates each step and what each person expects to happen next. Render the focal character's private thought through concrete operations: a phrase rehearsed and then not said; a glance at the lock; a sense that the room is too small for the public story they will have to tell later. The consequences should be immediate and social as well as emotional—tomorrow's shift, the shared space, the colleague who will ask where one person was.\n\nDo not impose seasonal imagery, silence, or a generic lyrical tone. Use the actual season only if it matters to the story's practical conditions—heat that makes the room close, rain that delays a departure, a holiday that empties the building. Do not import national stereotypes about dishonor or required reticence. Instead, let each character's own relationship to authority, language, and desire shape their actions. The response may stop on the threshold of the public world's return—a notification, a corridor light, a remembered promise—without resolving the aftermath. The erotic charge lies in the private interval and its exact social cost, not in a sudden confession or a completed reconciliation.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPafayam::♢ * [STYLE] Erotica / Terry Pratchett / Latin American Literary Traditions\n\nUse intimate psychological and urban fiction as the governing route. Keep the narration close to one participant's perception, using the city and its institutions as a concrete social medium rather than as background. Let the neighborhood, building, workplace, or transport system impose real constraints on the encounter: a shift that ends at a fixed hour, a flat with thin walls, a shared ride home, a family gathering that has not fully ended, a landlord or neighbor whose timing is known too well. These constraints create the physical and emotional rhythm of the intimacy; they are not obstacles to be cleared away all at once.\n\nApply Pratchett's social intelligence to the practical institutions around the couple. Show what people officially say and what they really arrange. A guard may enforce the rule about the lift only until nearly midnight; a market vendor's stall may remain open a little longer for a regular; an office may require a signed form for a key, but the practical keeper knows an older method. Let a comic correction be embedded in the urban texture: one lover explains that the supposedly romantic view is better from the service stairs, or that the famous corner is actually a bus depot after dark. Such corrections should not mock the setting; they should make the desire feel lived-in and particular.\n\nKeep the participants' perspectives distinct through the focal narration's available evidence. Give the focal character's own desire, uncertainty, and calculations in direct private thought where the contract allows: a worry about the time, a calculation about which neighbor might hear, a phrase rehearsed and then abandoned. Observe the other person's gestures, pauses, and small statements closely—a hand on a railing, a key turned, a jacket removed because the room is hot, a remark that changes from routine to invitation—so that the other's initiative remains visible even though the reader is inside one mind. Let the erotic sequence be physically continuous. Show consequences in the immediate afterward: the exact situation that has changed because the couple took this risk, the people who might notice, the practical thing one person now wants to say or avoid.\n\nPreserve the language and community actually supplied by the story. If the setting includes a named place, use the streets, foods, bus routes, or local expressions that fit it without turning them into a postcard. If the setting is unspecified, choose a definite but non-stereotyped urban milieu—perhaps a port city with mixed traffic, a hill neighborhood with unreliable water, a new development where two old families still keep keys—so the erotic scene has the same historical and social density as realistic fiction. Do not insert magical realism, a supernatural predestination, or any factual element the world excludes.\n\nThe response may end without a completed resolution: after the couple hears a door in the building, or one person realizes the time, or a phone lights up with a name both of them know. Do not require the encounter to harmonize every social obligation or to produce a final confession. The erotic consequence remains in the two participants' altered reading of the room, the street outside, and the people they will have to face tomorrow.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPafayan::♢ * [STYLE] Erotica / Terry Pratchett / Light Novel\n\nOpen inside a concrete situation that the reader can picture from the first moments: where the characters are, how they got there, and what social or institutional rule is currently governing the space. Use short, clean establishing beats for any relevant object or spatial change—a closing door, a window with a broken latch, a coat dropped on a chair by the person who usually hangs it up. Do not stack description in a block; move between action, speech, and a brief private response so the scene reads quickly without losing physical continuity.\n\nLet the erotic progression unfold as a sequence of staged actions and reactions. Assign each participant a distinct kind of initiative and inhibition drawn from the setting's practical facts. One may be using the official task as an excuse to remain; the other may be waiting for the rule to be named so it can be set aside. Show each new touch, hesitation, or offered alternative through clear spatial cues: who steps where, whose hand reaches, which piece of clothing is removed first. Between these beats, use one or two lines of private reaction or practical thought—not a speech—to keep the character's desire, embarrassment, or calculation present.\n\nApply the Pratchett social intelligence by letting the institutional context generate the scene's particular comedy and pressure. A schedule everyone pretends to follow, a form nobody reads, an old rule about the room's proper use, a supervisor's habit, a budget item that explains why the lights are like this—these details can be noticed at the exact moment they affect intimacy. A comic correction should be specific and not merely clever: a character explains why the meeting was never really mandatory; another notices that the careful phrasing they used outside was itself the invitation. Each participant's relationship to language and authority should differ: one speaks in the institution's terms without believing them, the other only remembers the practical words.\n\nMake the consequences visible in the reaction beats and the scene's immediate aftermath. After the main intimacy or a significant private admission, stop for a short beat in which one character notices a specific change: the time on a shared screen, the sound of the corridor, the exact part of the public story that no longer fits. Do not resolve all institutional tensions. The response may end on such a beat, while desire or worry remains active. Do not require a cliffhanger, a confession, or a complete separation from the outside world. The light-novel form is a presentation method, not a demand for youthful dialogue, first-person narration, or a final break line; keep the treatment usable for any cast the story supplies.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPafayao::♢ * [STYLE] Erotica / Terry Pratchett / Lyrical Prose\n\nWrite the encounter as a small human institution with its own unspoken regulations, official explanations, and practical exceptions, and give each participant a distinct relationship to those rules—one may treat anticipation as a procedure to be completed, another as a private language, a third as an inherited habit under quiet revision. Carry the desire through rhythm and shaped image rather than adjective accumulation: anchor each new physical or emotional turn in one concrete sensory detail from the scene, and let syntax do the moving, so that a long suspended sentence can hold the moment before a touch arrives and a short one can land it. Unfold a comparison only when it changes what the focal participant understands about the other person or about their own want; an image may begin as a simile and return later as a remembered truth. Plain statements supply the contrast that makes the figurative register felt, and the partners keep their own speech rather than borrowing the narrator's figures. Hold the language of desire apart from the language of administration, then let them leak into one another when the point of view notices the collision—a carefully chosen word failing against an actual body, an official-sounding expectation dissolving into something simpler and kinder. That comic correction should expose something