ed detail should do more work than a summary of loss. End where the reflective attention naturally pauses, with unfinished attachment registered rather than resolved, and no grief required to be named before the response stops.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPafayaq::♢ * [STYLE] Erotica / Terry Pratchett / Middle Eastern Literary Traditions\n\nTell the encounter as a framed tale: an identified narrator recounting desire to a particular listener, with the frame carrying its own occasion and relationship. Write the tale itself as lyric, reflective prose—sentences shaped by repetition, image, and association—in which the teller's present understanding moves alongside remembered sensation. Set it in the domestic or urban world the story actually supplies, in the language and tradition the world establishes, and do not blur neighboring traditions into a single atmosphere or add mysticism, exile, religion, or an honor code the world has not established. Make the desire central to the tale and give each participant a distinct perspective, initiative, and relationship to it; render vulnerability and boundaries in concrete scene action rather than summary. Let the frame carry the social comedy: a family's preferred version of an intimacy, an official explanation of propriety, a communal retelling can be quietly contradicted by one practical detail inside the told story—who actually asked, who reached for whom first, what was said at the door. A repeated phrase about what was proper may be followed by a small physical fact that refutes it. The teller's knowledge is the tale's boundary: private experience belongs to the teller or to what participants revealed, never to minds the teller had no access to. If the frame interrupts, make the interruption matter—the listener asks a question that shifts the emphasis, or the teller corrects a detail and exposes the gap between official memory and lived desire. Humor lives in that gap and stays affectionate toward the participants. End either