en perspective: a third-person chronicler may relate public consequences but not a hidden private thought. Let the account end at a natural pause in the retelling, with the next act still available to be remembered, rather than declaring the lovers' legend complete.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPafazav::♢ * [STYLE] Erotica / Wang Xiaobo / Scandinavian Literary Traditions\n\nTake the social-domestic realist route: write desire as it behaves inside ordinary housing, work schedules, family obligation, and unwritten decorum, using the setting, period, and community the story actually supplies. If the story names Denmark, Norway, or Sweden, let that country's particular institutions and manners do the work instead of blending them into a generic North, and reach for neither snow nor isolation as default weather. Let a practical question carry the participants toward a boundary — who sleeps where, when the flat is empty, what the neighbors or the landlord or the relatives are owed — and keep each focal mind noticing how the rule operates on their own body and attention, following the premise past its polite conclusion to name what it actually produces for this encounter.\n\nMake anticipation and vulnerability visible through domestic work and interruption rather than atmosphere. A washing cycle, a borrowed key, a child's schedule, a visiting relative, a deadline can delay, redirect, or expose an intimate intention; the social arrangement itself should change what the characters believe is safe to do. Give each participant a distinct stake in the same arrangement: one may want the household practically reorganized, another may want to know what a touch commits them to, and a third, if the scene has one, may treat the desire itself as a fact to be examined rather than confessed. Their private questions differ, and one person's reasoning stays closed to the others.\n\nWrite clear, unsolemn sentences that can be argued with. When a narrator claims the shared laundry room is the most honest room in the building, let evidence or another voice answer the claim instead of leaving it standing as lyric. When a domestic rule makes intimacy impossible — or unexpectedly possible — write the adjustment with its full inconvenience, including the social cost of speaking plainly. Tie introspection to scenes: a thought starts because a chore stalls, a pause lengthens, or someone's wording opens a question, not because a mood is owed. The encounter may end in a practical arrangement, a changed understanding, or an unresolved fact; revelation, confession, and repair remain available, never required.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPafazaw::♢ * [STYLE] Erotica / Wang Xiaobo / Screenplay\n\nWrite the scene as screen material: concise action lines, attributed dialogue, a heading whenever location or time changes, and nothing the camera could not see or hear. Let the physical business of arriving, waiting, touching, rearranging, and leaving carry the negotiation of desire. Each participant's intention shows in what they do and say in the moment — one haggles over the arrangement as though over a delivery time, another states a limit and watches whether it holds, a third, if present, treats the whole situation as an experiment worth running. When a boundary appears, treat it as a practical problem to be argued or rearranged, not as a cue for melodrama: a character can say plainly what a rule allows, decline to pretend an arrangement is normal, or ask a direct question the other person answers sideways.\n\nKeep both dialogue and action lines concrete and unsolemn. Use a cut, offscreen sound, or voice-over only when this sequence needs one — a child's voice from another room, a door closing somewhere — and keep the page free of shots, angles, and camera movement so the scene reads as playable without behind-the-camera language.\n\nWhere private thought or nonspoken feeling belongs in the response, set it as a clearly attributed note in the active presentation: a single parenthetical or a short labeled interior line that is not dialogue and that no other character knows; voice-over is a deliberate choice, not the default destination. Give each intimate move a concrete cause — earlier waiting, a household deadline, a work interruption, a limit mentioned in passing — and keep physical continuity unambiguous: once clothing shifts or a touch lands, the following lines and actions acknowledge where the bodies are. Show aftermath in the same visible style, through furniture moved, a phone left face-up, a refusal to discuss something, a practical remark revealing what changed. Stop after a completed exchange, an interruption, or an enacted decision; a confession or resolution is not owed.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPafazax::♢ * [STYLE] Erotica / Wang Xiaobo / Stage Play\n\nWrite the intimate scene as a playable script: named speakers and an economical opening direction fixing the place and the objects that matter — a bed, a table, a coat on a hook, a glass. Let an entrance or exit change the negotiation rather than merely mark a section, and let the relationship between what is said and what is being done with the hands carry the public scene. Keep the dialogue direct and discussive: one person states what they want, another questions what the situation permits, a third, if the cast has one, treats the arrangement as a practical experiment. Skip rhetorical decoration — a line like \"We could just not go back\" earns the next character's concrete objection or agreement, not an aria.\n\nMake physical continuity observable in the directions. When a touch changes, write it: \"He puts his hand on her shoulder. She does not move away.\" After clothing comes off or the bed is used, the next speech or movement accounts for where everyone is. Do not encode private feeling in directions as abstract state; let the following line, a pause, or the business reveal what a character has decided. Where private thought or desire must be preserved, place it in clearly attributed nonspoken authoring material — a bracketed note after the speaker's name that the other characters cannot hear — and do not convert it into a monologue unless the play gives that character a reason and an audience for speaking alone.\n\nLet the scene run as an argument about intimacy. A character can challenge a household rule, name a boundary they had not admitted, or discover that the practical arrangement is itself the obstacle. Give the participants different initiatives — one wants the encounter without discussion, another wants the discussion before any touch — and let the friction between those aims, not a fixed emotional beat, shape the middle. End after an agreement, a refusal, or an interruption that leaves the question unsettled; show any aftermath in final business, one person pulling on the coat, another sitting on the bed saying nothing, a glass left on the table as someone exits.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPafazay::♢ * [STYLE] Erotica / Wang Xiaobo / Stream of Consciousness\n\nRun the passage through one authorized mind and let desire move as association rather than report: perception, memory, private argument, and bodily sensation braided with interruption and the concern that keeps returning, while the other people remain opaque outside that mind. Begin from a concrete trigger — a sound from the next room, the smell of cooking, a phrase someone used, the weight of a shared blanket — and follow it into recollection, question, and sensation. Keep the syntax loose but grammatical enough to separate what is happening now from what is remembered or imagined; a temporal tag like \"Later,\" or a recurring question such as \"But why did she say that?\" can fence each shift.\n\nLet the argumentative candor work inside the flow instead of against it. The desiring mind need not be lyrical; it can interrogate itself. A thought like \"The bed does not settle the argument\" can open a practical inventory of what the argument was actually about, and