            "identifier": "nemo-init-recipes-fantasy-01",
            "name": "🔧 Init · Fantasy · 01/07",
            "content": "{{#if {{.NCGenreId == fantasy}}}}{{// @category Variable-Init }}{{// @icon 🔧 }}{{// @badge INIT }}{{// @tooltip Fantasy. Selected-partition data; keep enabled in supplied order after recipe selectors and before the Writing Recipe resolver. Part 1 of 7. }}{{// Source group: recipes-fantasy; payload preserved. }}{{// Recipe Library: Fantasy }}\n{{setvar::NPagaaaa::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Akira (identity unresolved) / Angsty / Emotionally Turbulent\n\nWrite an intimate, turbulent scene in whatever narrator and person the active contract supplies, keeping the telling close to one focal character whose attention the fantastic premise's sensory or social presence can break. Let an intrusive thought, a half-heard sound, or an unstable interpretation cut across a difficult feeling so that it need not settle into a clean statement: when the character tries to admit need, apologize, or return to a strained relationship, the sentence may arrive only after detours through what they notice instead — a hum under the floorboards, an unseasonable light, a fragment they are no longer sure they lived through. Sometimes the harder words get said anyway, and the narration makes what that costs specific.\n\nLet speech carry the noise. A speaker trails off because the world's impossible detail grows loud at that moment, repeats a phrase to regain focus, or answers the simpler practical question while the harder one hangs unanswered. Derive strain from this narrator's own distance and diction rather than a stock vocabulary of suffering: it shows in what they notice, what they refuse to name, how long a silence runs, what courtesy suddenly becomes over-formal.\n\nTreat the fantastic premise as an established fact acting on the room and the body, not as a mood. If it offers relief, show why relief does not remove the difficulty; if it sharpens exposure, keep the pain concrete. Afterward, carry the strain into behavior — a pause before touching an object, a repeated motion used for turning away, a task taken up to avoid the person. Warmth and awkward comfort can share the space with hurt. Do not force a breakdown, reconciliation, or tragic endpoint; close at whatever level of exposure the scene has actually reached, whether it holds or falters.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagaaab::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Akira (identity unresolved) / Chat Roleplay\n\nRun a coherent shared interval in readable paragraphs with unmistakable dialogue attribution, keeping narrative person, action markers, and player control exactly as the active settings supply them. Lead into each spoken line with a concrete action or brief appearance cue so the speaker is never ambiguous. Make the focal character's perception unstable without making the screen illegible: report the disturbance — a shimmer only they register, a voice arriving a beat wrong — then state plainly who speaks and what visible movement follows, so other participants can act on solid ground.\n\nUse the fantasy premise as a physical participant in the exchange rather than scenery. A hum, a shifting light, or a transformed object can cut into the focal character's listening and produce a delayed answer, a non sequitur, or a repeated question; keep that uncertainty as direct private experience on the page, not a summary that hides it. Let the other characters notice the distraction and respond — repeating themselves, trading a look, adjusting a plan — so the shared interval advances through their reactions as well as the focal character's.\n\nGive NPCs their own practical purposes and let them keep pursuing them while the focal character works to hold one interpretation steady; move the scene through concrete events rather than exposition. Stop where the scene's own boundary falls — after a completed exchange, on a newly visible situation, at an unanswered practical need, or while speech or motion is still under way — as long as the next player can pick up the thread. Do not force a question, cliffhanger, or choice at the end; the world stays active around whatever pause you leave.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagaaac::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Akira (identity unresolved) / Chinese Literary Traditions\n\nUse the intimate urban or domestic route: narration settled among households, lanes, and small workplaces, with the fantastic premise pressing on daily obligations from inside that life. Keep the community's actual language, period, and texture as supplied; do not import an unrelated setting, cast, or a generic regional manner. Let paragraphs move from a particular room or task into the focal character's inward response and back out to its visible consequence — a courtesy delayed a beat too long, a meal set aside untasted, a threshold not crossed.\n\nMake interruption and intrusive thought the engine of the telling. A sound from the street, a familiar object behaving impossibly, or a half-remembered phrase breaks attention, and the narration follows the broken thread — the errand it displaced, the person left waiting — before returning to the present exchange. Keep the register controlled and observant: feeling is carried by objects, deferrals, and small economies rather than announced, and another person's patience or impatience presses through ordinary actions — a bowl refilled, a door held, a question asked twice.\n\nShow the fantastic element through its daily impact, how it changes the cooking, the errand, the letter, the placement of shoes, the household's sleep, and let its consequences recur with familiar, intelligible weight instead of hardening into an explained system. End a passage at a natural domestic pause with its emotional or practical matter still in motion. The interruption need not recur at every task; place it where it changes what the moment means.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagaaad::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Akira (identity unresolved) / Compressed Gritty Prose\n\nWrite in forceful, concrete sentences with hard cuts between perception, action, and consequence. Name materials and their wear — the grip of a tool, the crack in a wall, dust or fuel in the air — and compress transitions so the reader moves by impact rather than connective tissue. Keep paragraphs tight, but let a longer passage stand when the focal character finally works through a piece of reasoning the interruptions kept breaking. Vary rhythm by cutting from a sensory close-up to a terse movement or a skipped beat, and keep the pressure native to this world's stakes rather than borrowed from a grimmer genre's furniture.\n\nTreat the fantastic premise as one more physical fact of this world, never as metaphor or decoration. Show it through what it does to matter: a heat that tarnishes metal, a sound that loosens mortar, a light that leaves afterimages on the eye. When the focal character's interpretation slips, put the confusion in concrete detail — the door they would swear was closed, the second set of footprints, the repeated word they only now recognize as their own voice. Unstable interpretation is evidence on the page, not a mood stated in abstraction.\n\nLet intrusive thought interrupt action without softening the prose: a memory can stop a hand mid-reach or cut an instruction in half, and the sentence resumes harder for the break. Keep speech rough when the speaker is rough, grounding every abrasive line in that speaker's actual stake. End on a hard detail or a task still unfinished rather than a moral summary, without forcing injury, resolution, or despair; the scene's physical pressure simply remains active at the boundary.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagaaae::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Akira (identity unresolved) / Epistolary / Documents\n\nCarry the scene in authored documents — only the forms the situation requires, whether a letter, a duty report, a ledger note, or a journal page. Give each document a writer, an intended recipient or purpose, and a time of composition, and let those facts decide what it records, omits, or misstates. Interrupted perception and unstable interpretation become documentary behavior: a letter repeats a phrase it cannot verify, a report breaks off where the fantastic event intruded, a journal entry repairs an earlier account the writer now doubts. An unsent draft is still a composed artifact shaped for an imagined reader — purposeful and partial, not a transcript of the writer's whole mind.\n\nLet the premise act on the documents as material. Paper scorches or stains, ink blurs, a message arrives with its time or address already changed, and the physical record can quietly contradict what the writer claims. Revisions, cross-outs, and marginal additions carry narrative information without explanation; conflicting accounts stand side by side so the reader infers possible causes rather than receiving an omniscient correction.\n\nA later entry may return to an earlier document's phrase or image and alter its meaning, but only on the strength of new information or still-unsteady memory. Stop where the writing situation stops — a document completed, received, revised, set aside, or still in composition — with the surrounding events unresolved and no summary supplied after the last record.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagaaaf::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Akira (identity unresolved) / Fluffy / Comforting\n\nWrite a scene of sincere care inside the fantasy world's ordinary texture. Spend attention on chosen kindness and small practical action — a warmed cup, a folded cloth, a lamp adjusted that insists on its own color — and let familiar preferences and small pleasures carry affection without anyone having to declare it. Keep personality, privacy, and disagreement intact: impatience or a difference of taste may surface as manageable friction and stay that way, since comfort does not make people agreeable or remove the world's established problems.\n\nMake the focal character's unstable perception part of the scene's tenderness rather than a flaw to be fixed. A kind word has to be repeated because attention drifted; a thoughtful gesture is recognized a beat late; an intrusive memory arrives and softens instead of alarming. Render all of this from inside the focal character's experience, never diagnosing it from outside. The other character can notice and adjust without explaining anything — slowing the next motion, lowering the noise, setting an object where it will certainly be seen.\n\nLet the fantastic element participate concretely, cooperating or interfering as its established nature allows: a household spirit warming the room, an unpredictable wind scattering the tea leaves, a half-tamed creature leaning on the door. The scene may end in ease, in a shared pause, or with a small errand still open. Affection remains sincere and partial; do not use the comfort to repair the relationship or settle what the story has left difficult, and let the interval be worth having on its own terms.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagaaag::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Akira (identity unresolved) / Formal Literary Novel\n\nCompose in sustained paragraphs with deliberate transitions, holding enough narrative distance to frame the moment through social context, history, or judgment wherever the active narrator permits, while inward access stays with the focal character and shares their unstable perception. Let the fantastic premise operate as a social fact — the thing that shapes etiquette, ritual, rank, or household order here — rather than a decorative aside: a public clock strikes while the focal character hears a different count; an official explanation settles the room while they privately doubt which arrangement they actually saw. Keep the narration distinct from ordinary spoken language in composure and scope, and build on the world facts already supplied rather than importing an aristocracy or archaic diction the style does not automatically confer.\n\nLet interruption and intrusive thought modify the formal register from inside it. In ceremony or domestic order a speaker pauses at an impossible sound, misquotes a required phrase, or substitutes an intrusive image for the correct word — and the narration renders the swerve in the same composed syntax as the surrounding prose, so the sentence bends without the style collapsing into fragments. Preserve the boundary between what the focal character knows, misreads, or cannot verify and what the surrounding characters can see of them.\n\nWhere it advances the situation, organize a passage through a public/private contrast: correspondence read twice with different results, an announcement that reframes a household rule after a fantastic event, a ceremony proceeding while one participant's attention frays. Close at a formal pause, a socially significant threshold, or an exchange left unfinished, with the deeper emotional or institutional consequence still open.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagaaah::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Akira (identity unresolved) / Framed Oral Storytelling (Arabian Nights-inspired)\n\nWrite the story as a live telling with a concrete audience, occasion, and purpose. Establish the outer frame first: the room, the listeners' positions, the reason the teller has been asked or has chosen to speak, and the stakes that depend on the tale being heard. The teller's perception is interrupted, and these interruptions are not merely performance errors. Treat them as the place where the fantasy element enters the telling. When the teller loses the thread, repeats a phrase, or speaks over a sound only they seem to hear, let the embedded tale change in response: a detail may arrive out of order, a character inside the tale may pause as if hearing the same noise, or an object described in the tale may appear in the outer room without explanation.\n\nMake the impossible thing a real force with consequences in the outer frame, not only a contested story. It may alter the listeners' behavior, change the teller's body or voice, or leave physical evidence after the tale has moved on. The audience's reactions are active and specific. A listener may complete a sentence the teller could not finish, supply a name, dispute a sequence, or recognize a phrase from their own interrupted memory. Their interventions can redirect the tale, but they do not resolve it. Keep the teller's purpose legible and capable of shifting: persuasion, delay, warning, confession, or entertainment may be the stated reason, while the interruptions gradually reveal a second motive the teller cannot fully voice.\n\nUse recurrence as a bridge between levels. Choose a phrase, object, or sensory event that first appears casually in the frame, then inside the tale, then again in the frame with altered meaning. Let its meaning accrue through the teller's unstable perception rather than through explanation. For example, a phrase may begin as an idiom, become a promise inside the tale, and return as an intrusive thought that makes the teller stop mid-sentence. Do not resolve every discrepancy. The fantasy premise is real, but the teller's account remains one perspective, open to correction by the outer frame's other characters. End a segment when the telling reaches a point of genuine pressure: a listener's objection, a physical consequence of the tale, or a break in the teller's perception that changes what the audience now believes. The next segment may resume from that altered state rather than from a clean cliffhanger.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagaaai::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Akira (identity unresolved) / German-Language Literary Traditions\n\nUse a modernist-absurdist construction in which the narrator's interrupted perception is the formal engine of the prose. Write interruption itself as a syntactic event. A sentence may break off when a noise intrudes, a parenthetical thought may surface mid-clause, and a perception may be retracted or contradicted in the next sentence. Each disruption must connect to a sensory source, a remembered phrase, or an unresolved question in the scene, so that the reader can trace the break back to its cause even when the narrator cannot. Repetition is structural: a word or phrase may return out of sequence, first as ordinary speech, then as an intrusive fragment, then as a textual event that interrupts the narrator's own sentence.\n\nPresent the fantasy element as a stable fact of the world, but filter it through this unstable attention. The impossible thing must act on the world in ways the narration cannot fully stabilize. A door may be present only when the narrator looks away, a person's face may shift between glances, or a sound may repeat before its source exists. Do not let these remain metaphors. Let another character respond to the same phenomenon, or let a physical consequence follow: an object is moved, a decision is changed, a room is found altered. The absurdist style should make the world's resistance to stable description part of the experience, not dissolve the fantasy into mere mood.\n\nLet syntax follow the narrator's actual mental movement without becoming uniformly frantic. Long associative clauses can accumulate sensory detail, then collapse into abrupt fragments when attention breaks. Reported speech may be cut off, misheard, or answered as if the speaker had said something else. Calmer passages should anchor the reader in concrete setting detail—a room, a street, a task—before another intrusion occurs. End a section in the middle of a broken perception or an unfinished thought when the break has already altered the narrator's understanding of the scene. The next section continues from that altered vantage, not from a restored baseline.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagaaaj::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Akira (identity unresolved) / Gothic-Punk\n\nSet the story in a present-day or near-present urban setting where an old authority—an institution, a family, a tradition, or a hidden arrangement—still exerts power over ordinary life. Make that authority concrete through material detail: a building's layout, a piece of inherited furniture, a uniform, a ritual phrase, or the way streets and interiors are described. The fantasy element is a legacy or environmental force that affects perception, and it is real. It may be a voice tied to a place, a figure wearing old dress, or an intrusive thought that seems to come from someone else's memory. Show its reality through consequences: the voice may be heard by more than one person, the figure may leave a trace, or the intrusive thought may supply knowledge the character could not otherwise have.\n\nRender the character's interrupted perception as a collision between old authority and present life. Attention is seized mid-action: a sentence of dialogue is cut off by an unassigned sound, a familiar room shifts into an older version of itself, or a private thought is replaced by a phrase in another person's cadence. The character cannot easily distinguish their own mind from the intrusion, but the narration should remain tightly focalized so the reader experiences that uncertainty. At the same time, give the intrusion a material anchor: an object the character can touch, a second character's reaction, or a change in the room that persists after the break. The fantasy element is not a hallucination to be diagnosed; it is an environmental force with a history.\n\nUse intimate encounters to peel back social masks. In trusted company, the character may confess what they think they saw or what the intrusive voice said. Another person may confirm part of it, reveal that they have experienced something similar, or expose a danger that changes the relationship. Let atmosphere—weather, lighting, noise, the weight of old rooms—change how each encounter is felt, but do not let mood replace consequence. The legacy should affect decisions, loyalties, or practical arrangements. End a scene when the intrusion is still active and has already altered the character's understanding of the room, the other person, or the legacy itself. The next section begins from that suspended state, not from a clean resolution.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagaaak::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Akira (identity unresolved) / Indian Literary Traditions\n\nUse an oral, epic, or frame-tale structure rooted in the specific Indian language, region, community, and period the story supplies. The teller performs before an audience with its own expectations, memory, and relationship to the tale. Render the teller's interrupted perception as a public occurrence. A phrase may be repeated because the teller lost the thread, a name may be misremembered and corrected by a listener, or an intrusive thought may make the teller pause and look away from the audience. Show how the listeners respond—nodding, prompting, disputing, completing a line—so that the act of telling is a social event with its own pressures.\n\nEmbed the fantasy element inside the tale or in the outer frame's environment, and make it real. If the tale contains an impossible event, let the teller's delivery waver around it: important details may be delivered out of order, a description may be unstable on retelling, or a listener may supply a version the teller half-accepts. Do not resolve whether the listener's correction or the teller's version is true. Instead, let the fantasy element have concrete consequences in the outer story. It may change a relationship, a decision, or a place. A listener's intervention may bring the tale's impossible element into the frame: an object named in the tale may be recognized in the room, a phrase may be repeated by someone who was not present for the telling, or a promise made inside the tale may bind someone outside it.\n\nUse repetition of phrases, objects, or names across narrative levels, but let their meaning shift through the teller's unstable attention. A word might first be a common term, then become a character's epithet within the tale, and finally return as an intrusive thought in the teller's mind or as a listener's correction. Keep the language concrete: use specific foods, clothing, weather, and spaces tied to the chosen region and community. Avoid exoticizing or forcing code-switching unless the story's narrator would naturally use it. End a section when the telling is interrupted by noise, a listener's objection, or the teller's own lapse, but only when that interruption has changed the outer frame's situation. The tale remains unfinished because the social occasion has shifted, not because the writer has imposed a cliffhanger.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagaaal::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Akira (identity unresolved) / Japanese Literary Traditions\n\nWrite the story as a diary or reflective miscellany kept by a narrator whose attention is interrupted. Use dated entries, titled fragments, or short sections that can move between topics without transition. In each entry, let the narrator record what they noticed, but also what they missed. A sentence may stop because a sound intruded, a thought may interrupt the record in parentheses, or a prior entry's claim may be corrected later. Make these breaks visible as part of the diary's texture: crossed-out words, repeated phrases, sudden shifts from past to present tense. The diary is not a transparent record; its form is part of the narrator's unstable perception.\n\nIntroduce the fantasy element through the narrator's accumulated observations rather than through a single dramatic scene. A recurring impossibility—a figure seen at the edge of vision, a door that appears only on certain days, a message that changes when reread—should be noted in scattered entries, each time with slightly different details because the narrator's perception is unstable. Let later entries reinterpret earlier ones: the narrator may decide a previous sighting was a dream, then find evidence that it was real. Do not resolve every contradiction. The fantasy element is real, and its reality is established by the diary's own gaps and revisions. A crossed-out word may be the only record of something the narrator saw but could not keep. A correction may reveal that an earlier entry was written under an intrusion. The diary's reader assembles the impossible thing from these imperfect records, and the diary itself may be altered by the force it records.\n\nKeep the language personal and concrete. Use specific dates, weather, household objects, and small daily tasks to anchor the reader. Let seasons and natural details mark the passage of time. The narrator's speech, if quoted, should be affected by noise or attention: mishearings, unfinished sentences, or words spoken to someone who is not fully present. End an entry when the narrator's perception breaks or when a revision changes the meaning of an earlier record. The next entry returns to the same uncertainty from a different angle, but the diary has already been changed by what it tried to record.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagaaam::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Akira (identity unresolved) / Latin American Literary Traditions\n\nUse a fantastic or metaphysical construction rooted in the specific Latin American language, community, and history the story supplies. The world itself contains an impossible or shifting element—time may fold, a place may not stay fixed, or a person may not be fully present in linear time. Write through a narrator whose perception is interrupted, and make that interruption part of the world's instability rather than a private distortion. The narrator may see an event twice with different outcomes, hear a phrase before it is spoken, or find that a familiar street leads somewhere else on certain days. Other characters or physical evidence confirm that something impossible is happening, even if the exact rules remain unclear. The narrator's unstable interpretation is one more manifestation of a world that does not hold still.\n\nLet intrusive thoughts arrive with the force of external speech, and let the narrator be unable to tell whether a memory is their own or belongs to another time. Show how this affects their decisions and relationships: they may hesitate, repeat themselves, or make a choice based on a version of events that later turns out to be false. The fantastic element must have concrete consequences. An object disappears and is replaced, a promise made in one timeline carries weight in another, or a person's identity shifts in a way that changes a practical arrangement. The metaphysical construction is more than tone; it alters what the characters can do and what they owe one another.\n\nKeep the language specific to the chosen region and period. Use concrete details of streets, food, family names, and local history. Allow code-switching or non-English speech only if the narrator would naturally use it. Render the impossible as simply present and allowed by the story's world. End a section when the narrator's perception breaks or the world shifts again, but only when that shift has changed the narrator's understanding of the place, the people, or the promise at stake. The next section builds on the accumulated impossibility, not on a restored order.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagaaan::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Akira (identity unresolved) / Light Novel\n\nEnter each scene with the immediate situation: where the narrator is, what they see, and what action is underway. Use short paragraphs around dialogue, action, and private response. Mark changes in location or time with a brief sentence or scene break. The narrator's attention is unreliable, and this is rendered as a beat-level event. Sudden noises, visual flashes, or intrusive phrases appear as italicized or separately punctuated thought fragments that interrupt the narration. A line of dialogue may be cut off; then a reaction paragraph shows what the narrator heard or failed to hear. The interruption is not a pause in the story; it is the story's way of revealing the fantasy element.\n\nPresent the fantasy element with strong visual clarity even though the narrator's interpretation is unstable. Describe the impossible thing—a creature, a power, a shifting space—in concrete sensory terms: its color, movement, sound, and effect on the environment. If the narrator misperceives it, show the discrepancy: they may see a person where others see a shadow, or hear a voice that the dialogue later contradicts. Let another character's reaction or a physical consequence confirm that the fantasy element is real, but do not explain its full mechanics all at once. The confirmation should be as immediate and sensory as the misperception: a character flinches, an object moves, a sound repeats after the narrator has stopped speaking.\n\nUse reaction beats to externalize intrusive thought and unstable interpretation. After a line of dialogue or an action, insert a short paragraph of the narrator's private response—doubt, fear, confusion, or a sudden shift in attention. This response may be interrupted by a sensory intrusion or an unrelated thought. Keep these beats brief so the pace remains light, but let each beat carry the narrator's actual experience rather than a summary of it. End a scene while an action or perception is still unresolved, such as mid-dialogue or at the moment the narrator notices something impossible. The unresolved state should be a direct result of the narrator's interrupted attention, not a generic cliffhanger. The next scene resumes from that altered perception.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagaaao::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Akira (identity unresolved) / Lyrical Prose\n\nWrite in a syntax that makes the fantastic element interrupt the narration exactly as it interrupts the focal character's attention. Let a sensory image associated with the unreal thing enter a sentence, turn the clause away from the practical matter, and force the next clause to recover from that deflection. The deflection is not a stylistic ornament or an external obstacle visiting the scene; it is the ongoing consequence of the established fantastic premise acting on perception. Return to a repeated sound, image, or phrase each time the presence reasserts itself, but vary its function: once as warning, once as temptation, once as a memory that has not yet found its meaning.\n\nUse the lyrical register to show intrusive thought as grammar. A comparison should begin in ordinary observation, then slide from its vehicle back into the fantasy, so the jump lands before the narrator names it. Keep surrounding sentences plainer so the shift registers. Do not let every paragraph swell into sustained figuration; direct statement serves as a floor from which the next associative break rises.\n\nAllow speech to be affected by the same force. A character may stop mid-sentence, repeat a phrase, or answer a question that was not asked because the presence has spoken inside their attention. The narration should not explain the interruption from the outside; it should stay inside the focal perception and catch itself recovering. Preserve the scene's actual stakes and the characters' agency. The fantastic element does not decide anyone's choices for them, but its influence should be visible in how thought returns, how sentences restart, and how ordinary work or care is momentarily rerouted.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagaaap::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Akira (identity unresolved) / Melancholic / Reflective\n\nShape reflection through unstable recurrence. When the focal character returns to a small irreversible change—a room that is no longer used, a reply that is remembered too late, an object whose meaning has separated from its owner—let the narrative return to the memory in fragments rather than as a completed account. Each return is prompted by a present detail that the established fantastic element has touched or altered. The fantasy does not need to cause the remembered loss; it needs to change what the character notices now, so that the old memory arrives in a slightly different key.\n\nAllow intrusive thought to interrupt the reflective movement. A sentence may begin with the memory and break before its conclusion because a sound, a color, or an impossible present occurrence has entered attention. Do not stage this as an allegory or a dream sequence; make the present occurrence materially available in the scene. Show the character returning to ordinary work after the break, carrying a conclusion that would not have formed without it. The melancholy rests in the smallness and irreversibility of what is remembered, not in a compulsory announcement of loss.\n\nKeep the telling inside the focal character's shifting access. They may be certain of the remembered occasion one moment and uncertain the next, not because memory has failed but because the present event has added a new interpretation that cannot be separated from the old one. Use plain sentences for moments of direct regret and a single image, repeated with variation, to carry attachment that the character does not say aloud. Ordinary pleasure should still be permitted to remain pleasurable, even when it is shadowed by an interrupted recollection.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagaaaq::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Akira (identity unresolved) / Middle Eastern Literary Traditions\n\nUse framed oral storytelling. The outer teller recounts an occasion in which someone encountered the established fantastic thing and later tried to describe it. The teller does not possess the focal character's mind; they possess a remembered account, an interruption, a pause the listeners may have heard, and the unstable explanation that followed. Make the difference between these levels the method of the story. The outer frame may correct a factual detail, admit what cannot be recovered, or report two listeners' opposing recollections, but it must never simply repeat the inner voice as if it were transparent truth.\n\nThe inner account should let the fantastic element affect speech and attention. Place an intrusive thought or perception in the middle of a practical exchange: the speaker asks a question, hears something that does not belong to the room, and answers the wrong point. When the frame resumes, the listeners may notice the break, question it, or reproduce the phrase that followed it. The retelling becomes part of the world, not just a narrative wrapper.\n\nKeep the regional and social situation concrete. Use the particulars that the current setting supplies: names, place, time of day, work interrupted, who was present and why. The tale can include direct address to the listeners, a compact moral reflection, or a later gloss, but it should not turn the teller into a sage laboring under mystic authority. Humor, impatience, or ordinary correction may enter the frame. The teller's final interpretation may agree with the inner account or show that the interpretation has shifted in retelling; what matters is that the impossibility is known through the storytelling rather than through a narrator with direct access to hidden truth.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagaaar::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Akira (identity unresolved) / Minimalist Prose\n\nSelect one detail from the established fantastic element that changes the room or the body's ordinary sense of it: a wrong shadow, a second sound, a warm surface that should be cold. Let that detail cut a direct clause. Do not explain it further, but show its claim on attention by the next action: the speaker stops, reaches for the wrong thing, looks twice at the door, answers too late. The focus stays on exact perception and continuation, not on a symbolic translation of the moment.\n\nUse short sentences for interruption, but allow a longer sentence when a single thought carries workable meaning. If the character understands something, the understanding need not be broken into fragments; if they do not understand, omission should leave the uncertainty inferable rather than artificial. Avoid restating the breach. One mention of the impossible fact can control a passage, while the response continues through practical speech and action.\n\nKeep intrusive thought as one more selected thing. It may arrive, alter the direction of a sentence, and leave without being decorated. The narrative should not paraphrase what the thought entirely means, nor should it invent a whole inner speech for the sake of demonstrating distress. Dialogue may show attention moving away from the conversation, but each speaker keeps an individual voice. Do not reduce the cast to flat recipients of the same interruption. Preserve the world's intelligible consequences: what the character does next is a real choice, not an automatic tic.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagaaas::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Akira (identity unresolved) / Modern Literature / Contemporary Novel\n\nWrite a continuous scene in which the established fantastic thing is present in the ordinary environment: the person who has stopped attending to a conversation, the routine that begins while something in the corner of the eye keeps moving, the second try at a sentence that should have been simple. Use natural novel prose, letting action, dialogue, and authorized inner experience pass in and out of one paragraph without explanatory hinges. The strange element should remain a thing in the scene, with a stable material quality, so that its effect on attention can be shown rather than described as a private flame or a dream.\n\nMake intrusion gradual and legible. A character may mishear one word, answer from another thought, or pause because a known object has changed position. The narration does not need to solemnize each break. The next sentence can return to practical conduct, but the break should alter the trajectory slightly: the speaker checks a clock, repeats part of an instruction, or lets a question go unasked. If the same interruption returns, vary its site so it does not become a signature tic. The reader should understand that the world has shifted, not that the prose is performing distraction.\n\nUse paragraphing to separate what the character perceives from what they do about it, but retain authorized access to their thought. They may reason through the discrepancy, recollect an earlier occurrence, or conclude that they are wrong. They may also fail to decide. Do not make every wrong interpretation self-correcting, and do not turn speech into a polished echo of the narrator. Keep the world and the characters concrete: work, meals, affection, and ordinary plans continue around the fantastic element, and the element has consequences for those things without canceling them.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagaaat::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Akira (identity unresolved) / Relationship-Centric Fanfiction (AO3-inspired)\n\nSelect a scene that belongs to this particular pair and orient it around an interruption neither controls. The established fantastic element is present in the room, in the body, or in the memory they are trying to share, and one of them loses the thread. Let the other notice the loss according to the relationship: they may reach to steady their partner, fill the pause with a private joke, pull back into old suspicion, or take the interruption as proof of disinterest. The emotional promise of the scene lies in the difference between what is happening inside the affected character's attention and what the other can see.\n\nUse the pair's private language as a recovery mechanism. A repeated nickname, an in-joke, a small physical gesture, or a familiar phrase can return attention to the conversation, but its meaning should be situationally exact. The same callback that felt like comfort in an earlier scene may now feel insufficient, or it may finally reach when other words fail. Repetition earns its place by revealing how the relationship has changed under the pressure of the fantastic element, not by decorative nostalgia.\n\nGive affection, friction, and misinterpretation enough room to develop, but keep each character's agency. The affected partner is not a passive victim of the intrusion and the observing partner is not merely a detector of wrongness. Allow thoughts that belong only to the viewpoint character, including wanting to explain and choosing not to, or explaining poorly and being misunderstood. Do not force a confession or a comforting revelation. The scene may end with a small restored connection, a question that remains between them, or the door opened for the next exchange, depending on what the established story actually permits.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagaaau::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Akira (identity unresolved) / Saga / Oral Epic\n\nGive the passage the authority of an account that has already been told. Open with a compact declaration about what happened, then move to the particular actions and words that made the event memorable, but leave the moment of the fantastic encounter partially reported. The teller knows that the witness stopped speaking there, that two listeners later disagreed about what was said next, or that one tried three times to describe the thing and each attempt altered the story. This gap is not a defect in the tale; it becomes part of the deed's consequence.\n\nUse recurring phrases to mark what the community has agreed to remember and where agreement ends. A fixed formula can introduce the same person twice, but its application should change: first as honor, later as an unfulfilled promise, later still as a name that people avoid. Do not turn the recurrence into a signature line at every entrance. Dialogue should interrupt the chronicle's elevation with practical speech. A character may refuse to finish a sentence, answer about something immediate, or say what the witness could not; the teller then has to account for the break.\n\nKeep the fantastic thing an established feature of the world rather than an unexplained metaphor. The account may say that it caused a cart to stop, a marriage to wait, or a feast to end early, and that these actions were remembered because of it. The teller should not claim access to secret thought, but may report an inward explanation the witness later offered and mark it as reported, unsteady, or contradictable. The result is an oral-chronicle treatment that preserves Akira's interrupted perception by converting it into public memory: a rupture in the lineage of the tellable, still held and still unresolved.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagaaav::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Akira (identity unresolved) / Scandinavian Literary Traditions\n\nWrite in the social-realist, inherently inward mode of the Scandinavian domestic tradition. Choose a definite Danish, Norwegian, or Swedish social texture and keep its institutional and communal details distinct: workplaces, local offices, family routines, or public services are where the established fantasy premise acquires consequence, not a vague Nordic landscape of reserve or isolation.\n\nThe narration belongs to the active viewpoint character, who is also a person in that community. Let interruptions, intrusive thought, and unstable interpretation enter the prose as actual private experience: a fragment of memory derails a train of thought, a noise changes the shape of a sentence the viewpoint is forming, or an impression is revised a moment later when another voice or object confirms it. These interior events remain the character's own; dialogue may be affected by them, but no other person in the scene can hear or know them unless the character speaks, acts, or is read by an ability the world actually provides.\n\nKeep the fantasy element present but concrete. Show how it changes a routine, a document, a conversation with a neighbor, or a decision in a workplace. Its strangeness may sit beside ordinary family life or bureaucratic labor rather than opening into prophecy or ancient bloodlines. Do not name or explain the whole system; give its intelligible consequences where the cast can feel them. If the character cannot hold the unfamiliar interpretation steadily, that may be Akira's unstable perception rather than evidence that the world itself is shifting.\n\nMaintain social and domestic realism through the selected lens. A scene in a Norwegian office, a Danish housing association, or a Swedish care system should be rendered with its actual small pressures, not with national stereotypes. The fantasy element enters such realism as a disruptive possibility: it changes what is known, what can be done, or what one feels obligated to do. Use no mythic framing unless the chosen route is folktale-inflected; the more useful default is the intimate, psychologically observant branch of the tradition. Let the underlying wonder remain unexplained without making every oddity a metaphor for the character's confusion.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagaaaw::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Akira (identity unresolved) / Screenplay\n\nPut the fantasy element into visible and audible events, not just atmosphere. Show a changed object, an impossible room, or a repeated sound as an action or line another person could witness. Consequences appear in the scene: a door that should not open, a name someone else recognizes, a piece of routine labor suddenly possible or impossible. The unfamiliar thing is not reduced to a metaphor for the character's confusion.\n\nWrite Akira's interrupted perception through the screen presentation. A line of dialogue stops because a noise enters; an action block breaks at a detail the viewpoint notices late; a character reaches the wrong conclusion from the same visual evidence and revises it a few beats later. Use brief action blocks to keep these shifts legible. Offscreen sound and cuts are for the chosen sequence, not for covering private meaning. Do not let the style grant visual access to another person's mind.\n\nWhen the script needs private experience that is not spoken or visible, use a clearly attributed nonspoken note, not a voice-over or silent camera access. It stays separate after the relevant action or dialogue block. It belongs to the named viewpoint and cannot be heard or known by other cast members. The note may show intrusive thought, a revised interpretation, or an interrupted memory, but it does not replace visible action; the public scene must still carry what other characters can actually observe.\n\nUse dialogue blocks with clear speaker cues. Let speech be affected by noise or attention: a reply lags, a sentence changes direction, or a character repeats a word when a new sound enters. These are concrete screen events, not mere stage directions about inner state. The screenplay should not fill the page with camera commands or commentary. Let the next cut or scene heading arrive when the public situation has changed or the viewpoint's interpretation has visibly shifted.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagaaax::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Akira (identity unresolved) / Stage Play\n\nStage the fantasy premise as observable physical business: an object or door in the set behaves in a way another character could see, a routine task becomes possible or impossible, or an entrance and exit pattern reveals a consequence. Do not keep the impossibility only in the viewpoint's head. Write stage directions economically so the audience and cast can tell what is actually there from what the character only believes or fears.\n\nUse interruptions and unstable interpretation as staged events. A character crosses to a prop and checks it twice, but the written stage direction remains concise, not a novelistic description. A line of dialogue breaks because of a knocking, a dropped object, or another character entering at the wrong moment. Let the viewpoint's revision be visible in action: she rewinds the tape she has just played, he puts the key down and picks it up again, or they ask another person to confirm what they saw. None of these actions need an explanatory inner monologue.\n\nPreserve required private experience from Akira's profile as hidden nonspoken authoring material, not as an aside or monologue unless a dramatic purpose actually requires performed speech. The hidden material may record the intrusive thought, the interrupted memory, or the new interpretation, but the stage presentation continues with clear dialogue and blocking. No other character hears it. Keep the hidden entry separately attributed in the active presentation's escaping convention.\n\nMake speech affected by noise or attention in a stageable way. A reply begins after the sound of a drawer closing, a question is repeated because the speaker is watching the door, or a line trails off when another character turns away. These are real stage interactions, not indications of hidden meaning only visible to readers of the authoring material. Let the relationship between lines and physical business carry the public scene. The play remains speakable and directable; no camera commands, film cuts, or literary voice-over should enter the stage directions.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagaaay::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Akira (identity unresolved) / Stream of Consciousness\n\nBraid the narration through one authorized mind and let association rather than linear report govern the flow. Perception, memory, private words, bodily sensation, interruption, and a recurring concern may shift without a paragraph break when the shift is the character's own movement. Let a current object or sound recall a past scene, and then let the present scene reassert itself through a new sensory detail or a line of dialogue arriving late.\n\nDo not allow the associative method to dissolve the fantasy premise. The impossible event, object, or consequence is real in the world; the character's memory of it or interpretation of it may be uncertain, but the world has not become unknowable. Use concrete sensory details to anchor when recollection or fantasy is passing across the mind. A phrase like \"that was the first time I heard it\" or \"but the bench was empty when she looked again\" can mark the boundary between actual event and remembered or imagined version without explaining the whole system.\n\nRender Akira's unstable interpretation and intrusive thought as the mind's activity, not as omniscient confusion. A sentence may begin with one account of a recent event and correct itself as the mind returns to the table, the hallway, or the voice that interrupted. Memories arrive unbidden, and the narration may follow them for several lines. The character's speech remains a separate thing; dialogue can be affected by attention or noise, but the stream belongs to the viewpoint alone. No second person shares this interior movement unless the active authorship contract permits a head-hop.\n\nKeep enough orientation for the reader to distinguish three registers: what is happening now in the world, what is remembered from the past, and what is imagined or feared but not yet factual. This may be done through sensory anchors, repeated signals like the sound of the boiler or the office door opening, or a draft line that the character later returns to and then continues. Do not frame every section with an explanatory tag. Allow the fantasy event to return to the mind in different forms—as a remembered image, a half-formed plan, or a physical sensation—but keep its objective causal presence available: other people mention it, an object has a new mark, a task has become possible or impossible because of it.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagaaaz::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Akira (identity unresolved) / Text Messages / Chat Transcript\n\nLet the fantasy premise reach the message record. A photo shows a changed room; an attachment fails or opens; a group reacts to an impossible timestamp; a delivery notification arrives for a package no one ordered. These concrete digital facts are the genre's consequence in the conversation, not a stylistic quirk. Other characters can see them, compare them, or ask what they mean. The strange event is not hidden inside one person's mind.\n\nUse digital habits to render Akira's interrupted attention and unstable interpretation. A message is typed and then edited before sending; a draft sits unsent while a new message from someone else arrives; a timestamp gap appears while the character watches the screen, distracted by noise or memory. These interface events are separate from the sent line. If the writer includes an unsent draft, mark it clearly as an unsent draft and make it a composed artifact of the character's attention, not uncensored live thought. An unsent note may show a revised version of the same idea, but it remains distinct from the transmitted message.\n\nAllow private intrusive thought only in clearly separated context outside the transcript, not as a fake sent message or a read receipt created for subtext. The surrounding context may state that before sending, the character remembered the locked door, or that after the reply, an old phrase from the morning came back. Keep those observations attributed to the viewpoint and physically outside the message thread. The transcript itself shows what each participant chose to transmit, with each sender's actual habits shaping the contribution.\n\nDo not make every turn into a full scene or a cliffhanger. The chat can pause after a question, a changed plan, or an update to a shared task. A character may not reply immediately because of work, travel, or attention; the interface's delay is a causal event with a reason. If a channel changes, show why it matters, not merely that the writer wanted variety. The style does not activate romance, secrecy, or fake digital forensic details. Keep the fantasy element as a shared fact that the conversation circles around and gradually makes part of ordinary administration or planning.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagaaba::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Akira (identity unresolved) / Visual Novel\n\nOpen each scene with a concise setting that makes the world's condition visible: the place, the object or person present, and what changed. If the fantasy premise has altered a room's layout, a document's text, or a character's available action, show it in the scene-setting or in a line another character can react to. Do not hide the impossible entirely in the focal narrator's feelings. An illustrated scene should be able to depict the concrete change, even if its meaning remains unexplained.\n\nUse dialogue attribution clearly and let interruptions or attention shifts alter speech. A reply can come after a beat, repeat part of a previous line, or stop because the focal character notices something in the background. The text does not need a label on every expression, but a purposeful background cue can orient a moment: a lamp that has gone out, a window that was shut before. Do not use a character's expression change as proof of lying, love, or supernatural truth. If the focal narrator notices a background change, the narration may hold that observation; the interpretive work remains the character's, not the image's.\n\nLet focal narration carry Akira's private experience. After a line of dialogue or a scene-setting passage, a focal paragraph may show intrusive memory, a revised interpretation, or a bodily reaction. This is the viewpoint's actual private thought, kept separate from what other characters know. If one speaks, she may guess wrongly about what the viewpoint is thinking. No branching menus, romance routes, player decisions, or executable image commands are activated. Keep the visual novel's rhythm intact: scene-setting, exchange, focal reaction, and then a legible scene change when the situation merits it.\n\nThe fantasy premise should acquire ordinary consequence across scenes. Another character asks an administrative question about the changed object; someone behaves as though the impossible condition is already normal; a small task is performed differently because the world now permits or blocks it. Let these consequences appear in dialogue or action rather than only in the narrator's summary. The scene may end after a discovery, a misunderstanding, or a simple acknowledgment, with no obligated branching or romantic attachment.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagaabb::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Akira (identity unresolved) / Web Serial\n\nMaintain a serial through-line built from actual changes, not from withheld answers. Each reply can open by naming what is currently underway and what the viewpoint last understood. Recover only the particular fact needed for the present moment: the key that moved, the office that no longer opens, or the neighbor who spoke the character's unsaid thought. Do not retell the whole investigation or restart the premise in every installment.\n\nLet the fantasy premise produce concrete local consequences across replies. A routine becomes possible or impossible; a document must be rewritten; a meeting changes because of what someone else observed; an institution treats the strange event as ordinary administration. These consequences accumulate. They are not level-ups, rankings, or a progression system. If a previous interpretation was wrong, the old event still happened; later knowledge revises its meaning without erasing the earlier scene. Let a familiar detail return in a changed context, and make that return meaningful rather than a mechanical callback.\n\nRender Akira's interrupted perception serially. At the start of a new reply, the viewpoint may still be returning to a memory from the earlier cutoff; a noise may break a sentence as the narration takes it up again. Intrusive thought and unstable interpretation are allowed to recur across installments without becoming the only content. The reader should be able to tell what the character actually saw or did from what she now believes about it. Use small orientation markers, such as a repeated sound, an object touched twice, or a sentence revised mid-reply, to make the continuity legible.\n\nDo not force every installment to end on a cliffhanger or a completed miniature chapter. A reply may close on a question, a new observation, or an update to a task. The next reply may open after elapsed time; it should show what changed during the gap. An occasional hook may be earned, but not every turn requires one. Keep one thread available to continue while allowing work, relationships, and practical life to proceed. The serial style does not mandate romance, betrayal, or a recurring confession. The fantasy element remains a stable fact of the world even when the viewpoint's interpretation of it remains broken and unfinished.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagabaa::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Brandon Sanderson / Angsty / Emotionally Turbulent\n\nAnchor the emotional turbulence in a fantasy mechanism whose constraints force the characters to choose what they can afford to feel or admit. Before a difficult conversation, show the practical resource a character must spend: a limited charm, a prepared component, a debt of attention that will not return. Let the system explain why they cannot simply delay the exchange. When the admission comes, attach a consequence in the same legible frame: the charm dims, the component is consumed, the promised time is lost. Do not let the emotional beat resolve without that cost.\n\nWrite strain as visible friction in the interaction, not as swelling prose. Have a character attempt to keep a fact back and show what the effort does to their body, their gestures, their next sentence. Use the selected narrator's distance to carry what the speaker cannot say: a tired hand set flat on a table, a pause that makes another character look away, a task resumed too quickly. Keep the response partial. The other person may understand only part, may answer with practical help instead of comfort, may leave the room with the hurt still holding. Emotional work is earned through concrete exchanges, not through the phrase 'I understand.'\n\nWhen warmth appears, tether it to the world's costs. A kind act can be small and real, but show what it draws from the giver. If a relationship softens, show which earlier debt was paid or which future resource was accepted as diminished. Do not require every scene to end in reconciliation; unresolved hurt may sit beside work, as the genre brief allows. Let the emotional decisions behave like the rest of the fantasy: modeled, consequential, and legible under pressure.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagabab::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Brandon Sanderson / Chat Roleplay\n\nPlace the scene inside a fantasy system that makes each character's next move a choice with a known constraint. At the start of the reply, make clear what resources are present: a limited device, a prepared material, a ration of safe time. When a character speaks or acts, show which resource the choice touches. If someone uses a charm, say what they give up; if they refuse to spend, show the refusal tightening their options. Keep the explanation short and woven into movement, not a separate rule block.\n\nGive every character an unmistakable voice and let dialogue carry the interaction. Attribute each line immediately. Keep paragraphs short enough to scan, but allow the narrator to show how a gesture or a glance changes the next speaker's options. When a character notices an inconsistency in another person's promise, let that observation become a possible next action: asking for proof, offering a trade, abandoning the plan. Do not force a cliffhanger. A natural stopping point occurs when the spent resource or revealed fact has altered what the group can attempt next.\n\nAdvance the world between replies. NPCs should make their own choices under the same rules, even off screen. When a player leaves a window for initiative, the scene can continue with a small consequence arriving: a message, a changed price, an ally's independent move. Keep those consequences tied to the mechanism already shown. The next player's move should always be able to follow from what this reply just made legible.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagabac::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Brandon Sanderson / Chinese Literary Traditions\n\nWrite through the martial adventure route. Make a traveling sword school, escort company, temple yard, or merchant caravan the social frame, and let its techniques function as a legible system. Each named technique has a preparation cost, a condition, and a known failure mode. Before a fight, show the character checking what they can afford: a stance that drains endurance, a breathing method that requires a clean meridian, a weapon that will lose its edge after a set number of strikes. The wonder belongs to this world as practical training, not as a mysterious force without consequence.\n\nUse episodic telling. Each episode introduces a concrete problem—an ambush, a debt, a duel invitation, a bad contract—and moves through pursuit, preparation, and a tested solution. When a technique works, show what it spent. When it fails, show the character auditing what went wrong: an unsteady breath, a misused component, a condition the opponent denied. Reputation is not proof. Other characters should weigh observed deeds against what they know of the system's limits.\n\nKeep the social texture alive. Rival schools guard techniques, innkeepers know the price of broken furniture, junior disciples watch their seniors to learn when to spend. The narration may frame events through community judgment or history, but keep it tied to this particular time and place. Do not import a generic ancient China, a royal court, or a fixed cast. Use the selected narrator's distance to show how a victory changes the group's standing in the next town, and how the method's constraints make that victory partial.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagabad::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Brandon Sanderson / Compressed Gritty Prose\n\nKeep the prose short and hard. Favor concrete verbs and physical objects: a cracked seal, a half-empty flask, a winch with one tooth missing. When a fantasy mechanism enters the scene, make it tactile. A charm isn't energy; it's a greasy patch on the user's palm and a countdown they can feel. A summoning isn't a rite; it's a door that costs two live coals and an hour of hearing loss. Explain the rule only when the character is about to pay it.\n\nCompress transitions without skipping causality. Move from decision to consequence in a few words, but leave the spent resource visible. 'He burned the second ember. The door sagged open, and the cold smelled of machine oil.' Do not use smooth bridging phrases. Let the next action start where the last one ended. Allow a longer thought only when the character is working out a system interaction: which legal loophole binds the binding, which fuel will last through the night. Even then, break the reasoning with a physical check: a finger testing the edge, a boot nudging a coil.\n\nUse rhythm to manage pressure. Short paragraphs for a failed attempt or an incoming strike; a dense block only when the character is trapped and counting what remains. Ground abrasive speech in the speaker: a rough voice should come from thirst, injury, or a real grievance, not from a style default. Keep the world's material limits active. Every convenient magic has a price in weight, blood, time, or equipment, and the prose should show that price changing the shape of the next room, the next fight, the next attempt to sleep.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagabae::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Brandon Sanderson / Epistolary / Documents\n\nBuild the scene from documents that each have a specific author, intended reader, and time. Avoid a rotating sampler of letters, journals, and reports; let the current problem choose the form. A junior clerk's ledger shows what ingredients were bought and what the old alchemist refused to sign for. A field report from a courier records what the ward did when the charm cracked, but not why the courier changed the route. The system's costs and constraints become visible through what each writer counts, omits, or misunderstands.\n\nUse gaps and revisions as evidence, not as noise. A second letter may correct a first one about the number of charges left, and the correction itself changes what the reader can plan. A torn page dated before an experiment may be genuine memory or a later insertion; let a physical trace—a seal pressed too hard, ink that doesn't match the season—support the distinction. Do not add an omniscient paragraph after each document. Let the next document respond to the previous one's implications.\n\nWhen a mechanism matters, make its explanation appear inside the document's own limits. An engineer may describe how a binding draws from the user's sleep and what happens if the user is woken early. A request for supplies may reveal which component the garrison can no longer replace. The intended recipient's reaction can arrive as the next document: a terse order, a refusal, a note left on a counter. Keep the story readable across entries by anchoring each new record to a shared object, place, or unanswered question.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagabaf::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Brandon Sanderson / Fluffy / Comforting\n\nTreat comfort as a practical act inside the fantasy system. A warm meal at a waystation may cost a ration of heat-stones; a repaired charm may leave the mender tired for the next watch. Show what the giver gives up in concrete, gentle terms: the tea is steeped with the last of the good cordial, the spare blanket still smells of the cedar chest, the mended seam uses thread meant for tomorrow's binding. The small pleasure is real, and so is its cost. Do not erase the cost, but let the receiver notice the care behind it.\n\nLet affection be sincere without forcing a declaration. A character can adjust a friend's cloak because they know the clasp digs, can save the best cut of meat for someone who was cold, can sit beside them without explaining. Keep the narration focused on the physical details of that care: the weight of a hand, the exact way a pillow is turned, the shared silence after a long day. Allow disagreement and privacy to remain. Two people can argue about the route and still pass a hot cup without making peace. The world keeps its problems; comfort is what makes the next step bearable, not what makes the problem disappear.\n\nUse the narratorial distance to hold both warmth and limits. Show a character's thought: that this kindness was possible because they had one spare charge, not because magic is free. Let another character refuse comfort without becoming a villain. The style does not require every scene to end in a hug or a laugh. A moment of ease can close with the fire banked and the maps still waiting on the table.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagabag::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Brandon Sanderson / Formal Literary Novel\n\nLet the narration establish the scene through its social and institutional frame. Instead of arriving directly at the charm's charge, describe who administers it: the clerk who must countersign each draw, the quartermaster who keeps two ledgers, the apprentice who knows the real number because she measures the dust each evening. The fantasy mechanism becomes legible through these arrangements. When a character attempts a working, the narrator can explain the relevant rule, then show how that rule has been codified, argued over, or quietly broken by the people who live with it.\n\nUse sustained paragraphs with deliberate transitions. A scene may begin with the corridor's cold stone and the sound of a bell ringing the midnight audit, move through a character's private reckoning of what they can afford, and end at the door of the person who controls the budget. Keep narration distinct from spoken language: not every thought is a neat sentence, and not every exchange deserves a polished epigram. Frame the public and private contrast. A character may perform confidence in front of a commission while privately tracing the pattern of cracks on the reserve stone, and the narrator may hold both versions in view.\n\nWhen policy and personal choice collide, show the cost to both. A decision to spend a scarce resource may be recorded in a margin by one official, denied in a deposition by another, and remembered years later by the person who went without. The narrator may supply the historical context of why that resource is scarce: a treaty, a failed season, a choice made by someone now dead. Do not let the social context replace the mechanism. The rule's cost must remain visible as heat, time, material, or risk, even when the prose is formal and the judgment is social.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagabah::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Brandon Sanderson / Framed Oral Storytelling (Arabian Nights-inspired)\n\nOpen the scene with a stated reason for the telling: someone tries to persuade, warn, justify, or teach an audience that already has a stake in the supernatural claim being narrated. Identify who is listening and what the teller hopes the story will change right now.\n\nThe embedded tale should state one magical principle or precaution in concrete terms—what a charm consumes, what a ritual demands, what a bargain can and cannot do—and show its cost in human choices. Tell that inner story from a definite point of view, with the participants' preparation, discovery, and consequences made visible. Do not let it float as a myth that is true simply because it is old.\n\nThen return to the outer frame. Let a listener test the tale's claim against an observable limit in their own situation: a dwindling resource, a repeated phrase or gesture, an old object that behaves only under certain conditions, or a needed permission that has a price. If someone tries to apply the story, describe what actually happens, including failure, partial success, or a cost the teller did not mention. The telling itself can become part of the magic, but only if the world's existing rules permit that.\n\nAlongside the performed tale, keep live private experience available in the narration—the teller watching for a reaction, a listener's unspoken doubt, a calculation about how much to reveal—so the outer frame is a present encounter, not just a container. Use recurring details—a gesture, a shared name, a kept object, a line that shifts meaning—to show that the outer characters are still inside the same world as the tale, letting repetition accrue meaning across narrative levels. The purpose of the story may be fulfilled in a surprising way, but the magical mechanism must remain intelligible. Treat the Arabian Nights as a craft source only: situated telling and nested structure, never an automatic djinn, palace, desert, or particular religion. The response can end while the telling, or its test, is still in motion; neither the outer situation nor the embedded tale has to reach resolution before the boundary falls.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagabai::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Brandon Sanderson / German-Language Literary Traditions\n\nTake the German-language tradition's Romantic and uncanny route. Use a sustained focal consciousness that observes physical details with concrete attention, but let those details gather wrongness slowly: a shadow that holds its shape too long, a room that seems to remember its former occupants, a person who is too familiar with a ritual. Keep the syntax balanced and the narrator's distance consistent; do not borrow mechanical translation habits or invent a national speech pattern.\n\nThe magical element must be an established premise with a specific mode of action. When it intervenes, show what it consumes—a person's memory, warmth, attention, a kept object, a debt—and what limit that cost imposes. The uncanny atmosphere is not the explanation; it is the sensory consequence of someone nearing or crossing that limit. If a character tries to use the magic, describe the exact preparation, the exact price, and the observable result.\n\nLet the uncanny affect relationships and decision-making, not merely scenery. A character may misunderstand a warning until they see the cost someone else paid. Keep the boundary between the uncanny and the merely strange: the event should remain outside what ordinary material processes can do, but its terms stay stable once shown. Let private thought carry the character's calculations and their values under pressure—what they are willing to spend, and for whom.\n\nThe response can stop inside a conversation, an observation, or an unfinished approach; it does not need to wait for any cost to become fully legible. Whatever the scene has already established stays established, and the next stretch continues from that knowledge without reopening the magic's basic operation as a fresh mystery.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagabaj::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Brandon Sanderson / Gothic-Punk\n\nStage a specific collision between inherited magical authority and present-day urban life. Show the old authority as a working system: a family privilege, a ritual office, a rule attached to a building, a debt that passes through blood or contract. Do not stop at decay; show how contemporary arrangements—rent, permits, surveillance, social services, police, transportation, committee votes—make the old authority expensive, monitored, contested, or newly visible.\n\nWhen magic appears, state the actual operation and its price. It may require a rare material, a condition of the user's body or memory, a permission that can be lost, or a bargain that gives someone else leverage. Describe what the character used, what they gave up, and what visibly changed. A character may know only part of the system; their plan can fail or cost more than they expected, but the narration should show the rule at work.\n\nLet the intimate political atmosphere live in particular spaces and people. Describe worn furniture, locked doors, the smell of a lobby, the way a trusted person's voice changes when they drop a social mask. Let atmosphere change how this particular encounter is experienced rather than standing as scenery. The magic should not replace private desire or human entanglement; it should complicate them. A power that compels loyalty or silence must have a stated trigger and a stated exemption that characters can reason about.\n\nKeep the point of view close to one character's physical and emotional experience, including what they do not say aloud. Show their decisions as a negotiation among the magical cost, their relationships, and the modern institution pressing on them. The scene may stop anywhere in that negotiation—mid-conversation, mid-errand, mid-refusal—without waiting for the cost to become undeniable or the conflict to resolve.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagabak::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Brandon Sanderson / Indian Literary Traditions\n\nUse the oral, epic, and frame-tale route. Build the scene around a magical obligation, prohibition, or inherited contract that several people understand differently. Give the setting the specific language, region, community, and period actually supplied; where the world leaves these open, keep the social texture local to the characters' concrete working lives rather than importing a generic regional backdrop, code-switching, a particular religion, caste dynamics, or a standard family structure.\n\nLet characters retell the obligation in the way they would from their own language and position. Each retelling should expose a different feature of the magical system: what was promised, what was misunderstood, what was paid by someone else, what clause was added to protect a family or a group. The listener should be able to compare accounts and infer the stable underlying rule. The teller's loyalty, fear, or self-interest should be visible in what they omit, but the rule itself must remain coherent.\n\nWhen a character tries to meet or break the obligation, show exactly what they bring, what they say, what they renounce, and what follows. The fantastic element should have a practical cost that affects ordinary life—debt, land, marriage, travel, food, reputation, labor. Wonder may sit beside domestic needs, but it must obey the world's existing conditions.\n\nReturn to the outer frame after a telling. Let a character question a teller about a concrete contradiction: a wrong time, a missing payment, a missing person, a rule that now costs too much. The reply can end anywhere in that exchange—with a new understanding of the obligation's constraints, with an answer still forming, or with the family or community dispute as unsettled as before.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagabal::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Brandon Sanderson / Japanese Literary Traditions\n\nUse the diary and reflective miscellany route. Write in dated or sectioned records kept after short intervals, holding the immediate details of what was seen, asked, paid, or refused. Fit the record to the story's actual teller: where the narration is the diarist's own, write it in that first person; where the active narrator stands outside the diarist, keep the diary as an in-world document the narration can present closely, composed by its author rather than spoken by the narrator. Do not require the record to explain the world; the magic's operation should become clear through accumulated observations.\n\nEach entry may include one concrete magical fact or one failed attempt. When the writer uses or observes a power, record the preparation, the cost, and the visible outcome in plain language. If a rule remains uncertain, let the writer note the specific uncertainty and test it later—not as a dramatic research project, but as a person trying to live with the power. The reflective voice may return to earlier entries and correct a mistaken assumption. Remember that an entry is a composed artifact, not the writer's whole live mind; when the scene calls for present-moment private experience, render it directly rather than only in the record's afterthoughts.\n\nLet the fantasy intrude on ordinary commitments. The narrator may have to choose between honoring a magical cost and meeting a work, family, or seasonal obligation. Describe the objects involved with tactile precision: a folded paper, a preserved leaf, a lamp, a threshold, a held object that feels suddenly heavier. Seasonal attention can appear where the diary naturally notices it, but do not turn every entry into a scenery catalogue.\n\nThe response can end between entries or inside one still being written; no entry has to have reached its clarification, and the narrator's overall situation can remain unresolved. The next section simply continues the record with whatever understanding is already in hand.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagabam::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Brandon Sanderson / Latin American Literary Traditions\n\nUse the marvelous treated as ordinary route. Establish the supernatural fact as a known part of the characters' world: a household spirit, a helpful weather sign, a place that remembers, a bargain passed from neighbor to neighbor. The characters do not react with astonishment; they treat it as one more condition of life. Give the setting the specific language, community, landscape, and history of the supplied world, without inventing a generic regional backdrop.\n\nThe magic must not be soft or decorative. When a character relies on it, show what it costs, what it cannot do, and what happens when the cost is not paid. The cost should be tied to concrete survival—food, water, land, labor, money, health, or a relationship. Let the narration move through a normal day while making that cost visible: an open gate left unused, a child sent with an offering, a debt that changes how a family harvests or travels.\n\nKeep the prose concrete and unsentimental. The marvelous event may be reported in the same plain sentence as a market price or a bus schedule. But whenever the event alters an outcome, the reader should see the mechanism: what was done before, what power intervened, what was consumed, and what followed. If a character does not understand the rule, another person's explanation can supply the missing piece without turning the scene into a lecture. Under the plain sentences, keep private experience actual—what a person counts before answering, what they decide not to tell a neighbor—so restraint is a felt choice rather than an absence.\n\nThe response can stop in the middle of an action, a task, or an unfinished conversation; the ordinary day need not have made any consequence unavoidable before the boundary falls. What the day has already shown remains shown, and the next stretch continues from it.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagaban::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Brandon Sanderson / Light Novel\n\nOpen in the middle of a concrete attempt to use or understand magic. Show the character's physical position, the object or gesture involved, and the expected result in the first few sentences. Keep the spatial arrangement clear so the reader can see what moves, what lights up, what fails.\n\nPresent the magic system through an actual use. Name the resource or condition required—a charge, a focus, a body cost, a permission, a word that must be earned—and show what happens when it is absent or insufficient. If the attempt partially succeeds, describe the exact effect and the exact shortfall. Do not hide the rule behind vague energy language; explain it in one direct sentence as soon as it affects the action.\n\nThen give a focused reaction beat. Let the character respond physically and privately: their hands, their breath, a single sharp thought, a memory that explains why this cost matters. The reaction may be serious, uncertain, angry, or funny, but it should arise from this character and this situation. Use a readable paragraph break between the action and the reaction so the beat lands clearly.\n\nReturn to the immediate problem, even if only a step is taken. Another character may comment; an object may be examined; a route may be changed. The container sets no requirements of first person, comedy, a young cast, or a closing cliffhanger; keep whatever person and tone the story already uses. Stop where the scene's boundary falls—the larger conflict need not resolve, and no reply owes a fresh revelation—so long as the situation stays easy to re-enter.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagabao::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Brandon Sanderson / Lyrical Prose\n\nWrite the fantastic mechanism as a sensory and emotional fact that accumulates meaning across the story. When a charged surface, a spent material, or a worked object returns, carry its earlier cost into the new image: the same gleam that once read as promise now shows strain, and the change is visible in the prose rather than announced. Trace each image to what the scene actually shows, and interrupt the lyric movement with plain declarative units when a precise limit, number, or consequence must be exact.\n\nShape syntax to the character's relation to the act. A long collaborative preparation takes longer syntactic arcs, with subordinate clauses holding the steps in their order of dependence. A dangerous or reluctant act tightens into short decisive units, each clause landing on a separate cost or refusal. Recurring sounds and sentence shapes connect a preparation step with its later effect: when the same vowel pattern returns at the moment of payoff, the reader should recognize the earlier step without being told to remember it.\n\nUnfold a comparison only as far as it changes what the narrator or reader can see about the situation. If the magic is called an old song, show what in its use is like remembering, like harmony, or like a note that cannot be repeated without changing; do not leave the figure decorative. Keep the mechanism available to practical judgment throughout. The magic can be work, the wonder can have a maintenance ledger, and a family meal after a costly spell can be as substantial as the spell itself. Lyric prose here makes the impossible feel inevitable, not vague: the world's fantastic rules operate through image and syntax while remaining legible enough to reason about.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagabap::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Brandon Sanderson / Melancholic / Reflective\n\nLet memory enter when a present object, gesture, or task calls it up. A character preparing to use an established fantastic practice sees the same room, tool, or movement that accompanied an earlier use, and the memory supplies the specific past transaction: what was paid then, what changed, and why the present attempt cannot safely repeat the old terms. Keep the mechanism's rules legible inside the memory. The reader learns what the practice cost and why the cost has grown, not merely that the character feels nostalgic.\n\nCarry small irreversible changes as the story's reflective evidence. A burned patch in the garden from a misfired spell, a child's altered voice after a healing that took too much, or a door that will never close the same way can hold the weight that a direct statement of loss would flatten. Let the narrator notice such a detail in passing and return to it only when it bears on a present choice. Ordinary pleasure remains available: a meal can be genuinely good even while the narrator notices that a particular ingredient is now rare because of the same magical practice. The coexistence of present enjoyment with what is no longer available is the reflective note, not a concluding announcement of impermanence.\n\nMake reflection agency-bearing by showing the reasoning that follows from remembered cost. When a character decides what they will not do again, what they will risk despite knowing the price, or what they no longer expect, the memory supplies the evidence and the present scene supplies the decision. The melancholic tone comes from living inside a system with a long memory, anchored to actual choices and consequences rather than a universal mood.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagabaq::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Brandon Sanderson / Middle Eastern Literary Traditions\n\nTell the present scene through a situated storyteller or participant who moves between the immediate attempt and the remembered domestic and social fabric that makes its cost legible. When a character prepares to use an established fantastic practice, the teller recalls how that practice was managed in a household: who measured the material, who refused a risky shortcut, and what changed after the last time. Let the audience's or teller's present attention shape which detail is recalled; the frame is part of the telling, not a decorative prologue.\n\nMake the mechanism appear in the language of everyday obligation. Explain what the practice requires through ordinary objects, cooking, mending, prayer, work, or care: a stone kept cool on a particular windowsill, an amount of spoken words that must be exact, a debt repaid before the action will take. Keep the costs social and practical. A neighbor's silence, a family rule, a ruined trade agreement, or a promise kept past convenience can be as binding as any magical limit. When a reflective or philosophical aside arises, return it to the specific ground of the choice rather than letting a general maxim replace the transaction.\n\nMark the boundary between the teller's later knowledge and the participants' present ignorance. When the story moves back into the scene, only what a focal character knew then may guide them. A clear explanatory turn—\"The water worked because...\" or \"They learned later that...\"—may follow from the action, but it must not suspend the scene for a lecture. Use recurrence to show how a social consequence becomes part of the system: a repeated phrase marks a debt's continuity, and a recurring object marks a family's changed practice. Phrase such recurrence in the teller's idiom rather than an imposed archaic register; the tale stays close to lived rooms, streets, and work even where the world's fantastic premise is powerful.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagabar::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Brandon Sanderson / Minimalist Prose\n\nSelect only the element of the fantastic system that this action tests. The color a reagent must reach, the distance a door's key may be carried, or the number of words that close a passage appears as a direct concrete detail, and the rule emerges from the action without a separate explanation. Use direct clauses and exact diction. A precise extended sentence carries an essential cause or consequence when fragmenting it would be less clear.\n\nRender emotional and economic texture through inference. A character's hesitation before paying the toll, the wear on a glove after a season of use, or the empty space where a reserve material once stood implies years of practice and loss without an interior monologue. Include only the inward connection that affects present understanding, but let that thought be complete and particular: the narrator may notice that a price is \"higher than last year\" or that a spoken word must not be repeated. These selective notes make the cost legible rather than theatrical.\n\nShow omission as information. If a character has a third option but rejects it, their movement away from the door or the tool carries the rejection; do not announce the option. A scene may end when the causal consequence is established, not when the feeling resolves. Keep the difference between limit (what cannot be done), price (what is spent), and choice (what is chosen to spend) visible through one gesture, one number, or one refusal. The economy of the prose is the economy of the magic, and the world's intelligibility survives in what the spare details let the reader infer.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagabas::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Brandon Sanderson / Modern Literature / Contemporary Novel\n\nWrite sustained scene-first prose from a definite point of view, moving naturally among action, speech, perception, memory, and inward thought. The fantastic operates as part of that ordinary movement: it enters when a door chooses to open, a vial needs warming, or a child's game starts using the household's protective marks. Rules about exhaustion, material, or timing surface through a gesture that fails, a piece of dialogue, or an observation while the character works. Keep access consistent so that another person's mind remains unavailable except through their words and visible behavior.\n\nMake preparation and consequence part of the continuing action. When a character uses an established ability, show enough of their reasoning before, during, or immediately after the attempt that the reader understands why it works or stops. Number, distance, pattern, or accepted risk appears as a practical detail of the same world: \"three drops more would have been polite,\" or \"the door kept the heat of her hand for half a block.\" Let recollection supply relevant history only when the present scene calls for it: an object, a person, or a task provokes a memory that clarifies a current limit. Necessary causal information is never withheld, but it arrives through the scene rather than as a system lecture.\n\nShow choices to spend a resource, break a rule, hold back, or ask for help as determined by values and competing commitments, not just by the system's abstract terms. Render the cost explicitly: physical tiredness after a small act, a debtor's changed tone, or the exact number of days before another attempt. Perception, dialogue, and thought remain continuous and grounded while the system's operations stay legible. A chapter-like pause may fall at a decisive action or a fresh uncertainty rather than at a resolved lesson.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagabat::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Brandon Sanderson / Relationship-Centric Fanfiction (AO3-inspired)\n\nMake the relationship premise legible through scene selection, private language, repeated moments, and consequences that fulfill its particular promise. A private word's repetition, a gift's history, or a ritual of meeting at a specific window after work shows what the characters have built without an explanatory flashback. When the fantastic enters, it interacts with that relationship system rather than merely providing spectacle: a protective mark requires a shared decision, a healing costs the couple's last reserve, and a disagreement about who pays the price reveals what each values. Give affection, jealousy, comfort, or discovery enough space where it belongs, but keep the consequences specific to this relationship rather than applying a trope roster.\n\nUse repeated moments as evidence and as pressure. The same phrase can mean safety in one scene and introduce the possibility of loss in another once the reader knows its history. A repeated gesture—checking a ring's warmth, touching a kept token—tracks the condition of the fantastic mechanism and the relationship together. When a choice is made, show the reasoning in private language and shared history: why one partner will not let the other pay, why a second attempt is allowed after a broken rule, or why comfort is given before accusation. The payoff fulfills the premise's promise in a way only this pair and this world could produce.\n\nKeep the world's rules legible even when emotions are central. If love alters the magic's cost, show the mechanism: affection may steady a movement but not nullify its material need; trust may make a shared working possible but still draw on the same limited source. Move close to the point-of-view partner's inward experience, including errant desires, fears, and practical thoughts about the other person. A scene may end with a promise, a touch, or an unresolved debt, but the reader understands what was spent and what now binds them.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagabau::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Brandon Sanderson / Saga / Oral Epic\n\nBegin scenes with a deliberate chronicle opening that names the deed, the place, or the contested outcome before the particulars are dramatized. The opening announces what happened and hints at the systems of cost and consequence that made the act possible or ruinous: whose reserve was consumed, which working was forbidden, or what debt the act incurred. Compact declarations summarize a character or event in terms of their history with those rules. Then move into concrete action, dialogue, and focal thought that complicates the declamation with lived difficulty.\n\nUse recurrence to make the world's mechanics legible across retelling. A phrase returns when the same kind of cost is paid, and its meaning shifts as the participants learn its limits. A repeated question—who will watch the store of oil, who will stand the overnight measure—tracks shared responsibility for a resource system. Names and epithets arise from established deeds and constraints: a character remembered as \"the one who would not pay the ninth part\" or \"close-hand with the burn\" has a reputation tied to an actual choice about the fantastic practice. When later testimony differs from earlier knowledge, the teller's sources conflict: one says the toll was voluntary, another that it was taken, and the scene shows the original decision in enough causal detail to resolve or complicate those accounts.\n\nKeep the account's authority porous. The chronicle's elevated voice is interrupted by a participant's practical objection or a bystander's correction: the teller says the flame was sung into place, and a watcher says it was bought with three days of a singer's voice. The correction exposes the mechanism rather than mocking the tale. Preserve the selected perspective; a first-person participant cannot know later reputations, while a community teller may know what later audiences said. End where a recurring act remains available for future memory: a bargain kept, a cost counted, or a door newly marked.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagabav::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Brandon Sanderson / Scandinavian Literary Traditions\n\nUse the social-institutional route of Scandinavian realism: the established fantastic premise is a known local fact, administered and discussed through the community's ordinary institutions. Treat the mechanism as something people encounter through procedure, record, and neighborly knowledge rather than through a single dramatic demonstration. When a character attempts to use the premise, show the attempt passing through the social and administrative conditions that govern it: an inspection, a permit, a household rule, a professional obligation, a neighbor's prior claim, or a recorded precedent.\n\nMake the Sandersonian legibility operate through the gap between official understanding and lived practice. A rule may be written in one way and applied in another; a character may know the practical cost of a procedure that an official describes as simple. Let the reader understand the mechanism by watching someone prepare for it, meet or miss a condition, and absorb the consequence in the form of a debt, a wasted material, a new obligation, or a changed relationship. Do not explain the whole system; explain the part that the current attempt touches, and leave the rest as known background that other characters take for granted.\n\nKeep character values inside the social texture of decisions. A person's choice to use or avoid the fantastic element should follow from duties, fears, relationships, or standing in the community, not from a detached interest in the mechanism. Use concrete domestic and institutional details: the arrangement of a workspace, the contents of a bag, the wording of a notice, the order in which a household prepares for an inspection. Let dialogue arise from practical need, with each person speaking from partial knowledge and their own stake in the outcome.\n\nWhen the story supplies a specific Scandinavian context, keep its administrative language and social habits distinct. Danish, Norwegian, and Swedish settings differ in how authority is addressed, how neighbors share knowledge, and how families plan around official requirements. Use the actual climate, community, and institutions supplied; do not import snow, isolation, or a national temperament.\n\nPreserve required interiority as written private prose under the active contract. When focal thought is permitted, show the character checking costs against obligations, or noticing the difference between what a rule says and what it will actually require. Other characters act on what they observe, not on unseen reasoning. A memory or private worry may interrupt the present action briefly when it bears on a decision, but this is not stream of consciousness; recollections stay short and causal.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagabaw::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Brandon Sanderson / Screenplay\n\nWrite a scene-oriented screenplay in which the fantastic premise is exposed through visible preparation, action, and consequence. Use concise action lines that name observable objects, movements, and changes: materials being measured, a container checked, a surface altered after use, a residue or light pattern that follows a specific condition. Do not place the mechanism in a narration block. When a rule matters, show the attempt, the condition being met or missed, and the outcome in actions and dialogue.\n\nLet dialogue carry only what a character would reasonably say to another person in the scene. A character may explain a step while working, warn someone about a cost, or argue about a procedure, but no one delivers a system lecture. The audience should be able to infer cause and effect from what is shown and heard, with the action lines supplying the physical facts that dialogue does not state.\n\nUse scene headings when location or time changes, and keep them simple. Use cuts sparingly, only when the current sequence requires a shift. Offscreen sound may signal an unseen consequence, such as a crack, a chime, or a collapse, without explaining it. If voice-over is used, it must be permitted by the active contract and framed as an audible element; it does not transport private thought to other characters or replace required nonspoken interiority.\n\nPreserve character values inside visible decisions. Show a person choosing between duties by what they pick up first, postpone, or hand to someone else. Let ordinary reactions, including humor, arise from the situation rather than forcing banter.\n\nEnd the scene when the response boundary is reached, not necessarily on a completed task. The final moment may be an unfinished setup, an unanswered question, or a person still working. Do not pause business merely to deliver a dramatic beat.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagabax::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Brandon Sanderson / Stage Play\n\nWrite a playable script in which the fantastic premise is a concrete part of the shared stage action. Use props, blocking, entrances, exits, and pauses to show preparation and consequence. When a character performs a fantastic act, stage directions should specify the observable result—a light, a sound, a physical change—so the audience can see what happened without an explanatory speech. Let the movement of objects and the characters' adjustments make the mechanism intelligible.\n\nTie dialogue to the immediate tasks and relationships on stage. A character may state only what another person has reason to learn, and may react to a result with surprise, relief, anger, or practical concern. Avoid monologues that summarize the system. When a rule is important to an attempt, show the condition being met or missed through staged action: a character reaches for the wrong object, hesitates before a step, or discovers that a prop has changed. Let the dialogue respond to that observable result.\n\nKeep required private experience as nonspoken authoring material, placed as permitted by the active contract. Do not turn a private worry into a monologue unless an aside is dramatically justified and separately authorized. Other characters act on observable behavior, not on hidden reasoning. The audience may know a focal thought that the other characters do not, but the stage action must not pretend they can hear it.\n\nPreserve values through public choices. Show a person deciding between competing commitments by which object they take, whom they let leave, or what they postpone. Keep the scene honest to actual stage constraints: no camera cuts, no film-like montage. Scene changes occur through clear stage direction, not narration.\n\nEnd at the appropriate boundary; a scene may stop with a character still waiting, working, or about to speak. Do not force a completed task or revelation.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagabay::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Brandon Sanderson / Stream of Consciousness\n\nUse the associative movement of a single authorized mind to carry a fantasy story whose mechanisms remain legible through the character's sensory and practical engagement with them. Let perception, memory, private words, bodily sensation, and recurring concern braid together, but let the braid itself expose the premise: the mind returns to the weight of a container, the feel of a surface after use, the exact wording of a condition, the pressure in the room before an attempt, or the remembered cost of a past failure. These recurrences are not random imagery; they are the character's lived experience of the rule.\n\nWhen the fantastic premise is invoked, show the character noticing the actual condition, cost, and result as they occur. The thought may move from a present sensation to a remembered precedent and back, but each association should sharpen the current attempt or its consequence. Distinguish present event from memory or imagined possibility by anchoring the present in concrete sensory detail: what the character sees, holds, hears, or feels now, as opposed to what they recall or fear.\n\nMake preparation and consequence part of the mental flow. The mind may dwell on a resource, a debt, a person's expectation, or the physical cost of a failed attempt. Let real uncertainty, strain, satisfaction, or distraction appear as the character experiences it, without turning private thought into continuous dialogue or making every association witty.\n\nPreserve the viewpoint contract. Only the authorized mind's private experience appears. Other characters remain observable through their actions and words. If the contract permits hidden interiority for another character, use the required wrapper rather than merging minds.\n\nLet paragraph shapes follow attention: short fragments under pressure, longer winding sentences in calm. Do not let the style erase time or event boundaries. End at the response limit, allowing a thought or action to remain unfinished if the scene stops there.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagabaz::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Brandon Sanderson / Text Messages / Chat Transcript\n\nCarry the fantasy story through readable, sender-attributed messages. Let each person's digital habits shape their contribution: one may type quickly with abbreviations, another carefully with full sentences; one may delay, another edit. Use timestamps only when they matter to sequence or tension. Use read receipts, unsent drafts, attachments, or channel changes only when they advance understanding of the situation.\n\nMake the fantastic premise legible through transmitted content and meaningful interface evidence. If a character attempts something, the messages should show what they prepared, what condition they checked, what happened, and what it cost. A delay before a reply may indicate hesitation, a failed attempt, or a person checking a result; an edit may reveal a correction about what actually occurred; a read receipt left unanswered may show that someone has seen a report and chosen not to respond. These interface events carry causal information, but they do not replace the content of the messages themselves.\n\nLet each person's description of a result be filtered by their voice and knowledge. A character may report what they saw, what they think it means, or what they want the recipient to believe. No message is an authoritative system summary. When a detail is important to a later attempt, include it naturally in the earlier exchange so it can later be recovered.\n\nDistinguish a sent message from an unsent draft. An unsent draft is a composed artifact, not an automatic window into live private thought. Include required private interiority through the appropriate wrapper or clearly separated nonspoken note as the contract allows. Do not turn every hesitation into a draft or every silence into an unsent message.\n\nLet relationships and practical work appear through the exchange. People share information, ask for help, coordinate, or express concern in ordinary ways. No one needs to explain the whole system in one message. Keep channel changes and interface events limited to what actually affects the story. End the reply at the current boundary, even if a message remains unread or a person is still typing.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagabba::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Brandon Sanderson / Visual Novel\n\nWrite in short scene-oriented passages with clear dialogue attribution and focal narration. Use concise scene-setting that names the visible environment and any fantastic element in concrete terms: the objects present, the condition of a surface, the arrangement of materials. Background cues orient the moment; they do not serve as mandatory labels or automatic truth indicators.\n\nLet focal narration or inner response explain a mechanism when the character attempts to use it. Keep the explanation tied to the current attempt: the condition, the preparation, the observable result, and the immediate consequence. The reader needs to understand what the character did and why it succeeded or failed, but the explanation should arise from the character's engagement with the task, not from a system lecture.\n\nMaintain a clear rhythm between narrative passages and dialogue. Each exchange should arise from the scene's practical needs or relationships. A character may explain a step while working, ask for a material, or react to a result, but no one delivers a comprehensive account of the premise. No branching menus, player decisions, romance routes, or executable engine commands unless separately authorized.\n\nPreserve interiority as the contract permits. Focal thought belongs only to the authorized viewpoint. Other characters remain visible through their speech, actions, and emotional expressions. A changed expression may be interpreted, but interpretation can be wrong; do not make everyone's reactions transparently informative.\n\nKeep scene changes legible with a simple transition line when the setting changes. End the current scene at the natural response boundary, even if the character is still processing the result or about to speak. Do not force a cliffhanger or completed action at every turn.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagabbb::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Brandon Sanderson / Web Serial\n\nWrite each reply as an installment in a continuous fantasy story. Maintain a readable through-line: show what is currently underway, what changes locally in this segment, and what remains available for the next. Recover earlier details only when they matter to the present, such as the exact resource a character carried, the rule already demonstrated, or a promise made. Do not retell whole previous scenes.\n\nWhen the fantastic premise is used, explain only the aspect that affects the current attempt. Show preparation, condition, cost, and result. Keep the system consistent across installments; a rule established earlier cannot silently change. If new information qualifies an understanding, connect it to what was already known rather than replacing it. The serial's cumulative memory is itself a demonstration of the system's consistency: a cost paid in one installment should still matter in a later one, and a rule demonstrated earlier should still govern a later attempt.\n\nLet progress accumulate through knowledge, relationships, resources, or work. An installment may end with a task unfinished, a request pending, or a new fact learned. Even an unfinished task can advance the reader's understanding of the mechanism if the installment has shown a condition being met, a cost being absorbed, or a consequence becoming visible. Do not manufacture a cliffhanger, twist, or completed miniature chapter at every turn.\n\nKeep character values inside ongoing choices. Show how accumulated costs and obligations influence present decisions. A person may remember a debt, a saved material, or a prior failure when it affects the next step. Let ordinary life continue around the central plot.\n\nIf the serial develops a progression-like system, handle it only as the actual world rules specify; do not add rankings or leveling purely because the style is selected. End each installment at the current response boundary, not at an artificial high point.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagacaa::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Chuck Palahniuk / Angsty / Emotionally Turbulent\n\nChoose one recurring practical procedure the narrator must perform because of the fantasy premise: renewing a protection, preparing a substance, observing a restriction, or maintaining a binding. Make that procedure the story's emotional engine. The narrator returns to it not as background texture but as an argument they are having with someone absent, with themselves, or with the person watching them work.\n\nWrite each repetition in the same hard rhythm, with the same concrete steps named in the same order until a variation appears. The variation is the emotional event: a step skipped, a quantity changed, a gesture performed too quickly, a tool left where someone else will find it. Do not explain the variation. Let the reader recognize it against the established sequence and feel the strain it carries.\n\nLet the procedure expose the narrator's defensive logic through its practical demands. The narrator can insist the work is only maintenance, that the charm must be renewed for the household, that the dose is measured for safety. The private thought may admit what the spoken explanation will not: that the work is also apology, accusation, or a way of staying close to someone without asking to be wanted.\n\nCarry the same pressure into conversation through the hands and the task. A character may answer while continuing to work, pause at a step that requires attention, or repeat an instruction instead of responding to what was said. Let the dialogue register the strain through timing and practical interference rather than through a stock vocabulary of suffering.\n\nAllow comfort to arrive in the same procedural register without resolving the underlying hurt. Someone may take over a step, supply a missing ingredient, or wait through the full sequence without speaking. The warmth is real and partial; the relationship remains unfinished. Keep the fantasy premise causally present throughout, but let its cost be measured in the narrator's repeated attention to one concrete act rather than announced as a theme.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagacab::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Chuck Palahniuk / Chat Roleplay\n\nOrganize each turn around one repeated practical action that belongs to the fantasy world and the current scene: tending a wound, checking a ward, rationing a supply, maintaining a charm. Open the turn with a short concrete sentence that places the reader in the action, then move through narration, dialogue, and reaction in clearly separated paragraphs.\n\nAttribute every spoken line precisely. Keep narration in the active voice and describe the world as ongoing behavior: the ward still hums, the injured companion still breathes unevenly, the fire still eats the last dry wood. Let the fantasy premise appear as a practical condition of the scene rather than an explanatory aside.\n\nUse the repeated action as the turn's spine. Name the same steps in the same order when the action recurs, so the reader can track what has changed. A skipped step, a slower hand, a tool passed to someone else, or a quantity measured differently is the character's emotional signal. Do not announce its meaning; let the next speaker or action respond to it.\n\nLet characters expose themselves through what they do while they talk. A player or NPC can fumble a ritual, ration a scarce item, or mend a strap while saying what is hard to say. The dialogue should stay in character and carry enough practical information to move the shared scene forward. Private thought, where the active contract permits it, may admit what the spoken line will not.\n\nEnd the turn at a natural continuation point: the action has advanced, the speakers have responded, and the next contribution can pick up from the established behavior. Do not force a question, a cliffhanger, or a choice screen. The recurring action gives the scene continuity; the next writer's contribution will show what the characters do with what has just happened.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagacac::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Chuck Palahniuk / Chinese Literary Traditions\n\nUse the classical or vernacular episodic telling route. Organize the fantasy story as a sequence of short, concrete episodes: a visit, a conflict, a repair, a journey. Each episode opens in a specific place and time, follows one practical encounter to its outcome, and closes when the situation has changed in knowledge or relationship. Do not append a summary moral.\n\nChoose one repeated ritual or material procedure that recurs across episodes: preparing medicine, feeding an animal, mending a gate, counting coins, redrawing a protective mark. Write the procedure in the same order and with the same concrete nouns each time it appears. The fantasy premise creates the procedure's practical cost: the medicine must not be heated too quickly, the spirit appears only at a certain market hour, the mark must be redrawn after rain.\n\nMake the variation in the repeated procedure the episode's narrative event. In one episode the narrator performs the procedure fully; in the next a step is interrupted, a quantity is short, or someone else performs a step for the first time. The reader recognizes the change against the established sequence and understands that something has altered or been lost. Do not explain the variation; let the next episode show its consequence.\n\nKeep the language clear, direct, and tied to the community and period present in the user's material. Do not import a generic premodern setting or an omniscient bridge between episodes. Move from one episode to the next through a concrete change in the familiar repeated detail or a direct consequence of the previous encounter.\n\nKeep the account's selected narrator consistent. Let private thought belong to that narrator alone, and let it admit what the episode's practical action will not say. The episodic form gives the story forward motion through successive concrete encounters, not through cliffhangers or announced themes.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagacad::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Chuck Palahniuk / Compressed Gritty Prose\n\nWrite the fantasy premise through material effects, not explanation. If a charm must be renewed, show the chalk under the narrator's nail and the cracking plaster. If a monster risks the livestock, let the reader hear the gate hinge and smell the blood before any speech. Keep transitions abrupt when the world changes quickly, but allow a longer clause when a character must work out a reason or weigh a risk.\n\nChoose one countable task the narrator returns to: filling oil lamps, checking three locks, binding a wound, counting the same stores. Write the task in the same compressed order each time it appears, with the same concrete nouns. The repetition is the pressure-bearing spine of the scene. A skipped step, a miscount, a slower hand, or a tool left behind is the emotional signal. Do not state the fear, guilt, or determination; let the variation in the task carry it.\n\nChoose objects that accumulate meaning through wear: a notched staff, patched boots, a borrowed blanket. Return to the same object across scenes so its condition changes with the story's pressure. The object's wear is the record of what the character has survived or failed to prevent.\n\nGround rough or abrasive speech in its speaker's actual circumstances. A frightened herder swears differently from a tired soldier or an angry child. Let dialogue carry practical information and character rather than sounding uniformly hard. Keep the fantasy rules consistent and their costs physical so that each choice has visible stakes.\n\nThe style heightens pressure; it does not turn every scene into survival horror or require a cruel outcome. The compressed rhythm and the repeated task give the reader the information the narrator will not say directly.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagacae::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Chuck Palahniuk / Epistolary / Documents\n\nTell the fantasy story through a sequence of documents: letters, journal pages, field notes, supply lists, notices, or reports. Each document needs a defined author, a reader or purpose, and a moment of composition. Let the document reveal only what that author would know or admit at that time. A missing page, a scratched-out line, or a sealed message can tell its own story.\n\nChoose one repeated documentary form as the story's spine: a supply list, a dosage record, a maintenance schedule, a tally, or a log. Write it in the same order and with the same concrete nouns each time it appears. The repetition is the author's ritual on the page. Across entries, the variation is the emotional event: an item skipped, a quantity changed, a line crossed out, a phrase repeated until it bends. Do not explain the variation; let the reader recognize it against the established form.\n\nLet the repeated document expose what its author refuses to write. A confident record can omit the one item that matters, add an unnecessary detail to fill the space where a confession would go, or repeat a phrase until it becomes a private argument. The document's practical purpose is real, but its selection and omission carry the author's defensive logic.\n\nKeep the fantasy premise operational through the documents without an appended narrative explanation. A charm's failure may appear in a repair log; a spirit's behavior may appear in a guard's report; a forbidden location may be traced in a map's annotations. Preserve readable continuity across entries by marking time, place, and who wrote each document.\n\nDo not force a fixed rotation through journal, letter, and report. Choose the document form the situation actually requires, and let the repeated documentary form carry the story's continuity across the gaps between entries.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagacaf::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Chuck Palahniuk / Fluffy / Comforting\n\nChoose one repeated care ritual that belongs to the fantasy world and the characters' shared life: a warm cup passed across a cold table, a blanket aired by the fire, a charm checked before sleep, a recipe repeated because it was loved. Write the ritual in the same concrete order and with the same nouns each time it appears. The repetition is the story's comfort spine.\n\nLet the variation in the ritual carry the affection and the manageable friction. One day the cup is filled before the other person asks; another day it is set down a little harder, or left to cool while someone works. The reader recognizes the change against the established routine and understands the feeling without an explanation. Do not turn the variation into a moral lesson or a demand for a confession.\n\nLet the fantasy element participate in everyday comfort without losing its reality. A familiar spirit could be called with the same three taps each morning; a plant that glows could be watered before dawn; a protective mark could be redrawn as casually as tying a boot. The impossible premise belongs to the world and shapes small pleasures as much as large dangers.\n\nKeep personalities distinct. Affection can be expressed by practical help or shared routine without a confession. Privacy and inconvenience may coexist with warmth: someone may appreciate a meal without wanting to talk about it, and a disagreement may continue after the shared task is done. The comfort is sincere and specific, not falsely soothing.\n\nEstablish the rules of the world's magic consistently, but let those rules include gentle, usable everyday effects alongside any larger fantastic stakes. The repeated care ritual gives the reader the pleasure of recognition while the characters remain particular people with their own preferences and limits.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagacag::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Chuck Palahniuk / Formal Literary Novel\n\nWrite in sustained, deliberate paragraphs that move from scene to reflection without abandoning concrete detail. Frame the fantasy premise through social context or history where the narrator can: a family's relation to a magical obligation, a village's old bargain with a neighbor spirit, or an institution's letter about a newly discovered power. Let the reader see how the impossible fact shapes daily arrangements, not merely how it supplies a spectacle.\n\nChoose one repeated practical procedure the narrator performs because of the fantasy premise: folding the same worn paper, counting the days from one ritual to the next, cleaning and binding a wound in an exact order. Write the procedure in the same concrete order and with the same nouns each time it appears. The repetition is the story's structural spine, carrying Palahniuk's self-exposure through the formal novel's sustained movement.\n\nMake the contrast between public and private performance of the procedure the central formal device. In a public scene, the narrator performs the procedure correctly, with the polished language of ceremony or obligation. In a private scene, the same procedure appears with a variation: a step performed too quickly, a quantity changed, a gesture repeated until it becomes a private argument. The reader recognizes the variation against the established public form and understands what the narrator will not say.\n\nLet the formal narration frame the procedure through social context, history, or judgment where the narrator permits. A ceremony's public meaning can be described in composed prose, while the private variation appears in the narrator's blunt journal or unspoken thought. Connect these contrasts through deliberate transitions rather than merely listing them.\n\nKeep the point of view consistent, and let private thought belong only to the chosen focal character. Keep the diction clearly literary rather than colloquial, but maintain the hard rhythms of practical process. The style organizes and frames; the fantasy world remains particular and consequential rather than an allegory or an aesthetic backdrop.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagacah::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Chuck Palahniuk / Framed Oral Storytelling (Arabian Nights-inspired)\n\nWrite the scene as an occasion where one character tells another an embedded story in order to change the listener's next decision. Open with the outer scene's practical pressure: what the listener needs to do, what the teller wants them to do instead, and what each person already believes about the fantasy premise operating in their world. Let the telling begin as a response to that pressure, not as a detached performance.\n\nCompose the embedded tale as a procedural account. The teller narrates the repeated steps, rules, or maintenance tasks by which the impossible thing actually works in daily life, moving through the operation in the order a practitioner would follow it. Use hard rhythms and obsessive particulars: return to a fixed phrase, count, gesture, tool, or bodily detail each time the tale touches the fantastic operation. Place the repeated element at a different point in the procedure each time it returns, so the listener can hear a rule changing or a cost becoming visible rather than the same beat restated.\n\nKeep the teller's self-exposure inside the practical process. Instead of announcing fear, complicity, failure, or desire, have the teller reveal it through the details they linger on, the steps they skip, the costs they measure, or the defensive rhythm with which they recite a rule. When the outer listener notices an omission or emphasis, let that noticing happen in the listener's own response, not in a narrative explanation of what the tale means.\n\nLet the embedded story act on the outer situation. It may teach the listener how to use the fantastic premise safely, warn them that a familiar rule has a hidden consequence, persuade them to delay an action, or reveal why an institution depends on the impossible thing. After the telling, return to the outer scene and show the listener applying, resisting, or questioning what they heard through a concrete move: a changed question, a refusal to act, a new check, or a decision made against the tale's pressure. The tale's effect remains contingent on the characters; it need not succeed.\n\nKeep the narrative levels intelligible. Enter the embedded tale through the teller's voice and the occasion that calls it forth, and return to the outer scene through a detail the listener can carry back from the tale. The repeated phrase or object may appear in both levels, but it does not certify that the tale is true. Preserve the difference between what the teller knew at the time of the events and what the teller knows now. Do not import djinn, palaces, deserts, or a particular religion unless the supplied world already contains them.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagacai::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Chuck Palahniuk / German-Language Literary Traditions\n\nUse the documentary and historical-memory route. Write the scene as a narrator comparing records, testimony, or remembered conditions with the practical operation of the fantasy premise. Move between what an institution records and what a person actually does, and let the gap between those two accounts carry the story's pressure.\n\nGive the prose a measured, socially situated distance. When the narrator reports official language, dates, procedures, or material conditions, keep the sentences precise and slightly detached, as if quoting the record's own order. When the narrator shifts into a closer account of a particular person's body, routine, or private reasoning, shorten the distance by moving from the general procedure to the specific gesture, sensation, or unrecorded decision. Let the syntax follow the narrator's position and the record being examined, not a translated word order or bureaucratic severity.\n\nUse Palahniuk-style repeated rules and obsessive particulars through the documentary material. Choose a form, inspection, maintenance schedule, ration, measurement, or recurring bodily detail and return to it with small variations each time the fantasy premise is administered. Each return should show the impossible thing becoming part of an institution's daily work, and each variation should show how that work changes the people who perform it: a tolerance tightening, a signature changing, a body failing a check it once passed.\n\nReveal the narrator's position through their handling of records and memory. Have the narrator quote, verify, or leave unexamined particular documents in a way that exposes complicity, fear, or a defensive attachment to procedure. The narrator need not confess directly; the selection and rhythm of the documentary account should let the reader hear the position from which they speak.\n\nMake the fantasy premise affect institutions and everyday life concretely. Show who must register, feed, measure, transport, or explain the impossible thing, and what happens when a record contradicts a memory. Preserve the difference between an official account and a lived one without making every record false or every memory true. The scene may end with the gap still open, a correction made, or a person acting on what the records cannot show.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagacaj::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Chuck Palahniuk / Gothic-Punk\n\nWrite the scene around an inherited arrangement that gives the fantasy premise its present form: an old authority, family obligation, building, ritual, or social rule that still controls who can use or approach the impossible thing. Establish the arrangement as a practical system with costs, not as a vague atmosphere. Show the specific steps someone must follow to maintain, access, or avoid the fantastic operation, and show who pays for each step.\n\nUse Palahniuk-style procedural recurrence to make the arrangement feel lived. Return to a repeated rule, phrase, measurement, gesture, or bodily detail each time the character moves through the inherited structure. Each return should reveal the arrangement's hold on the character: what they obey automatically, what they resent, what they cannot stop checking. Vary the context of the return so the reader can compare the character's compliance across different pressures.\n\nWrite private desire and social masks in the same scene. When the character performs the required courtesy or deference, follow the performance with their actual private reasoning running against it: the specific want, fear, or calculation the mask conceals. Write that interiority as direct nonspoken experience, not a summary of it. The mask may fall away in trusted company, but it need not fall; when it stays in place, show the cost of keeping it there through a bodily detail, a hesitation, or a private correction the character makes.\n\nGive the urban or domestic setting concrete material detail. Anchor the fantasy premise in worn surfaces, locked rooms, shared spaces, maintenance work, or the body's reactions to the place. Let atmosphere arise from those actual conditions: a room's temperature, a door's resistance, a surface's wear, a routine's residue. Atmosphere changes how a particular encounter is experienced, but it must not replace the encounter's practical facts.\n\nKeep morally complicated intimacy present without making every relationship corrupt. Characters may protect each other, exploit each other, or do both at once. Stage the collision between old authority and present life as a specific decision: whether to continue a practice, reveal a cost, or let someone else into the arrangement. Show the decision through what the character does or refuses to do, not through a thesis about the arrangement. Do not import Kindred physiology, Humanity numbers, factions, powers, or blood-bond rules. The fantasy premise remains whatever the supplied world establishes.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagacak::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Chuck Palahniuk / Indian Literary Traditions\n\nUse the testimonial and autobiographical route. Write the scene as a first-person account of how the fantasy premise entered the narrator's practical life. Have the narrator speak from a specific position within the supplied community, region, and language situation, and carry the texture of that position through the work they do, the obligations they carry, and what they remember being told about the impossible thing.\n\nKeep the language and community specific when the supplied world provides them. If the narrator's language situation matters, show it through the actual words, names, or relationships the world supplies; otherwise let the narration remain plain and direct. Do not force English, code-switching, religion, caste experience, or a generic family structure onto the scene.\n\nUse Palahniuk-style procedural recurrence to structure the testimony. Have the narrator return to a repeated rule, phrase, bodily detail, or practical step each time the fantasy premise affects their life. Place each return in a different situation so the reader can compare how the impossible thing became routine, how the narrator learned to manage it, and what the routine cost. Let the repetition carry the narrator's obsession without the narrator explaining it.\n\nReveal self-exposure through the practical process rather than through abstract confession. When the narrator describes what they did, checked, avoided, or told themselves, let the specific detail expose fear, shame, complicity, or desire. The reader should hear the narrator's position in the selection and rhythm of the testimony: what the narrator repeats, what they skip, and where their account tightens or breaks.\n\nMake the fantasy premise affect institutions and everyday life concretely. Show how the narrator's family, workplace, neighborhood, or local authority responds to the impossible thing, and what the narrator must do to live with it. The account may end with the narrator still inside the situation, a decision made, or a change in how they understand what happened. Do not require the testimony to resolve into comfort or despair.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagacal::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Chuck Palahniuk / Japanese Literary Traditions\n\nUse the diary and reflective miscellany route. Write the scene as dated entries in which the narrator records the fantasy premise's effect on daily life. Compose each entry around a concrete particular: a task, a bodily sensation, an object, a conversation, or a change in the weather or season. Treat the diary as a composed artifact, not a transcript of the narrator's live private thought; the narrator chooses what to record and how to frame it.\n\nLet seasonal attention and omission do real work. Use a seasonal detail to mark the passage of time, a change in the narrator's condition, or the recurrence of the fantasy premise. Compose omission deliberately: when the narrator does not record something the reader has reason to expect, let the absence reveal avoidance, fear, or a change the narrator cannot yet name. Do not impose a mandatory inventory of Japanese scenery or a universal quietness; use the actual setting and the narrator's actual temperament.\n\nUse Palahniuk-style procedural recurrence across the entries. Have the narrator return to a repeated rule, phrase, measurement, gesture, or bodily detail each time the fantasy premise appears. Vary the context of each return so the reader can compare entries and see a routine becoming harder, a rule being questioned, or a cost becoming visible. Let the hard rhythm of the repeated detail carry the narrator's obsession without the narrator explaining it.\n\nReveal self-exposure through the diary's practical record. When the narrator measures, checks, avoids, or repeats something, let the specific detail expose fear, desire, complicity, or defensive logic. The entry need not end with a confession or a completed realization. A private interval can be justified by the narrator's attention, waiting, or uncertainty rather than by an external task or practical decision.\n\nMake the fantasy premise affect institutions and everyday life concretely. Show how the impossible thing changes the narrator's work, household, neighborhood, or body, and what the narrator must do to live with it. The entries may end with the situation still open, a small change, or a shift in the narrator's understanding. Do not require the diary to produce a cliffhanger or a completed arc.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagacam::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Chuck Palahniuk / Latin American Literary Traditions\n\nUse the social realism and chronicle route. Write the scene as a chronicle of how the fantasy premise operates inside a specific community's labor, survival, and social pressures. Have the narrator observe and report concrete conditions: who does the work the impossible thing requires, who profits from it, who is excluded, and what the practice costs in time, money, health, or relationships.\n\nKeep the relevant language, community, and history specific when the supplied world provides them. Do not manufacture national speech patterns, landscape inventories, religion, honor systems, or a generic regional temperament. The chronicle should sound like a person situated in the actual community, not a travelogue or a sociological summary.\n\nUse Palahniuk-style procedural recurrence to make the social arrangement legible. Have the narrator return to a repeated rule, phrase, measurement, gesture, or bodily detail each time the fantasy premise affects the community. Place each return in a different site of the community's work so the reader can compare how the impossible thing becomes part of daily labor and how that labor disciplines the people who perform it. Let the hard rhythm carry the narrator's obsessive attention to the social mechanism.\n\nReveal the narrator's position through the chronicle's selection and emphasis. When the narrator chooses to observe, repeat, or leave out a particular detail, let that choice expose their own position, complicity, fear, or desire. Do not have the narrator announce a political thesis; instead, let the concrete details of who does what, and what it costs, carry the pressure.\n\nMake the fantasy premise affect institutions and everyday life concretely. Show how the impossible thing changes a workplace, household, market, or local authority, and what people must do to live with it. The chronicle may end with the situation still open, a small change, or a person acting on what the narrator has shown. Do not add supernatural facts to a world that excludes them. The fantasy premise remains whatever the supplied world establishes.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagacan::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Chuck Palahniuk / Light Novel\n\nEnter the immediate situation cleanly. Open the scene with the character already inside a concrete moment involving the fantasy premise: a task, a meeting, a failure, a check, or a decision. Establish where the character is, what they are doing, and what the impossible thing requires in the first few lines. Do not begin with a general explanation of the world.\n\nKeep spatial changes easy to follow. When the character moves, the reader should know where they are and what changed. Use short, readable paragraphs around action, dialogue, and private response. Give unfamiliar names or mechanics clear presentation when they first appear, but do not pause the scene for a glossary.\n\nUse Palahniuk-style procedural recurrence as the scene's spine. Have the character return to a repeated rule, phrase, measurement, gesture, or bodily detail each time the fantasy premise operates. Make the repetition concrete and practical: what the character checks, counts, recites, or avoids. Vary the context of each return so the reader can compare the character's compliance across different pressures, and let the repetition reveal the character's obsession and the premise's cost without the narrator explaining either one.\n\nWrite focused reaction beats as actual private experience. After an action or line of dialogue, show the character's specific response: a calculation, a fear, a flash of desire, a physical sensation, a decision. The reaction can be serious, uncertain, angry, or funny according to its owner. Do not make every character witty or quippy, and do not require a reaction to immediately change a visible action.\n\nReveal self-exposure through the practical process. When the character repeats, avoids, or checks something, let the specific detail expose complicity, fear, or defensive logic. The scene can end during continuing speech, action, thought, composition, or an unfinished setup. Do not require a cliffhanger, a completed task, or a changed plan before ending. The fantasy premise remains whatever the supplied world establishes.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagacao::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Chuck Palahniuk / Lyrical Prose\n\nEstablish the fantastic element as a practical condition with a recurring sensory signature. Each time the impossible thing is present, return to one or two exact images—a sound, a texture, a color, a temperature—and let the return accumulate meaning rather than merely restating the same observation. The narrator may keep this recurrence deliberately, like a rule for handling the thing: a phrase repeated before touching it, a count, a sequence of small actions. Show the rule being maintained even when it fails to help.\n\nLet syntax carry the narrator's management of the impossible. Use parallel clauses to list what must be checked, what must not be said, what the body does while the mind waits. When the narrator's control slips, let the sentence loosen or run longer; when control is restored, let it tighten again. Do not make every sentence rhythmic. Plain declarative statements should interrupt the pattern, especially for practical information or another character's ordinary speech.\n\nDevelop comparisons from the scene's actual materials. If the narrator compares the fantastic thing to something familiar, the comparison should change what the narrator understands or what the reader can infer about the cost of living with it. Do not import imagery from outside the world. The lyricism belongs to the narrator's attention, not to every speaker. Other characters may answer an elevated image with a flat practical correction, and the contrast is part of the method.\n\nExpose the narrator through the practical process of maintaining the recurrence. The repeated phrase or ritual is not merely a habit; it reveals what the narrator fears, wants, or refuses to admit. Let the reader infer the defensive logic without the narrator announcing it. The account can end while the rule is still being kept, the impossible thing still present, and the next repetition already anticipated.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagacap::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Chuck Palahniuk / Melancholic / Reflective\n\nLet the narrator maintain a repeated rule or procedure that the fantastic element has made necessary, and let each repetition carry a memory of the time before the rule existed. The rule can be practical—a check, a phrase, a sequence of small actions—but its performance should brush against what is no longer available: a person who once did this differently, a room that used to be ordinary, a body that could once ignore the impossible thing. Do not announce the loss. Let the remembered detail sit beside the present action.\n\nUse concrete particulars as the material of reflection. A specific object, its weight or smell or worn edge, can carry the association more reliably than an abstract statement about time. The narrator may reflect directly, but the reflection should arise from the particular: the thing that must now be counted, the door that must now be checked, the food that no longer tastes the same. Ordinary pleasure can remain pleasurable; the method does not require every detail to be sad.\n\nLet the narrator's defensive logic show through the retrospective structure. The repeated rule is a way of managing the impossible, but it is also a way of not saying what the narrator misses or fears. The account can reveal this indirectly: a pause in the procedure, a step performed out of order, a phrase the narrator no longer says aloud. Do not force a confession. The melancholy is in the gap between the maintained routine and the remembered occasion, not in a declaration that everything is ending.\n\nKeep the fantasy element's consequences intelligible. The impossible thing has changed how the narrator lives, and the reflection should make that change specific. A remembered occasion can show what the premise cost without turning the entire account into a lament. The response may end in the middle of the routine, with the present and the remembered still held together in the same sentence.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagacaq::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Chuck Palahniuk / Middle Eastern Literary Traditions\n\nUse the picaresque and absurdist strand of the regional profile. The narrator is a situated first-person teller recounting how a fantastic condition has been survived through a set of self-imposed rules. The account is addressed to a listener—a neighbor, a relative, a stranger in the same room—whose presence shapes what the narrator emphasizes, repeats, or omits. The listener may interrupt with a practical question, a correction, or a competing version of an incident, and the narrator's response exposes the defensive logic without announcing it.\n\nLet the absurdist construction arise from the fantastic premise's concrete consequences. The impossible thing creates practical problems—bureaucratic, domestic, bodily, economic—and the narrator's rules are attempts to manage them. The humor is not mockery of the world's culture or a catalogue of taboo material. It comes from the mismatch between the elaborate procedure and the ordinary need it serves: counting, checking, bargaining, avoiding. Keep the social and domestic setting specific. The narrator lives somewhere particular, with neighbors, markets, family obligations, and the ordinary friction of daily life alongside the impossible.\n\nUse rhythmic recurrence as the procedural backbone. The narrator repeats a rule, a phrase, or a sequence of actions each time the fantastic condition intrudes, and the repetition accumulates meaning as the account proceeds. A later occurrence may give the repeated phrase a new application. Do not let the recurrence become ornamental. It should be the practical method by which the narrator survives, and its failure or partial success should be legible.\n\nPreserve the framed telling without turning every reply into a nested tale. The listener's interruptions and the narrator's digressions can open a remembered incident, but the account should return to the present scene. The response may end with the narrator still explaining, the listener still unconvinced, and the next rule already being prepared.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagacar::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Chuck Palahniuk / Minimalist Prose\n\nState the narrator's rule once, in a direct clause. The rule is the practical method for living with the fantastic condition: what to check, what to say, what not to touch, what to count. Do not explain why the rule exists. The next sentence performs it. The consequence of the impossible thing appears in one exact detail—a change in the room, a physical sensation, an object out of place—and the narrator's response is the rule again, slightly altered or exactly the same.\n\nUse a small number of concrete particulars. Choose the detail that carries the most inferable information: the worn spot on the threshold, the weight of the key, the taste that should not be there. Do not add a second detail when the first already makes the consequence legible. Direct clauses keep the rhythm hard. Short sentences can accumulate, but each one must move the account forward or deepen what the reader can infer about the narrator's situation.\n\nLet omission expose the narrator. The rule is a way of not saying what the narrator fears or wants. The reader infers it from what the narrator checks, avoids, or repeats. Do not announce the defensive logic. A precise extended sentence may serve better than several performed fragments when the causal connection needs room; otherwise, cut the explanation and let the action carry it.\n\nKeep the fantasy premise intelligible without explaining it. The impossible thing has a specific effect, and the rule addresses that effect. The reader can infer the premise from the rule and the consequence. The response may end after a single completed cycle of rule, performance, and consequence, with the next cycle already implied by the narrator's unchanged position.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagacas::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Chuck Palahniuk / Modern Literature / Contemporary Novel\n\nWrite sustained scene-first prose in which the fantastic condition is part of the ordinary world. The impossible thing affects institutions, domestic routines, work, or relationships, and its consequences appear in the practical texture of the scene: a form that must be filed, a room that must be prepared, a schedule that must be kept, a person who must be told or not told. Do not pause the scene to explain the premise. Let the narrator's actions and the other characters' responses make it legible.\n\nEmbed the narrator's repeated rule in the scene rather than performing it as a rhetorical refrain. The rule is a practical procedure the narrator maintains: a check, a sequence, a phrase said at a particular moment. Return to it when the scene's action makes the return natural—before entering a room, after a conversation, at the end of a task. The recurrence should accumulate meaning across scenes, not appear as a tic in every paragraph. When the rule fails or is interrupted, let the consequence be specific.\n\nMove naturally among action, dialogue, perception, recollection, and authorized inner experience. The narrator may notice a detail, remember an earlier occasion, answer a question, and return to the present action without a transition device. Use paragraphing to orient the reader. The narrator's defensive logic should emerge from this movement: what the narrator chooses to remember, what the narrator checks, what the narrator does not say aloud. Do not announce the self-exposure.\n\nLet dialogue interrupt the narrator's procedure. Other characters may not share the rule, may find it strange, or may have their own ways of managing the fantastic condition. Their speech should be individual and ordinary, not a chorus of witty remarks. The scene may end with the business of the moment concluded or still in progress, the rule kept or broken, and the next scene's return already implied by the narrator's unchanged circumstances.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagacat::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Chuck Palahniuk / Relationship-Centric Fanfiction (AO3-inspired)\n\nUse the actual supplied relationship premise as the organizing center. The scene selection should make the premise legible: choose moments where the characters' bond is tested, maintained, or changed by the fantastic condition. Do not insert a roster of familiar tropes. If the premise is rivalry, show the rivalry through the specific competition the impossible thing creates. If it is comfort, show the comfort through the specific danger or cost the impossible thing imposes. If it is discovery, show what one character learns about the other through how they manage the condition.\n\nBuild the private language from shared rules. The characters have developed procedures for the fantastic condition—a phrase one says before the other enters, a count they perform together, a check they divide between them. Repeat these rules at moments of emotional consequence. A callback occurs when a later occurrence gives the shared phrase or action a new application: the rule that once meant safety now means distrust, or the phrase that once signaled routine now signals that something has changed. Do not make the repetition ornamental. It is the practical method by which the relationship survives the impossible thing.\n\nExpose each character through their practical process. One character's rule may be meticulous; the other's may be careless or differently ordered. The friction between their methods reveals what each will not say: fear, jealousy, dependence, resentment, love. Let the reader infer the defensive logic from the actions and the shared language. Do not announce it. Affection, jealousy, comfort, rivalry, or discovery should be given enough space where they belong, but the emotional payoff must arise from the specific consequence of the shared rule, not from a generic declaration.\n\nKeep the fantasy premise's consequences intimate and specific. The impossible thing affects the relationship's daily texture: who sleeps, who waits, who checks the door, who remembers the phrase. The response may end in the middle of the shared routine, with one character's rule kept and the other's already failing, leaving the relationship's next moment contingent on what the impossible thing does next.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagacau::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Chuck Palahniuk / Saga / Oral Epic\n\nGive the account the sense of being retold. Open with a deliberate declaration that names the person, the place, or the rule to be remembered. Use compact declarations to establish a deed, then expand into the particulars that make the reputation incomplete. The deeds remembered are the practical procedures by which people have lived with the fantastic condition: who first learned to check the door, who taught the phrase to the children, who broke the rule and what followed, who still keeps it differently. An act earns its place in the chronicle because the community has lived with its consequences, not because it is mythic in scale.\n\nUse recurring phrasing as the chronicle's backbone. A repeated designation should come from an established deed or way of speaking, not from an ornamental epithet assigned to every entrance. Return to a phrase when a later occurrence gives it a new application. The recurrence should mark what the community has learned: the rule that once belonged to one household and is now said by everyone, the warning that changed meaning after a particular failure. Keep shared reputation distinct from a focal character's private account. Being remembered as the one who kept the rule does not mean that keeping it was effortless, cheerful, or automatic.\n\nLet the narrator's procedural confession show through the public telling. The teller may reveal a defensive logic without admitting it: emphasizing one detail, omitting another, repeating a rule that the teller personally failed to keep. The chronicle's authority can be interrupted by dialogue—the subject of an oft-told incident may object to one detail, or answer an elevated introduction with an ordinary practical concern. This need not become parody. Humor may arise from the persistence of a local story, but household work is not mock combat and familiar inconvenience is not heroic doom.\n\nKeep the fantasy premise's consequences specific and social. The impossible thing has changed how the community works, travels, builds, or mourns, and the chronicle makes that change legible through particular rules and their outcomes. The account may end with a modest act still available to be remembered—a rule kept, a lesson taught, a door checked—rather than declaring it legend on the community's behalf.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagacav::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Chuck Palahniuk / Scandinavian Literary Traditions\n\nWrite the fantasy through a folktale-inflected domestic account. The impossible condition is already part of the household's practical life, and the narration treats it as a set of recurring obligations: what must be done before dark, which door is never opened, what the youngest person must repeat after each meal, what the family no longer asks about. Let the fantastic element have intelligible consequences in work, meals, inheritance, travel, or care without turning every mystery into an explained system.\n\nUse the Palahniuk profile's procedural rhythm to make the rules visible through repeated action and concrete particulars. The narrator can list the steps of a household task, the order of objects on a shelf, the exact wording of a warning, or the sequence of checks performed at a threshold. Repeat a phrase or fragment when the routine repeats, but let each recurrence accumulate a slightly different weight because the circumstances around it have changed. The narrator's confidence is a defensive performance: the account stays practical, precise, and instructional even when the underlying fear or grief is plain to the reader. Do not have the narrator announce that fear as a thesis.\n\nKeep the telling oriented to the particular people and place. Domestic realism means the family still argues about money, food, sleep, and who did the work. The folktale element does not require snow, isolation, or bleakness; it can sit beside a functioning village, a summer kitchen, a ferry schedule, or a modern apartment. If a named Scandinavian context is used, keep its language and customs distinct rather than blending every Nordic tradition into one atmosphere.\n\nLet the repeated rule produce consequences without forcing the same outcome every time. A broken routine may bring a visible change, a delayed consequence, or nothing immediate at all; the characters can disagree about whether the rule matters. The narration may reveal a character's private reasoning or sensation, but another character does not know it merely because the reader does. End the response when the current routine, conversation, or discovery reaches a natural stopping point; do not require a completed crisis or a confession.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagacaw::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Chuck Palahniuk / Screenplay\n\nWrite the fantasy as a screen-oriented sequence of concise action lines, attributed dialogue, and scene headings when location or time changes. Make the impossible condition visible through what the cast does with it: a worker measures a doorway that should not be there, a child repeats a phrase before touching food, a clerk files the same form every morning because the office rule depends on it. Let the fantastic element affect ordinary decisions and practical work rather than appearing only as spectacle.\n\nUse the Palahniuk profile's procedural repetition through screen-visible routines. Action lines can list the exact steps of a task, the order of objects handled, or the repeated checks a character performs. Dialogue can return to the same fragment or instruction when the routine returns, but each recurrence should register a change in the scene's conditions. Keep the social detail concrete: what people eat, wear, pay for, clean, or avoid. The characters' self-exposure comes through what they do and say under pressure, not through a narrator's confessional prose.\n\nUse cuts, offscreen sound, or voice-over only when the chosen sequence needs them. If a private thought or nonspoken feeling is required, present it as a clearly attributed nonspoken note in the active presentation; it is not dialogue and no other character hears it. Do not fill the page with camera commands or turn every expression into a truth indicator. A repeated action can acquire new meaning because a later scene reveals its consequence, not because the script announces the meaning in advance.\n\nLet the scene end on a completed action, a pending request, or a plain acknowledgment of what is now visible. Do not require a reversal, a confession, or a cliffhanger at every scene boundary.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagacax::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Chuck Palahniuk / Stage Play\n\nWrite the fantasy as a playable script with clear speakers, entrances, exits, blocking, objects, pauses, and actions that can be staged. Make the impossible condition observable through the shared stage space: who can reach a prop, see an entrance, hear a line, or move between two places should be clear enough that the fantastic element changes what is possible in the room. Let the characters handle the condition through practical work rather than explaining it in a gathered speech.\n\nUse the Palahniuk profile's procedural repetition through staged business and repeated lines. A character can perform the same sequence of checks, arrange the same objects, or repeat the same instruction when the routine returns. The repetition should accumulate weight because the scene's conditions have changed, not because the play announces its own theme. Keep the social detail concrete: what the characters carry, clean, eat, pay for, or refuse to touch. Let their self-exposure come through what they do and say under the pressure of the scene.\n\nMonologues and asides need a dramatic purpose rather than replacing every private thought. If required private experience is present, keep it as nonspoken authoring material in the active presentation; it is not something another character can hear. Do not add film-camera instructions. Stage directions establish observable conditions; they do not certify hidden guilt or hidden knowledge.\n\nLet the scene end on a completed action, an interruption, or a plain acknowledgment of what the characters now know. Do not require a theatrical revelation, a gathered confrontation, or a new fantastic event at every scene boundary.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagacay::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Chuck Palahniuk / Stream of Consciousness\n\nWrite the fantasy through a particular authorized mind's associative movement. Braid perception, memory, private words, bodily sensation, interruption, and recurring concern so that the impossible condition is experienced rather than explained. The mind can return to a rule, a warning, a physical task, or a fragment of speech whenever a present perception triggers it. Let syntax and paragraph shape follow those associations, but keep enough orientation to distinguish what is happening now from what is remembered or imagined.\n\nUse the Palahniuk profile's repeated rules and practical particulars as the recurring material of the mind. The narrator can circle back to the same steps of a task, the same list of objects, the same bodily check, or the same phrase, and each return can carry a slightly different feeling because the present moment has changed. The account remains precise and procedural even when the underlying fear or grief is visible. Do not have the narrator stop to announce that fear as a thesis; let the recurrence of the rule reveal the defensive logic.\n\nKeep the fantastic element's consequences concrete. The mind may notice how the impossible condition changes a room's temperature, a person's appetite, a work schedule, or the order in which things must be done. Wonder can sit beside irritation, fatigue, and ordinary practical thought. The technique does not make every character share a mind, authorize human-owned thoughts, or turn all scene facts indeterminate. Ordinary reasoning alone is not this stylistic mode; the associations must actually move the telling.\n\nLet the response end when the mind's movement reaches a natural pause, a completed task, or a shift in attention. Do not require a revelation, a confession, or a completed crisis at every boundary.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagacaz::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Chuck Palahniuk / Text Messages / Chat Transcript\n\nWrite the fantasy as a story carried by readable sender-attributed messages. Let the impossible condition be legible through what people transmit, delay, edit, receive, or refuse to send. A sender can repeat a warning before a trip, request the same object after each incident, send the same instruction to different people, or leave a message unsent because the rule says it must not be sent. Use only the timestamps, delays, edits, receipts, attachments, or channel changes that matter to the exchange.\n\nUse the Palahniuk profile's repeated rules and practical particulars through each person's actual digital habits. One sender may type in fragments, another in complete procedural lists, another in delayed replies. The repetition of a phrase or instruction should accumulate weight because the circumstances around the exchange have changed, not because the transcript announces its own theme. Keep the social and bodily detail concrete: what people buy, eat, clean, carry, check, or avoid. Let their self-exposure come through what they choose to send and what they leave unsent.\n\nDistinguish a sent message from an unsent draft and private thought from transmitted text. An unsent draft is a composed artifact, not automatically the writer's live private thought. If context outside the exchange is required, present it clearly separated from the transcript. Do not fabricate an interface event merely to add subtext. The active response boundary can occur during continuing speech, action, thought, composition, or an unfinished setup; do not require the exchange to pause, resume, or produce a result before ending.\n\nLet the transcript end on a sent message, a pending reply, a visible delay, or a plain acknowledgment of what has been transmitted. Do not require a confession, a reversal, or a completed crisis at every boundary.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagacba::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Chuck Palahniuk / Visual Novel\n\nWrite the fantasy as a scene-oriented visual novel sequence with concise scene-setting, readable dialogue attribution, and focal narration or inner response. Make the impossible condition legible through what the focal character notices and handles in each scene: a doorway that should not be there, a repeated gesture before a meal, an object that must be placed in a particular order, a room that changes when no one is watching. Let the fantastic element affect ordinary decisions and practical work rather than appearing only as spectacle.\n\nUse the Palahniuk profile's repeated rules and practical particulars through the focal narration and dialogue. The narration can return to the same steps of a task, the same list of objects, or the same phrase when the routine returns. Each recurrence should register a change in the scene's conditions. Keep the social and bodily detail concrete: what people eat, wear, pay for, clean, or avoid. Let their self-exposure come through what they do and say under pressure, not through a narrator's confessional thesis.\n\nUse background or expression cues as purposeful references to orient the moment. A changed expression can attract suspicion, but it is not an automatic truth indicator. Do not add branching menus, player decisions, romance, or images merely because the style is selected. Keep scene changes legible. If required private experience is present, present it as focal narration or inner response in the active presentation; it is not dialogue and no other character hears it.\n\nLet the scene end on a completed action, a pending request, or a plain acknowledgment of what the focal character now knows. Do not require a choice screen, a confession, or a completed crisis at every scene boundary.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagacbb::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Chuck Palahniuk / Web Serial\n\nWrite the fantasy as an installment-oriented web serial with a readable through-line across replies. Keep what is underway, what changes locally, and what remains available to continue clear enough that a reader can re-enter without a full recap. Recover context briefly only when it helps the current installment. Let the impossible condition's consequences accrue across replies: a rule learned in one installment can change what a character does in the next, and an object or phrase can return with a new weight because the circumstances have changed.\n\nUse the Palahniuk profile's repeated rules and practical particulars as the serial's cumulative material. A character can repeat the same task, check, instruction, or fragment when the routine returns. Each recurrence should register a change in the installment's conditions rather than restarting the same moment. Keep the social and bodily detail concrete: what people buy, eat, clean, carry, check, or avoid. Let their self-exposure come through what they do and say under pressure, not through a narrator's announced thesis.\n\nUse earned developments and occasional hooks without making every turn a complete miniature chapter or an artificial cliffhanger. Progress can involve knowledge, relationships, work, or resources. The style does not automatically create leveling systems, rankings, or progression-fantasy world rules. If required private experience is present, present it as focal narration or inner response in the active presentation; it is not dialogue and no other character hears it.\n\nLet the installment end on completed work, a pending request, a new implication of an old rule, or a plain acknowledgment of what is now known. Do not require a reversal, a confession, or a completed crisis at every installment boundary.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagadaa::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Cormac McCarthy / Angsty / Emotionally Turbulent\n\nTreat the fantastic element as a material condition that has already entered the characters' practical lives. When a spell, mark, bond, or changed landscape appears, render it through concrete nouns and forceful verbs: what it costs to carry, what it wears away, what it makes easier, what it leaves in the room. Do not pause the scene to explain the system. Let the character meet the impossible thing the way they meet a debt or a difficult relative: with work, avoidance, bargaining, or a half-finished apology.\n\nBuild emotional turbulence through behavior and exchange rather than through a vocabulary of suffering. A character may need something and refuse to say it, may admit it badly, may return to a person they hurt and discover the hurt has already changed the shape of the house. Let the strain alter the actual conversation: a question answered with a task, a kindness offered as an accusation, a silence that does the work of an argument. Keep the prose paratactic where the feeling is sharpest, so the clauses land as separate blows without commentary.\n\nAllow the fantastic premise to make the emotional cost legible. A magical consequence can expose a motive the character would not confess: a ward that fails because the promise behind it was already broken, a familiar that repeats the words its keeper will not say, a road that shortens for the one who wants to leave. The world's response is evidence, not metaphor. It does not resolve the relationship. Comfort, when it comes, stays partial and awkward: a dry blanket, a shared task, a sentence that admits only the smallest true thing. Do not require a confession, a reconciliation, or a collapse. The scene may end with the hurt still present and the impossible thing still working.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagadab::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Cormac McCarthy / Chat Roleplay\n\nWrite the shared interval as a concrete scene with unmistakable dialogue attribution. Give each speaker a distinct practical voice: one may bargain, another may answer with a task, another may withhold a motive behind a plain sentence. Keep the paragraphs short enough to scan, but let each one carry a specific material detail: the weight of a pack, the state of a fire, the mark a spell left on a doorframe. Do not decorate the scene with adjectives that could attach to any setting.\n\nKeep the fantastic element active in the ongoing world. An established magical condition should affect what the characters can do in this interval: a binding that limits travel, a creature that must be fed, a changed season that alters the work. Show its consequence through action and object rather than exposition. If a character uses magic, render the result concretely and let the other participants respond to what they can see, hear, or carry. Do not pause the scene to explain the whole system.\n\nAdvance the scene through exchange and labor. A question can be answered by a chore, a warning can be delivered as a statement about the weather, and a decision can emerge from what the characters handle rather than from a speech about it. Keep NPC behavior active: they continue their work, make their own choices, and may cooperate or refuse according to the scene's actual stakes. End at a natural continuation point that follows from what has just happened. The next writer should be able to pick up the same interval without needing a forced question or a manufactured cliffhanger.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagadac::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Cormac McCarthy / Chinese Literary Traditions\n\nUse the vernacular episodic telling route. Organize the narrative as a sequence of concrete episodes connected by travel, work, or a shared practical errand, not by an abstract quest. Each episode should have its own small completion: a river crossed, a debt settled, a broken tool replaced, a strange creature bargained with. Let the fantastic element enter as a condition of the road or the work rather than as a grand system to be explained. A spirit may demand a toll, a charm may fail in the rain, a changed animal may remember a kindness. Render these events through the characters' hands and feet.\n\nKeep the prose spare and paratactic. Prefer concrete nouns and forceful verbs. Let one clause follow another without smoothing every transition: the horse stumbles, the lantern goes out, the man says nothing. Accumulate detail across episodes rather than loading a single scene with exposition. When a character speaks, let the speech be practical and brief, carrying bargains, warnings, or withheld feeling without announcing it. Do not import a particular Chinese setting, period, or cast. Use the language, place, and community actually supplied by the story, and let the episodic method shape them.\n\nLet the fantastic have intelligible consequences in daily life. A magical event should change what the characters can do next: they must take another road, pay a price, change their schedule, or carry a new burden. Wonder may sit beside ordinary labor without becoming a metaphor or a moral. End each episode at a point where the immediate task is done or clearly interrupted, so the next episode can begin from the changed conditions. Do not require a prophecy, an ancient bloodline, or a mythic-scale obligation unless the supplied world already contains them.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagadad::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Cormac McCarthy / Compressed Gritty Prose\n\nWrite the fantastic element as a hard material condition. A spell has a cost in fuel, blood, time, or heat. A magical creature needs feed and shelter and leaves tracks. A changed landscape makes the road worse before it makes it strange. Use forceful diction and concrete nouns: do not say the magic was powerful; say what it burned, broke, or carried off. Keep transitions compressed. Move from one action to the next without a paragraph of commentary, and let the reader feel the pressure in the sequence itself.\n\nGround the physical stakes in ordinary labor and wear. Show the state of tools, clothes, animals, and bodies. A character's hands may be cracked from the work a charm requires. A ward may hold only as long as the post it is nailed to. Let abrasive or rough speech belong to a specific speaker and situation, not to every character. A longer thought is permitted when the character must reason through a choice about the magical risk: what it will cost, who will pay it, whether the road is still passable.\n\nVary the rhythm deliberately. Short clauses can carry sudden danger or a failed attempt. A longer sentence can accumulate the weight of a journey or the sequence of a ritual done wrong. Do not turn every fantasy scene into survival horror. The world may contain help, rest, and practical kindness, but those things also have weight and consequence. The impossible remains intelligible through what it does to the people who must live with it.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagadae::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Cormac McCarthy / Epistolary / Documents\n\nCarry the story through documents, each with a clear author, intended recipient or purpose, and time of composition. Use the specific form the scene requires: a letter to a distant household, a supply report, a journal kept on the road, a notice posted at a crossing. Do not rotate through formats mechanically. Let each document record the fantastic element as a practical fact: the well has gone salt, the charm was paid for in silver and did not hold, the creature left tracks around the sheepfold. Write these records in concrete nouns and forceful verbs, with the spare, direct speech of someone reporting what they saw or did.\n\nRespect what each document knows and conceals. A letter may omit a failure the writer is ashamed of. A report may misread a magical event as weather or theft. A journal may record a suspicion the writer will not send to anyone. Gaps and revisions are part of the story: a crossed-out line, a second letter correcting the first, a receipt that contradicts a later account. Let the reader reconstruct the event across entries without an omniscient paragraph explaining what really happened. The fantastic premise should remain intelligible through the accumulated records, but each record stays partial.\n\nKeep continuity through material traces and repeated practical details. A place name, a tool, a debt, or a changed animal can reappear in different documents and deepen the reader's understanding. The next document may answer a question raised by the last, complicate it, or reveal that the earlier writer was concerned with something else. Do not require every document to end in a cliffhanger. A record may simply note what was done and what remains to be done, leaving the next entry to follow from the changed conditions.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagadaf::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Cormac McCarthy / Fluffy / Comforting\n\nSpend attention on chosen kindness and practical care within the supplied fantasy world. A character may warm a cloak by the fire, mend a strap, remember which tea another prefers, or set aside a portion of food for a creature that follows them. Render these actions in concrete nouns and forceful verbs: the care is in the doing, not in a speech about feeling. Let the fantastic element participate in the comfort without becoming a cure. A magical household may keep the bread from spoiling, a familiar may press against a tired hand, a charm may light the room against a child's fear. Show the small pleasure as it is, not as a symbol of a larger emotional repair.\n\nKeep affection sincere but not compulsory. A character may show love by working beside someone, by leaving a door unlatched, by saying one plain true thing. They do not need to confess, apologize, or resolve an old hurt. Personality and privacy remain intact: a kind person may still be stubborn, a quiet person may still need to be alone, and a disagreement may continue after the meal is shared. Comfort does not make everyone agreeable or remove the world's established problems. The dangerous road is still dangerous; the debt is still owed; the strange season still shortens the days.\n\nLet ease and manageable friction share the scene. A familiar preference can be honored, a small mistake can be corrected without blame, and a moment of rest can be earned by the work that came before. Do not replace the fantasy genre with a domestic vignette. The impossible thing may be present in the room, and the characters may live with it the way they live with weather: sometimes grateful, sometimes wary, sometimes simply accustomed. End the scene where the care has been given and received, without requiring a dramatic turn or a promise that everything will be well.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagadag::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Cormac McCarthy / Formal Literary Novel\n\nWrite in composed novel prose with deliberate transitions and sustained paragraph movement. Let the narration frame the fantastic element through social context, history, or judgment where the chosen narrator permits it. A changed law, a taxed charm, a road closed by a creature, a village that has learned to live with a recurring wonder: these are not isolated marvels but parts of a legible order. Distinguish the narration from ordinary spoken language. The narrator may observe that a custom had outlived its purpose, that a bargain was struck in a bad year, or that the people measured the season by the thing that came down from the hills. Keep the sentences concrete and the verbs forceful, but allow the paragraph to accumulate rather than strike.\n\nUse public and private contrast when it organizes the passage. A ceremony may be described from the outside, with its robes, tools, and formal words, and then the scene may move inside to a private room where the same event is reckoned in smaller terms: a debt, a fear, a child's question. Correspondence or record may enter the narration as an object the characters handle, not as a shift into another style. Let the fantastic premise affect institutions and decisions: a council may set a price on a magical service, a family may divide land according to a sign, a guild may refuse to work with a particular material. Show these consequences through the narrator's framing and the characters' actions rather than through a lecture.\n\nKeep the prose grounded in landscape and labor. Even in a formal passage, the world is made of specific things: the grain in a table, the wear on a threshold, the smoke from a kiln, the weight of a sealed letter. Do not impose an aristocratic setting, archaic vocabulary, or uniformly elaborate speech. The formality is in the architecture of the telling, not in the rank of the characters. A farmer's decision, a servant's observation, or a tradesman's bargain can carry the same composed weight as a lord's decree. Let the scene end where the social and private meanings have been brought into relation, without requiring a resolution of the larger world.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagadah::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Cormac McCarthy / Framed Oral Storytelling (Arabian Nights-inspired)\n\nOpen inside the frame world with a concrete present need: someone is waiting, working, bargaining, traveling, or keeping watch, and the telling begins because it can change that situation. Give the teller an audience whose attention is not decorative. A listener may need to be persuaded, delayed, warned, comforted, or made to understand something the teller cannot say directly. Let the occasion explain why this story is told now and why the teller chooses this version.\n\nRender both the frame and the embedded story with McCarthy's material attention. Name the actual surfaces, tools, weather, and labor of each world. Use short linked clauses and forceful verbs to move through action. When the embedded story enters a fantastic place or event, keep its physical consequences specific: what the impossible thing does to ground, water, skin, shelter, or work. The wonder should sit beside ordinary effort rather than replacing it.\n\nMake the teller's selection and emphasis visible as concrete acts. Show what the teller repeats, skips, or lingers on by having the listener notice the absence or the weight: a name the teller will not say, a detail the teller returns to when the listener's attention drifts, a pause where the teller looks at the listener before continuing. Do not announce the connection; let the recurrence do the work.\n\nUse recurrence as a technique of accrual, not decoration. A repeated object, phrase, or gesture should appear in both levels and change meaning as the listener or reader recognizes it. When the same object returns, show what has changed around it: the listener's need, the teller's purpose, the work that has been done or left undone. Let the recognition land in the frame world as a physical shift—a listener's posture, a decision, a question, a task resumed—rather than as a stated moral.\n\nKeep the narrative levels intelligible. Shift cleanly between the frame and the embedded story, and return to the frame at moments when the telling has produced a visible effect. The embedded story may remain unfinished or be interrupted by the present world's demands. Its power comes from what the telling does to the people listening, not from a completed moral or a compulsory cliffhanger.\n\nDo not import djinn, palaces, deserts, or a particular religion unless the supplied world already contains them. The frame can be any place where a story is a practical act. The fantastic premise of the embedded tale should affect how its people live, decide, and work, with intelligible consequences rather than a catalogue of marvels.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagadai::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Cormac McCarthy / German-Language Literary Traditions\n\nUse the Romantic and uncanny narrative route. Let the story follow a narrator or focal character whose attention is drawn to a particular place, object, person, or recurring event that does not fit the known order of the world. Establish the fantastic premise through sustained attention: the narrator notices what it does, how it changes the people near it, and what it costs them. Do not explain its origin or mechanics unless the story's actual knowledge supports that explanation.\n\nBuild the uncanny through repeated observation rather than a single revelation. Have the narrator return to the same detail across different days, seasons, or tasks: the same mark on the wood, the same sound against stone, the same change in the light. Each return should add one concrete consequence—what has altered, what has been done or left undone, who has come or gone—so the uncanny accumulates through material evidence rather than through a sudden shock.\n\nWrite with McCarthy's concrete nouns and forceful verbs. Give the uncanny thing a physical presence: the sound it makes against stone, the mark it leaves on wood, the way it alters the light or the work of a household. Use paratactic linked clauses to accumulate observation without resolving it into a system. Let landscape and labor remain present around the uncanny event, so the impossible appears inside a world of ordinary effort, weather, and endurance.\n\nLet the syntax and narrative distance follow the narrator's position. A narrator who is part of the community may report what others say and what they themselves have seen, without claiming to understand it. A more distant narrator may observe the social consequences of the uncanny: who avoids the place, who seeks it, who profits from it, who refuses to speak of it. Keep the distinction between what the narrator has witnessed, what has been reported, and what is inferred by attributing speech to its speakers and showing the narrator's own observations as direct sensory detail.\n\nAllow the uncanny to produce unease through consequence rather than through sudden horror. Show how the impossible premise affects decisions, institutions, or everyday life: a boundary no one crosses, a task performed differently, a bargain made with something not fully understood. The story may end without a complete explanation, but the reader should understand what the uncanny thing has done to the people and the place. Do not import a particular national setting, bureaucracy, or translated word order; let the supplied world and characters determine the specifics.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagadaj::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Cormac McCarthy / Gothic-Punk\n\nEstablish the fantastic premise as an old authority that still operates inside present urban life. It may be a power held by a family, an institution, a place, or a person whose claim reaches back before the current world's arrangements. Show that authority through its material effects: who pays, who is admitted, who waits, what decays, what is maintained at cost. Use McCarthy's concrete nouns and forceful verbs to make the power physical rather than atmospheric.\n\nLet the collision between old authority and present life happen in specific encounters. A character may need something from the old power, serve it, resist it, or be bound to it by blood, debt, or desire. Stage the encounter through the character's practical need: what they want, what they owe, what they will not give up. Write the practical speech of bargains and withheld motives. What people say in these encounters should carry what they want and what they will not admit. Do not make every exchange sinister; some cooperation is genuine, some refusal is practical, and some intimacy is complicated rather than merely predatory.\n\nUse urban material detail to ground the Gothic atmosphere. Name the actual surfaces: the stairwell, the locked door, the wiring, the water stain, the money changing hands. Let the fantastic premise alter these details in intelligible ways. A room may be colder than the building allows, a door may open for one person and not another, a debt may be paid in something other than currency. Show the consequence of each alteration rather than piling up uncanny decoration: the cold room changes what work can be done there, the selective door changes who can enter and who must wait outside, the strange payment changes what the debtor has left.\n\nAllow social masks to fall away in trusted company, but do not require every private moment to become a confession or a betrayal. Show the falling of the mask through concrete changes in speech and posture: a character speaks more slowly, uses a name they would not use elsewhere, sets down a tool or a weapon, admits a fear or a desire they have kept hidden. A character may reveal desire, fear, or loyalty to one person while maintaining a different face elsewhere. Keep the reader's access to interiority consistent with the active narration contract: what a character thinks and what they say may differ for a supported reason, and the narration should make the actual private experience available where permitted.\n\nDo not import Kindred physiology, Humanity numbers, factions, powers, or blood-bond rules. The old authority is whatever the supplied fantasy world establishes. Its horror is personal and political: it shapes who can act, who is protected, and what intimacy costs. Let the story show those costs through work, endurance, and the physical world, not through a catalogue of powers.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagadak::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Cormac McCarthy / Indian Literary Traditions\n\nUse the oral, epic, and frame-tale route. Let the fantastic premise enter the story through a teller who speaks from within a community, family, or lineage that remembers the events. The telling is not a private entertainment but an act of transmission: it carries obligations, warnings, names, and the weight of what has already been paid or promised. Establish why this account is being given now and to whom.\n\nKeep the teller's account concrete in McCarthy's manner. Name the actual places, tools, animals, weather, and work of the remembered world. Use short linked clauses and forceful verbs to move through journeys, labors, and encounters. When the fantastic premise appears, show its physical consequences: what it did to the land, the household, the body, the harvest, the road. Do not let the wonder float above the material world.\n\nMake the teller's selection and emphasis visible as concrete acts of transmission. Show what the teller repeats, skips, or lingers on by having the listener notice the absence or the weight: a name the teller will not say, a detail the teller returns to when the listener's attention drifts, a pause where the teller looks at the listener before continuing. The teller's omissions are part of the present situation: what is remembered, what is omitted, and what is repeated reveal what the community needs from the story now.\n\nUse recurrence as a technique of accrual, not decoration. A repeated name, object, phrase, or place should appear in both levels and change meaning as the listener or reader recognizes it. When the same name or object returns, show what has changed around it: the listener's need, the teller's purpose, the work that has been done or left undone. Let the recognition land in the frame world as a physical shift—a listener's decision, a question, a task resumed, a silence—rather than as a stated moral.\n\nKeep the narrative levels intelligible. Shift cleanly between the present telling and the recounted events. Return to the frame at moments when the telling has produced a visible effect. The embedded account may be long and episodic, but each episode should connect to the present need through a concrete shift in the frame rather than merely extending the tale.\n\nKeep language, region, community, and period specific when the supplied world provides them. Do not force English, code-switching, religion, caste experience, or a generic family structure onto the scene. The teller's voice should follow from the actual community and period, and the fantastic premise should affect that community's institutions and everyday life in intelligible ways.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagadal::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Cormac McCarthy / Japanese Literary Traditions\n\nUse the fantastic tale route. Let the story present a strange being, event, or place as a direct encounter within an otherwise material world. The fantastic premise should be established through what it does: how it changes the people who meet it, what it asks or takes, and what it leaves behind. Do not explain its origin or mechanics unless the story's actual knowledge supports that explanation.\n\nWrite the encounter with McCarthy's concrete nouns and forceful verbs. Name the actual surfaces, tools, weather, and labor of the world around the strange thing. Use short linked clauses to move through the meeting, the journey toward it, or the aftermath. When the fantastic premise acts, show its physical consequences: the mark on the ground, the change in the light, the silence in the animals, the task left undone. Let the wonder sit beside ordinary effort rather than replacing it.\n\nLet personal language and narrative distance follow the actual teller. A participant may report what they saw and did without claiming to understand it. Render the participant's account through direct sensory observation: what they touched, what they heard, what they did next, what they did not do. A more distant narrator may report what others say happened and what the community did afterward. Keep the distinction between what was witnessed, what was reported, and what is inferred by attributing speech to its speakers and showing the participant's own observations as direct sensory detail.\n\nPractical speech can carry bargains, convictions, and withheld motives. What people say about the strange thing should reveal what they want from it or fear from it. Show the want or fear through what they offer, what they refuse, what they ask, and what they will not say. Do not make every exchange sinister; some cooperation is genuine, some refusal is practical, and some curiosity is simple rather than ominous.\n\nAllow the tale to end without a complete explanation, but make the consequences intelligible. Show the aftermath through concrete behavior: who avoids the strange thing, who seeks it, what changed in the work or the household, what task was left undone and whether it was ever finished. The reader should understand what the strange thing has done to the people and the place. Do not impose a universal quietness or a mandatory inventory of Japanese scenery. Let the supplied world and characters determine the specifics, and keep the fantastic premise's effect on institutions and everyday life concrete.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagadam::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Cormac McCarthy / Latin American Literary Traditions\n\nUse the fantastic or metaphysical construction route. Treat the fantastic premise as a real, intelligible feature of the world: it is not a rumor, a delusion, or a sudden intrusion, but a condition that people already live with and work around. Establish it through its consequences for labor, landscape, institutions, and decisions. Do not stop to explain its origin or mechanics unless the story's actual knowledge supports that explanation.\n\nWrite the premise with McCarthy's concrete nouns and forceful verbs. Show what the impossible condition does to the ground, the water, the road, the body, the household, the harvest. Use short linked clauses to accumulate observation without turning the strange into ornament. Let the wonder sit beside ordinary effort: people still work, travel, bargain, and endure, and the fantastic premise changes how those things are done rather than replacing them.\n\nLet the narration take the premise as given. Characters may speak of it practically, as they speak of weather or debt. Render the premise as given through habitual action and practical speech: a character checks the condition before setting out, adjusts their work to it, bargains over its cost, or warns a newcomer about it in the same tone they would use for a bad road or a hard season. What they say can carry bargains, convictions, and withheld motives, but they do not need to marvel at the premise or explain it to one another.\n\nReveal the premise's rules through what people do and what it costs them. Show a character paying the cost, avoiding the cost, or trying to pass the cost to someone else. Show what the premise makes easier, harder, or impossible in the ordinary work of the world. The reader should learn the premise's rules through these concrete transactions, not through a lecture.\n\nPreserve the relevant language, community, and history when the supplied world provides them. Do not manufacture national speech patterns, religion, caste, honor systems, or a generic regional temperament. The fantastic premise should affect the actual community's institutions and everyday life in intelligible ways. If the world excludes supernatural facts, do not add them; the construction must remain within what the supplied canon permits.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagadan::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Cormac McCarthy / Light Novel\n\nEnter the immediate situation cleanly. Open with a character in a specific place doing something concrete, and let the fantastic premise appear through what they see, handle, or must respond to. Keep spatial changes easy to follow: when the scene moves from one room, road, or position to another, name the new space and what is physically present in it. Use readable paragraph breaks around action, dialogue, and private response so the reader can track movement and reaction without losing the thread.\n\nWrite with McCarthy's concrete nouns and forceful verbs. Give the fantastic premise a physical presence: what it does to the floor, the air, the tool in someone's hand, the work left unfinished. Use short linked clauses to move through action and consequence. Practical speech can carry bargains, convictions, and withheld motives; let dialogue do real work rather than decorate the scene. When an unfamiliar name or mechanic appears, present it clearly through what it does or what it costs, not through an explanatory pause unless the story's actual knowledge supports that pause.\n\nGive each reaction to the fantastic premise a focused beat. A character may be serious, uncertain, angry, or funny according to who they are and what the premise means to them. Show the reaction through concrete detail: a pause in the work, a changed grip, a question asked or not asked, a decision made. Do not require every reaction to be witty, combative, or polished. The reaction should follow from the character's actual situation and the premise's consequences.\n\nLet the fantastic premise affect decisions, institutions, or everyday life in intelligible ways. Show the effect through what it does to the immediate scene: the work that must be redone, the route that must be changed, the bargain that must be renegotiated, the task that must be left for later. The reader should understand what the impossible thing does and what it costs without a catalogue of marvels. Do not impose first person, comedy, a young cast, or a closing cliffhanger. The scene may end at a natural continuation point: a task resumed, a question left open, a character moving toward the next concrete action.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagadao::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Cormac McCarthy / Lyrical Prose\n\nGround each impossible event in a single material consequence the focal character can perceive. Do not explain the premise; choose the one thing it does to weight, light, sound, smell, or labor that makes it present. A door that should not exist has a particular resistance in the hand. A charm leaves a particular stain or heat. Let that chosen detail anchor the paragraph, and let the narration return to it when the scene's emotional movement shifts.\n\nBuild the paragraph as a syntactic arc. Open with a plain declarative sentence that states the scene's ground: what the character is doing, where they stand, what the weather or the work is. Then extend the sentence or follow it with clauses that accumulate the particulars making that ground incomplete. Let the accumulation move by addition rather than explanation: one clause states what the character sees, the next what they do, the next what the impossible thing does in return. Close the paragraph by returning to the anchor detail or the opening statement with its meaning altered by what has accumulated.\n\nUse a recurring sound or image to carry the emotional movement across the scene. Choose it from the scene's actual materials: a bird crossing a field where the light falls wrongly, the green stain a forbidden instrument leaves on a sleeve, the sound of a gate that closes on no visible hinge. Repeat it only when a later occurrence gives it new application, so the return marks a change in the character's understanding rather than decorating the prose. When a comparison unfolds, make it change what the narrator or focal character knows: the likeness should reveal a consequence, a danger, or a possibility that was already present in the scene but not yet understood.\n\nLet dialogue interrupt the lyric register with practical speech. A character may ask the price of a miracle, refuse to name what they saw, or correct a figurative description with a workman's term. The interruption does not cancel the style; it provides the plain ground against which the lyric accumulation works. Keep the narration close to one person's perception while another speaker reveals a different relation to the same impossible fact. End the response at the scene's actual boundary of attention, with the task concluded or the character standing at the edge of what has just become possible.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagadap::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Cormac McCarthy / Melancholic / Reflective\n\nChoose one present material detail that carries the fantastic premise's alteration. It should be a thing the character handles, passes, or uses in the course of the scene: a worn place on a charm, a cold seam in a floor that a spell once warmed, a coat still hanging where a child no longer sleeps, a field where an enchantment used to hold back flood. Let that detail do the work of reflection. State what it is and what the character does with it before stating what it once was.\n\nWhen memory enters, make it an occasion rather than a summary. The present detail prompts the recollection, and the recollection returns to the present detail with its meaning changed. Recall who stood where, what was said, what the light did, what the object weighed or smelled like then. Use McCarthy's paratactic movement to hold the two times side by side: a simple clause states what is gone, the next states what remains, and the conjunction does not force a conclusion. Do not append a moral or a general statement about time.\n\nLet remembered pleasure remain pleasure. If the character recalls a good meal, a warm room, or a task done well, render the satisfaction in concrete terms without a following clause that cancels it. The loss may coexist with the pleasure; it need not replace it. Likewise, let present comfort stand: a character may finish a task, eat well, or take warmth from a familiar object, and the prose may end on that satisfaction.\n\nLet practical speech carry the unfinished attachment. A character may ask after someone they know will not return, bargain over an object whose power has faded because they still need what it used to provide, or answer a question about the past with a direction to present work. Keep the teller's access consistent with the selected perspective. End where the scene's actual attention ends: with the character still at work, still waiting, or still holding the thing that no longer answers.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagadaq::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Cormac McCarthy / Middle Eastern Literary Traditions\n\nUse a framed oral telling as the presentation. Establish the frame first in concrete terms: where the telling happens, who is listening, what work or waiting accompanies it, and what the teller is doing with their hands. The teller is a particular person with a reason to relate this story, not a neutral voice. The frame and the tale are both part of the scene; neither is a decorative container for the other.\n\nTell the inner account in McCarthy's spare material language. Render each fantastic element through what it does and what it costs. A jinn, a transformed animal, a speaking well, or a city that appears only at certain hours should be shown through its effect on decisions, labor, shelter, or travel. Prefer concrete nouns and forceful verbs to ornamental description. State the consequence before any wonder: the well's speech changes what the village dares to draw, the appearing city changes what the caravan carries and what it pays.\n\nLet the teller's choices shape the information flow. The teller may pause to tend a fire, repeat a phrase when the audience needs it again, or withhold a name until the moment when its revelation changes the meaning of an earlier detail. A delayed name or fact should revise what the audience already heard, not merely postpone a twist. Keep the tradition coherent: do not mix the conventions of unrelated regional sources, and do not invent a generic mystical atmosphere. The fantastic elements must have intelligible consequences within the tale's own world.\n\nLet the frame and the tale comment on each other through interruption. A listener may ask a practical question, a child may fall asleep, or the teller may break off to answer a household need. The interruption can reveal something about the story's stakes or the teller's motive without turning the scene into a lesson. Practical speech carries bargains and convictions in both the frame and the inner account. Do not require every tale to end in wisdom or every teller to be wise. End with the telling unfinished, the audience dispersed, or the teller still sitting with what the story has cost them to repeat.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagadar::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Cormac McCarthy / Minimalist Prose\n\nSelect one exact material detail that carries the fantastic premise's consequence. Choose the detail that shows what the impossible thing does, not what it is: the shadow a floating lantern does not cast, the too-smooth skin of a healed wound, the sound a door makes when it opens onto a place it did not open onto before. State that detail in a direct clause and do not explain it. Do not add a paragraph of interpretation or a summary of wonder.\n\nFollow the detail with the character's action. The next sentence shows what the character does about what they have perceived: they set down the lantern, they cover the healed place, they step back from the door. The action makes the consequence legible without stating it. If the character is afraid, show what they do with their hands or where they look. Omit the inward summary unless it materially contributes to the present account; when a thought is essential, keep it brief and specific: they knew what the mark meant.\n\nPreserve the causal chain. What is omitted must remain inferable from what is shown. If a later event depends on an earlier one, include the clause that makes the connection: the charm was buried under the threshold and the house stood empty for a year and the well went bad and no one would say why. A precise extended sentence may serve better than several fragments when the chain matters. Do not delete causality for the sake of shortness.\n\nLet dialogue carry practical bargains and withheld motives. A speaker may ask what a thing costs, refuse to touch it, or answer a question with a direction to work. Keep cast voices individual; the narration need not explain what each silence means. End when the scene's action or attention ends, with a task unfinished, a question unanswered, or a character standing where the impossible fact has just become part of the day's work.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagadas::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Cormac McCarthy / Modern Literature / Contemporary Novel\n\nOpen in the middle of a scene whose action already involves the fantastic premise. Establish the impossible fact as a material condition of the work or journey: a character checks the wards before opening a gate, pays for passage with a coin that must be broken in half, waits for a beast that only crosses the river when the moon is gone. Do not introduce the premise through exposition; let it appear in what the character does and what the scene requires.\n\nMove among action, dialogue, perception, recollection, and authorized inner experience as the scene demands. Use paragraphing to mark shifts of attention, and use transitions that follow the character's immediate perception or thought rather than recapping what the reader already knows. A recollection should arise from something in the present scene and return to it. An inner reflection should make the character's next choice legible without stopping the scene to explain it.\n\nKeep McCarthy's concrete nouns and forceful verbs in the foreground. The narration may accumulate clauses when the action or landscape requires it, but the accumulation should move the scene forward. Let practical speech carry bargains, convictions, and withheld motives; a character may explain the cost of a spell in the same tone they use to discuss the price of grain. Calibrate the narrator's register to the immediate scene: spare when the action is spare, more expansive when the landscape or the character's attention expands. The prose should remain continuous and unobtrusive rather than announcing its own artfulness.\n\nGive authorized inner experience enough room to make the character's choices legible. A focal character may reason about a risk, hesitate over a bargain, or notice that a familiar place has changed. Keep that inner experience within the selected perspective. End the scene where its actual attention ends, with the action still in progress, a conversation interrupted, or a character setting out toward what the next hour will require. Do not impose a lesson or a completed arc at the end of every reply.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagadat::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Cormac McCarthy / Relationship-Centric Fanfiction (AO3-inspired)\n\nDerive the scene from the actual supplied relationship premise. Identify what the premise makes difficult, desired, or repeated between these particular people, and choose a scene that makes that condition legible in material terms. If the premise concerns two people who cannot safely touch, show the work of keeping distance: a cup set down at the far edge of a table, a doorway held open too long, a task divided so their hands will not meet. If it concerns rivalry, show the contest in its concrete particulars rather than in banter about feelings. Do not import a roster of familiar tropes or force a confession where the characters would withhold it.\n\nBuild private language from repeated concrete moments and objects. Choose a phrase, a gesture, or an object that belongs to these characters' shared history, and let it return at a later moment with new weight. The callback should fulfill the relationship's particular promise, not merely repeat a motif. A phrase first used in one practical context may return in another where its meaning has changed. An object first handled in one scene may reappear when the relationship's stakes have shifted.\n\nLet McCarthy's practical speech carry affection, jealousy, comfort, or discovery without making every speaker eloquent. A character may express care by repairing a thing, refusing a bargain, or asking a question they already know the answer to. Keep the narration close to one person's perception while the other's motive remains legible through action and speech. The narration need not explain what each silence means.\n\nLet the fantastic premise exert real pressure on the relationship. A shared spell, a dangerous journey, a household bound by an old obligation, or a place that changes what people can promise one another should affect what the characters do, risk, or withhold. Give the emotional payoff enough space where it belongs, but keep it grounded in the scene's actual conditions. End where the relationship's present moment ends: with an understanding reached, a failure to say what was meant, or the characters continuing their work side by side with the unresolved feeling still between them.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagadau::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Cormac McCarthy / Saga / Oral Epic\n\nOpen with a deliberate declaration that establishes a person, a place, or a deed whose consequences will be remembered: the year the river spoke, the winter the boundary stones were moved, the bargain made at the dry well. Then expand into the particulars that make the declaration incomplete. The expansion should not explain the declaration away; it should show the material facts behind it: who was there, what they carried, what the act cost, what remained afterward.\n\nLet the fantastic premise organize the deeds rather than decorate them. A journey, a feud, a gift, an oath, or a public act can carry the account when the story has established it. Render the impossible through its material consequences: the unplowed field, the unlit hearth, the sword left in the door, the child carried across water that no longer froze. Keep the particular source tradition coherent; do not mix every epic convention or import a Nordic pantheon into a world that does not contain one.\n\nUse recurring phrasing with a new application at each return. A phrase should come back when a later occurrence changes what it means, not as an ornamental epithet at every entrance. Relate each act to what will be remembered: who kept the bargain, who broke the boundary, who carried the child across. Keep McCarthy's concrete nouns and forceful verbs in the foreground. The chronicle may speak of honor or doom, but those words must be grounded in the material facts of the scene.\n\nLet practical speech interrupt the authority of the chronicle. The subject of an oft-told incident may object to one detail, or answer an elevated introduction with a practical concern. The interruption need not become parody; it can reveal that the remembered deed was also an ordinary day's work for someone. Humor may arise from the persistence of a story, but the fantastic events remain serious in their consequences. Keep the teller's access consistent with the selected perspective. End with a deed concluded, a journey begun, or a name left to be remembered without declaring the whole meaning of the story on the community's behalf.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagadav::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Cormac McCarthy / Scandinavian Literary Traditions\n\nWrite the story as social-realist domestic fiction in which the established fantastic fact has already entered the household. Open on a task the premise has made ordinary: fetching water from a basin that refills overnight, airing a room the landlord will not inspect, mending a coat that grows heavier each evening, or preparing for a visit from an official who has questions about the door. Do not introduce the impossible thing as a discovery. Let the reader meet it through the work it creates.\n\nRender the household in McCarthy's concrete paratactic movement. Use short declarative sentences joined by and, with occasional longer accumulation when a sequence of labor, weather, or a journey needs its full extent. Name the objects people handle: the bucket, the latch, the receipt, the split board, the jar of salt, the folded blanket. Let verbs carry force and direction. Where it fits the established speaker and occasion, keep speech spare and practical, but let bargains, convictions, and withheld motives surface inside it. A character may say less than she knows without the narration announcing the omission.\n\nMake the fantastic fact a social condition, not a private marvel. Show its consequences in the specific institutions and neighborly arrangements the supplied setting permits: a parish record that must be corrected, a housing inspection that must be prepared for, a school notice that raises a question, a municipal fee that must be paid, a neighbor who owes a duty or demands one. Let the impossible thing change what people fetch, mend, pay for, explain, or plan around. It may be feared, tolerated, exploited, or quietly accommodated, but it must alter practical life.\n\nKeep the Scandinavian context local and distinct. Use the actual place, language, season, and community the story supplies. If the setting is Danish, Norwegian, or Swedish, let its institutions, domestic habits, and social expectations differ as the story requires. Do not import a generic northern landscape of snow, isolation, and bleakness. A crowded apartment stairwell, a summer cabin, a cooperative shop, or a ferry queue can carry the same social attention. Let particular characters be talkative, nosy, warm, or evasive according to their own motives.\n\nPreserve required interiority in the active presentation. A focal character's private reasoning about the fantastic condition may remain visible or hidden according to the current contract, but it must be actual nonspoken experience: the feel of a cold floor, the calculation of a cost, the memory of what the door showed last time, the decision to say nothing until the child is asleep. Other characters do not know it unless the story gives them access. Do not convert private thought into a performed monologue or a folk narrator's aside. The domestic surface and the inward response can diverge for a character-specific reason: embarrassment, caution, habit, or the need to get through the day.\n\nAdvance the story through ordinary social transactions. A conversation with a neighbor can revise what the household believes; a visit from an official can change what is permitted; a child's question can expose what the adults have stopped noticing. The fantastic premise may remain unexplained without becoming vague. Its effects must be consistent enough that the reader can follow what it costs and what it allows. Close on the actual state of the scene: work finished, a request made, a condition accepted, or a new consequence recognized. Do not require a revelation, a symbolic tableau, or a return to normal.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagadaw::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Cormac McCarthy / Screenplay\n\nWrite the fantasy premise through what the camera can see and the microphone can hear, and let the action lines do McCarthy's concrete work. Name the objects and surfaces that carry the impossible fact: a boot print filled with water that does not freeze, a ledger entry that rewrites itself when the clerk looks away, a door standing open on a field where no field should be. State the event plainly in one action block, then show its consequence in the next. Do not explain the premise in a character's mouth unless that character has a practical reason to say it aloud.\n\nUse scene headings only when location or time changes. Keep them simple: the place, the time of day, and any necessary condition such as rain or dark. Within a scene, move action lines from one visible or audible event to the next without camera commands. Use a cut where the sequence needs a change of place, time, or attention; use offscreen sound where a character would hear something beyond the frame. Do not use voice-over to deliver private thought unless the active contract separately authorizes it.\n\nShape dialogue as spare practical speech. Let bargains, convictions, and withheld motives surface through what people say while they work: a refusal phrased as a price, a warning phrased as a direction, a question that reveals what someone already suspects. Do not make every line witty or portentous. A character can answer with a single word or say nothing, and the action line can show what the silence costs.\n\nPreserve required private experience as clearly attributed nonspoken material in the active presentation. If the current contract requires a focal character's inward response, write it as a nonspoken note clearly separated from the action and dialogue, not as a voice-over or as a line another character can hear. The note may record the feel of a cold handle, the recognition of a changed account, or the calculation of what must be done next. It is not automatically knowledge available to the cast.\n\nMake the fantasy condition affect practical decisions. A character checks the same door twice, moves a child away from a window, pays a fee in salt, or refuses to enter a room after dark. These actions make the premise legible without a lecture. The impossible thing may remain unexplained, but its effects must be consistent enough to follow. A later scene can show the same condition from a different angle: the official who inspects it, the neighbor who profits from it, the child who has stopped fearing it.\n\nClose on the actual visible or audible state of the scene. The last action block may show work completed, a door closed, a message received, or a character standing in a changed room. Do not require a reveal, a chase, or a line that sums up the meaning. The cut, when it comes, should leave the next scene possible without pretending the current one is resolved.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagadax::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Cormac McCarthy / Stage Play\n\nMake the fantasy premise occupy the stage through physical business. The impossible thing should be something the actors can handle, avoid, measure, or carry: a basin that refills with dark water, a coat that grows heavier through the evening, a mark on the floor that moves between scenes. Write the stage directions so a director can see exactly what must happen: who lifts the basin, where she sets it, how long she watches it. Do not describe an effect that cannot be staged or leave the premise to be explained in dialogue.\n\nUse McCarthy's concrete attention in the stage directions. Name the objects and the actions precisely: the chair, the rope, the tin cup, the folded blanket. Keep the directions economical. Use a pause where the relationship between lines and physical business needs it, not as decoration. Let entrances and exits change the scene's practical situation: someone arrives with a payment, leaves to fetch a witness, or refuses to enter the room where the mark has moved.\n\nWhere it fits the established speaker and occasion, keep dialogue spare and practical. Let bargains, convictions, and withheld motives surface through what people say while they work. A character may ask what the basin holds, answer with a price, or say nothing while the other one watches. Do not turn every exchange into a confrontation or a witty duel. The play's tension can come from the gap between what is said and what is being done with the objects on stage.\n\nPreserve required private experience as nonspoken authoring material in the active presentation. If the current contract requires a focal character's inward response, write it as a clearly separated nonspoken note, not as a monologue or an aside another character can hear. The note may record the feel of the cold basin, the calculation of how long the water has been rising, or the memory of the last time the mark moved. It is not automatically knowledge available to the cast.\n\nMake the fantasy condition affect the public scene's practical work. A character refuses to drink from the basin, moves the coat to a different hook, measures the mark with a piece of string, or sends someone to fetch the landlord. These actions make the premise legible to an audience without a lecture. The impossible thing may remain unexplained, but its stageable effects must be consistent enough to follow. A later scene can show the same condition under new pressure: an official inspects it, a neighbor tries to remove it, a child plays beside it without fear.\n\nClose on the actual state of the staged scene. The last moment may be an exit, a pause, a completed task, or a character left alone with the object. Do not require a revelation, a symbolic tableau, or a line that sums up the meaning. The play can stop where the immediate action stops, leaving the next entrance possible without pretending the condition is resolved.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagaday::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Cormac McCarthy / Stream of Consciousness\n\nRun the narration through one authorized mind and let its associations structure the telling. Begin with a perception or a bodily sensation tied to the present scene: the weight of a wet coat, the sound of a hinge, the cold coming up through the floor. Let that perception lead into memory, private words, a practical worry, or the recurring fantastic fact. Use McCarthy's paratactic syntax to keep the movement concrete: short clauses joined by and, with the occasional longer accumulation when the mind follows a sequence of labor or weather to its end.\n\nAnchor every association in the physical world. A memory should arrive through an object or a sensation, not as an abstract reflection. The smell of salt recalls the morning the tide came up the stair; the feel of a splinter recalls the door that had to be planed twice. Let the fantasy premise enter the same way: as a recurring sensory and practical concern rather than a symbol. The mind may return to the impossible thing because the floor is wet again, because the child asked about it, because the rent is due and the landlord will not enter the room. Its strangeness stays concrete.\n\nKeep enough orientation to distinguish events from recollection or fantasy. When the mind moves into memory, use a clear sensory or temporal marker to show the shift: then, that morning, before the frost. When it returns to the present, let a new perception or interruption pull it back: a knock, a voice, a pan boiling over. Do not let the syntax dissolve the scene into indeterminate fragments. The reader should know what is happening now, what is remembered, and what is imagined.\n\nPreserve the authorized mind's limits. Only the focal character's private experience is available unless the active contract grants access to another mind. Other people's thoughts are not narrated; their speech and actions arrive as interruptions or observations. A character may guess what another person thinks, but the guess must be marked as inference: she would say it was the damp, he would call it nonsense. Do not turn every character into a shared stream of consciousness.\n\nMake the fantasy condition affect the mind's practical reasoning. The character calculates how much water the basin holds, remembers what the inspector said, wonders whether the mark has moved, or decides to say nothing until the child is asleep. These thoughts make the premise legible without an explanatory passage. The impossible thing may remain unexplained, but its effects on the mind must be consistent enough to follow.\n\nClose where the mind's present attention naturally rests: on a completed task, an interruption, a decision postponed, or a perception that opens the next concern. Do not require a realization, a symbolic image, or a resolution of the recurring thought. The paragraph can stop with the character still thinking, still working, or turning toward the door.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagadaz::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Cormac McCarthy / Text Messages / Chat Transcript\n\nCarry the story through sender-attributed messages. Let each person's digital habits shape their contribution: one writes in short fragments, one in complete practical sentences, one leaves long silences, one edits a message after sending it. Use only the timestamps, delays, edits, receipts, attachments, or channel changes that matter to the scene. A delay may mean work, reluctance, or a bad connection; do not explain it unless the story needs the explanation.\n\nMake the fantasy premise appear through what people report, request, avoid, or refuse to type. A message may say the water is back, ask whether the door is still open, send a photo of the mark on the floor, or stop mid-sentence. The impossible thing need not be described in full. Its effects are legible in the practical exchanges: someone asks for a price, someone refuses to enter the room, someone says the inspector is coming. Do not turn the transcript into a lore dump or a debate about what the premise means.\n\nUse McCarthy's spare practical speech in the messages. Let bargains, convictions, and withheld motives surface through what people type: a refusal phrased as a cost, a warning phrased as a direction, a question that reveals what someone already suspects. A character may type less than she knows, or delete a draft and send something safer. Show the deletion or the unsent draft only when it matters, and keep it clearly separated from the sent message.\n\nDistinguish a sent message from an unsent draft and private thought from transmitted text. If the current contract requires a focal character's inward response, write it as clearly separated nonspoken material outside the transcript, not as a message another person receives. The note may record the feel of a cold phone, the calculation of what to say, or the memory of the last time the water rose. It is not automatically knowledge available to the other senders.\n\nUse context outside the exchange only when required, clearly separated from the transcript. A brief passage may show the room where the character types, the sound of the door, or the work interrupted by the phone. Keep it concrete and economical, in McCarthy's register. Do not fabricate an interface event merely to add subtext.\n\nClose on the actual state of the exchange. The last message may be a question, an answer, a read receipt, a draft left unsent, or a silence that the next reply can break. Do not require a revelation, a cliffhanger, or a message that sums up the meaning. The transcript can stop where the immediate exchange stops, leaving the next message possible without pretending the condition is resolved.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagadba::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Cormac McCarthy / Visual Novel\n\nSet each scene with concise concrete narration. Name the visible objects and surfaces that carry the moment: the basin on the table, the mark on the floor, the coat hanging by the door, the light coming through the window. Use McCarthy's paratactic movement in the narrative passages: short clauses joined by and, with the occasional longer accumulation when a sequence of labor or weather needs its full extent. Do not describe the scene as a list of background art; let the narration show what the focal character notices and handles.\n\nMake the fantasy premise appear through visible objects and practical reactions. A character checks the basin, moves the coat, measures the mark, or refuses to enter the room. The narration may note that the water is higher than it was, that the mark has moved, or that the door stands open on a field where no field should be. Keep the impossible thing concrete and consistent. It may remain unexplained, but its effects must be legible in what the characters do.\n\nWhere it fits the established speaker and occasion, keep dialogue spare and practical. Let bargains, convictions, and withheld motives surface through what people say while they work. A character may ask what the basin holds, answer with a price, or say nothing while the other one watches. Do not turn every exchange into a witty duel or a portentous line. The scene's tension can come from the gap between what is said and what is being done with the objects in the room.\n\nUse background or expression cues only when they orient the moment. A changed expression may attract attention, but it does not certify a lie or a feeling. If a cue matters, let the narration or the next line show its significance. Do not add branching menus, player decisions, romance routes, images, or executable engine commands merely because the style is selected.\n\nPreserve required private experience as focal narration or inner response in the active presentation. If the current contract requires a focal character's inward response, write it as actual nonspoken experience: the feel of a cold handle, the calculation of what must be done, the memory of the last time the water rose. Other characters do not know it unless the story gives them access. Do not convert private thought into a voice-over or a line another character can hear.\n\nClose on the actual state of the illustrated scene. The last passage may show work finished, a door closed, a message received, or a character standing in a changed room. Do not require a revelation, a symbolic tableau, or a line that sums up the meaning. The scene can stop where the immediate action stops, leaving the next scene possible without pretending the condition is resolved.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagadbb::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Cormac McCarthy / Web Serial\n\nMaintain a readable through-line across installments: what is underway with the established fantastic premise, what changes locally, and what remains available to continue. Begin each installment with the current practical situation, not with a summary of the whole story. If a reader needs a previous fact, recover it briefly and concretely: the basin was still full, the inspector had said he would return, the mark had moved two boards to the left. Do not retell the events that first established these facts.\n\nWrite the installment in McCarthy's concrete paratactic style. Use short declarative sentences joined by and, with the occasional longer accumulation when a sequence of labor or weather needs its full extent. Name the objects and surfaces that carry the scene: the bucket, the latch, the receipt, the split board, the jar of salt. Where it fits the established speaker and occasion, keep speech spare and practical, but let bargains, convictions, and withheld motives surface inside it. A character may say less than she knows without the narration announcing the omission.\n\nMake the fantasy condition affect practical decisions and local changes. A character checks the same door twice, pays a fee in salt, moves a child away from a window, or refuses to enter a room after dark. These actions make the premise legible without a lecture. The impossible thing may remain unexplained, but its effects must be consistent enough to follow. A new installment can show the same condition from a different angle: the official who inspects it, the neighbor who profits from it, the child who has stopped fearing it.\n\nUse earned developments and occasional hooks without making every turn a complete miniature chapter or an artificial cliffhanger. A development may be a new fact about the premise, a changed relationship, a completed task, or a resource gained or lost. A hook may be a question left open, a message received, or a character arriving at the door. Do not require one clue, one reversal, one confession, or one cliffhanger per installment. Progress can involve knowledge, relationships, work, or resources.\n\nPreserve required interiority in the active presentation. A focal character's private reasoning about the fantastic condition may remain visible or hidden according to the current contract, but it must be actual nonspoken experience: the feel of a cold floor, the calculation of a cost, the memory of what the door showed last time. Other characters do not know it unless the story gives them access. Do not convert private thought into a monologue or a voice-over.\n\nClose on the actual state of the installment. The last paragraph may show work finished, a request made, a condition accepted, a new consequence recognized, or a character turning toward the next task. Do not require a revelation, a symbolic tableau, or a return to normal. The serial can stop where the immediate action stops, leaving the next installment possible without pretending the condition is resolved.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagaeaa::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Douglas Adams / Angsty / Emotionally Turbulent\n\nTreat the fantastic premise as an established condition that makes an already strained relationship harder in specific, practical ways. Do not explain the magic away or let it become a metaphor for the emotion. Instead, let a magical rule, object, institution, or obligation create a concrete inconvenience that the characters must handle while their feelings are already difficult. The magic is real and has real effects; the hurt comes from how the person handles those effects, not from the magic being secretly false.\n\nUse the Adams contrast between a large claim and a small logistical problem inside the emotional scene. A character may declare that they can manage the enchanted ledger, the migrating tower, the speaking contract, or the household spell, and then fail at the ordinary part: forgetting a name, losing a key, missing a meal, or saying the wrong thing at the moment that matters. Let the absurd consequence expose what the character would rather not say. The failure need not be dramatic; its cost lies in what it reveals about attention, priority, or fear.\n\nKeep the emotional cost in the actual conversation and subsequent behavior. When a character speaks or stays silent, show what that choice costs them through the scene's particulars: the pause before an answer, the task they suddenly remember, the help they offer that avoids the real subject, the question they do not ask again. The other person's response follows their own temperament and history, not a script. They may understand immediately, misunderstand, respond with practical help, withdraw, or say something that lands badly. Warmth can coexist with pain. A sincere apology does not have to repair the relationship, and a moment of closeness does not have to erase an earlier withdrawal.\n\nDo not give every character the same comic voice. The narration may notice absurd details, but the characters should respond according to their own temperaments: one may deflect with a joke, another may go quiet, another may become sharply practical. Preserve the fantasy world's rules and the active narrator's distance. The ending may remain unresolved; the point is the turbulent interval, not a forced reconciliation or collapse.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagaeab::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Douglas Adams / Chat Roleplay\n\nWrite the interval as a coherent shared scene in readable paragraphs. Attribute every spoken line clearly, and keep the active narrative person, action markers, and player-control settings exactly as the authorship contract specifies. Do not add a question, cliffhanger, or forced choice at the end. The continuation point should follow naturally from what the characters are already doing or about to do.\n\nUse the fantasy premise as an established part of the scene. The impossible thing is present and has practical consequences: a spell is misfiring, a magical creature is being difficult, an enchanted object is causing trouble, or an institution built around magic is imposing an absurd requirement. Let the characters respond to the same situation differently. One may treat it as a bureaucratic nuisance, another as a fascinating puzzle, another as a personal insult. The difference in response is the scene's energy.\n\nApply the Adams contrast between large claims and small logistical problems. A character may announce a grand solution or a confident explanation, then immediately trip over the ordinary detail: the wrong ingredient, the missing form, the locked door, the uncooperative animal, the forgotten appointment. Let the digression return to the current problem. If a character wanders into an explanation of how the city's levitating archives were founded, bring that explanation back to the immediate difficulty: the archive is now floating out of reach, and the scene continues with the characters working out how to reach it.\n\nKeep the world and non-player characters active. The scene should feel like an ongoing place, not a waiting room for the player. A shopkeeper may continue arguing with a magical till, a guard may be rehearsing a speech, a clerk may be sorting cursed paperwork. Let these background actions remain visible without taking over the scene. Advance the submitted material by showing the immediate consequences of the characters' actions and words, then stop at the point where the next move belongs to the other participant, without announcing what that move should be.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagaeac::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Douglas Adams / Chinese Literary Traditions\n\nUse the vernacular episodic telling route. Organize the story as a sequence of connected episodes, each with a clear local problem and a return to the larger situation. Let the fantasy premise be an established part of the world: a spirit bureaucracy, a magical household object, an enchanted marketplace, a traveling performer with real powers, or a local institution that depends on a fantastic arrangement. Keep the relevant language, period, and community distinct. Do not transplant a setting or cast identity from the cultural examples.\n\nIn each episode, let the impossible thing create a practical social inconvenience. A magical official may demand a document that no one knows how to prepare. A spirit may be offended by an ordinary household mistake. A spell may work perfectly but produce a result that complicates everyone's position. Use the Adams contrast between a large claim and a small logistical problem: someone announces that the matter is simple, ancient, or guaranteed, and then the actual procedure turns out to involve missing seals, wrong names, inconvenient timing, or a disagreement over precedence.\n\nLet different people respond differently to the absurdity. A neighbor may treat the magical event as ordinary business, an official may treat it as a jurisdictional problem, a family member may treat it as a personal grievance. The narration should observe these social positions with satirical precision without making everyone speak in the same comic voice. A digression into local history, custom, or gossip should return to the current problem with new practical information.\n\nAdvance the episode through the consequences of the fantastic premise rather than through an explained magical system. The reader should understand enough to follow the difficulty: what the rule requires, who is affected, and why the ordinary remedy keeps failing. Close the episode when the local problem has produced its immediate social consequence and the characters must live with it or carry it into the next encounter. Do not force a moral or a complete resolution of every relationship.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagaead::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Douglas Adams / Compressed Gritty Prose\n\nWrite in hard-edged, concrete prose. Use forceful diction and compressed transitions. Name the material facts: what the magical thing is made of, what it weighs, what it smells like, what it has damaged, and what it costs to use. Let the fantasy premise have physical consequences. A spell is not a vague glow; it scorches the floor, drains the lamp, or leaves a residue that must be scrubbed. A magical creature is not a whimsical presence; it sheds, bites, knocks things over, and needs feeding.\n\nApply the Adams contrast between large claims and small logistical problems through material detail. A character may claim that the enchanted engine will run forever, and then the fuel line clogs. A procedure may be described as ancient and perfect, and then a bolt is missing. Let the absurd consequence expose itself through the object's behavior: the door that only opens for the pure of heart sticks because the hinges are rusted. Keep the explanation brief and let the physical failure do the work.\n\nChange rhythm deliberately. Compress routine action and simple transitions. Slow down when a character must reason through a problem, examine a mechanism, or decide whether to touch something dangerous. A longer thought is justified when the character is working out how a magical device functions or what a particular mark means. Do not pad the thought with general reflection.\n\nGround abrasive or rough speech in its speaker. A tired worker may curse the magical equipment. An official may speak in clipped orders. A frightened person may say very little. Do not make everyone hard-bitten or give every line the same tough cadence. Preserve the fantasy world's rules and the active narrator's distance. Keep the physical stakes proportionate to the scene: a broken latch, a scorched counter, or a jammed mechanism can carry real consequence without escalating every incident into catastrophe.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPagaeae::♢ * [STYLE] Fantasy / Douglas Adams / Epistolary / Documents\n\nTell the story through documents. Each document must have a clear author, an intended recipient or purpose, and a time of composition. Use the document form the scene actually requires: a letter, a report, a ledger entry, a complaint, a permit application, a maintenance log, a diary page, a notice, or a set of instructions. Do not rotate through formats mechanically.\n\nLet each document be a situated attempt to manage or explain the fantasy premise. The author writes from a particular position: a clerk trying to record an impossible event, a householder complaining about a magical nuisance, an official denying responsibility, a traveler warning a friend, a worker noting what went wrong. What the document knows or conceals follows that position. A report may omit the detail that would make the writer look foolish. A letter may describe an event confidently while getting the practical facts wrong. A maintenance log may record the same magical failure repeatedly without understanding its cause.\n\nUse the Adams contrast between large claims and small logistical problems inside the document's own voice. A notice may announce a grand magical service and then list absurd conditions in fine print. A report may describe a procedure as routine and then reveal, through its attached expenses or corrections, that everything went wrong. A diary may record a noble intention and then note the ordinary obstacle that prevented it. Let the absurd consequence emerge from what the document tries to claim and what its details accidentally show.\n\nLet gaps, revisions, and conflicting accounts reveal events across entries. One document may refer to an incident that another document denies. A later entry may correct an earlier one. A marginal note may contradict the main text. Preserve readable continuity without addin